b1e4ac78c274987d:
Tivicay® (Dolutégravir) – Efficiency opinion
3. Annex 2 - Detailed critical analysis of the efficiency model
3.1 Objective of the proposed economic evaluation
3.1.1 Objective as proposed by the authors
This study aims to evaluate the efficiency of dolutégravir (Tivicay®) versus raltégravir (Isentress®), in adult HIV-infected patients, pretreated, integrase inhibitor (INI)-naïve, with virological failure, in France, through the clinical and economic consequences over the lifetime.
3.1.2 Critical analysis of the objective
The chosen approach of a cost-effectiveness analysis responds to the objective of an efficiency evaluation. However, it should be noted that the objective does not allow answering the question of the efficiency of dolutégravir across the entire indication and for all comparators. The evaluation concerns the efficiency of dolutégravir versus raltégravir only in the management of adult pretreated patients, INI-naïve, with virological failure.
3.2 Structuring choices concerning the evaluation
3.2.1 Structuring choices as presented by the authors
► The economic analysis and the choice of the outcome criterion
The analysis implemented is of the cost-utility type, taking the QALY as the health outcome criterion. It is supplemented by a cost-effectiveness analysis taking the life year gained as the health outcome criterion.
The authors specify that they present other health outcomes expressed in terms of:
 occurrence of an opportunistic infection;
 time before a patient enters AIDS stage (defined by a CD4 count below 200 cells/mm3 or following the occurrence of an opportunistic infection);
 occurrence of adverse events;
 mortality and cause of mortality;
 duration of treatment for each line of treatment;
 number of lines of treatment;
 CD4 count per year, CD4 count at death;
 proportion of patients presenting an adverse event under their 1st line of treatment (by class of adverse events);
 development of resistance.
► The perspective
The perspective adopted is collective.
► The time horizon and discounting
The time horizon chosen by the authors is the lifetime.
The discount rate for health outcomes and costs is 4% per year and 2% per year beyond 30 years. Discount rates of 0%, 2.5% and 6% are considered in sensitivity analysis.

