Kisqali (ribociclib) - Efficiency opinion
1.2.1. Regarding methodological compliance
The method on which the cost-results analysis of ribociclib + IANS is based, for the analysis population specified by the company, is not acceptable. It raises in particular one major reservation that invalidates the results of the efficiency frontier presented in the report. Six important reservations and seven minor reservations are also identified.
The major reservation concerns the method used in the submitted assessment to compare the ribociclib + IANS combination with tamoxifen in terms of survival and quality of life. This method is not acceptable because:
- the patients in the ribociclib + IANS sub-arm were never compared with those in the placebo + tamoxifen sub-arm in the MONALEESA-7 trial;
- including tamoxifen in the analysis population, by considering a comparison of ribociclib with tamoxifen derived from a statistical regression, is not acceptable. This comparison is based neither on a direct comparison founded on randomised data nor on the results of meta-analyses (which are likewise based on randomised data). The proposed adjustment via a Cox model does not compensate for the absence of a direct, randomised comparison between ribociclib + IANS and tamoxifen.
The 6 important reservations are:
- the very limited scope of the strategies compared in the management of the disease according to the sub-indication studied:
• non-robust integration of data on tamoxifen (the relevant comparator for non-menopausal patients);
• limitations related to the use of IANS: absence of marketing authorisation for non-menopausal patients for certain IANS (letrozole), or very limited scope of IANS management (anastrozole);
- the transposability of the simulated population to the French population in question, based solely on the opinion of two experts;
- the adoption of a non-conservative assumption regarding the consequences of adverse events in the model (costs and utilities) and the absence of a scenario sensitivity analysis or, at a minimum, a discussion of the impact of adverse events on the ICER beyond the 1st cycle of the model;
- the limitations affecting the analysis of utilities (examples: absence of new data on quality-of-life instruments updating the results of the MONALEESA-7 study evaluated by the CT in September 2019, and insufficient justification for using a statistical model (i.e. GEE model) to adjust utility values corresponding to the various health states, without resulting in a conservative choice);
- the efficiency frontier results based on the unacceptable integration of the ribociclib + IANS vs tamoxifen comparison (see specification of the analysis population). In the technical report, an error was detected in the calculation of the ICER for ribociclib + IANS vs IANS (total discounted costs of ribociclib + IANS);
- the absence of discussion of the very limited scope of the uncertainty analysis (deterministic and probabilistic), based on arbitrary variability for the majority of parameters included in the model.
The 7 minor reservations are:
- the absence of comparison of the new adverse event data presented in this re-evaluation dossier with those evaluated by the CT in September 2019;
- the exclusion of grade 1-2 adverse events, which represent at least 20% of all adverse events, and the absence of discussion (or scenario sensitivity analyses) of the impact of these adverse events on costs and quality-of-life losses;
- the absence of a description of the design, characteristics and limitations of the IPSOS study used in modelling post-progression treatments;
HAS / Economic and public health evaluation department
