cdec000795654275:
Kisqali (ribociclib) - Efficiency opinion
- the underestimation of drug acquisition costs (when extrapolating treatment duration) was discussed only for the non-steroidal aromatase inhibitors (NSAIs, IANS) and not for ribociclib;
- calculation errors in the estimation of end-of-life costs (i.e. calculation formulas);
- errors in the handling of external utility values from the Peasgood et al. 2010 study;
- the absence, in the report, of the parameters associated with the distributions of the variables included in the probabilistic sensitivity analysis.
The exploratory cost-result sub-analysis comparing only ribociclib + IANS with IANS is the only analysis compatible with the sub-indication studied. Its results are nonetheless subject to the reservations mentioned above.
1.2.2. Regarding efficiency
The reference analysis proposed by the manufacturer does not demonstrate the efficiency of ribociclib in combination with a non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor (letrozole or anastrozole) and a LH-RH agonist in the sub-indication of the marketing authorisation (AMM), owing to the limitations summarised in the major reservation concerning the comparison of ribociclib + IANS versus tamoxifen.
The exploratory sub-analysis of the MONALEESA-7 trial comparing ribociclib + IANS with IANS is compatible with the sub-indication of the AMM (i.e. reimbursement population) and yields an ICER equal to 194 362 €/QALY. However, in the absence of tamoxifen, the only relevant comparator in the sub-indication of non-menopausal patients, the scope of this sub-analysis is very limited and uncertain. Indeed:
- letrozole has an AMM in non-menopausal women in a restricted indication for patients previously treated with antioestrogens (thus excluding patients diagnosed directly at the advanced stage) and in whom artificial menopause is achieved; anastrozole does not have an AMM in non-menopausal women;
- the efficacy results come from exploratory subgroup analyses.
1.3. On the budget impact analysis
1.3.1. Regarding methodological compliance
The comparators selected in the budget impact analysis are relevant with regard to practice in France but are not consistent with the comparators selected in the efficiency analysis. The budget impact study proposed by the manufacturer is informative on the weight of ribociclib acquisition costs and adverse event management costs in the expenditure financed by the national health insurance for the management of HR+/HER2- breast cancer in the sub-indication studied.
Concerning the methodological choices strictly linked to the budget impact, the manufacturer's analysis is subject to three major reservations and one minor reservation.
The major reservations concern:
- the absence of integration of post-progression treatments in the reference budget impact analysis. This modelling is not consistent with that of the efficiency analysis, which integrates post-progression treatments in the modelling of costs;
- the absence of justification for the market shares assigned, in particular to the 1st line of treatment:
• market shares of NSAIs higher than those of tamoxifen, whereas NSAIs are non-reimbursed hormone therapies and do not have an AMM in the indication studied;
• equal distribution between the NSAIs (anastrozole and letrozole), whose indications are not superimposable;
- the absence of discussion of the very limited relevance of the uncertainty analysis (deterministic and probabilistic) based on an arbitrary variation of parameters (±10 %).
