fb3596045bb33415:
Regarding methodological compliance
The method on which the Tecartus cost-effectiveness analysis in the indication population rests
raises 9 major reservations (see summary table of reservations) concerning:
‒ the time horizon is too long given the survival rates in the indication assessed, the available data and the
uncertainty generated by extrapolation;
‒ the transposability of the simulated population to the analysis population is not guaranteed;
‒ the lack of robustness of the indirect comparison, which does not allow the relative efficacy of the treatments to be estimated with certainty;
‒ the PFS/OS ratio estimated by the meta-analysis and applied to the OS data of the "SoC" arm of the real-world study SCHOLAR-2, without analysing the comparability of the populations;
‒ the choice and application of a statistical distribution for extrapolating the OS and PFS data of the Tecartus arm, not justified;
‒ the transposability of the utility scores related to the post-progression state from the ibrutinib assessment, not ensured;
‒ the absence of post-progression costs, not justified;
‒ the probable underestimation of the costs associated with Tecartus, linked to uncertainty on the cost items specific to patient pathways;
‒ the meta-analysis allowing the uncertainty of the efficacy data of the "SoC" arm integrated in the modelling to be tested is too fragile.
Regarding efficiency
According to the manufacturer's assumptions, at the claimed price of € TTC per injection, the use of Tecartus is associated with an ICER of 111 649 €/QALY versus conventional treatments over a time horizon of 15 years.
The uncertainty around this result is major.
The first source of uncertainty comes from the efficacy data integrated in the modelling. Tecartus was evaluated in a single-arm phase II trial, therefore requiring the use of an indirect comparison.
Different data sources were used to estimate the clinical data of the "SoC" arm: on the one hand, the individual data from the real-world study SCHOLAR-2, documenting the OS of the "SoC" arm, and on the other hand, OS and PFS data from aggregate data of a manufacturer-conducted meta-analysis, allowing calculation of a PFS/OS ratio.
The indirect comparison documenting the overall survival of the "SoC" arm is fragile. Consequently, the results must be interpreted with caution. To make the populations comparable, a first selection of patients from the real-world study SCHOLAR-2 was made on the basis of the inclusion criteria of the pivotal trial ZUMA-2 (patients with an ECOG score less than or equal to 1 and having started post-BTKi treatment). The characteristics of the selected patients were then adjusted to those of the ZUMA-2 trial patients by propensity score, which considerably reduced the sample size and resulted in very high uncertainty on the estimated treatment effect. Moreover, only 5 of the 11 covariates identified in the indication assessed were retained.
The manufacturer justifies this restriction by a substantial reduction in numbers. This would imply that large initial differences remain between the populations, and that the trial population is ultimately not superimposable on the real-life population. All variables would nevertheless deserve
