‒ the lack of documentation and justification for the differentiation of the event-free survival (EFS) health state into two sub-states (EFS without and with treatment), which appears to favour oral azacitidine;
‒ the assumption that the treatment effect of oral azacitidine is maintained beyond the follow-up of the clinical trial until the end of the time horizon, in the absence of data justifying this assumption. In addition, there is uncertainty about the duration of treatment, and the scenario sensitivity analyses rely on arbitrary thresholds. ;
‒ the lack of robustness of the method for estimating utility scores derived from the publication by Leunis et al. (2014):
 the population in the utility score publication is not representative of surviving patients with AML and is not transposable to the population of the indication being evaluated;
 utility score estimates were obtained from subgroups not prespecified as health states or groups to be compared;
 the post-relapse utility score retained (0.780) is probably overestimated.

