The objective of the analysis is to document the efficiency of daratumumab within the therapeutic strategy, i.e. in combination with cyclophosphamide, bortezomib and dexamethasone in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed systemic light chain (AL) amyloidosis.
Regarding methodological compliance
The method underlying the cost-effectiveness analysis of the product raises 3 major reservations (see summary table of reservations) concerning:
‒ methodological inconsistency between the assumptions and the approach adopted, in a context of immature clinical data;
‒ the unacceptable approach to utility valuation;
‒ the uninformative analysis of the uncertainty surrounding the efficacy of the treatment.
Regarding efficiency
The evaluation proposed by the manufacturer presents a number of limitations, including on elements of the analysis that are not called into question by the major reservations:
‒ The product under evaluation has orphan drug status, in a disease where the treatments used do not have a marketing authorisation (AMM), which limits the available data and the comparison. Thus, only a comparison versus the clinical trial comparator could be performed, which represents only 40 to 50% of practice, which limits the scope of the evaluation;
‒ Given the inclusion criteria of the clinical trial, the analysis covers only Mayo stages I to IIIA. The absence of data in patients at stage IIIb does not allow efficiency to be assessed in this subpopulation, which would represent between 10 and 20% of the total population and for which the risk of death is highest;
‒ Treatment depends on the patient's risk level, and the proposed analysis is performed across all stages combined. Exploratory data suggest that efficiency may vary by disease stage;
‒ The clinical trial data are immature which, combined with the absence of sufficient external data, prevents a robust simulation of the efficiency of the product over the chosen time horizon;
‒ Quality of life by disease stage and by treatments received remains poorly documented.
