1.1.2. On the economic analysis
The objective of the economic evaluation is to assess the efficiency of sotrovimab in addition to standard care for the population of adult patients and adolescents (aged 12 years and over and weighing at least 40 kg) with coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) who do not require oxygen supplementation and who are at risk of progressing to severe COVID-19.
The scope of the evaluation is restricted relative to the marketing authorisation (AMM) and the reimbursement request. Indeed, the economic evaluation does not include all high-risk subjects covered by the indication (for example, severely immunocompromised patients, for whom this treatment is particularly intended) and adolescents (patients aged 13 to 17 years).
Regarding methodological compliance
The method on which the cost-effectiveness analysis of sotrovimab in the indication population is based raises 9 major reservations (see the summary table of reservations) concerning:
- the transposability of the simulated population to the French population is highly uncertain;
- the distribution by age class and comorbidities of patients at model entry, estimated from different sources, introduces a distortion between the distribution observed in the trial (from which the reduction in hospitalisation rate is derived) and the distribution considered in the model;
- no justification is provided for the choice of cycles (daily follow-up up to 90 days, and annual beyond 90 days);
- the intermediate calculation steps for the transition probabilities integrated into the model are not sufficiently detailed;
- the assumptions relating to probabilities and extrapolations of death are not justified, and there is no sensitivity analysis on the impact of COVID-19 on mortality beyond the acute phase;
- the method for estimating utility scores is not robust, and the scope of the sensitivity analyses on utility scores (sources and estimation methods) is limited;
- certain strong assumptions for estimating quality of life appear implausible in real-world practice and raise questions that have not been explored in sensitivity analysis;
- the inclusion of an emergency department visit cost in the model is not consistent with the model's assumptions and risks double counting with the hospitalisation cost;
- the incorporation of the treatment effect in the probabilistic sensitivity analysis is not based on the observed data from the COMET-ICE trial.
Regarding efficiency
According to the manufacturer's analyses, at the claimed price of € per 500 mg dose, sotrovimab plus standard care is associated with an ICER of 95 230 €/QALY versus standard care over a 5-year time horizon. The uncertainty surrounding this result is major.
Two important and interdependent sources of uncertainty must be distinguished in the manufacturer's economic evaluation of sotrovimab:
