1.1.2. On the analysis of efficiency
The objective of this analysis is to assess the efficiency of iptacopan in monotherapy in the treatment of adult patients with paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) and presenting with haemolytic anaemia (Hb < 10 g/dL) after treatment with a C5 complement inhibitor.
1.1.2.1. Regarding methodological compliance
The method on which the cost-utility analysis of the product in the indication population is based is acceptable, although it raises four major reservations and three minor ones (see summary table of reservations).
The major reservations concern:
− the transposability of the simulated population, which is not assured given the composition of the mixed arm, which does not reflect expected current French practice, and the absence of dose adjustments in the APPLY-PNH trial, which may lead to an underestimation of the costs and efficacy associated with eculizumab;
− the equivalence between iptacopan and pegcetacoplan, which remains a strong and uncertain assumption in the absence of comparative data of robust level of evidence;
− the integration of a treatment-dependent haemolysis rate for iptacopan and pegcetacoplan, which is a source of uncertainty;
− the integration of pooled utility data between the clinical trials APPOINT-PNH and APPLY-PNH, which does not exclude a bias in the estimation of utility scores by health state. In addition, the impact on the results is unknown in the absence of a sensitivity analysis in a scenario retaining only the quality-of-life data from the APPLY-PNH clinical trial.
1.1.2.2. Regarding efficiency
At the claimed price of € incl. VAT per box of 56 tablets, over a time horizon of 10 years and according to the structuring choices and methodological assumptions retained by the manufacturer, the efficiency analysis of iptacopan leads to an efficiency frontier composed of iptacopan and C5 complement inhibitors and results in an ICER of 2 626 340 €/QALY versus a mixed arm of C5 complement inhibitors, with:
− a difference in discounted total costs of 1 043 530 € between iptacopan and the mixed arm of C5 complement inhibitors;
− a difference in discounted health benefits of 0,4 for quality-adjusted life years (QALY);
The efficiency analysis concerns only part of the MA population, estimated at 40%. This restriction is explained by the fact that only the indication of patients with PNH presenting with haemolytic anaemia (Hb < 10 g/dL) after treatment with a C5 complement inhibitor for at least 6 months was eligible for a health economic evaluation, whereas the MA covers all patients with PNH presenting with haemolytic anaemia.
