The reservations are detailed in Table 1, the summary of reservations, and in the critical analysis of the efficiency analysis.

Regarding efficiency

At the claimed prices of € incl. VAT per 0.25 mg, 0.5 mg and 1 mg pen, € incl. VAT per 1.7 mg pen and € incl. VAT per 2.4 mg pen, over a 10-year time horizon and according to the structural choices and assumptions adopted by the manufacturer in the modelling, the efficiency analysis of semaglutide in combination with a diet and exercise regimen results in:
‒ a discounted incremental cost of 22 043 € per patient, equal to the difference between total discounted costs of 34 210 € for the semaglutide arm versus 12 167 € for the diet and exercise arm;
‒ a difference in discounted health benefits of 0.202 quality-adjusted life years (QALY) and 0.088 life years gained (LYG), equal to the difference between benefits of 7.65 QALY and 8.54 LYG gained for the semaglutide arm and 7.45 QALY and 8.46 LYG for the diet and exercise arm;
‒ an ICER of 109 374 €/QALY and 251 663 €/LYG versus the diet and exercise arm alone.

The method for estimating utility scores is highly uncertain due to the methodological limitations raised, in particular regarding the longitudinal data, which were not used through a mixed model with repeated measures, and a risk of double counting linked to a correlation between BMI and the occurrence of complications that cannot be excluded.

Other sources of uncertainty in the analysis are noted, in particular:
‒ the risk of overestimation of mortality due to modelling BMI-specific mortality on the one hand and complication-specific mortality on the other. All other things being equal, when only complication-related mortality is considered, the ICER amounts to 165 845 €/QALY (+51.6%);
‒ the method for estimating complication rates, which relies on external and heterogeneous data.

Regarding the probabilistic analysis, the 80% probability for semaglutide of being cost-effective is reached at a willingness to pay of 144 000 €/QALY. However, in the absence of integration of transition probabilities and utility scores at baseline in this analysis, the exploration of parametric uncertainty is incomplete and provides limited information.

