At the price used in the modelling of € in PPTTC for ITOVEBI (inavolisib), over a time horizon of 3 years and under the assumed similar efficacy of CDK 4/6 inhibitors in the modelling, the introduction of ITOVEBI (inavolisib) in combination with palbociclib and fulvestrant generates an increase in the Assurance Maladie's expenditure of approximately 635 million euros cumulated over 3 years, driven mainly by the additional costs linked to acquisition of the treatments.
Under the market share assumptions set by the manufacturer, the population treated with ITOVEBI (inavolisib) in combination with palbociclib and fulvestrant over 3 years amounts to patients, for a cumulated target population of 4 361 patients.
When a time horizon of 4 years is retained, the budget impact amounts to approximately 972 million euros, assuming that uptake of the combination in clinical practice reaches a plateau from year 3 of the analysis.
Scenario sensitivity analyses were conducted exploring uptake dynamics of the combination of +/- 20 %, leading to respective variations in budget impact of +17,5 % and -20 % compared with the reference analysis.
Regarding deterministic sensitivity analyses on parameters, larger variations could have been expected if the parameters relating to efficacy data and treatment duration had been included.
1.1.4. Conclusion of the Committee
Considering the current state of knowledge, the Economic Evaluation and Public Health Committee (CEESP) retained the assumption of similar efficacy of CDK 4/6 inhibitors.
Under this assumption, and at the price of € PPTTC for ITOVEBI (inavolisib), the CEESP concludes that:
‒ over a time horizon of 10 years, the ICER is estimated at 775 608 €/QALY and 775 169 €/AVG versus VERZENIOS (abemaciclib) in combination with fulvestrant;
‒ the ICER is explained by the substantial additional costs of 350 430 € compared with a marginal efficacy gain of 0,46 QALY. At the claimed price, the ICER is very high and is unacceptable for the community;
‒ the ICER could be underestimated if the benefit observed in the clinical trial were not maintained over 10 years;
‒ the willingness to pay at which inavolisib in combination with palbociclib and fulvestrant has an 80 % probability of being cost-effective is approximately 980 000 €/QALY;
‒ the treatments IBRANCE (palbociclib) in combination with fulvestrant and KISQALI (ribociclib) in combination with fulvestrant are strictly dominated, as they are more costly for similar efficacy;
‒ the introduction of ITOVEBI (inavolisib) in combination with palbociclib and fulvestrant generates an increase in the Assurance Maladie's expenditure of approximately 635 million euros cumulated over 3 years, driven mainly by the additional costs linked to acquisition of the treatments. Given the expected stabilisation of market shares in the indication and the proportion of patients still treated with ITOVEBI (inavolisib) at 3 years, it is regrettable that no analysis was conducted over 5 years. With a time horizon of 5 years, it would be expected to exceed 1 billion euros, which raises questions about the financial sustainability for the health system.

