e6fd70c165233412:
Re-evaluation.
The Transparency Committee re-evaluated 12 medicinal products based on 5 different gliptin molecules, including 6 fixed-dose combinations with metformin. This re-evaluation concerns the indication in the treatment of type 2 diabetes, in adult patients, to improve glycaemic control in combination with other antidiabetic medicines, after failure of diet and physical activity and then of a 1st-line drug treatment based on metformin or a sulfonylurea.
The Transparency Committee issued:
In the indications previously recommended for reimbursement by the Committee, a favourable opinion on maintaining reimbursement only in combination with other antidiabetic medicines (dual therapy with metformin or a sulfonylurea, or triple therapy with metformin and a sulfonylurea, or with metformin and insulin), for the following products:
alogliptin (VIPIDIA and VIPDOMET), except in triple therapy with metformin and a sulfonylurea;
linagliptin (TRAJENTA and JENTADUETO);
saxagliptin (ONGLYZA and KOMBOGLYZE);
sitagliptin (JANUVIA/XELEVIA and JANUMET/VELMETIA);
and vildagliptin (GALVUS and EUCREAS).
Nevertheless, the actual clinical benefit (SMR) of these products is now moderate in all indications, except for saxagliptin (ONGLYZA) and its fixed-dose combination with metformin (KOMBOGLYZE), and for vildagliptin (GALVUS) and its fixed-dose combination with metformin (EUCREAS), for which it is low. Previously, the SMR was major for all these products, except in a minority of clinical situations for which it was low or moderate.
In the indications previously not recommended for reimbursement, the Committee maintained:
an unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the monotherapy indication, except for sitagliptin (JANUVIA/XELEVIA) at the 25 mg and 50 mg doses adapted to type 2 diabetic patients with moderate, severe or end-stage renal failure, which retains a favourable opinion on reimbursement (with an SMR now moderate);
an unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the dual therapy indication with insulin.
No progress in the management of type 2 diabetes in the situations recommended for reimbursement.

