072afdfff6ee59a8:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of superiority of PLUVICTO (lutetium [177Lu] vipivotide tetraxetan) in combination with best standard of care versus best standard of care alone, in a Phase III, randomised, open-label study, in terms of:
radiographic progression-free survival with a difference in median of 5,3 months and HR=0,4 (99,2% CI = [0,29 ; 0,57]; p < 0,001), and overall survival with a difference in median of 4 months and HR=0,62 (95% CI = [0,52 ; 0,74]; p < 0,001) (alternative primary endpoints);
response rate of 29,8 % in the experimental arm versus 1,7 % in the comparator arm (OR = 24,99; 95% CI = [6,05 ; 103,24]; p<0,001) and median time to first symptomatic skeletal event with a difference in median of 4,7 months (HR = 0,5; 95% CI = [0,4 ; 0,62]; p<0,001) (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints with alpha risk management);
of the tolerability profile judged acceptable,
the Transparency Committee considers that PLUVICTO (lutetium [177Lu] vipivotide tetraxetan) 1000 MBq/mL, solution for injection for infusion provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the therapeutic strategy.

f9f7f54d35ac5bab:
Le service médical rendu par IBRANCE (palbociclib) est important uniquement :
en association au létrozole chez les femmes non prétraitées pour le stade avancé de la maladie et n'ayant pas reçu un inhibiteur de l'aromatase non stéroïdien (letrozole ou anastrazole) dans le cadre d'un traitement adjuvant dans les 12 mois précédents ;
en association au fulvestrant chez les femmes prétraitées par hormonothérapie (au stade avancé ou lors d'un traitement adjuvant pour les progressions précoces).

e30303c7f4f5b6e3:
Given:
the demonstration, in a comparative, randomised, open-label phase III study, of the superiority of trastuzumab deruxtecan over a chemotherapy of the investigator's choice (capecitabine, eribulin, gemcitabine, paclitaxel or nab-paclitaxel), for the treatment of adult patients with unresectable or metastatic HER2-low breast cancer who have received one prior line of chemotherapy at the metastatic stage or who developed a recurrence during or within 6 months following the end of adjuvant chemotherapy, in terms of:
progression-free survival with a median difference of +4.8 months and an HR = 0,50, 95% CI [0,40; 0,63];
overall survival with a median difference of +6.6 months and an HR = 0,64, 95% CI: [0,49; 0,84]; and despite the absence of a formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
a safety profile judged acceptable compared with chemotherapy but marked by excess toxicity resulting in more frequent treatment discontinuations in the trastuzumab deruxtecan arm, notably with more gastrointestinal adverse events (constipation, vomiting) and haematological adverse events (anaemia), as well as diffuse interstitial lung disease/inflammatory pneumonitis;
a development running concurrently with TRODELVY (sacituzumab govitecan), for which no direct comparison was expected at the date of this assessment;
the Committee considers that ENHERTU 100 mg (trastuzumab deruxtecan), powder for concentrate for solution for infusion provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with chemotherapy.

f967fd793e371ed8:
Given:
the efficacy data from a non-comparative study, with an overall response rate of 48% (95% CI = [27.8; 68.7]) in the solid organ transplantation cohort (including 5 CR and 7 PR) and of 50% (95% CI = [23; 77]) in the haematopoietic stem cell transplantation population (FAS population);
the results of the indirect comparison between data from the ALLELE study and data from the retrospective observational study RS002, suggesting an improvement in overall survival in the treated group (ALLELE study) with an HR=0.37 and a 95% CI: [0.20; 0.71];
the value of having a medicine that has been evaluated after failure of a prior treatment including rituximab and/or chemotherapy, with an adverse event profile considered acceptable;
and despite:
the lack of follow-up for this therapy in terms of both efficacy and tolerance;
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, and pending in particular the final results of the ALLELE study, EBVALLO (tabelecleucel) provides a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy, namely in adult and paediatric patients aged 2 years and older with relapsed or refractory Epstein-Barr virus-positive post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease (EBV+ PTLD) who have received at least one prior treatment.

f5801f4d560e9b05:
Given:
the place of fixed triple inhaler combinations reserved for patients with severe COPD inadequately treated with an ICS/LABA or LABA/LAMA dual therapy;
the possibility for ICS, LABA and LAMA to be prescribed by any physician as free combinations, and the risk of unsuitable dissociated prescriptions;
the existence of multiple inhalation devices, and the risk of incorrect technique of use for each device;
poor adherence to the various inhaled products;
the high level of misuse observed with triple combinations in the treatment of COPD;
the similar level of misuse when treatment is initiated by pulmonologists and by general practitioners;
the Committee considers that:
the initial prescription of TRELEGY ELLIPTA (fluticasone / umeclidinium / vilanterol) may be initiated by any physician, subject to performance of a respiratory function test (EFR) confirming the presence of an obstructive ventilatory disorder, within the year preceding initiation of treatment with this medicinal product;
follow-up by a pulmonologist must be carried out within the year following the initial prescription of TRELEGY ELLIPTA (fluticasone / umeclidinium / vilanterol) in order to ensure the need for inhaled corticosteroid therapy.

50c8654d2c693795:
Having regard to:
the place of fixed triple combinations reserved for patients with severe COPD inadequately treated with dual therapy combining an ICS with a LABA or a LABA with a LAMA;
the possibility of prescribing ICS, LABA and LAMA by any physician as free combinations, and the risk of unsuitable dissociated prescriptions;
the existence of multiple inhalation devices, and the risk of incorrect technique for each device;
poor adherence to the various inhaled products;
the high level of misuse observed with triple combinations in the treatment of COPD;
the level of misuse of the same order when treatment is initiated by pulmonologists and by general practitioners;
the Commission considers that:
the initial prescription of TRYDONIS (beclometasone/formoterol/glycopyrronium) may be initiated by any physician, subject to performance of respiratory function testing (EFR) confirming obstructive ventilatory impairment within the year preceding initiation of treatment with this specialty;
follow-up by a pulmonologist must be carried out within the year following the initial prescription of TRYDONIS (beclometasone/formoterol/glycopyrronium) in order to confirm the need for inhaled corticosteroid therapy.

e7ad8b00c8fa14d6:
The service médical rendu by the specialties TADALAFIL EG LABO 5 mg, comprimé pelliculé, 10 mg and 20 mg, en comprimés pelliculés sécables, is important only in adult men with erectile dysfunction related to one of the following conditions:
Paraplegia and tetraplegia, whatever their origin;
Pelvic trauma complicated by urinary disorders;
Sequelae of surgery (aortic aneurysm; radical prostatectomy, total cystectomy and colorectal excision) or of abdominopelvic radiotherapy;
Sequelae of priapism;
Diabetic neuropathy;
Multiple sclerosis;
And in those with erectile dysfunction due to long-term treatment with an antipsychotic.

4e056dcd10542dba:
Taking into account:
the demonstration, in a phase 3, randomised, open-label study, of the superiority of ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) over investigator's choice treatment (among 5 different regimens), in terms of:
progression-free survival assessed by an independent committee according to IMWG criteria: HR=0,49 [IC95% : 0,38-0,65];
overall response rate assessed by an independent committee according to IMWG criteria: OR=3,5 [IC95% : 2,3-5,5];
subgroup analyses, conducted in patients who had received 3 or 4 prior lines of therapy, partly corresponding to the current marketing authorisation (AMM) population, which suggested similar results, notably in terms of progression-free survival: HR=0,49 [IC95% : 0,36-0,67], median of 11,9 months (min-max : 9,2-14,1) in the ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) group, and of 4,2 months (min-max : 3,1-5,9) in the investigator's choice group, i.e. a point estimate of the absolute difference of 7,7 months;
and despite:
the exploratory nature of this subgroup analysis;
the open-label design of the study;
the limitations of the control group, since patients could already have received the treatment previously;
the absence of demonstrated impact on overall survival, based on current data;
the absence of any firm conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the absence of data in patients who have received more than 4 prior lines of therapy, although they are part of the marketing authorisation (AMM) indication;
the safety profile, marked by significant toxicity;
the Transparency Committee considers that, on the basis of the current file, ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy, excluding CARVYKTI (ciltacabtagene autoleucel) and bispecific antibodies (teclistamab and elranatanab).

6399b7ff93b4ad15:
Given:
the impossibility of drawing formal conclusions on the magnitude of the treatment effect compared with available alternatives, given:
the weakness of the evidence of its efficacy (data from a phase II, non-comparative study), with a primary endpoint (overall response rate) that is not the most relevant in the context of an indolent disease;
the small size of the populations studied (n = 66), combined with a limited median duration of follow-up;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the results on progression-free survival, overall survival and quality of life, owing to their exploratory and non-comparative nature;
the safety profile of BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib), marked by grade 3 and higher adverse events of the order of 48.5% and serious adverse events reported in 41.1% of patients;
the limits of interpretation surrounding the indirect comparison provided;
the Commission considers that BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for adult patients with marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) who have received at least one prior anti-CD20-based treatment.

e20047e6d35b8203:
LUMYKRAS (sotorasib) demonstrated a statistically significant superiority in terms of progression-free survival and objective response rate assessed by an independent review committee, in a randomised open-label phase 3 study, compared with docetaxel. However, this result is limited by:
the lack of clinical relevance of the absolute difference in median progression-free survival, of approximately 1 month;
the open-label design of the study, which may have led to possible assessment bias, particularly in terms of treatment decisions;
the uncertain reproducibility of the progression-free survival measurement in this study;
the absence of demonstrated effect on overall survival, in a context of advanced-stage disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
the lack of clinical relevance of the objective response rate in this context;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the safety profile of sotorasib, which does not appear more favourable than that of docetaxel;
the Commission considers that LUMYKRAS (sotorasib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with docetaxel.

fd6ae43783fe68e9:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) is low, as is that of SYNAGIS (palivizumab), in the prevention of severe lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection, and eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season:
Children born at 35 weeks gestational age or less and under 6 months of age at the start of the seasonal RSV epidemic;
Children under 2 years of age who have required treatment for bronchopulmonary dysplasia in the last 6 months;
Children under 2 years of age with congenital heart disease with haemodynamic repercussions.

edbfd6bd418975ea:
Considering:
the unmet medical need in preventing lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants during their first RSV season;
the absence of demonstration of superiority or non-inferiority of nirsevimab versus palivizumab (the clinically relevant comparator in infants at high risk of severe disease) in terms of clinical efficacy, although this comparison is feasible;
the limited clinical data in patients eligible for palivizumab, based on the MEDLEY study, whose objective was to determine the safety and pharmacokinetic profile of nirsevimab versus palivizumab in infants at high risk (preterm infants, or infants with chronic lung disease or haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease):
the efficacy of nirsevimab in infants at highest risk of severe RSV infection is extrapolated from the efficacy of nirsevimab in studies D5290C00003 and MELODY (primary cohort) on the basis of pharmacokinetic exposure;
the tolerability profile of nirsevimab appears comparable to that of palivizumab, despite a higher incidence in the nirsevimab group (n = 614) than in the palivizumab group (n = 304) in terms of serious adverse events (bronchiolitis [11 cases versus 4 cases], bronchitis [5 cases versus 2 cases], pneumonia [5 cases versus 1 case], RSV bronchiolitis [4 cases versus 2 cases], viral upper respiratory tract infections [3 cases versus 1 case]), deaths (5 deaths versus 1 death), adverse events of particular interest reported only in the nirsevimab group (1 case of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, 1 case of thrombocytopenia and 1 case of maculopapular rash), as well as hypersensitivity-type adverse events, including anaphylaxis (18,1 % [111/614] versus 15,1 % [46/304]);
the absence of data supporting a possible impact of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) in terms of reduction in length of hospitalisation, transfer to intensive care or resuscitation, and mortality;
the simplicity of use with single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of nirsevimab);
the use as monotherapy and the long half-life of nirsevimab, which may raise concern about selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacological properties of the SmPC);
the Commission considers that BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with SYNAGIS (palivizumab) in the prevention of severe lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection and eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV season.

11ba68ea06676e76:
Given:
the data available demonstrating efficacy vs placebo in the maintenance treatment of UC; but not allowing conclusions on the induction treatment;
the absence of comparative data versus an active comparator or placebo in the induction treatment of mild to moderate UC flares, as well as the absence of equivalence data not allowing positioning relative to the other 5-ASA-based specialties;
the absence of demonstrated benefit on quality of life in patients with mild to moderate UC;
the already known safety profile of the 5-ASA class of medicines;
the Transparency Committee considers that OSPERZO (mesalazine) 1.5 g and 3 g, prolonged-release granules does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of mild to moderate ulcerative colitis in adults.

735aef48a86ce06e:
Considering:
the demonstration, in a Phase IIIb (TALON) comparative, randomised, double-blind, multicentre study in patients with exudative retrofoveolar age-related macular degeneration (AMD) who were naïve to anti-VEGF, comparing brolucizumab 6 mg with aflibercept 2 mg, administered at variable intervals depending on disease activity (8, 12 or 16 weeks apart), of:
the non-inferiority of brolucizumab versus aflibercept on the mean change in best corrected visual acuity (BCVA) at week 32,
the superiority of brolucizumab versus aflibercept in terms of lengthening of the last treatment interval (p < 0,0001), with in particular a percentage of patients having had a 12-week interval of 38,5% in the brolucizumab group versus 19,8% in the aflibercept group;
the absence of robust demonstration in this study of superiority of brolucizumab versus aflibercept on anatomical endpoints (central retinal thickness and presence of intra- or subretinal fluid);
the absence of demonstration of superiority of brolucizumab versus aflibercept in terms of quality of life;
a tolerability comparable to that of other anti-VEGF agents but with an additional risk of intraocular inflammation, in particular a risk of retinal vasculitis or retinal vascular occlusion, requiring a review of medical history and screening for ocular inflammation at treatment initiation, as well as closer monitoring of the patient during the first 3 months and discontinuation of treatment should these adverse events occur;
the Commission considers that BEOVU 120 mg/ml (brolucizumab), solution for injection in vial and in pre-filled syringe, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with EYLEA (aflibercept) in the management of exudative retrofoveolar AMD.

fc3655220d6f192c:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of CARMUSTINE ACCORDPHARMA (carmustine) is important "in the treatment of the following malignant neoplasms as monotherapy or in combination with other antineoplastic agents and/or other therapeutic measures (radiotherapy, surgery):
Brain tumours: glioblastoma, medulloblastoma, brain stem gliomas, astrocytoma, ependymoma and metastatic brain tumours;
Secondary therapy in the treatment of non-Hodgkin lymphoma and Hodgkin's disease;
Malignant melanoma in combination with other antineoplastic medicinal products;
As preparative conditioning prior to autologous haematopoietic stem cell (HSC) transplantation in cases of haematological malignancies (Hodgkin lymphoma/non-Hodgkin lymphoma)."

e113dd649928de31:
Considering:
the demonstration of the superiority of durvalumab-tremelimumab in combination with platinum-based chemotherapy compared with chemotherapy alone, in patients not previously treated for metastatic non-small cell lung cancer, in terms of overall survival (HRstratified = 0,77; IC95% [0,650; 0,916], p=0,00304) with a point estimate of absolute gain of 2,3 months in an open-label randomised phase III study;
and despite:
a comparator (chemotherapy) that is not optimal at the date of the evaluation;
the impossibility of quantifying the contribution of this combination compared with the available alternatives and thus of specifying its place in the therapeutic strategy;
the tolerability profile marked by an excess of serious adverse events (44,2%), grade ≥ 3 adverse events (65,8%), definitive treatment discontinuation due to an adverse event (22,1%) and immune-mediated AEs (33,6%);
the absence of demonstrated impact on quality of life;
the Committee considers that the combination IMFINZI (durvalumab) and TREMELIMUMAB ASTRAZENECA (tremelimumab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with platinum-based chemotherapy, on a par with the combination OPDIVO/YERVOY (nivolumab/ipilimumab), in the first-line treatment of adult patients with metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) in the absence of an activating EGFR or ALK mutation.

17456b61a492bfaa:
Having regard to:
the insufficiently met medical need in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections (LRTIs) due to RSV in newborns and infants during their first RSV circulation season;
the magnitude of effect of BEYFORTUS (nirsévimab) in reducing the incidence of medically attended RSV-LRTI (PCM) within 150 days after administration in newborns and infants at low risk of severe RSV infection (no comorbidities such as immunodeficiency, and not eligible for palivizumab, these being non-inclusion criteria in the clinical studies), which was statistically significant in:
the phase IIb study (D5290C00003): 2,6 % (25/969) versus placebo 9,5 % (46/484), i.e. RRR = 70,1 % [52,3 ; 81,2], p < 0,0001;
the phase III study (MELODY): 1,2 % (12/994) versus placebo 5,0 % (25/496), i.e. RRR = 74,5 % [49,6 ; 87,1], p < 0,001;
an expected impact on the relative risk reduction of hospitalisations due to RSV-LRTI, but with heterogeneous results (significant or not) reflecting uncertainty on this clinically major endpoint, with, for:
the phase IIb study (D5290C00003): 0,8 % (8/969) versus placebo 4,1 % (20/484), i.e. RRR = 78,4 % [51,9 ; 90,3], p = 0,0002;
the phase III study (MELODY): 0,6 % (6/994) versus placebo 1,6 % (8/496), i.e. RRR = 62,1 % [-8,6 ; 86,8], NS;
the phase IIIb, pragmatic, open-label study (HARMONIE): 0,3 % (11/4 037) versus no intervention 1,5 % (60/4 021), i.e. RRR = 83,2 % [67,8 ; 92,0], p < 0,0001;
the absence of data supporting a possible impact of BEYFORTUS (nirsévimab) on reducing the duration of hospitalisation, transfer to an intensive care unit or resuscitation, and mortality;
an acceptable safety profile of nirsévimab (BEYFORTUS), characterised by adverse events (AEs) that are mostly grade 1 (mild) or grade 2 (moderate), such as upper respiratory tract infections, nasopharyngitis, pyrexia or gastroenteritis, and AEs of particular interest such as rash, reported in the clinical studies and consistent with the warnings in the SmPC, as well as the absence of any important identified or potential risk under its EU risk management plan (RMP);
the simplicity of its use via single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of nirsévimab);
the monotherapy use and the long half-life of nirsévimab, which may raise concerns about selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacological properties of the SmPC);
the Transparency Committee considers that BEYFORTUS (nirsévimab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants with or without risk factors and not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season.

948a59821f21f8f7:
Having regard to:
the demonstration, after 26 weeks of treatment in the phase III CADENZA study, of the superiority of sutimlimab over placebo in patients with symptomatic haemolytic anaemia (haemoglobin ≤ 10 g/dl) without recent transfusion history, in terms of the percentage of responder patients (primary endpoint defined as an increase of 1,5 g/dl in Hb, without transfusion and without prohibited treatment), of haemoglobin level increase and of improvement in fatigue (FACIT-Fatigue score);
the results of the open-label, non-comparative CARDINAL study, conducted in patients with recent transfusion history (median of 2 within the last 12 months), which also suggest an improvement in haemoglobin levels under sutimlimab;
the medical need only partially met by the available alternatives, all used off-label;
but also taking into account:
the non-comparative nature of the CARDINAL study, which included more severe patients (patients with recent transfusion history) than those included in the CADENZA study and therefore more representative of the population likely to receive this treatment in routine practice;
the absence of direct or indirect comparison with the maintenance treatments currently used in practice and recommended in the PNDS, in particular rituximab as monotherapy or in combination, which does not allow positioning of this treatment within the current therapeutic strategy;
the absence of robust demonstration of a reduced need for transfusions with sutimlimab in patients with transfusion history;
the uncertainties regarding the magnitude of effect of sutimlimab, notably due to the small numbers of patients included in the studies, the short duration of the studies in a context where MAF is a chronic disease with seasonal evolution and predominantly winter flares, and the proportion of patients who received an unauthorised treatment (including iron and EPO) that may have contributed to the observed efficacy (a higher proportion in the sutimlimab group than in the placebo group in the CADENZA study);
the demonstration of symptom improvement only for fatigue, with no demonstration for other symptoms that may be associated with anaemia (notably breathlessness and palpitations);
the absence of expected benefit, given the mechanism of action of sutimlimab, on vasomotor manifestations of the extremities, which are among the predominant symptoms of MAF and may be highly disabling for patients;
the Commission considers that ENJAYMO (sutimlimab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the therapeutic strategy for the treatment of haemolytic anaemia in adult patients with cold agglutinin disease (MAF) only in patients with Hb = 10 g/dl.

9ab611b5bc9a70b0:
In previously untreated CLL:
In patients presenting with the 17p deletion and/or TP53 mutation
Given:
the results from a non-comparative cohort of patients in the SEQUOIA study;
the impossibility of drawing any conclusion regarding the contribution of zanubrutinib compared with the other available alternatives, both in terms of efficacy and tolerability, in the absence of comparative data;
the Committee considers that BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy.

b9d399ccde9b74ad:
Given:
the efficacy data from an open-label, single-arm study compared with an external placebo control group, which suggested superiority of ravulizumab in terms of reduction in the risk of relapse (primary endpoint), annualised relapse rate and the walking effort index, with however numerous limitations associated with the methodology of this study (different inclusion criteria between the two groups, heterogeneity of the two populations, different follow-up, selection bias);
the absence of demonstrated benefit on disability and on patients' quality of life;
the known safety profile of ravulizumab but with however cases of meningococcal infections having been reported during the study concerned;
the uncertainties that persist regarding long-term efficacy and tolerance as well as the optimal duration of treatment;
the uncertainties regarding the strategy of use in the absence of comparison with the background therapies used in practice, in particular rituximab as first-line and eculizumab, for which a comparison was possible in the absence of concomitant development;
the Commission considers that ULTOMIRIS (ravulizumab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for the treatment of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder in adult patients with anti-aquaporin 4 (AQP4) antibodies who have failed immunosuppressive background therapies (rituximab, azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil).

7d7c4dfedd6446fd:
Given:
the results of the indirect comparison study (TOSCA study) suggesting an overall survival benefit (reduction in risk of death of 43%, HR = 0,57; 95% CI: [0,45; 0,73], p<0,0001), and progression-free survival (reduction in risk of progression or death of 43%, HR = 0,57; 95% CI: [0,43; 0,76], p=0,0001) in favour of cemiplimab;
the significant unmet medical need for patients with no remaining therapeutic options;
and despite:
certain methodological limitations, and the limited follow-up (approximately 2 years), making it difficult to assess the long-term impact of cemiplimab on patient survival;
the known safety profile of anti-PD1/PD-L1 agents, marked by immunological toxicities, respiratory toxicities (mainly pneumonitis) and infusion-related reactions;
the Commission considers that LIBTAYO (cemiplimab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy for patients with metastatic or locally advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CECm or CECla) who are not candidates for curative surgery or curative radiotherapy and who are not eligible for chemotherapy (in situations of failure of or contraindication to chemotherapy).

923a88541230b8a2:
Given:
the persistent medical need for vaccines for the prevention of SARS-CoV-2 infections, in line with changes in the epidemiological situation and the regular emergence of new variants;
the data taken into account by the HAS's current recommendations, namely:
the demonstration versus placebo of the efficacy of the original monovalent SPIKEVAX vaccine (mRNA (nucleoside-modified) vaccine against COVID-19) in reducing the number of symptomatic COVID-19 cases, including in patients aged over 65 years, with a vaccine efficacy of 94,1%, 95% CI = [89,3 ; 96,8] in primary vaccination among 30 415 adult subjects (COVE P301 study, phase III),
the immunogenicity data obtained with the different variants in primary vaccination and in booster vaccination, taken into account in establishing the vaccination recommendations currently in force,
the observational data from real-world studies that corroborate the results in terms of hospitalisations and deaths in primary vaccination and in booster vaccination, notably in France,
the pharmacovigilance and pharmacoepidemiology data that identified a higher risk of myocarditis in subjects under 30 years of age and led to this population being excluded from the HAS recommendations;
the new data included in the file which, to date, have not led to an update of the vaccination recommendations;
and despite:
the absence of new clinical efficacy data in addition to the immunogenicity data available;
the particular points regarding tolerability, such as menstrual disorders, Parsonage-Turner syndrome, hearing loss, arterial hypertension and autoimmune diseases subject to monitoring by the ANSM within the framework of the national pharmacovigilance survey on COVID-19 vaccines;
long-term uncertainties, notably regarding the duration of protection conferred;
the Commission considers that SPIKEVAX (mRNA (nucleoside-modified) vaccine against COVID-19) provides an important improvement in the actual clinical benefit (ASMR II) for active immunisation to prevent COVID-19 caused by SARS-CoV-2, in the context of primary vaccination and booster vaccination, in adults aged over 30 years, and in accordance with the national recommendations in force.

927e61368fb91c6c:
Taking into account:
the demonstration of superiority of BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel) over a therapeutic sequence consisting of salvage chemotherapy, possibly followed, in case of response, by intensification and autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, in a randomised phase III study (TRANSFORM study) in 184 adult patients with refractory or relapsed aggressive B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma within 12 months after the end of first-line treatment and eligible for autologous HSCT, in terms of event-free survival (superiority of liso-cel in terms of SSE versus standard treatment (HR=0,36; 95% CI [0,24; 0,52]; p<0,0001)), complete response rate (higher in the liso-cel group than in the control group 73,9% vs 43,5%; p<0,0001) and progression-free survival (HR=0,40; 95% CI [0,26; 0,62]; p<0,0001);
data from two non-comparative studies, showing overall response rates of 80,3% (95% CI: 68,2-89,4) and 63 % (95% CI: 42,4; 80,6) in patients not eligible for a strategy incorporating autologous transplantation;
the tolerability profile, marked mainly by haematological toxicity (neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, anaemia) and neurological toxicity (15 to 64% of patients depending on the study), as well as cytokine release syndrome (38 to 49% of patients depending on the study);
and despite:
the absence of demonstrated benefit in terms of overall survival in the comparative phase III TRANSFORM study, but considering the non-negligible percentage of patients in the standard arm who received CAR-T in third line after progression or insufficient response;
the non-comparative and purely descriptive nature of the studies conducted in patients not eligible for a strategy incorporating autologous transplantation (TRANSCEND PILOT and TRANSCEND-WORLD), leading to uncertainty about the observed efficacy results, but considering the absence of consensus regarding the assessment of eligibility or not for autologous transplantation, which is left to the physician's decision;
the small number of patients with the LF3B subtype (1 patient in each of the 3 studies), considering that this rare form is treated in the same way as other diffuse large B-cell lymphomas;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life data;
the Committee considers that BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel), 1,1-70x10^6 cellules/mL / 1,1-70x10^6 cellules/mL, dispersion for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 5.2).

52010037e70bf06f:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of ADTRALZA 300 mg (tralokinumab), solution for injection in pre-filled pen, is important:
in the treatment of adults with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment, in case of failure, intolerance or contraindication to ciclosporin;
in the indication for the treatment of adolescents aged 12 years and older with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment.

bf1e5a0bc74962e1:
Considering:
the demonstration of superiority of TIBSOVO (ivosidenib) in combination with azacitidine versus azacitidine alone in a double-blind randomised study, in terms of:
complete response rate (hierarchised secondary endpoint): OR = 4,76, (95% CI = [2,15 ; 10,50], p < 0,0001);
overall survival (OS) (hierarchised secondary endpoint): HR = 0,44, (95% CI = [0,27 ; 0,73], p=0,0005). Median OS was 24,0 months (95% CI = [11,3 ; 34,1]) in the Ivosidenib + azacitidine group versus 7,9 months (95% CI = [4,1 ; 11,3]) in the Placebo + azacitidine group, i.e. an absolute difference in median of 16,1 months;
of an overall tolerability profile similar to azacitidine alone and a lower proportion of serious adverse events compared with azacitidine alone;
of the need for treatments improving overall survival and quality of life in adult patients with newly diagnosed AML who are not eligible for standard induction chemotherapy;
and despite:
the absence of any conclusion possible on the primary endpoint of the study (progression-free survival), due to methodological biases;
the absence of demonstrated impact on quality of life given the exploratory nature of the analysis;
the risk of differentiation syndrome and the need for ECG monitoring due to the risk of QT interval prolongation;
the absence of direct comparison versus the combination of venetoclax and azacitidine, but considered acceptable given the concurrent development of TIBSOVO (ivosidenib) and VENCLYXTO (venetoclax);
the Transparency Committee considers that TIBSOVO 250 mg (ivosidenib), film-coated tablet provides a moderate improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with azacitidine monotherapy, in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) with an IDH1 R132 mutation who are not eligible for standard induction chemotherapy.

a8089f49d981c275:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of IBRANCE (palbociclib) 75 mg, 100 mg and 125 mg, capsules and tablets, is important only:
in combination with letrozole in non-pretreated women with advanced disease who have not received a non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor (letrozole or anastrozole) as adjuvant treatment in the preceding 12 months;
in combination with fulvestrant in women pretreated with hormone therapy (in advanced disease or during adjuvant treatment for early progression).

3faab985298bc46f:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of palbociclib in combination with letrozole over letrozole alone on progression-free survival, with a difference in medians of 10.3 months in a phase III, randomised, double-blind study (PALOMA 2);
but the absence of demonstration of a gain in overall survival, (the Commission recalls that the specialty KISQALI (ribociclib) demonstrated a benefit in terms of overall survival in this setting);
the absence of a relevant clinical benefit in terms of quality of life assessed in exploratory analyses;
and the safety profile marked by a significant risk of haematotoxicity;
the Commission considers that IBRANCE (palbociclib) 75 mg, 100 mg and 125 mg, capsules and tablets, in combination with letrozole does not provide an improvement in the actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with letrozole alone in first-line metastatic treatment of HR+/HER2- breast cancer in postmenopausal women who have not received a non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor (letrozole or anastrozole) as adjuvant treatment within the previous 12 months, in the absence of symptomatic visceral involvement threatening vital prognosis in the short term.

Given:
the demonstration of superiority of palbociclib in combination with fulvestrant over fulvestrant alone on progression-free survival, with a difference of 5.4 months in a phase III, randomised, double-blind study (PALOMA 3);
but the absence of demonstration of a gain in overall survival;
the absence of a relevant clinical benefit in terms of quality of life in exploratory analyses;
and the safety profile marked by a significant risk of haematotoxicity;
the Commission considers that IBRANCE (palbociclib) 75 mg, 100 mg and 125 mg, capsules and tablets, in combination with fulvestrant does not provide an improvement in the actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with fulvestrant alone in first-line treatment in cases of early progression (less than 12 months after the end of adjuvant treatment) or as second-line and subsequent treatment in postmenopausal women with HR+/HER2- breast cancer previously treated with hormonal therapy, and in the absence of symptomatic visceral involvement threatening vital prognosis in the short term.

19bf46a94a0f4763:
Given:
- the demonstration of superiority of mavacamten over placebo in 2 randomised controlled phase III studies (EXPLORER-HCM and VALOR-HCM), on improvement in physical capacity (peak VO2) and NYHA clinical symptoms at week 30, and on reduced use of septal reduction therapy procedures at week 16, respectively, in adult patients with symptomatic oHCM (despite optimised background therapy for most included subjects);
- the demonstration of superiority of mavacamten over placebo on quality-of-life endpoints assessed using validated, disease-specific scores (KCCQ-23 clinical summary score and the dyspnea domain score of the HCMSQ questionnaire);
- an acceptable safety profile for mavacamten, comparable to placebo according to safety data from clinical studies;
- a partially met medical need;

and despite:
- the absence of demonstrated efficacy of mavacamten on morbidity-mortality endpoints (given the epidemiological profile of the disease and according to expert opinion, it nevertheless seems difficult to assess the efficacy of mavacamten on mortality endpoints);
- a clinical study follow-up duration (30 weeks for EXPLORER-HCM and 16 weeks for VALOR-HCM) that does not allow conclusions on the long-term efficacy and safety of mavacamten;
- a potential deleterious effect of mavacamten on cardiac function (reduction in left ventricular ejection fraction) that cannot be excluded in at-risk subjects (patients with a serious intercurrent condition such as an infection or arrhythmia, or undergoing major cardiac surgery);

the Commission considers that CAMZYOS (mavacamten) 2.5 mg, 5 mg, 10 mg and 15 mg, capsule, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the current therapeutic strategy for obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (oHCM) in adult patients with persistent symptoms (NYHA, New York Heart Association, functional class II-III) under background therapy for oHCM.

06ca4b1b7f2a96ef:
Having regard to:
the demonstration in 2 phase III studies with the same protocol, which included adult patients with moderate to severe hidradenitis suppurativa, most of whom (approximately 83 %) had been treated with antibiotic therapy before inclusion;
the superiority of secukinumab (300 mg every 2 weeks) over placebo in the 2 studies SUNSHINE and SUNRISE, with a modest magnitude of effect on all endpoints, including the percentage of HiSCR50 responders (primary endpoint; differences of the order of 11 %, p = 0,007 and p = 0,0149), the reduction in the number of inflammatory lesions and the improvement in the NRS30 skin pain score (pooled analysis of the 2 studies);
the superiority of secukinumab (300 mg every 4 weeks) over placebo only in the SUNRISE study, with a modest magnitude of effect, on the percentage of HiSCR50 responders (difference of 15 %, p = 0,0022), the reduction in the number of inflammatory lesions and the reduction in the number of flares;
exploratory medium-term results at week 52, suggesting maintenance of the clinical responses observed at week 16 for both dosing regimens;
the short-term (16 weeks) and medium-term (52 weeks) safety profile of secukinumab, similar to that established in the other indications of the marketing authorisation (AMM) and similar between the two dosing regimens;
the absence of demonstrated benefit in terms of quality of life, although it is particularly impaired in moderate to severe forms of this disease, with the exception of the NRS30 pain score for the Q2W dosing regimen only;
the absence of demonstration of an impact on the use of scheduled surgery (cancellation or reduction of its extent);
the absence of a comparison with adalimumab (HUMIRA), another treatment with a marketing authorisation (AMM) in hidradenitis suppurativa with an indication wording identical to that of COSENTYX (secukinumab),
the Transparency Committee considers that COSENTYX (secukinumab) 150 mg and 300 mg, solution for injection in pre-filled syringe, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for the management of active moderate to severe hidradenitis suppurativa in adults.

60bc27a535a1d72b:
Considering:
the medical need to broaden the pneumococcal vaccine offer in line with the changing epidemiological situation of infections caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, notably due to the emergence of new non-vaccine serotypes;
the non-inferiority of the immune response induced by APEXXNAR (20-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, adsorbed, or PCV20) compared with PREVENAR 13 (13-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, adsorbed, or PCV13) in terms of opsonophagocytic antibody geometric mean titres (OPA GMT) for the 13 common serotypes covered by PREVENAR 13 (PCV13);
the non-inferiority of the immune response induced by APEXXNAR (PCV20) compared with a sequential PREVENAR 13 (PCV13) - PNEUMOVAX (23-valent non-conjugate pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine, or PPSV23) schedule in terms of OPA GMT for 6 of the 7 additional serotypes;
the interchangeability data showing an increase in the immune response induced by APEXXNAR (PCV20) in terms of OPA GMT in subjects aged 65 years and over who had received a dose of PPSV23 at least 5 years before enrolment, or a dose of PCV13, or who had received a sequential PCV13 - PPSV23 schedule;
the co-administration data with a quadrivalent, inactivated, adjuvanted seasonal influenza vaccine (FLUAD TETRA) showing non-inferiority of APEXXNAR (PCV20) in terms of OPA GMT compared with deferred administration (influenza vaccine on Day 0 then PCV20 on Day 30) for the 20 serotypes covered by PCV20;
the co-administration data with an mRNA COVID-19 vaccine (COMIRNATY, BNT162b2) showing a comparable immune response of APEXXNAR (PCV20) in terms of OPA GMT compared with administration of PCV20 or BNT162b2 alone for the 20 serotypes covered by PCV20;
a favourable tolerability profile;
but:
a potential additional impact on reducing morbidity and mortality conferred by the addition of 7 serotypes (8, 10A, 11A, 12F, 15B/C, 22F and 33F) that cannot be assessed in the absence of clinical efficacy data and in the absence of demonstrated non-inferiority against serotype 8 compared with a PCV13 - PPSV23 schedule. Note that in 2020 and 2021, serotypes 8 and 3 were the serotypes most frequently isolated in invasive pneumococcal disease (IPD) in adults. In 2020, among adults aged 65 years and over, the main serotypes observed were serotypes 8 (not included in PCV13, included in PCV20 and PPSV23) and 3 (included in all three vaccines), representing respectively 15,38 % and 11,37 % of bacteraemias and 10,24 % and 9,45 % of meningitis cases;
APEXXNAR (20-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, adsorbed) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active immunisation for the prevention of invasive diseases and pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae in individuals aged 18 years and over.

ea97eb24ae867288:
Given:
the absence of any clinical study conducted with the specialty MAPAKNA LP (nifedipine) in the threat of premature delivery;
the clinical data available on nifedipine in threatened preterm labour (MAP), based on a literature review, including in particular 3 identified comparative studies of low level of evidence because they are not free of limitations and biases, which do not allow an assessment of a possible difference in the magnitude of effect of nifedipine compared with atosiban;
the well-established and recommended use of nifedipine in threatened preterm labour and its decline in off-label use in this indication;
the known tolerability profile of nifedipine;
the medical need for an alternative to intravenously administered atosiban, oral administration of nifedipine representing an advantage in this context of use in pregnant women threatened with preterm labour;
the Committee considers that MAPAKNA LP 20 mg (nifedipine), prolonged-release tablet does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy including atosiban.

2e6bb5c922ded9b2:
Given:
an unmet medical need in the treatment of severe alopecia areata;
the demonstration of the superiority of baricitinib 2 mg and 4 mg over placebo, in adult patients with severe to very severe alopecia areata, in two methodologically sound, randomised, double-blind phase III studies:
on a clinically relevant primary endpoint assessing hair regrowth covering more than 80% of the scalp (i.e. an absolute SALT score ≤ 20), with percentages of patients who were SALT ≤ 20 responders at week 36 similar across treatment groups in both studies (differences versus placebo): 29,9% and 16,4% respectively for the 4 mg and 2 mg doses in the JAHO study and 29,9% and 14,7% in the JAIR study,
on a stricter endpoint representing almost complete scalp hair regrowth (SALT ≤ 10), from week 24, in the JAHO study,
on eyebrow and eyelash regrowth assessed by the clinician, at week 36;
of the safety profile of baricitinib, marked mainly by an increase in infections (mainly nasopharyngitis or upper respiratory tract infection), headache and an increase in plasma CPK;
but:
of the absence of demonstrated superiority of the 2 mg dose over placebo on many hierarchically tested secondary endpoints, owing to the interruption of the hierarchical analysis upstream, in the JAIR study;
of the absence of demonstrated impact in terms of quality of life, even though alopecia areata has a significant psychological, emotional and social impact on patients' lives and a strong social repercussion;
of the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class of medicines, requiring that patients' exposure to these medicines be limited and that their use be restricted in certain patients;
the Committee considers that OLUMIANT 2 mg and 4 mg (baricitinib), film-coated tablet, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of severe alopecia areata.

b13df45c12a88525:
Within the scope of reimbursement:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of nivolumab in combination with doublet chemotherapy over doublet chemotherapy alone on two co-primary endpoints: the pathological complete response rate (OR=13,94, IC99% [3,49 ; 55,75]; p<0,0001; pCR percentage: 24,0% vs 2,2%) and event-free survival (HR=0,63, IC97,38% [0,43 ; 0,91]; p=0,0052; median SSE: 31,6 months vs 20,8 months) in a Phase 3, randomised, open-label study;
results for event-free survival suggesting heterogeneous results according to PD-L1 level (interaction test: p=0,031), in favour of the subgroup of patients whose tumours have a PD-L1 expression = 1%;
a safety profile that appears equivalent to that observed with platinum-based chemotherapy, despite an excess of immune-related adverse events;
and despite:
the lack, to date, of a demonstration of an overall survival gain (overall survival, OS);
the absence of formal conclusions from the subgroup analyses, given the multiplicity of tests and the absence of a method for controlling the alpha risk;
the absence of a formal conclusion on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Commission considers that OPDIVO (nivolumab) in combination with platinum-based chemotherapy provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with platinum-based chemotherapy in the neoadjuvant treatment of adult patients with resectable non-small cell lung cancer at high risk of recurrence, whose tumours express PD-L1 at the threshold of = 1% and whose tumours do not harbour a known sensitising EGFR mutation or a known ALK translocation.

e68d166a442d5a31:
Considering:
the medical need to expand the pneumococcal vaccine offer in line with changes in the epidemiology of infections caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, particularly due to the emergence of new non-vaccine serotypes;
the non-inferiority of the immune response induced by VAXNEUVANCE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine (15-valent, adsorbed) or PCV15) compared with PREVENAR 13 (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine (13-valent, adsorbed) or PCV13), in terms of serological response rates and geometric mean antibody titres for the 13 common serotypes covered by PREVENAR 13 (PCV13) at D30 after the last dose;
the superiority of the immune response induced by VAXNEUVANCE (PCV15) compared with PREVENAR 13 (PCV13), in terms of serological response rates and geometric mean antibody titres for the 2 additional serotypes (22F and 33F) at D30 after the last dose;
co-administration data with paediatric vaccines (INFANRIX HEXA, ROTARIX, RECOMBIVAX HB, ROTATEQ, PENTACEL, VAQTA, HIBERIX, MMRII and VARIVAX) showing non-inferiority of VAXNEUVANCE (PCV15) compared with PREVENAR 13 (PCV13) in terms of immunogenicity against the antigens contained in these vaccines;
a favourable safety profile, although marked by a higher incidence of irritability in the VAXNEUVANCE (PCV15) group compared with the PREVENAR 13 (PCV13) group;
but,
the potential additional impact on reducing morbidity and mortality conferred by the addition of 2 serotypes (22F and 33F), which cannot be assessed in the absence of clinical efficacy data and because these circulating serotypes are poorly represented in France (22F and 33F being responsible for 5,13 % and 1,71 % of bacteraemias and 0 % and 5,88 % of pneumococcal meningitis, respectively, in 2020). Note that the serotypes most frequently isolated in IIP in children under 15 years of age in 2020 were serotypes 24F, 10A and 23B, which are not included in pneumococcal vaccines. They account for 16,24 %, 10,26 % and 10,26 % of bacteraemias and 11,76 %, 13,73 % and 5,88 % of meningitis cases, respectively;
heterogeneous results concerning the immune response against vaccine serotype 6A at D30 after the third dose (non-inferiority criterion not met in the PNEU-PED study and lower immune response in the VAXNEUVANCE (PCV15) group in the PNEU-LINK study);
the Transparency Committee considers that VAXNEUVANCE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine (15-valent, adsorbed)) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active immunisation for the prevention of invasive pneumococcal disease, pneumonia and acute otitis media caused by pneumococci in infants, children and adolescents aged 6 weeks to under 18 years.

d5358c93ce6c344d:
Given:
the demonstrated superiority of dapagliflozin over placebo in the randomised, double-blind phase III DELIVER study, which included patients with heart failure and LVEF > 40%, in terms of:
reduction in the number of cardiovascular events, a composite primary endpoint combining cardiovascular deaths, hospitalisations for HF or urgent visits for HF; with an absolute difference of 3,1 % (HR=0,82 ; IC95% [0,73 ; 0,91] ; p=0,0008);
reduction in the incidence of cardiovascular deaths and documented hospitalisations for heart failure (composite criterion) and improvement in the total symptom score of the KCCQ (Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire) (first two hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints);
of a partially met medical need in HF with mildly reduced LVEF;
but in view of:
the moderate size of the effect of the results demonstrated on the primary endpoint and the hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints;
the absence of demonstration of dapagliflozin's effect on reducing cardiovascular deaths and all-cause deaths (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints not significant);
uncertainty regarding the transposability of the long-term effect of gliflozin treatment beyond the study period;
the known safety profile of gliflozins, notably marked by the risk of ketoacidosis, genital infections, lower-limb amputations or Fournier's gangrene;
the Committee considers that FORXIGA (dapagliflozin) 10 mg, film-coated tablet, provides a minor improvement (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic management strategy for symptomatic adult patients with chronic heart failure with LVEF > 40%.

7bdccba6a4d419a0:
In view of:
de la démonstration de la supériorité... 

In view of the demonstration of superiority in reducing LDL-c levels after 6 weeks of treatment with the fixed-dose combination rosuvastatin/ezetimibe, at doses of 20 mg/10 mg and 40 mg/10 mg, compared with rosuvastatin at the maximum tolerated dose of 40 mg, in patients at very high cardiovascular risk with primary hypercholesterolaemia (polygenic or heterozygous familial) not adequately controlled on a stable daily dose of 10 or 20 mg of rosuvastatin or an equivalent statin;
a favourable tolerability profile, with adverse events observed with the fixed-dose combination consistent with those known and expected for the two active substances taken separately;
but in view of:
the efficacy of adding ezetimibe to rosuvastatin having been demonstrated only on a biological endpoint (reduction in LDL-c levels), with efficacy in terms of morbidity and mortality not having been demonstrated to date;
a medical need currently covered by the available alternatives (free-molecule combinations constituting statin/ezetimibe dual therapy, and fixed-dose combinations comprising a statin and ezetimibe authorised in the indication concerned);
the Transparency Committee considers that SUVREZA (rosuvastatin/ezetimibe) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for primary hypercholesterolaemia (heterozygous familial and non-familial) in adult patients who are not adequately controlled by a statin alone.

929858996f3707ca:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of STELARA (ustékinumab) 45 mg and 90 mg (ustékinumab), solution for injection in pre-filled pen, is important:
in adult plaque psoriasis;
in the treatment of moderate to severe active Crohn's disease in patients who have failed (inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance) a conventional treatment (corticosteroids or immunosuppressants) and at least one anti-TNF, or who have contraindications to these treatments;
only in third-line treatment of moderate to severe active ulcerative colitis in adult patients who have failed (inadequate response, loss of response, intolerance or contraindication) conventional treatments (aminosalicylates, corticosteroids and immunosuppressants) and at least one biologic medicine among the anti-TNFa agents and vedolizumab.

d236c059f2bee2a9:
Given:
the use of methacholine for several decades for the diagnosis of non-specific bronchial hyperresponsiveness in adults and children (aged at least 5 years), able to perform reproducible spirometry, who present normal spirometry and no clinically apparent asthma but have a medical history suggesting this condition;
the demonstration of the efficacy of methacholine based on bibliographic data;
the low robustness of the clinical study results linked to the methodological limitations of the studies provided;
the good tolerability of methacholine;
the partially met medical need;
the Committee considers that CHLORURE DE METHACHOLINE PANPHARMA 100 mg, poudre pour solution pour inhalation par nébuliseur (chlorure de méthacholine) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current diagnostic strategy for bronchial hyperresponsiveness.

5fe856b6bf42e98f:
Considering:
the absence of demonstrated superiority over IMBRUVICA (ibrutinib) and the uncertainties regarding the magnitude of the proper effect of BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib), given the methodological weaknesses of the ASPEN clinical study;
the longer follow-up provided by the final results of the ASPEN study, which do not suggest any loss of chance for patients in terms of efficacy from receiving BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib);
the tolerability profile of BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib), which benefits from longer follow-up and is notably marked by neutropenias, whereas the tolerability profile of IMBRUVICA (ibrutinib) includes signals of cardiac toxicity;
the value of having an additional alternative for patients in relapse or refractory after at least one line of treatment, given the profile of elderly patients at cardiovascular risk or already suffering from cardiac comorbidities;
the Commission considers that BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with IMBRUVICA (ibrutinib) in monotherapy for the treatment of Waldenström's macroglobulinemia (WM) in adult patients who have received at least one prior treatment.

482110192f04b63d:
Considering:
the demonstration of superiority in the phase III MAGNITUDE study of the niraparib/abiraterone acetate combination compared with placebo + abiraterone acetate, in combination with prednisone, in terms of radiographic progression-free survival in the population of patients with a BRCA1/2 mutation in cohort 1 (i.e. a point estimate of an absolute difference in median rPFS of 7,6 months with HR=0,533; 95% CI [0,361 - 0,789]; p = 0,0014);
and despite:
the absence of a demonstration of superiority of the niraparib + AAP combination compared with placebo + AAP on overall survival;
an excess of toxicity, notably concerning grade ≥ 3 adverse events, with 72,2% in the niraparib + AAP group and 49,3% in the placebo group;
the risk of myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) or acute myeloid leukaemia (AML), identified as an important potential risk in the risk management plan (RMP), and in a context of treatment at an advanced stage of the disease;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on secondary endpoints (TCC, TSP, OS) in the population of patients with a BRCA1/2 mutation in cohort 1 (marketing authorisation population; exploratory analysis);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Committee considers that AKEEGA (niraparib/abiraterone acetate) in combination with prednisone or prednisolone provides a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with abiraterone acetate in combination with prednisone or prednisolone in the treatment of adult patients with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (mCRPC), with BRCA1/2 gene mutations (germline and/or somatic) and for whom chemotherapy is not clinically indicated.

3ea4cad80ab16a51:
Given:
the unmet medical need when optimised antihistamine treatment fails;
the demonstration, in two methodologically sound phase III studies (ASTERIA I and II), in patients aged 12 to 75 years with severe chronic spontaneous urticaria refractory to antihistamine treatment at up to 4 times the marketing authorisation (AMM) dose, of the superiority of omalizumab over placebo, as add-on treatment, on all endpoints (p < 0,0001 for all comparisons), namely:
the change from baseline in the weekly ISS pruritus score at W12 (primary endpoint): change of -9,40 and -9,77 with omalizumab vs -3,63 and -5,14 with placebo, but not clinically relevant in the ASTERIA II study;
and the hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints, notably the percentage of UAS7 ≤ 6 responders (52 % and 66 % in the omalizumab group vs 11 % and 19 % in the placebo group) and the percentage of complete remissions (34 % and 44 % in the omalizumab group vs 5 % and 9 % in the placebo group);
the results of the phase IV EXTEND study, a randomised, double-blind, multicentre study, which showed a lower percentage of disease worsening (UAS7 score > 12 for at least 2 consecutive weeks between weeks 24 and 48) in the omalizumab group (21 %) than in the placebo group (60,4 %, p < 0,0001) among patients initially responding to 24 weeks of omalizumab treatment;
the results of the post-registration study LUCIOL, with one year of follow-up, which showed use of XOLAIR (omalizumab) consistent with the SmPC (RCP) and recommendations for a majority of patients, and efficacy results consistent with those of the pivotal studies;
the 1-year tolerability data from the XTEND and LUCIOL studies, which showed an unchanged safety profile, marked mainly by sinusitis, headache, arthralgia, injection-site reactions and upper respiratory tract infections;
but given:
the uncertainties that persist regarding the carcinogenic and arterial thromboembolic risks identified as important potential risks in the Risk Management Plan (RMP);
the Committee considers that the improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR) of XOLAIR 150 mg (omalizumab) solution for injection in pre-filled syringe remains minor (ASMR IV) in the management of chronic spontaneous urticaria, as add-on treatment, in adults and adolescents (from 12 years of age) with insufficient response to anti-H1 antihistamine treatments.

8163b67601c0d0cf:
Considering:
the demonstration of efficacy of the solifenacin / tamsulosin combination versus placebo and versus tamsulosin alone in men with filling symptoms and moderate to severe voiding symptoms) ;
the magnitude of effect observed with the dual therapy versus monotherapy, which remains small and established in the short term;
the absence of comparison with solifenacin monotherapy and of data in patients not responding to monotherapies;
the absence of comparative data versus other dual therapies;
the medical need already covered by the existence of alternatives that have demonstrated efficacy;
the Committee considers that VECALMYS 6 mg/ 0,4 mg (tamsulosin and solifenacin), modified-release tablet does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy in patients failing monotherapy, compared with separate intake of the individual components of the fixed-dose combination.

9f0862b70b3b4665:
Adults:
Given:
the medical need currently met, given the presence of numerous therapeutic alternatives available;
the absence of new clinical efficacy data;
an overall favourable safety profile for HYQVIA, marked by adverse events, the most frequent of which were infections (notably sinusitis, bronchitis, upper respiratory tract infection) and injection-related general disorders (notably fever, injection site pain), based on the new post-authorisation safety studies (European study 161302, US study 161406), and serious acute bacterial infections occurring only in patients with primary immunodeficiency (PID) (observational study FIGARO);
the presence of anti-rHuPH20 antibodies with a titre ≥ 160 (positive test) occurring in a small number of patients included in the studies, with an overall incidence of a positive test for anti-rHuPH20 antibody binding estimated between 0,04 and 0,317 cases per patient-year;
the establishment of a prospective pregnancy registry (PASS study 161301) that included only 9 pregnant women, 7 of whom received replacement therapy with HYQVIA, and whose results showed a negative test for anti-rHuPH20 antibody binding in the 4 patients who underwent testing;
the Commission considers that HYQVIA (human normal immunoglobulin co-administered with recombinant human hyaluronidase) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with other human normal immunoglobulins administered intravenously or subcutaneously.
Children and adolescents (aged 0 to 18 years):
Given:
the medical need partially met, given the presence of numerous therapeutic alternatives available;
the absence of comparative clinical efficacy data versus other immunoglobulins administered intravenously or subcutaneously;
an overall favourable safety profile for HYQVIA, marked by local adverse events (notably pain and pruritus at the infusion site) and injection-related general disorders (notably fatigue, pyrexia and headache), based on the new post-authorisation safety studies (European study 161504 and US study 161406) and efficacy study (US study 161503);
the lack of follow-up, particularly on safety, in 5 infants exposed in utero to HYQVIA and followed during the first two years of life (PASS study 161301);
the presence of anti-rHuPH20 antibodies with a titre ≥ 160 (positive test) in one patient (US study 161503), and without any neutralising antibodies detected at any visit during the 2-year additional follow-up, without however resolving the uncertainty regarding the consequences of concomitant administration of hyaluronidase on fertility;
the Commission considers that HYQVIA (human normal immunoglobulin co-administered with recombinant human hyaluronidase) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the available therapeutic alternatives.

aa2e973ae86aaf8c:
Within the scope of reimbursement:
Considering:
the demonstration of the superiority of deucravacitinib 6 mg, in two phase III studies (POETYK PSO-1 and 2), with a moderate magnitude of effect, in adults with moderate to severe plaque psoriasis eligible for systemic treatment, compared with:
placebo on several endpoints assessed at week 16, including sPGA 0/1 and PASI 75 responses (primary endpoints) and PASI 100, sPGA 0 and DLQI 0/1 responses (hierarchically ordered secondary endpoints),
aprémilast on several endpoints, including sPGA 0/1, PASI 75 and PASI 90 responses at weeks 16 and 24;
a tolerability profile of deucravacitinib in clinical studies, marked mainly by infections (predominantly nasopharyngitis or upper respiratory tract infection);
but considering:
the limited clinical relevance of the primary endpoint PASI 75, now replaced by more stringent endpoints such as PASI 90 and PASI 100 responses;
the absence of comparative data versus biologics (anti-TNF and anti-interleukins), although such a comparison was feasible;
the uncertainties that persist regarding the risk of skin carcinogenicity and cardiovascular risk, given the cases of malignancies (including lymphomas and non-melanoma skin cancers), major cardiovascular events and venous thromboembolic events observed in clinical studies with deucravacitinib;
the absence of long-term tolerability data;
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class medicines reassessed in 2022-2023 by the PRAC (Pharmacovigilance Risk Assessment Committee), imposing restrictions on their use in certain patients, and the uncertainties that persist concerning populations not studied in the ORAL Surveillance tolerability study, notably patients under 65 years of age (mean age of approximately 46 years in the plaque psoriasis studies with deucravacitinib);
the Committee considers that SOTYKTU 6 mg (deucravacitinib), film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management strategy for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis in adults eligible for systemic treatment.

e06b4afffa2fca1d:
Considering:
a management considered similar between adults and adolescents;
the extrapolation to adolescents of the results of the evaluation in adults with resectable or advanced (unresectable or metastatic) stage III melanoma;
the Transparency Committee considers, in the same way as for adult patients, with extension to adolescent patients, that OPDIVO/YERVOY (nivolumab/ipilimumab) in combination for the treatment of adolescent patients aged 12 years and over with advanced (unresectable or metastatic) melanoma, ECOG 0 or 1 and without active brain metastases does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V).

4d3d755b9b0c4201:
Given:
a management considered similar between adults and adolescents;
the extrapolation to adolescents of the results of the assessment in adults with resectable or advanced (unresectable or metastatic) stage III melanoma;
the Transparency Committee considers, in the same way as for adult patients, with extension to adolescent patients, that OPDIVO (nivolumab) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III):
as monotherapy in the treatment of adolescent patients aged 12 years and over with advanced (unresectable or metastatic) melanoma;
as monotherapy in the adjuvant treatment of adolescent patients aged 12 years and over with melanoma with lymph node involvement or metastatic disease, and who have undergone complete resection.

4d8fd5f64bb93f20:
Given:
the demonstrated gain in efficacy compared with ipilimumab monotherapy, which is not the standard of care in this situation;
the increase in toxicity (treatment discontinuation due to adverse events in approximately one patient in two) in selected patients (99.8% in good general condition or preserved general condition);
the evolution of management of BRAF-mutated patients, as highlighted in particular by the current recommendations;
the Commission considers that the combination OPDIVO/YERVOY (nivolumab/ipilimumab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the therapeutic strategy for the management of adult patients with advanced melanoma (see population for the SMR).

73b0a13c8bce1111:
Given:
the extrapolation considered valid of the efficacy data of the sacubitril/valsartan combination in children, based on the results of the adult PARADIGM-HF study, which had demonstrated a reduction in cardiovascular morbidity and mortality;
an insufficiently covered medical need, with therapeutic alternatives supported by a low level of evidence in children and pharmaceutical forms poorly suited to paediatric use;
the availability of a pharmaceutical form of the sacubitril/valsartan combination suited to children from 1 year of age;
an acceptable safety profile in children, comparable to that already known in adults;
but in view of:
the absence of demonstration of superiority of the sacubitril/valsartan combination over enalapril on clinical morbidity-mortality endpoints in the paediatric PANORAMA-HF study, although results considered clinically relevant in both treatment groups according to expert opinion;
a suboptimal representativeness of the population studied in the PANORAMA-HF study, with a small sample of children aged under 1 year (population excluded from the marketing authorisation), the exclusion of the peri-operative period (before cardiac surgery, before cardiac transplantation and the immediate post-operative period), and the exclusion of congenital hypertrophic and restrictive cardiomyopathies, which are rare but widely represented in paediatric heart failure;
the Committee considers that ENTRESTO (sacubitril/valsartan), granules in capsules to be opened at doses of 6 mg/6 mg and 15 mg/16 mg, and film-coated tablets at doses of 24 mg/26 mg, 49 mg/51 mg and 97 mg/103 mg, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy for symptomatic chronic heart failure with left ventricular systolic dysfunction in children and adolescents aged one year or older.

0dce82b51bf382ee:
Given:
the new clinical efficacy data do not modify the assessment of efficacy compared with placebo;
the absence of robust efficacy data comparing the JAK inhibitors with one another or with drug alternatives (anti-TNF and vedolizumab as second-line, and ustekinumab as third-line);
the absence of established effects on long-term quality of life (apart from upadacitinib up to 52 weeks versus placebo), on complications and on the need for colectomy;
and the updated safety profile of the JAK inhibitor class, including in particular an increased risk of malignancies, venous or arterial thromboembolic events and serious infections, up to and including death, compared with anti-TNF agents, and the absence of clinical data allowing these risks to be ruled out compared with drug alternatives;
the Commission considers that JYSELECA (filgotinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of UC in adults.

e20c82ab5c18f9e6:
Given:
the new clinical efficacy data, which do not change the assessment of efficacy compared with placebo;
the absence of robust efficacy data comparing JAK inhibitors with each other or with drug alternatives (anti-TNF and vedolizumab in 2nd line, and ustekinumab in 3rd line);
the absence of established long-term effects on quality of life (apart from upadacitinib up to 52 weeks versus placebo), on complications and on the need for colectomy;
and the updated safety profile of the JAK inhibitor class, including in particular an increased risk of malignancies, venous or arterial thromboembolic events and serious infections, and even death, compared with anti-TNFs, and the absence of clinical data allowing these risks to be ruled out compared with drug alternatives;
the Commission considers that RINVOQ (upadacitinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of UC in adults.

bf8ae8533d3019db:
In adolescents:
Considering:
the demonstration of the superiority of upadacitinib 15 mg versus placebo, with a large magnitude of effect, in terms of IGA 0 or 1 responses, EASI-75, EASI-90 and pruritus after 16 weeks of treatment (hierarchical endpoints), as monotherapy (MEASURE-Up 1 and 2 studies) or in combination with topical corticosteroids (AD-Up study) in populations including adults and adolescents (approximately 15 % of the sample) with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment;
the results of subgroup analyses in adolescents showing results consistent with those of the overall population of these studies;
the demonstration of clinically significant and clinically relevant differences versus placebo in terms of quality of life (various hierarchical endpoints including the DLQI) in the monotherapy studies in adults and adolescents;
but:
uncertainties regarding the maintenance of efficacy beyond 76 weeks of treatment;
the absence of a direct comparison of upadacitinib 15 mg versus dupilumab in a specific study in adolescents;
risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with drugs of the JAK inhibitor class, which impose restrictions on their use in certain patients, and persisting uncertainties regarding populations not studied in the ORAL Surveillance safety study, in particular patients under 65 years of age;
the Transparency Committee considers that RINVOQ (upadacitinib) 15 mg, prolonged-release tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of atopic dermatitis in adolescents aged 12 years and older requiring systemic treatment, where the comparators include DUPIXENT (dupilumab) and ADTRALZA (tralokinumab).

ab37228189ed9e5c:
Within the reimbursement scope retained by the Transparency Committee:
Having regard to:
the demonstration, in phase III studies of good methodological quality that included patients with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment:
the superiority of abrocitinib 100 mg and 200 mg over placebo, with a large magnitude of effect, in terms of IGA 0 or 1 responses, EASI-75 and pruritus after 12 weeks of treatment (hierarchically ranked endpoints), as monotherapy (JADE MONO-1 and 2 studies) or in combination with background topical treatment (JADE COMPARE study) in adult or adolescent patients;
the superiority of abrocitinib 200 mg over dupilumab, in terms of EASI-90 response at week 4 (28,5 % versus 14,6 %, p < 0,0001), which was also maintained at week 16 (54,3% versus 41,9%, p < 0,0008), and the percentage of PP-NRS4 responders at week 2 (48,2 % versus 25,5 %, p < 0,0001) in the JADE DARE study (hierarchically ranked endpoints);
the probability of flare-free status during the maintenance period in patients who responded to induction treatment, which was lower in the placebo group (19,1 %) compared with the abrocitinib 200 mg group (81,1 %, p < 0,0001) and the abrocitinib 100 mg group (57,4 %, p < 0,0001) in the JADE REGIMEN study;
a safety profile in studies of patients with a mean age of about 35 years, characterised mainly by acne, nausea, headache, vomiting, elevated creatine phosphokinase (CPK), upper abdominal pain, herpes simplex and dizziness;
but taking into account:
the absence of demonstrated benefit on patients' quality of life in a disease that markedly affects quality of life, with a strong psychosocial impact;
the uncertainties regarding the maintenance of efficacy beyond 26 weeks of treatment for the endpoints assessing disease severity and pruritus intensity, and beyond 52 weeks for the probability of flare-free status during the maintenance phase in patients who responded to induction treatment;
the absence of comparison with cyclosporine, the reference first-line systemic treatment in atopic dermatitis;
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class, which impose restrictions on their use in certain patients, and the persisting uncertainties regarding populations not studied in the ORAL Surveillance safety study, in particular patients under 65 years of age (mean age of about 35 years in the atopic dermatitis studies);
the Commission considers that CIBINQO 50 mg, 100 mg and 200 mg (abrocitinib), film-coated tablet, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with DUPIXENT (dupilumab) in adults with atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment in case of failure, intolerance or contraindication to cyclosporine.

d13bee61913c1b3f:
In adults, within the reimbursement scope retained by the Transparency Committee:
Taking into account:
the demonstration in good-quality methodological phase III studies, which included patients with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment:
the superiority of upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg over placebo, with a large magnitude of effect, in terms of IGA 0 or 1, EASI-75, EASI-90 and pruritus responses after 16 weeks of treatment (hierarchised endpoints), as monotherapy (MEASURE-Up 1 and 2 studies) or in combination with topical corticosteroids (AD-Up study) in adult or adolescent patients (approximately 15 % of the sample);
the superiority of upadacitinib 30 mg over dupilumab, the drug that currently has the highest level of evidence, in terms of EASI-75 response (71 % versus 61,1 %, p = 0,006), EASI-90 response (60,6 % versus 38,8 %, p < 0,001) and change in pruritus score (WP-NRS: -66,88 versus -49,04, p < 0,001) after 16 weeks of treatment, in the HEADS-Up study which included adults;
the demonstration of clinically significant and clinically relevant differences versus placebo in terms of quality of life (various hierarchised endpoints including the DLQI) in the monotherapy studies in adults and adolescents;
the safety profile of upadacitinib in atopic dermatitis studies, characterised mainly by upper respiratory tract infections, acne, herpes and headache;
but:
the uncertainties regarding maintenance of efficacy beyond 76 weeks of treatment;
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class of medicines, which impose restrictions on their use in certain patients, and the uncertainties that persist regarding populations not studied in the ORAL Surveillance safety study, notably patients under 65 years of age (mean age of approximately 35 years in atopic dermatitis studies);
the Committee considers that RINVOQ (upadacitinib) 15 mg and 30 mg, prolonged-release tablet, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with DUPIXENT (dupilumab) in adults with atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment in case of failure, intolerance or contraindication to ciclosporin.

ec7202f3e822f488:
Given:
the new clinical efficacy data do not alter the assessment of efficacy compared with placebo;
the absence of robust efficacy data comparing JAK inhibitors with each other or with drug alternatives (anti-TNF and vedolizumab in 2nd line and ustekinumab in 3rd line);
the absence of established long-term effects on quality of life (apart from upadacitinib up to 52 weeks versus placebo), on complications and on the need for colectomy;
and the updated safety profile of the JAK inhibitor class, notably an increased risk of malignancies, venous or arterial thromboembolic events and serious infections, potentially leading to death, compared with anti-TNFs, and the absence of clinical data allowing these risks to be ruled out compared with drug alternatives;
the Commission considers that XELJANZ (tofacitinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of UC in adults.

d710db5eeb2fe995:
Within the reimbursement scope retained by the Commission:
Having regard to:
the very modest effects observed with baricitinib versus placebo across the phase III studies (as monotherapy or in combination with topical corticosteroids, in particular in patients who had failed ciclosporin) and across all endpoints;
the absence of demonstrated benefit in terms of quality of life in a disease that markedly affects quality of life and has a strong psychosocial impact;
the absence of a comparison with ciclosporin, the reference first-line systemic treatment in atopic dermatitis;
the absence of a comparison with dupilumab after ciclosporin failure, although such a comparison was feasible;
a safety profile of abrocitinib in the atopic dermatitis studies, dominated mainly by infections (predominantly nasopharyngitis or upper respiratory tract infection, herpes simplex) and headache;
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class of medicines, which require limiting patients' exposure to these medicines and restricting their use in certain patients, and the uncertainties that persist regarding populations not studied in the ORAL Surveillance safety study, in particular patients under 65 years of age (mean age of approximately 35 years in the atopic dermatitis studies);
the Commission considers that OLUMIANT 2 mg and 4 mg (baricitinib), film-coated tablets, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults requiring systemic treatment, in case of failure, intolerance or contraindication to ciclosporin.

af7458b5a9e21ee7:
Within the scope of reimbursement:
Considering:
the demonstration of superiority of TIBSOVO (ivosidenib) over placebo in terms of progression-free survival (HR = 0,37 IC95% : [0,25 ; 0,54], p <0,0001), with an absolute difference in medians of 1,3 months in a randomised, double-blind, phase III study;
and despite:
this gain, judged as modest on this endpoint, and the absence of demonstrated gain in overall survival;
a marked toxicity profile, with serious adverse events reported in 35% of patients in the ivosidenib group and in 23,7% of patients in the placebo group, and a risk of QT interval prolongation;
the absence of demonstrated improvement in quality of life;
the impossibility of defining the precise place of this treatment relative to the FOLFOX regimen in the absence of robust comparative data;
the Committee considers that TIBSOVO (ivosidenib) 250 mg, film-coated tablet, provides a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the treatment of adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic cholangiocarcinoma with an IDH1 R132 mutation who have progressed after at least one line of systemic treatment and who are not eligible for FOLFOX chemotherapy.

6c4100eb5b15a4e0:
Given the absence of comparative data and the consideration of a phase II study (NP30179 and BiCAR) whose results do not allow conclusions to be drawn on the therapeutic contribution of glofitamab compared with the available alternatives in adult patients with relapsed or refractory DLBCL after at least 2 lines of systemic therapy;
given limited follow-up on the safety data of glofitamab, with a median follow-up of less than 2 years in the NP30179 study and of 2.3 months in the BiCAR study, and a tolerability profile notably marked by the frequent occurrence of cytokine release syndromes (CRS), grade 3-4 neutropenias and severe infections;
given a partially met medical need, with an interest in having an additional alternative for patients who relapse or are refractory after at least two lines of treatment for DLBCL;
and pending the results of the phase III study comparing glofitamab in combination with gemcitabine, oxaliplatin and rituximab (R-GemOx) to R-GemOx alone, in patients with DLBCL who relapse or are refractory after at least 1 line of systemic therapy (STARGLO study);
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, and pending the results of the phase III STARGLO study, COLUMVI (glofitamab) as monotherapy does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), after at least two lines of systemic therapy and who have failed or are ineligible for CAR-T cell-based medicinal products.

3e557658e9233b48:
Taking into account:
a demonstration in a randomised placebo-controlled trial of superiority of nivolumab on the primary endpoint, relapse-free survival, with HR=0,42 (95% CI [0,30 ; 0,59];
the absence of demonstration of an overall survival (OS) gain (immaturity of the available data (hierarchised secondary endpoint);
management considered similar between adults and adolescents. The efficacy results of the CheckMate76k study included no patient aged = 18 years; adult efficacy data were accepted as extrapolable to adolescents aged 12 years and over,
the Commission considers that, in the current state of the file, OPDIVO (nivolumab) provides, in the same way as KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab), a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) as monotherapy in the adjuvant treatment of adult and adolescent patients aged 12 years and over, with stage IIB or IIC melanoma and having undergone complete resection.

4f37e504812b42ac:
Within the scope of reimbursement retained by the Committee:
Taking into account:
the data from a non-comparative study, and the absence of robust data from indirect comparisons that are difficult to interpret due to methodological weaknesses;
the partially met medical need in cases of ineligibility for the FOLFOX protocol, notably with PEMAZYRE (pemigatinib);
the excess toxicity, notably the incidence of grade ≥ 3 adverse events (AEs) of 76,7 %. In addition, the occurrence of serous retinal detachment has been identified as a significant risk in the RMP;
the Transparency Committee considers that LYTGOBI (futibatinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the treatment of the subgroup of patients with locally advanced or metastatic intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma with fusion or rearrangement of the fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (FGFR2) gene who have progressed after at least one line of systemic therapy and who are not eligible for FOLFOX chemotherapy.

6b99f211801400bf:
In view of:
the results from a phase 3, randomised, double-blind study;
showing the superiority of olaparib + bevacizumab over placebo + bevacizumab, in terms of:
investigator-assessed progression-free survival: HR=0,33 [95% CI: 0,25-0,45], with a median progression-free survival of 37,2 months [95% CI: 36,0-NE] in the olaparib + bevacizumab group versus 17,7 months [95% CI: 15,8-19,9] in the placebo + bevacizumab group, i.e. a point estimate of the absolute difference of 19,5 months;
overall survival: HR=0,62 [95% CI: 0,45-0,85], with a median overall survival of 75,2 months [95% CI: 73,3-NA] in the olaparib + bevacizumab group versus 57,3 months [95% CI: 51,6-NA] in the placebo + bevacizumab group, i.e. a point estimate of the absolute difference of 17,9 months;
in subgroup analyses conducted in HRD+ patients for whom the interaction tests between treatment effect and HRD status were significant, reflecting a differing effect of olaparib + bevacizumab on progression-free survival and overall survival according to HRD status;
and despite:
the absence of stratification of randomisation by HRD status, which may lead to confounding bias in these estimates;
the persistence of certain limitations highlighted in the initial opinion, such as the excess toxicity, the occurrence of myelodysplastic syndromes/acute myeloid leukaemia, and the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the Committee considers that LYNPARZA (olaparib) in combination with bevacizumab provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with bevacizumab monotherapy in maintenance treatment of adult patients with advanced (FIGO stages III and IV) high-grade epithelial cancer of the ovary, fallopian tube or primary peritoneum who are in partial or complete response to a first-line treatment combining platinum-based chemotherapy with bevacizumab, and whose cancer is associated with a positive homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) status, defined by a mutation in the BRCA1/2 genes and/or genomic instability.

b0fbceff4879ed31:
Given:
a demonstration of superiority of the nivolumab–relatlimab combination over nivolumab alone in terms of progression-free survival (HR= 0.75; 95% CI [0.62; 0.92]; p=0.0055) [ITT population], with a point estimate of the absolute difference in medians of 5.49 months in a randomised, double-blind phase 3 study;

progression-free survival results suggesting heterogeneity of the treatment effect according to PD-L1 level (interaction test: p= 0.0301), in favour of the subgroup of patients whose tumours have PD-L1 expression < 1% [MA population];

a management considered similar between adults and adolescents. The RELATIVITY 047 study included no adolescents; the efficacy data from adults were accepted as extrapolable to adolescents aged 12 years and over;

and despite:

the absence of demonstration of an overall survival gain;

a safety profile notably marked by an excess of grade 3-4 adverse events (22% of patients in the nivolumab + relatlimab group and 12.0% of patients in the nivolumab group) and serious adverse events (16.1% and 8.4%), considered related to treatment;

the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the exploratory results of secondary endpoints, including quality of life;

the absence of comparative data versus the nivolumab–ipilimumab combination and thus the impossibility of defining the precise place of the nivolumab/relatlimab treatment relative to the nivolumab–ipilimumab combination;

the Committee considers that OPDUALAG (nivolumab/relatlimab) 240 mg/80 mg, solution for dilution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with nivolumab in first-line treatment of advanced (unresectable or metastatic) melanoma in adults and adolescents aged 12 years and over with PD-L1 expression in tumour cells below 1%, with an ECOG performance status of 0 or 1 and without active brain metastases.

68802145443224c5:
Considering:
the data in endemic areas available and taken into account by the HAS recommendations of February 2020, which suggest efficacy and tolerability similar to other available Vi polysaccharide vaccines in adults, adolescents and children aged over 5 years;
its oral presentation in gastro-resistant capsules, which broadens the therapeutic offer, given the absence of needles, which may have a potentially positive impact on vaccination coverage;
but:
the risk of misuse, which cannot be excluded given repeated administration in 3 doses (D1, D3 and D5), which requires, to obtain optimal protection, self-administration and good adherence by individuals;
the absence of robust comparative data versus other available Vi polysaccharide vaccines, in particular in terms of acceptability and/or adherence;
the Commission considers that VIVOTIF (typhoid vaccine, Ty21a), gastro-resistant capsule does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active oral immunisation against typhoid fever caused by Salmonella enterica serovar typhi (S. typhi) in adults and children aged 5 years and over, according to the HAS recommendations in force dated February 2020.

ef15437a7e7f9d5f:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of angiotensin II over placebo in a phase III study (ATHOS-3) only on an intermediate endpoint (increase in mean arterial pressure to at least 75 mmHg or an increase from baseline of at least 10 mmHg 3 h after treatment initiation), as an add-on to catecholamines and/or other vasopressors, in 321 adult patients in distributive shock with refractory hypotension;
the absence of demonstration of the efficacy of angiotensin II on mortality;
the absence of an active comparator in the ATHOS-3 study, which does not allow angiotensin II to be positioned relative to the other vasopressors available in the therapeutic strategy;
an acceptable safety profile but with identified adverse events (thromboembolic events and peripheral ischaemia) that require close monitoring of blood pressure, the use of concomitant prophylaxis of venous thromboembolic events, and administration of the product at the lowest possible dose to achieve or maintain adequate mean arterial pressure and tissue perfusion;
a medical need to benefit from a therapeutic alternative in the management of refractory hypotension in septic shock and other distributive shock;
the Commission considers that GIAPREZA 2,5 mg/ml (angiotensin II), solution to be diluted for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy of last resort for the treatment of refractory hypotension in adults with septic shock or any other distributive shock despite adequate fluid resuscitation and after administration of available catecholamines and other vasopressors.

580686f4c100270a:
Having regard to:
a demonstration of superiority of osimertinib (TAGRISSO), in a phase III, randomised, double-blind study, versus placebo in terms of disease-free survival, already noted in the registration opinion;
new overall survival data having demonstrated superiority of osimertinib on this endpoint:
in patients with stages II-IIIA (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint), HR= 0,49 ; IC95% [0,33 ; 0,73], and
in patients with stages IB-IIIA (total population, hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint), HR= 0,49 ; IC95% [0,34 ; 0,70];
and despite:
uncertainties regarding the size of the effect of osimertinib on overall survival, related to the risk of overestimating the overall survival gain (imbalance between the groups of patients receiving osimertinib after recurrence [27,2 % in the placebo group and 40,8 % in the osimertinib group], although it has had marketing authorisation for locally advanced and metastatic stages since 2018, leading to suboptimal management of patients in the placebo group in case of recurrence);
a decline in the size of the effect observed in the long term compared with the results presented at the 2022 registration review, suggested in the analysis with 22,1 additional months of follow-up;
excess toxicity compared with placebo, notably more adverse events of grade ≥ 3 (20,2 % versus 13,4 %) or those leading to treatment discontinuation (11 % versus 2,9 %);
the absence of quality-of-life data of demonstrative value;
the Transparency Committee considers that, in the current state of the file, TAGRISSO (osimertinib), in adjuvant treatment, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with the current management strategy for NSCLC stages IB-IIIA with activating EGFR mutations, exon 19 deletion or exon 21 substitution (L858R), after complete tumour resection and adjuvant chemotherapy if indicated.

43e8b809d0c991c3:
The service médical rendu (SMR) of DOVATO (dolutegravir/lamivudine) is important only in adults and adolescents aged over 12 years and weighing at least 40 kg:
treatment-naïve patients, with more than 200 CD4/mm3, a VL < 100,000 copies/mL and no known or suspected resistance to either of the two molecules;
pre-treated patients, with a stable VL < 50 copies/mL for at least one year, more than 350 CD4/mm3 and no known or suspected resistance to either of the two molecules.

148935a93c96c000:
Given:
the demonstration, in an open-label, randomised phase III study, of the superiority of sacituzumab govitecan over investigator's choice monochemotherapy (eribulin, capecitabine, gemcitabine or vinorelbine) in adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic RH+ / HER2- breast cancer (IHC 0, IHC 1+ or IHC 2+/ISH-) who had progressed after treatment with a CDK 4/6 inhibitor, hormone therapy, a taxane, and at least 2 but no more than 4 chemotherapies for metastatic disease, in terms of:
progression-free survival with a point estimate difference in median of +1,5 months and HR = 0,661, IC95% = [0,529 ; 0,826];
overall survival with a point estimate difference in median of +3,2 months and HR = ,789, IC95% = [0,646 ; 0,964]; objective response rate with OR = 1,625, IC95% = [1,034 ; 2,555];
partial improvement in quality of life concerning time to deterioration of global health status and fatigue, but without improvement in pain;
and despite:
a safety profile marked by excess toxicity, notably the incidence of grade = 3 adverse events (AEs) of 74% (60% in the monochemotherapy group) and serious AEs of 28% (19% in the monochemotherapy group);
furthermore, the risk management plan identifies as important identified risks notably serious infections secondary to neutropenia and severe diarrhoea;
the Transparency Committee considers that TRODELVY (sacituzumab govitecan) 200mg provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy for adult patients with unresectable or metastatic hormone receptor-positive (HR+) and HER2-negative breast cancer who have already received hormone therapy and at least two additional systemic treatments in the advanced stage of the disease.

a3f9758773405f14:
Considering:
its broad in vitro activity against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria and anaerobic bacteria, some of which are resistant to carbapenems and other antibiotics;
the demonstration of non-inferiority of eravacycline versus ertapenem and meropenem on the clinical cure rate, in patients with mild complicated intra-abdominal infections;
the partially covered medical need due to the existence of alternatives that have demonstrated efficacy in infections caused by multidrug-resistant bacteria,
but:
the lack of data in more severe patients or in patients with infections due to multidrug-resistant bacteria, who were not included in the clinical studies;
the difficult transposability of the experimental data, as the patients included in the trials are not representative of those likely to receive eravacycline in clinical practice;
the absence of comparative data versus tigecycline in patients with an infection due to multidrug-resistant bacteria, although this comparison was feasible;
the Transparency Committee considers that XERAVA (eravacycline) solution to be diluted for infusion does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of complicated intra-abdominal infections.

aad3e8f603c643bf:
Given:
the methodological quality of the two pivotal studies (controlled, randomised, double-blind, relevant choice of endpoints including the imperative needs affecting quality of life, sample size), but knowing that the choice of placebo is regrettable, in particular in patients naive to any biologic treatment, and that the efficacy assessment was carried out in a heterogeneous population grouping together patients naive (majority) and non-naive to biologic treatment;
the demonstration of superiority of mirikizumab over placebo, with an effect size at best moderate on clinical remission;
the absence of demonstration of an effect on the need for colectomy and on quality of life;
the absence of comparative data versus anti-TNFα agents in patients having had an inadequate response to a conventional background treatment and naive to anti-TNFα, and versus vedolizumab (ENTYVIO), whereas these comparisons were feasible;
and the good safety profile of mirikizumab,
the Commission considers that OMVOH 300 mg, solution à diluer pour perfusion and OMVOH 100 mg, solution injectable (mirikizumab) do not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy.

10b7758feea52eb4:
Having regard to:
the results of the non-comparative phase II SKIPPER study conducted in children aged 3 months to 17 years with anaemia associated with chronic kidney disease, whether or not on dialysis, previously treated with a stable dose of erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA), showing maintenance of the mean change in haemoglobin concentration from baseline within the target range ± 1 g/dl and with a standard deviation below 1,5 g/dl: change of +0,48 g/dl (± 1,03) (90% CI = [0,20 ; 0,76]);
the absence of comparative data, which does not allow methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta to be positioned relative to other ESAs in children and adolescents;
the numerous data in adults demonstrating the efficacy of methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta and its non-inferiority or equivalence relative to available alternatives in ESA-naive patients, whether or not on dialysis, and in dialysis patients previously treated with an ESA;
the safety profile of methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta in the paediatric population, established on the basis of low patient exposure in the phase II studies, comparable to that determined in adults;
the Transparency Committee considers that MIRCERA 30 µg, 50 µg, 75 µg, 100 µg, 120 µg, 150 µg, 200 µg, 250 µg, 360 µg (methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta), solution for injection in pre-filled syringe does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with other erythropoiesis-stimulating agents.

4f0c3b9e3b26a24d:
Considering:
the partially met medical need in adult cytomegalovirus-seronegative [R-] patients who have received a kidney transplant from a cytomegalovirus-seropositive [D+] donor, within the framework of CMV disease prophylaxis;
the demonstrated non-inferiority of letermovir compared with valganciclovir in terms of the proportion of patients with CMV disease occurring at week 52 post-kidney transplant (primary endpoint) whose donor is CMV-seropositive [D+/R-]: 10,4 % (30/289) versus 11,8 % (35/297), i.e. an adjusted difference = -1,4 % (95%CI = [-6,5 ; 3,8]);
a relatively favourable safety profile due to a lower risk of leukopenia and neutropenia (risk factors for CMV and other pathogen infections) compared with valganciclovir;
a genetic barrier to resistance of letermovir that appears convincing (low rate of resistance) and an absence of cross-resistance with other anti-CMV antivirals, in particular DNA polymerase inhibitors, leaving open the possibility of using other antiviral agents subsequently without risk of therapeutic failure;
but:
the absence of demonstration of superiority of letermovir over valganciclovir in terms of the proportion of patients with CMV disease occurring at week 52 post-kidney transplant whose donor is CMV-seropositive [D+/R-];
the absence of demonstration of an improvement in the time to onset of CMV disease with letermovir compared with valganciclovir, after 52 weeks of follow-up post-kidney transplant;
the Committee considers that PREVYMIS (letermovir) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the CMV disease prophylaxis strategy in adult cytomegalovirus-seronegative [R-] patients who have received a kidney transplant from a cytomegalovirus-seropositive [D+] donor.

6c71c99aef441700:
Considering:
the results of the randomised, double-blind phase III study 1VIT17044, which did not show a significant difference versus oral iron in mean change in Hb level at D35 from baseline (2.22 g/dl versus 1.92 g/dl, p = 0.3108) in children aged 1 to 17 years with iron deficiency anaemia;
the inclusion of a population only partially representative of the population targeted by the indication:
only 22% of patients included in the study were aged 1 to 12 years (25% in the ferric carboxymaltose group) and only 20% of patients were male;
the study protocol provided for the inclusion only of patients with iron deficiency with anaemia, whereas the target indication also covers patients with iron deficiency without anaemia;
the inclusion of patients with a documented history of an episode of inadequate response to any oral iron-based therapy at least 8 weeks (56 days) before randomisation, who responded to oral treatment during the study, which raises the hypothesis of a therapeutic compliance problem;
the absence of specific data in children aged 1 to 13 years with chronic kidney disease requiring haemodialysis;
the absence of data in patients in whom there is a clinical need to administer iron rapidly;
the absence of long-term efficacy and tolerability data (follow-up duration of 6 weeks);
the absence of comparative data versus other injectable iron specialities;
the Commission considers that FERINJECT 50 mg/ml (ferric carboxymaltose), solution for injection/for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy in patients aged 1 to 13 years, which includes the relevant comparators VENOFER (iron sucrose) and other injectable iron specialities based on iron sucrose.

1923871367754fda:
Given:
the results observed in children compared with those in adults, in a study evaluating the pharmacokinetics of riociguat, suggesting that the clinical results shown in adults can be transposed to children;
the absence of demonstration of clinical efficacy, in particular regarding morbidity-mortality, of ADEMPAS (riociguat) in children and adolescents with PAH in functional class II or III aged under 18 years and weighing at least 50 kg, due to the absence of comparative data;
the safety profile appearing similar to that in adults;
the Commission considers that ADEMPAS (riociguat), film-coated tablet does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the relevant comparators (see section 5.2) in the current therapeutic management strategy for children and adolescents under 18 years of age and weighing more than 50 kg with pulmonary arterial hypertension in functional class II to III.

e940177161aaf78b:
Taking into account:
the persistent medical need for vaccines to prevent SARS-CoV-2 infection, given the changing epidemiological situation and the regular emergence of new variants, particularly in people who do not wish or cannot receive an mRNA vaccine;
the data taken into account by the current HAS recommendations, namely:
the placebo-controlled demonstration of the vaccine efficacy of monovalent original NUVAXOVID (SARS-CoV-2 Spike protein and Matrix-M adjuvant) in reducing the number of symptomatic COVID-19 cases, including in patients over 65 years of age, with an estimated vaccine efficacy of 89,7 % IC95% [80,2 ; 94,6] in 15 139 subjects aged 18 to 84 years in trial 2019nCoV-302, conducted in the United Kingdom (enrolment from 28/09/20 to 28/11/20), and 90,4 % IC95% [82,9 ; 94,6] in 29 582 adult subjects in trial 2019nCoV-301, conducted in the United States and Mexico;
the immunogenicity data obtained with the different variants in primary vaccination and booster vaccination, taken into account to establish the current vaccination recommendations;
the new data added to the dossier which, to date, have not led to an update of the vaccination recommendations;
an acceptable safety profile, with reported cases of myocarditis whose course after vaccination does not differ from that in the general population;
and despite:
the absence of new clinical efficacy data in addition to the immunogenicity data available;
long-term uncertainties, in particular regarding the duration of protection conferred,
the Committee considers that, as with SPIKEVAX (modified-nucleoside mRNA vaccine against COVID-19), NUVAXOVID (SARS-CoV-2 Spike protein and Matrix-M adjuvant) provides an important improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR II) for active immunisation to prevent COVID-19 caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus in persons aged 12 years and over, in accordance with the current HAS recommendations.

cd4c9bf5466f606e:
Considering:
the methodological quality of the efficacy evidence for SKYRIZI (risankizumab) from three randomised, placebo-controlled studies, conducted mainly in patients who had failed at least one anti-TNF and mainly with moderate Crohn's disease (CD),
the clinically relevant magnitude of effect versus placebo on clinical remission and endoscopic response at S12 and S52, and a demonstrated effect on fatigue at S12,
the lower robustness of this evidence for maintenance treatment;
the results of the direct comparison between ustekinumab and risankizumab in a study of good methodological quality (randomised, albeit single-blind, versus ustekinumab, a clinically relevant comparator prescribed at optimised dosing), in favour of risankizumab, with a moderate effect size;
the absence of established additional impact of risankizumab on complications (fistulas, strictures), extra-intestinal manifestations of Crohn's disease and recourse to surgery;
and the known safety profile of SKYRIZI (risankizumab) in Crohn's disease,
the Commission considers that SKYRIZI 360 mg and 600 mg, solution for injection in cartridge (360 mg dosage), solution for dilution for infusion (600 mg dosage), provide a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with STELARA (ustekinumab) at optimised dosing (SC injection every 8 weeks) in adults in third-line treatment of moderately to severely active Crohn's disease, i.e. after failure of conventional treatment including an immunosuppressant (including azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine) or a corticosteroid, and at least one anti-TNF (adalimumab, infliximab).

a410210ee6812b39:
Having regard to:
the absence of comparative data and of a phase I/II study (EPCORE NHL-1) whose results do not allow a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of epcoritamab compared with the available alternatives in adult patients with relapsed or refractory LDGCB (diffuse large B-cell lymphoma) after at least 2 lines of systemic treatment;
limited follow-up on the tolerability data for epcoritamab, with a median follow-up of 25.5 months in the EPCORE NHL-1 study, and a safety profile marked in particular by the frequent occurrence of cytokine release syndrome (CRS) as well as grade 3-4 neutropenia;
a partially met medical need, with an interest in having an additional alternative for patients who relapse or are refractory after at least two lines of treatment for LDGCB;
and pending the results of the phase III study comparing epcoritamab monotherapy versus chemotherapy at the investigator's choice (rituximab-bendamustine or R-GemOx), in patients with relapsed or refractory LDGCB, after at least one line of treatment including an anti-CD20 antibody, or refractory/ineligible for autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (study DLBCL-1),
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, and pending the results of the phase III study EPCORE DLBCL-1, TEPKINLY (epcoritamab) monotherapy does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (LDGCB), after at least two lines of systemic treatment and in failure of or ineligible for CAR-T cell-based medicines.

6ce5844e81dbe5f6:
Given:
- an alopecia areata treatment need that is partially met in adults and not met in adolescents with severe disease;
- the demonstration of the superiority of ritlécitinib 50 mg once daily over placebo in adult and adolescent patients aged 12 years and older with severe to very severe alopecia areata, in a phase IIb/III study, on the primary endpoint of extent of alopecia areata and the key secondary endpoint of patient global impression of improvement of alopecia areata;
- the safety profile of ritlécitinib, mainly marked by an increase in infections (mainly nasopharyngitis or upper respiratory tract infection), diarrhoea and acne;
but taking into account:
a modest magnitude of effect versus placebo:
on the primary endpoint assessing hair regrowth over more than 90% of the scalp (i.e. an absolute SALT score ≤ 10) assessed at week 24 (13,4 % versus 1,5 %, p = 0,000311),
on the hierarchical secondary endpoint assessing the percentage of patients rating their alopecia areata as "moderately improved" or "greatly improved" at week 24 compared with baseline (49,17 % in the ritlécitinib group versus 9,23 %, p < 0,0001);
the weak robustness of the demonstration, relying on a single pivotal phase IIb/III study, with methodological limitations including:
a partially controlled alpha risk, because the multiplicity of statistical analysis plans (for the EMA, the FDA and for publication) was not taken into account in the alpha risk control method;
the hypothesis formulated of a difference of at least 20% between placebo and the ritlécitinib dose of 200 mg induction and 50 mg maintenance on the SALT ≤ 10 response was not achieved with the 50 mg dose;
the absence of demonstrated impact on quality of life, even though alopecia areata has a substantial psychological, emotional and social impact on patients' lives;
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class, requiring limitation of patients' exposure to these medicines and restriction of their use in certain patients;
the fact that the available data do not allow positioning it relative to baricitinib (OLUMIANT), which holds a marketing authorisation (AMM) in adults, in the absence of comparative data between the two treatments given concomitant development,
the Commission considers that LITFULO 50 mg (ritlécitinib), capsule, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV):
on a par with OLUMIANT (baricitinib), within the management strategy for severe alopecia areata in adults,
within the management strategy for severe alopecia areata in adolescents aged 12 years and older.

b79c4ddd202ecce6:
In first-line treatment:
Given:
the statistically significant superiority of RETSEVMO (selpercatinib) in terms of progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee, in a phase III, open-label randomised study versus chemotherapy and immunotherapy (LIBRETTO-431),
despite:
the absence of demonstrated effect on overall survival at the interim analysis, in a context of advanced-stage disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the safety profile of RETSEVMO (selpercatinib), notably marked by a higher number in the selpercatinib group than in the control group of grade ≥ 3 adverse events (70,3% versus 57,1%), serious adverse events (34,8% versus 23,5%) and deaths due to adverse events (4,4% versus 0,0%), as well as cardiotoxicity (notably grade ≥ 3 arterial hypertension (20,3% versus 3,1%)) observed in the LIBRETTO-431 study, and grade ≥ 3 QT interval prolongation (8,9% versus 0,0%), adverse events mentioned in the SmPC and subject to a RMP;
the Committee considers that RETSEVMO (selpercatinib) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with treatment with immunochemotherapy in first-line treatment.

1a0bb49217c5a0c3:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority over placebo;
but the absence of a robust comparison with a clinically relevant comparator, although such a comparison was feasible;
the Commission considers that BIMZELX (bimekizumab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 5.2.

94fae6f43cd1dad8:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of rucaparib monotherapy versus placebo, in a randomised, double-blind phase 3 study, in terms of progression-free survival assessed by the investigator in:
HRD subpopulation: HR = 0,47; 95% CI [0,31-0,72]; p = 0,0005; with a median progression-free survival of 28,7 months in the rucaparib + IV placebo group and 11,3 months in the oral placebo + IV placebo group;
ITT population: HR = 0,52; 95% CI [0,40-0,68]; p < 0,0001; with a median progression-free survival of 20,2 months in the rucaparib + IV placebo group and 11,0 months in the oral placebo + IV placebo group;
the absence of demonstrated superiority in overall survival in the HRD subpopulation and the ITT population in an interim analysis;
limited follow-up on the efficacy of rucaparib, with a median follow-up of approximately 26 months;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the adverse event profile of rucaparib, marked in particular by a high incidence of grade ≥ 3 adverse events (AEs) (61% and 23%), serious AEs (21% and 6%) and the occurrence of myelodysplastic syndromes/acute myeloid leukaemia;
the context of the current strategy, which includes an available treatment (olaparib/bevacizumab) that has demonstrated an overall survival gain as maintenance therapy in patients with homologous recombination deficient (HRD) tumours;
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the file, RUBRACA (rucaparib) 200 mg, 250 mg and 300 mg, film-coated tablets, like ZEJULA (niraparib), provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the maintenance treatment of adult patients with advanced, high-grade (FIGO stages III and IV) epithelial ovarian, fallopian tube or primary peritoneal cancer who are in (complete or partial) response after completion of first-line platinum-based chemotherapy.

78bdd3e2538ec3cc:
In first-line treatment, given:
a demonstration of superiority of RETSEVMO (selpercatinib) over cabozantinib or vandetanib in terms of progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee (HR = 0.280; 95% CI [0.165; 0.475], p<0.0001), in a randomised open-label phase III study (LIBRETTO-531);
a tolerance judged to be favourable;
a management considered similar between adults and adolescents. As the studies (LIBRETTO-001 and LIBRETTO-531) included only 3 adolescents, the efficacy data in adults were accepted as extrapolable to adolescents aged 12 years and over;
and despite:
the absence of demonstration of an overall survival gain (non-hierarchised secondary endpoint) at the interim analysis, in a context of advanced-stage disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the exploratory quality-of-life results;
results available with short follow-up (median follow-up of 1 year at the interim analysis that became the primary analysis);
the Committee considers that RETSEVMO (selpercatinib) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with vandetanib or cabozantinib, in the first-line treatment of adult patients and adolescents aged 12 years and over with advanced medullary thyroid cancer (MTC) harbouring a RET gene mutation.

d38732dfc1a03c5c:
Given:
the final results of the open-label extension (OLE) study with longer-term evinacumab treatment duration in 116 patients (median follow-up of 2 years, with approximately 25% of patients having a treatment duration of 2.5 years) which show sustained efficacy of evinacumab in reducing LDL-c levels and which confirm the favourable tolerability profile of evinacumab, with no new safety signal observed;
the insufficiently met medical need for effective and well-tolerated medicines allowing a reduction in the use of LDL apheresis, in patients uncontrolled despite optimised lipid-lowering therapy, particularly those with a biallelic mutation of the LDLR gene lacking functional LDL receptors;
the Transparency Committee considers that EVKEEZA (evinacumab) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the management strategy of adult patients and adolescents aged 12 years and older with homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia, as an adjunct to diet and other LDL-cholesterol-lowering therapies.

57b628c4c814c135:
Given:
the demonstrated efficacy of LOKELMA (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) versus placebo in reducing and normalising serum potassium during the correction phase, as well as in preventing recurrences of hyperkalaemia during the maintenance phase;
an safety profile judged acceptable despite the frequent occurrence of adverse events related to oedema;
the tasteless taste of LOKELMA (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) and the absence of sorbitol in its composition, which according to expert opinion could respectively favour adherence and tolerance in treated patients;
an partially met medical need; but in view of:
the absence of direct comparison with cation-exchange resins;
the absence of data on clinically relevant morbidity-mortality endpoints;
the selection of a majority of patients included in the clinical studies with a serum potassium between 5.1 and 5.5 mmol/L;
the Committee considers that LOKELMA (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) 5 g and 10 g, powder for oral suspension, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic management strategy for hyperkalaemia in adults.

b8bae62a81fc34b4:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of a 48,3 % reduction in LDL-c at 24 weeks in children aged 5 to 11 years with homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia (HFHo) not controlled by optimised lipid-lowering therapy, in a non-comparative phase Ib/III study whose results are consistent with those obtained in adults and adolescents;
the extrapolation, considered valid by the EMA, of the efficacy data for evinacumab in children with HFHo from the results obtained in adults and adolescents in the randomised, double-blind, multicentre ELIPSE-HoFH study, which had demonstrated a 49 % reduction in LDL-c at 24 weeks;
the insufficiently met medical need for effective and well-tolerated medicines in children not controlled despite optimised lipid-lowering therapy, in particular those with biallelic mutations in the LDLR gene lacking functional LDLR receptors;
the safety profile of evinacumab in the paediatric population, considered acceptable to date and consistent with that observed in adult and adolescent patients aged 12 years and over;
and despite:
the absence of comparative data;
the uncertainties regarding the safety profile of evinacumab due to a novel mechanism of action and limited follow-up in the paediatric population aged 5 to 11 years;
the absence of quality-of-life data;
the Transparency Committee considers that EVKEEZA (evinacumab) 150 mg/ml, solution to be diluted for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the management strategy for children aged 5 to 11 years with homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia, in addition to a diet and other therapies reducing LDL-cholesterol levels.

3175d159afaf1f57:
The service médical rendu by TECENTRIQ 1875 mg (atezolizumab) is insufficient to justify reimbursement by the national solidarity scheme:
in combination with bevacizumab, paclitaxel and carboplatin in adult patients with metastatic non-squamous NSCLC with EGFR mutations or ALK gene rearrangement (ALK+), after failure of appropriate targeted therapies;
as monotherapy in the treatment of adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC with ALK gene rearrangement (ALK+), after prior chemotherapy;
in other situations of hepatocellular carcinoma, outside the treatment of adult patients with advanced or unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) who have not received prior systemic treatment, only in patients with preserved liver function (Child-Pugh stage A), an ECOG score of 0 or 1, and who are not eligible for locoregional treatments or have failed one of these treatments.

0125112f146df607:
Given:
the demonstration of non-inferiority of subcutaneously administered efgartigimod alfa compared with IV administration, in terms of pharmacodynamics (percentage reduction in total IgG levels between baseline and day 29), shown in an open-label study in patients with generalised myasthenia gravis, and the methodological limitations raised;
the absence of comparative efficacy data versus IV efgartigimod;
the similar short-term safety profile between the 2 routes of administration, apart from injection-site reactions that were more frequent with subcutaneous administration of efgartigimod alfa compared with IV administration;
the uncertainties regarding long-term safety;
the absence of formally demonstrated impact in terms of improved quality of life of the subcutaneous formulation compared with the IV formulation;
the Commission considers that VYVGART (efgartigimod alfa) 1000 mg, solution for injection in subcutaneous formulation does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the product VYVGART 20 mg/mL (efgartigimod alfa), solution to be diluted for infusion.

5c4a8bf367b51589:
Given:
the absence of robust comparative data, interference with the specific effect of allogeneic transplantation, and the absence of robust demonstration of the clinical relevance of the primary endpoint (complete MRD response), which does not allow a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) compared with available alternatives in adult patients with CD19-expressing Philadelphia chromosome-negative precursor B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) in first or second complete remission with positive minimal residual disease (MRD);
of a tolerability profile marked in particular by the frequent occurrence of neurological disorders, infections, and cytokine release syndrome (3.4% of patients);
of a partially met medical need, with interest in having an additional alternative for patients in post-induction consolidation;
and pending the results of the phase III study (E1910) comparing blinatumomab in combination with chemotherapy versus chemotherapy alone, in consolidation in adult patients (= 30 and = 70 years) with CD19+ Ph- B-cell ALL and negative MRD;
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the file, BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) 38,5 µg, powder for solution for dilution and solution for infusion, as monotherapy, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with precursor B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia (ALL) expressing CD19 with Philadelphia chromosome-negative in first or second complete remission with positive minimal residual disease (MRD) equal to or greater than 0.1%.

88cbee517a816cee:
Having regard to:
the persistent medical need for vaccines that have demonstrated its efficacy in preventing herpes zoster and postherpetic neuralgia, particularly in persons with risk factors;
the placebo-controlled demonstration of the vaccine efficacy (VE) of SHINGRIX (recombinant adjuvanted zoster vaccine) in reducing the number of herpes zoster cases, estimated at:
91,3 %, 95% CI = [86,8 ; 94,5] in the pooled analysis of the randomised phase III clinical trials Zoster-006 and Zoster-022, in immunocompetent subjects aged 50 years and over,
68,2 %, 95% CI = [55,6 ; 77,5] in study Zostzer-002, in subjects aged 18 years and over whose immune system is impaired, such as subjects who have received an autologous HSC transplant;
the data taken into account by the current HAS recommendations, in particular:
efficacy data from available systematic reviews, such as the Mbinta et al. meta-analysis, which confirmed the VE results of SHINGRIX (recombinant adjuvanted zoster vaccine) in real-world data (79,3 %, 95% CI = [57,6 ; 89,7]), and the Tricco et al. network meta-analysis, which suggested a superiority of SHINGRIX (recombinant adjuvanted zoster vaccine) over ZOSTAVAX (live attenuated zoster vaccine) in preventing herpes zoster (confirmed and suspected cases);
immunogenicity data obtained in immunocompetent subjects aged 50 years and over, as well as in immunosuppressed subjects aged 18 years and over or with chronic diseases;
data from the ZOE-LFTU follow-up study (at ten years), which suggested an overall VE of the Shingrix vaccine of 81,6 %;
a favourable safety profile;
and despite:
the absence of robust comparative data in immunocompetent subjects versus the available ZOSTAVAX (live attenuated zoster vaccine);
long-term uncertainties, in particular regarding the duration of protection conferred and the need for booster doses, notably in immunosuppressed subjects. The need for booster doses after primary vaccination has not been established (see RCP),
the Committee considers that SHINGRIX (recombinant adjuvanted zoster vaccine) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the prevention of herpes zoster and postherpetic neuralgia (NPZ) in adults aged 65 years and over, and adults aged 18 years and over at increased risk of herpes zoster, in accordance with the current HAS recommendations dated 29 February 2024.

90190fcad4565cfc:
Considering:
the efficacy data from a non-comparative phase 1/2 study, with an overall response rate (primary endpoint) of 61% (95% CI [51,8; 69,6]) in a situation involving a life-threatening prognosis;
the absence of comparative data for an evaluation based on a phase II study (MagnetisMM-3), the results of which do not allow a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of elranatamab compared with available alternatives in adult patients with relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior treatments, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody;
limited follow-up on the tolerability data for elranatamab, with a median follow-up of 14,7 months in the MagnetisMM-3 study, and a profile marked in particular by the frequent occurrence of adverse events of particular interest, of low grade in most cases, such as cytokine release syndromes (CRS) as well as neurotoxicity (ICANS);
a partially met medical need, with an interest in having an additional alternative for patients who have relapsed or are refractory after at least three prior treatments, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody;
and pending the results of the phase III study (MagnetisMM-5) comparing elranatamab as monotherapy or in combination with daratumumab versus daratumumab + pomalidomide + dexamethasone in patients with relapsed/refractory multiple myeloma who have received at least 1 prior line of treatment and at most 3 prior lines of treatment, including lenalidomide and a proteasome inhibitor;
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, and pending the results of the phase III study MagnetisMM-5, ELREXFIO (elranatamab) as monotherapy does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior treatments, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody, and whose disease progressed during the last treatment.

fa722fc3f38b5b06:
The actual clinical benefit of TECENTRIQ 1875 mg (atezolizumab) is important:
as monotherapy for first-line treatment of patients with NSCLC whose tumours express PD-L1 = 50% on TC or = 10% on tumour-infiltrating immune cells (IC), and who do not have NSCLC with mutated EGFR or ALK gene rearrangement (ALK-positive);
as monotherapy in the treatment of adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC after prior chemotherapy, with patients harbouring EGFR activating mutations also required to have received targeted therapy;
in the treatment of small cell lung cancer;
in the treatment of adult patients with advanced or unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) who have not received prior systemic treatment, only in patients with preserved liver function (Child-Pugh stage A), an ECOG score of 0 or 1, and who are not eligible for locoregional treatments or have failed one of these treatments.

36b8c8ac394f1733:
Given:
the demonstration of the efficacy of ILARIS (canakinumab) versus triamcinolone acetonide (KENACORT RETARD) in reducing pain and in the probability of a new gout flare (result to be interpreted with caution given the difference in half-life between these two products);
an acceptable safety profile despite the frequent occurrence of adverse events related to infections;
a partially met medical need for the treatment of flares in patients with gout failing NSAID and colchicine treatments and unable to receive corticosteroids;
but in view of:
the low level of evidence of the available data (post-hoc subgroup analysis) in the population of the marketing authorisation (AMM);
the absence of data allowing positioning relative to KINERET (anakinra), the clinically relevant comparator;
the results of the EPI, involving a small number of patients (15 patients) and not meeting the Transparency Committee's initial request for data on the characteristics of patients treated with ILARIS (canakinumab) in France for gouty arthritis based on a cohort study,
the Transparency Committee considers that ILARIS (canakinumab) 150 mg/ml, solution for injection, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the clinically relevant comparator of ILARIS: KINERET (anakinra), used off-label in gout flares refractory to first-line treatments.

7e6b4f497708c610:
The medical benefit (SMR) of PRALUENT (alirocumab) at the 75 mg, 150 mg and 300 mg doses, injectable solution, is important only in children and adolescents aged 8 years and older with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia (HeFH) inadequately controlled (LDL-c > 130 mg/dL) by maximally tolerated oral lipid-lowering therapy, in addition to diet, and:
in combination with optimised lipid-lowering therapy;
or as monotherapy in case of contraindication or proven intolerance to both statins and ezetimibe.

d77cff53e07612db:
Considering:
the low methodological quality of a purely descriptive, non-comparative study that does not permit any formal conclusion on the results;
the results observed in this study, which suggest partial maintenance of AASR at 48h post-dose = 0,1 UI/mL (on a pharmacodynamic endpoint);
the acceptable safety profile, judged similar to that known for native crisantaspase;
the justification for the absence of a comparative study by the situation of persistent supply shortages of native crisantaspase between 2016 and 2023;
a partially met medical need;
the Commission considers that ENRYLAZE (recombinant crisantaspase) 10 mg/0,5 mL, solution for injection/for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the clinically relevant comparator CRISANTASPASE PORTON BIOPHARMA (formerly ERWINASE) (L-asparaginase derived from Erwinia).

3b8d5bf5f526932a:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of PRALUENT (alirocumab) over placebo in reducing LDL-c levels at 24 weeks (biological primary endpoint) in a randomised, double-blind, comparative study, with a moderate effect size (- 33,8 % ; IC97,5% = [- 46,4 ; - 21,2] ; p < 0,0001);
the absence of data on any possible effect of alirocumab on morbidity and mortality in children and adolescents aged 8 years and over;
the paediatric safety profile, considered acceptable, but with persisting uncertainties regarding the impact of the substantial reduction in LDL-c levels on neurocognitive function and on growth and weight development in children;
the absence of adherence or quality-of-life data;
the medical need currently only partially met;
the Transparency Committee considers that PRALUENT (alirocumab) at doses of 75 mg, 150 mg and 300 mg, solution for injection, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic management strategy for heterozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia in children and adolescents aged 8 years and over.

9c4de4f4f39faf1c:
ORSERDU (elacestrant) demonstrated a statistically significant superiority in terms of progression-free survival assessed by an independent committee, in an open-label randomised phase 3 study, compared with endocrine therapy alone.
However, taking into account the following limitations:
The low clinical relevance of this result, considering the point estimate of the absolute difference in median progression-free survival of less than 2 months, associated with the absence of demonstrated effect on overall survival at the final analysis, in a context of advanced-stage disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
The sub-therapeutic nature of the control group. Indeed, the Committee considers that:
other clinically relevant comparators, which were nevertheless available at the date the study was conducted and had superior efficacy, could have been used (expert opinion);
a substantial proportion of patients in the control group who received an aromatase inhibitor (60%) had previously received this drug class before. Retreatment after failure of this drug class may be considered a loss of chance compared with other available comparators (expert opinion);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the Committee considers that ORSERDU (elacestrant), film-coated tablet does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with endocrine therapy alone.

628b9bdda466e24f:
Given:
the need to strengthen the vaccine supply in view of a major constraint in the availability of cholera vaccines in Mayotte;
the HCSP recommendations in force dated 15 April 2024 on anticipation and management measures around cholera in Mayotte;
the available data suggesting an efficacy of approximately 90,3 % at day 11 and 79,5 % at day 91 in subjects aged 18 to 45 years in terms of reduction in the incidence of moderate to severe diarrhoea;
immunogenicity data that confirmed these results in subjects aged 46 to 64 years and in subjects aged 2 to 18 years;
the satisfactory safety profile, mainly characterised by fatigue and headache;
the clear advantage of using a vaccine with a 1-dose schedule, particularly in an epidemic context;
But:
the absence of clinical data comparing efficacy with DUKORAL (inactivated oral cholera vaccine), which is an inactivated vaccine available in France;
the contraindication in immunocompromised subjects and their household contacts, due to the currently unknown duration of vaccine strain excretion;
the impossibility of using it in cases requiring post-exposure antibiotic prophylaxis for persons living in the same household as an identified case;
the lack of data on the duration of protection,
the Transparency Committee considers that VAXCHORA (live, recombinant, oral cholera vaccine) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the preventive strategy including DUKORAL (inactivated oral cholera vaccine), for active immunisation against disease caused by Vibrio cholerae serogroup O1 in adults and children aged 2 years and over, in accordance with the official recommendations in force dated 15 April 2024 on anticipation and management measures around cholera in Mayotte.

143cc82b5349108a:
From the 2nd line of treatment onwards, considering:
updated data in the 2nd line and beyond from the LIBRETTO-001 study, a non-comparative phase I/II study with an intermediate endpoint (response rate) as the endpoint;
retrospective comparative data from the RECALIB-RET study with an external control group (indirect comparison) with important methodological limitations that do not allow establishing the place of selpercatinib compared with the alternatives used in routine practice;
comparative data from a phase III study in a different line from the one concerned (LIBRETTO-531 study in 1st line of treatment);
the very limited number of data in adolescents from 12 years of age in MTC;
frequent adverse events, notably increased transaminases (AST, 36,4% and ALT, 33,0%), QT interval prolongation (22,5%) and arterial hypertension (46,3%);
the Commission considers that RETSEVMO (selpercatinib), 40 mg and 80 mg, capsule, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for adult and adolescent patients from 12 years of age with advanced medullary thyroid cancer with a RET gene mutation in the 2nd line and beyond.

1a3c92e196cb0293:
Considering:
the partially met medical need in adult patients with severe invasive candidiasis, particularly in cases of associated neutropenia;
the demonstrated non-inferiority of rezafungin versus caspofungin in terms of (ITTm analysis):
overall response at J14 (EMA endpoint): 59,1 % (55/93) versus 60,6 % (57/94), i.e. an adjusted difference of -1,1 %, IC95% [-14,9 ; 12,7]);
all-cause mortality at J30 (FDA endpoint): 23,7 % (22/93) versus 21,3 % (20/94), i.e. an adjusted difference of 2,4 %, IC95% [-9,7 ; 14,4]);
an acceptable safety profile but marked by adverse events such as injection-related reactions, tremors and phototoxicity;
but:
a very wide non-inferiority margin for overall response and mortality versus caspofungin, accepting a risk of loss of efficacy of up to 20 % relative to the comparator (non-inferiority threshold specified in the protocol);
the absence of a reduction in mortality rate versus caspofungin (superiority remains to be demonstrated);
limited transposability of the results to the population of interest, which has a more severe prognosis, in particular:
the non-inclusion of patients with severe hepatic impairment or with complex infection (endocarditis, osteoarticular infection, endophthalmitis, central nervous system infections, chronic disseminated candidiasis, urinary tract infection),
a confirmed diagnosis of invasive candidiasis in only one third of patients,
a small number of neutropenic patients (absolute neutrophil count < 500 cells/µL): 8/100 in the rezafungin group and 5/99 in the caspofungin group,
an APACHE score ≥ 20 in approximately 17 % of patients;
the absence of data on failures and/or resistance of the strains selected in the clinical trials;
the Committee considers that REZZAYO (rezafungin) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the treatment of invasive candidiasis in adult non-neutropenic patients.

1e5f8ba16dc7df02:
Having regard to:
the partially met medical need in pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) to reduce the risk of sexually acquired HIV-1 infection in adults and adolescents at high risk of infection and weighing at least 35 kg;
the demonstrated superiority of cabotegravir (APRETUDE) in reducing the incidence of documented new HIV-1 infections compared with emtricitabine / tenofovir disoproxil (TRUVADA) in continuous administration:
in the HPTN 083 study (HIV-negative men who have sex with men and transgender women who have sex with men), with a relative risk reduction of 66 %,
in the HPTN 084 study (HIV-negative cisgender women who have sex with men), with a relative risk reduction of 89 %;
pharmacokinetic data (Pop PK-adultes model and extensions of studies HPTN 083-01 and HPTN 084-01) showing cabotegravir exposure similar between the adolescent population (12 years and older and weighing at least 35 kg) and adults, without showing any clinically relevant difference between these two populations;
a satisfactory safety profile, though marked by injection-site reactions, with no new safety signal identified. The important risks identified (risk management plan) include hepatotoxicity, HIV-1 seroconversion and development of resistance;
but:
limited transferability of the available data (studies HPTN 083 and HPTN 084) to the population likely to receive PrEP with APRETUDE (cabotegravir) in France, namely:
risk behaviours or practices of study participants that are of little or no transferability to those of the French population likely to receive HIV PrEP,
the non-inclusion of pregnant or breastfeeding women and of people who inject drugs in the clinical studies,
uncertainties regarding long-term adherence to a long-acting treatment, especially as adherence was already lower in both groups of study HPTN 083 after the first year,
the absence of cabotegravir data allowing its positioning against the other administration regimen of emtricitabine / tenofovir disoproxil (TRUVADA), an off-label regimen but validated by WHO and national recommendations;
a risk of selection of INI-resistant viruses in individuals not adhering to the injection schedule, and of transmission of these viruses, whereas INIs are the mainstay of first-line antiretroviral (ARV) treatment;
the Transparency Committee considers that APRETUDE (cabotegravir) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with the fixed-dose combination emtricitabine / tenofovir disoproxil (TRUVADA) in continuous administration, when APRETUDE (cabotegravir), combined with lower-risk sexual practices, is used in pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) to reduce the risk of sexually acquired HIV-1 infection in adults and adolescents at high risk of infection and weighing at least 35 kg.

73540e8e244835af:
Given:
the efficacy data from a non-comparative phase 1/2 study, with an overall response rate (primary endpoint) of 74,1% (95% CI: [66,1%; 81,1%]) for cohort A and 71,7% (95% CI: [63,7%; 78,9%]) for cohort C, with follow-up of 18,8 and 12,7 months respectively, in a situation that is life-threatening;

the absence of comparative data with an evaluation based on a phase I/II study (Monumental-1), the results of which do not permit a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of talquetamab compared with available alternatives in adult patients with relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior lines of therapy, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody;

the limited follow-up on talquetamab's tolerability data and a tolerability profile marked in particular by the frequent occurrence of adverse events of particular interest, mostly of low grade, such as cytokine release syndromes (CRS) and neurotoxicity (ICANS); frequent mucocutaneous toxicity is to be noted for this bispecific antibody;

the partially met medical need, with an interest in having an additional alternative for patients who have relapsed or are refractory after at least three prior lines of therapy, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody;

the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, TALVEY (talquetamab) 2 mg/ml and 40 mg/ml does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior lines of therapy, including an immunomodulatory agent, a proteasome inhibitor and an anti-CD38 antibody, and whose disease progressed during the last treatment.

082047f52376a2d4:
Given:
the partially covered medical need in the initial treatment of severe malaria in adults and children;
the magnitude of the effect of artesunate in terms of reduction of hospital mortality compared with quinine, in patients with a severe form of P. falciparum malaria, which was statistically significant in:
the ITT population of the SEAQUAMAT study (1 259 adults and 202 children under 15 years of age from Asia): 14,7 % (107/730) versus 22,4 % (164/731), i.e. HR = 0,60, 95% CI [0,45; 0,79], p = 0,0002;
the ITT population of the AQUAMAT study (5 425 children under 15 years of age from Africa): 8,5 % (230/2 712) versus 10,9 % (297/2 713), i.e. OR = 0,75; 95% CI [0,63; 0,90], p = 0,0022;
a satisfactory safety profile, but marked by anaemia, haemoglobinuria and blackwater fever in clinical trials. The important potential risk (risk management plan) is reproductive toxicity (in particular during the first trimester);
But:
the transposability of the results is limited to the P. falciparum species alone, the only species isolated in the clinical studies; yet it remains the species of particular concern because it is responsible for potentially severe or even fatal forms;
the absence of demonstrated impact on the reduction of the incidence of neurological sequelae (SEAQUAMAT and AQUAMAT studies);
the Commission considers that ARTESUNATE AMIVAS (artesunate) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with intravenous quinine in the initial treatment of severe malaria in adults and children.

30eec8f5489ada91:
Given:
the insufficiently covered medical need in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants during their first RSV circulation season;
the demonstration of superiority of the ABRYSVO vaccine over placebo in 6,975 infants born to mothers vaccinated during pregnancy in terms of reduction of severe RSV-LRTI cases up to D180 (0.5% in the ABRYSVO group vs 1.8% in the placebo group), i.e. a VE of 69.4% [44.3; 84.1];
an acceptable tolerability profile for the ABRYSVO vaccine, marked by adverse events predominantly of grade 1 (mild) or 2 (moderate), such as pain at the injection site (41%), headache (31%) and myalgia (27%). However, pharmacovigilance monitoring is necessary because of a potential excess risk of preterm births (not significant for this vaccine, but which led to the discontinuation of development of a competing vaccine);
But:
a non-significant reduction in the incidence of RSV-LRTI cases (co-primary endpoint): 0.7% in the ABRYSVO group vs 1.6% in the placebo group, i.e. a vaccine efficacy (VE) of 57.1% [14.7; 79.8]; significance is not retained because the lower bound of the CI is below 20% within the 90 days following birth;
a reduction in hospitalisations (secondary endpoint): VE of 67.7%, CI99.17% = [15.9; 89.5] reported at D90 after birth, before decreasing over time to reach values of 59.5% [8.3; 83.7], 56.4% [5.2; 81.5], 56.8% [10.1; 80.7], 33.3% [-17.6; 62.9] at D120, D150, D180 and D360 after birth respectively;
a non-demonstrated impact on the reduction of length of hospital stay, transfer to intensive care or resuscitation units, and mortality attributable to RSV infections;
the absence of comparative data allowing a robust differentiation of maternal vaccination during pregnancy from the alternative strategy of passive immunisation at birth with a monoclonal antibody;
the Committee considers that ABRYSVO (respiratory syncytial virus vaccine (bivalent, recombinant)) provides an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in passive protection against lower respiratory tract disease caused by respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) in infants from birth up to 6 months of age following maternal immunisation during pregnancy only between 32 and 36 weeks of gestation.

ebee0d7ea401d03e:
Having regard to:

the phase III PULSAR study, which compared aflibercept 8 mg every 12 weeks (Q12S) or every 16 weeks (Q16S) with a variable dosing regimen (after 3 monthly injections) against aflibercept 2 mg every 8 weeks (Q8S) with a fixed dosing regimen (after 3 monthly injections) in adults with retrofoveolar exudative age-related macular degeneration (AMD), and whose results demonstrated:
non-inferiority, without demonstration of superiority, of the aflibercept 8 mg Q12S and 8 mg Q16S groups compared with the aflibercept 2 mg Q8S group in terms of change in best corrected visual acuity (MAVC, ETDRS) after 48 weeks (primary endpoint) and 60 weeks (secondary endpoint) of treatment relative to baseline;
superiority of aflibercept 8 mg Q12S + Q16S (pooled analysis) compared with aflibercept 2 mg Q8S in the percentage of patients without intra- or subretinal fluid in the central subfield of the retina at week 16;
comparable tolerability between aflibercept 8 mg Q12S or Q16S and aflibercept 2 mg Q8S;
the absence of demonstrated superiority of aflibercept 8 mg Q12S or Q16S over aflibercept 2 mg Q8S in terms of quality of life;
a lighter dosing regimen of EYLEA 114.3 mg/mL compared with EYLEA 40 mg/mL during the maintenance phase (up to 5 months between 2 injections), but without demonstration of any additional impact on care organisation or on the patient's care or life pathway compared with other anti-VEGF agents used on a treat-and-extend regimen;
the Transparency Committee considers that EYLEA 114.3 mg/mL (aflibercept), solution for injection in a vial, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with EYLEA 40 mg/mL (aflibercept), solution for injection in a vial and in a prefilled syringe.

24e65ff9e6c44144:
Having regard to:
the randomised, double-masked, multicentre phase II/III PHOTON study, which compared aflibercept 8 mg every 12 weeks (Q12W) or every 16 weeks (Q16W) with a variable dosing regimen (after 3 initial monthly injections), with aflibercept 2 mg every 8 weeks (Q8W) with a fixed dosing regimen (after 5 initial monthly injections), in adults with visual impairment due to diabetic macular oedema (DME) (BCVA between 24 and 78 ETDRS letters inclusive) involving the centre of the fovea, in whom diabetes management had been optimised, and whose results demonstrated:
non-inferiority, without demonstration of superiority, of the aflibercept 8 mg Q12W and 8 mg Q16W groups versus the aflibercept 2 mg Q8W group in terms of change in best corrected visual acuity (BCVA, ETDRS) from baseline after 48 weeks (primary endpoint) and 60 weeks (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint);
non-inferiority of aflibercept 8 mg Q12W versus aflibercept 2 mg Q8W on the percentage of patients with an improvement of at least 2 severity steps on the ETDRS-DRSS scale at week 48 from baseline (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint);
comparable tolerability between aflibercept 8 mg Q12W or Q16W and aflibercept 2 mg Q8W;
absence of demonstrated superiority of aflibercept 8 mg Q12W or Q16W versus aflibercept 2 mg Q8W in terms of quality of life;
a dosing regimen for EYLEA 114,3 mg/mL allowing a reduction in the number of injections compared with EYLEA 40 mg/mL during the induction phase (3 monthly intravitreal injections instead of 5) and extension of the injection interval up to 5 months, but without demonstration of an additional impact on care organisation or on the patient's care or life pathway compared with other anti-VEGF agents used on a Treat & Extend regimen;
the Transparency Committee considers that EYLEA 114,3 mg/mL (aflibercept), solution for injection in vial, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with EYLEA 40 mg/mL (aflibercept), solution for injection in vial and in pre-filled syringe.

785ac495cf9cccfe:
Considering:
the demonstration, in a randomised, open-label, phase 3 study, of the superiority of ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) compared with an investigator's choice treatment, among 5 different protocols, in terms of:
progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee according to IMWG criteria: HR=0,49 [IC95% : 0,38-0,65], with a median of 13,3 months (min-max: 11,8-16,1) in the ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) group, and 4,4 months (min-max: 3,4-5,9) in the investigator's choice group, i.e. a point estimate of the absolute difference of 8,9 months;
overall response rate assessed by an independent review committee according to IMWG criteria: OR=3,5 [IC95% : 2,1-5,9];
and despite:
the open-label design of the study, which may have led to possible assessment bias, in particular regarding treatment decisions;
the fact that patients in the investigator's choice group could receive a previously received treatment (except the last treatment received);
the lack of clinical relevance of the overall response rate in this context, in particular given the absence of demonstration of its transposability to overall survival or improvement in quality of life;
the absence of demonstration, in the current state of the file, of an impact on overall survival, in an advanced disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the safety profile, marked in particular by significant toxicity;
the Transparency Committee considers that ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy that includes the relevant comparators (cf. 5 .2).

f2de75c8595d7d72:
Given:
the weak robustness of the evidence for the efficacy of TRECONDI (treosulfan) in combination with fludarabine, based on data from a descriptive phase II study;
the absence of data allowing the conclusion of an improvement in overall survival, quality of life, or better tolerability of the treosulfan-fludarabine combination compared with the busulfan-fludarabine combination;
the absence of comparative data versus the conditioning regimens used in paediatrics;
the significant unmet medical need, partially covered at this line of treatment;
the Commission considers that TRECONDI (treosulfan) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy that includes the relevant comparators (section 5.2).

a0cc0355e0c85510:
Given:
the initial data demonstrating the superiority of esketamine over placebo, in combination with a new oral antidepressant, in patients under 65 years of age with a severe major depressive episode that is resistant to at least two oral antidepressants:
in the 4-week induction treatment, a small effect size in terms of change in total MADRS score (difference of -3,5 points, 95% CI = [-6,7 ; -0,3]) below the threshold of clinical relevance;
in the maintenance treatment at 48 weeks or more in terms of time to relapse (median time not reached in the esketamine group versus 273 days in the placebo group, HR = 0,49; 95% CI = [0,29 ;0,84]);
new comparative data from a study that demonstrated superiority versus quetiapine LP, both in combination with SSRI or SNRI treatment, on the primary endpoint of remission at week 8 (MADRS ≤10), and the limitations associated with this study (open-label design, non-optimal comparator with insufficient SMR, poor representativeness of the severe population, short-term duration);
the tolerability profile, marked by significant identified risks such as dissociative or perceptual disorders and cardiovascular disorders, confirmed over the longer term with a mean total duration of exposure of 42,9 months (up to 79 months);
the absence of robust quality-of-life data;
the Transparency Committee considers that SPRAVATO (esketamine), in combination with an SSRI or SNRI, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic management strategy for adult patients under 65 years of age with treatment-resistant major depressive episodes who have not responded to at least two different antidepressants from two different classes during the current severe depressive episode.

e94945d8e99708d0:
Le service médical rendu (SMR) par LEQVIO (inclisiran) 284 mg, solution injectable en seringue préremplie, est important uniquement en prévention primaire dans le traitement des patients adultes présentant une hypercholestérolémie familiale hétérozygote (HFHe), en complément d'un régime alimentaire :
avec une statine seule ou une statine avec d'autres thérapies hypolipidémiantes chez les patients ne pouvant atteindre les objectifs de LDL-C sous statine à la dose maximale tolérée ;
seul ou en association avec d'autres thérapies hypolipidémiantes chez les patients avec une intolérance avérée aux statines ou chez qui les statines sont contre-indiquées.

a74894185cad5902:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of LEQVIO (inclisiran) over placebo in reducing LDL-C levels (at D510 and time-adjusted over the period [D90 – D540]; primary biological endpoints) in a randomised, comparative, double-blind study (ORION-9 study);
the favourable tolerability profile of inclisiran, comparable to that of placebo;
the currently partially met medical need; but in view of:
the absence of morbidity and mortality data in adult subjects with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia;
the absence of comparison versus an active comparator, although this was possible;
the absence of data on adherence or quality of life;
the Commission considers that LEQVIO (inclisiran) 284 mg, solution for injection in pre-filled syringe, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for primary prevention of heterozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia in adult patients.

4f73a0fb82ff3e07:
Taking into account:
the demonstration, in phase III studies of good methodological quality, that included patients with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis requiring systemic treatment:
the superiority of abrocitinib 100 mg and 200 mg over placebo, with a statistically significant difference, as monotherapy, in adult or adolescent patients (16 to 20 % of the samples):
in terms of IGA 0 or 1 responses, EASI-75 and pruritus reduction (hierarchical endpoints) after 12 weeks of treatment (studies JADE MONO 1 and 2),
in terms of maintenance of the treatment effect, with a greater probability of no flare during the maintenance period (up to 40 weeks) in the abrocitinib groups than in the placebo group (study JADE REGIMEN),
the superiority of abrocitinib 100 mg and 200 mg over placebo, with a statistically significant difference, in combination with topical corticosteroids (DC), in adolescent patients aged ≥ 12 years, on EASI-75 response, IGA 0 or 1 and pruritus reduction (only at week 2 for the 100 mg dose) after 12 weeks of treatment (study JADE TEEN),
exploratory results suggesting maintenance of clinical responses (IGA 0 or 1 and EASI-75) up to 96 weeks of follow-up (study JADE EXTEND);
the medium-term safety profile of abrocitinib, mainly marked by nausea, headache, acne, herpes simplex and an increase in plasma CPK;
but:
the absence of demonstrated impact on quality of life, even though moderate to severe AD has a major psychological, emotional and social impact on patients' lives,
the risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancies, serious infections and all-cause mortality associated with the JAK inhibitor class of medicines, which require limiting patients' exposure to these medicines and restricting their use in certain patients, although these risks are lower in the paediatric population;
the absence of available data allowing abrocitinib to be positioned against other first-line systemic treatments (anti-IL and anti-JAK) with a marketing authorisation in adolescents, in the absence of comparative data against these treatments, which may be justified by concomitant development;
the Commission considers that CIBINQO 50 mg, 100 mg and 200 mg (abrocitinib), film-coated tablets, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adolescents aged 12 years and older who require systemic treatment, this setting including three anti-interleukins (dupilumab, tralokinumab and lebrikizumab) and one anti-JAK (upadacitinib).

b0a6580c4aad760b:
In view of:
- the absence of demonstrated superiority on spleen response rate versus the best available treatment;
- the absence of demonstrated superiority on transfusion independence rate versus danazol;
- the questionable choice of danazol as comparator in the MOMEMTUM study, which makes the results obtained in this study difficult to interpret;

and despite:
- the demonstration of the superiority of OMJJARA (momelotinib) versus danazol on the proportion of patients with a reduction of at least 50% in total symptom score on the MFSAF questionnaire, with 24,62% of patients in the momelotinib group versus 9,23% of patients in the danazol group, a difference between the two groups of 15,67% (95% CI [5,54 ; 25,81], p=0,0095);
- the demonstration of the non-inferiority of OMJJARA (momelotinib) versus danazol on transfusion independence rate (co-primary endpoint), allowing account to be taken of results on the hierarchically tested secondary endpoints;
- a safety profile similar to that of the other members of the JAK inhibitor class;

the Transparency Committee considers that OMJJARA (momelotinib), 100 mg, 150 mg and 200 mg, film-coated tablets, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with ruxolitinib in the treatment of splenomegaly or disease-related symptoms, in adult patients with moderate to severe anemia and primary myelofibrosis, myelofibrosis secondary to polycythemia vera, or essential thrombocythemia, who have been treated with ruxolitinib.

dee7ddf23a14ef11:
Given:
its in vitro activity against a wide variety of Gram-negative bacteria, such as carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae with a metallo-β-lactamase [NDM, VIM, IMP] resistance mechanism, or non-fermenting bacteria such as Stenotrophomonas maltophilia;
the efficacy suggested in the descriptive, comparative phase III studies (C3601009 and C3601002);
the experience gained with aztreonam, already widely used in the treatment of severe infections due to aerobic Gram-negative bacteria;
the fact that the ceftazidime/avibactam and aztreonam combination is already used as the reference treatment for Enterobacteriaceae producing MBL-type carbapenemases. Note that the microbiological interest of this combination rests solely on the combination of aztreonam and avibactam (ceftazidime having no activity against this type of resistance mechanism). The use of two antibiotics in fact exposes the patient to unnecessary iatrogenic risk and may have a harmful ecological impact;
the known and acceptable tolerability profile of aztreonam alone, the sole representative of the monobactam class, and of avibactam (as part of its combination with ceftazidime in ZAVICEFTA), already in use, with no new safety signal for the aztreonam/avibactam combination;
but:
limited clinical data, particularly in severe forms and/or those due to multidrug-resistant bacteria, owing to:
the descriptive nature of the 2 comparative phase III studies (C3601009 and C3601002), which suggest efficacy in complicated urinary tract infections (including pyelonephritis), complicated intra-abdominal infections, as well as nosocomial pneumonia, ventilator-associated pneumonia and septicaemia due to Gram-negative bacilli (BGN);
the small number of patients enrolled in these studies, in particular patients with severe infections due to MBL-producing BGN;
the Committee considers that EMBLAVEO (aztreonam/avibactam) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the management of complicated intra-abdominal infections, nosocomial pneumonia/ventilator-associated pneumonia, complicated urinary tract infections including pyelonephritis, and infection due to an aerobic Gram-negative bacterium in adult patients for whom therapeutic options are limited, only as a last resort, for the treatment of patients with infections due to Enterobacteriaceae with a metallo-β-lactamase resistance mechanism [NDM, VIM, IMP]) or Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, susceptible to aztreonam/avibactam, and for whom the use of other available antibiotics is not appropriate in case of resistance.

90ac9d233ecfb6a0:
The service médical rendu by OMNIPAQUE 300 and 350 mg I/mL (iohexol), injectable solution, is important only in patients:
with contraindications to MRI, in:
diagnostic impasse situations;
locoregional extension assessment;
tumour assessment before and after neoadjuvant chemotherapy;
without contraindications to MRI, in locoregional extension assessment or before/after neoadjuvant chemotherapy, for assessment of tumour size, particularly for its perfect correspondence with mammography images.

ab5399f2ee8765b0:
Le service médical rendu par ULTRAVIST 300 et 370 mg d'I/mL (iopromide), solution injectable, est important uniquement chez les patients :
ayant des contre-indications à l'IRM, dans :
les situations d'impasse diagnostique ;
le bilan d'extension locorégional ;
l'évaluation tumorale avant et après une chimiothérapie néoadjuvante
ne présentant pas de contre-indications à l'IRM, dans le bilan d'extension locorégional ou avant/après une chimiothérapie néoadjuvante, pour l'évaluation de la taille tumorale, notamment pour sa parfaite correspondance avec les clichés de mammographie.

fb6dc6bb175ddc85:
Considering:
the demonstration, in a phase III study, of the superiority of YORVIAPTH (palopegtériparatide) versus placebo, as an add-on to conventional treatment (calcium supplementation and hydroxylated vitamin D derivatives), on a multiple endpoint comprising adjusted serum calcium within the normal range, independence from hydroxylated vitamin D derivatives, independence from therapeutic doses of calcium and absence of increase in the study drug prescribed, in 78,8 % of patients in the palopegtériparatide group versus 4,8 % of patients in the placebo group, p<0,0001;
the demonstration of the superiority of palopegtériparatide also on the ranked secondary endpoints of quality of life, via the HPES score and the SF-36;
the tolerability profile, which appears favourable, marked mainly by moderate adverse events, mainly injection-site reactions and headaches;
the uncertainties regarding prevention of renal complications or neuromuscular symptoms, not demonstrated in the study, particularly considering the short follow-up of patients in the phase III study, namely 26 weeks;
the medical need is partially covered by the available alternatives, but insufficiently covered in patients who would no longer be optimally controlled by standard treatment, namely calcium and vitamin D;
the Transparency Committee considers that YORVIPATH (palopegtériparatide) provides a minor improvement (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy.

7e8a85a9e15e4787:
Having regard to:
the data and conclusions of the HAS assessment report on the value of dual-energy angiomammography in the breast cancer diagnostic strategy, published in 2021, and in particular the clinical situations in which the value of angiomammography was recognised;
the diagnostic performance of angiomammography with OMNIPAQUE drawn from published literature and in the same clinical situations as those explored in the 2021 report;
the already well-established safety profile of ULTRAVIST, with no new data calling this safety profile into question;
the medical need currently partially met by the existence of diagnostic alternatives;
the Transparency Committee considers that OMNIPAQUE 300 and 350 mg of I/mL (iohexol), solution for injection, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current diagnostic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 5.2.

3aabc05135d6054e:
Having regard to:
the data and conclusions of the HAS assessment report of 2021 on the value of dual-energy angiomammography in the diagnostic strategy for breast cancer, and in particular the clinical situations in which the value of angiomammography was recognised;
the diagnostic performance of angiomammography with ULTRAVIST from publications in the literature and in the same clinical situations as those explored in the 2021 report;
the already well-established safety profile of ULTRAVIST, with no new data calling this safety profile into question;
the medical need currently partially met by the existence of diagnostic alternatives;
the Transparency Committee considers that ULTRAVIST 300 and 370 mg I/mL (iopromide), solution for injection, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current diagnostic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators cited in section 5.2.

0b25ebc7d823b2bb:
Given:
a demonstration of superiority of pembrolizumab versus placebo on the primary endpoint, investigator-assessed disease-free survival, with HR = 0,68 IC95% : [0,53 - 0,87], p=0,0010;
a demonstration of superiority of pembrolizumab versus placebo on the ranked secondary endpoint, overall survival, with at the 3rd interim analysis HR = 0,62 IC95% : [0,44 - 0,87], p=0,0024;
an excess of toxicity in the pembrolizumab arm, notably marked by grade ≥ 3 adverse events (32,0 % and 17,7 % in the placebo arm) and serious adverse events (20,7 % and 11,5 %);
an absence of demonstrated gain in quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Commission considers that, in the current state of the dossier, KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) as monotherapy in the adjuvant treatment of adults with clear cell renal cell carcinoma at increased risk of recurrence after nephrectomy, or after nephrectomy and resection of metastatic lesions.

8ee0b901de08b067:
Considering:
expert opinions which emphasise:
the place of vasopressin in international recommendations as a second-line treatment for patients with septic shock and inadequate mean arterial pressure despite noradrenaline treatment (weak recommendation, moderate level of evidence);
the value of decatecholaminisation to reduce adrenergic stimulation in the context of prolonged noradrenaline treatment in septic shock;
the potential benefit of a multimodal approach using different vasopressors in order to create a synergy of mechanisms of action against vasoplegia and to reduce the deleterious effects of each vasoactive drug;
but in view of:
the absence of good-quality evidence demonstrating the efficacy of vasopressin in reducing morbidity and mortality in patients with hypotension due to septic shock refractory to catecholamines;
the absence of a demonstrated additional benefit in terms of tolerability of adding low-dose vasopressin to noradrenaline compared with noradrenaline alone in the indication of the marketing authorisation (MA);
the uncertainties regarding the doses of REVERPLEG (argipressin) and noradrenaline that will be used in real-world conditions of use, in a context where high doses of REVERPLEG may induce skin and intestinal necrosis and increase the risk of cardiac arrest (in accordance with the elements of the SmPC);
the Transparency Committee considers that REVERPLEG (argipressin) 40 U.I./2 mL, solution to be diluted for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for managing catecholamine-refractory hypotension following septic shock in adult patients.

c3c7510fcb94a0d0:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) is moderate in:
the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants with or without risk factors as defined by national recommendations, and not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season;
the MA indication in the prevention of severe lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection and eligible for palivizumab, during their first and second RSV circulation seasons:
Children born at 35 weeks gestational age or less and under 6 months of age at the start of the seasonal RSV epidemic;
Children under 2 years of age who required treatment for bronchopulmonary dysplasia in the past 6 months;
Children under 2 years of age with congenital heart disease with haemodynamic repercussions.

f29937a17c007b7d:
Having regard to:
expert opinions highlighting the value of fixed-dose triple antihypertensive combinations, supported by international and European recommendations specifying that these fixed combinations ("single pill combination") should be preferred for dual and triple therapies because they promote patient adherence and compliance with antihypertensive treatment;
observational data from Italian medico-administrative databases reporting better patient compliance with the fixed triple antihypertensive combination perindopril / indapamide / amlodipine compared with a fixed dual combination associated with a free component or with a free triple combination;
the absence of any new safety signal identified since the last evaluations and tolerability data consistent with the known safety profiles of the 3 active substances of the medicine;
but in view of:
the limited clinical interpretation of the adherence data provided, owing to the low level of evidence of observational studies;
the absence of clinical data allowing evaluation of the efficacy of the fixed combination TRIPLIXAM (perindopril / indapamide / amlodipine) in the management of patients whose blood pressure is stabilised by the components of this combination;
the Commission considers that TRIPLIXAM (perindopril / indapamide / amlodipine) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the free combination of perindopril, indapamide and amlodipine administered concomitantly at the same dosage.

0dec748ad0269177:
Having regard to:
the insufficiently met medical need in preventing lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in subjects aged 75 years and over and in subjects aged 65 years and over with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions;
the demonstration of superiority of the mRESVIA vaccine over placebo in 35 088 subjects (study P301) in terms of reduction of RSV-LRTI with ≥ 2 symptoms and RSV-LRTI with ≥ 3 symptoms (VE 83,7 IC95,88 % [66,0 ; 92,2]) and 82,4 IC96,36 % [34,8 ; 95,3] respectively);
an acceptable tolerability profile for the mRESVIA vaccine, marked by adverse events (AEs) that were mostly grade 1 (mild) or 2 (moderate), namely injection-site pain (56,3%), fatigue (31,0%) and headache (27,0%). Serious adverse events considered by the investigator to be related to treatment were reported in four persons in the mRNA-1345 group (chills, dehydration, facial paralysis (occurring 5 days after injection of ARNm-1345) and superficial venous thrombosis) and in five persons in the placebo group (convulsions, fever, COPD, transient ischaemic attack and myelodysplastic syndrome).
However:
an impact not demonstrated on the reduction of severe RSV-LRTI and of deaths attributable to RSV infection, nor on healthcare organisation (reductions in conventional hospitalisations and intensive care admissions);
limited data in the populations at highest risk of decompensation during an RSV infection (subjects aged 75 years and over and subjects aged 65 years and over with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions), which limits the transferability of the results to this population;
the Committee considers that, on the current state of the data, mRESVIA (messenger RNA respiratory syncytial virus vaccine (nucleoside-modified)) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active immunisation for the prevention of lower respiratory tract disease caused by RSV in subjects aged 75 years and over and in subjects aged 65 years and over with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions at risk of decompensation during an RSV infection.

dee0020234393970:
Given the demonstration of superiority of DUPIXENT (dupilumab) versus placebo, added to background treatment with ICS/LABA/LAMA or LABA/LAMA in case of contraindication to ICS, in two randomised, double-blind studies, on the annualised rate of moderate or severe COPD exacerbations at 52 weeks, a clinically relevant endpoint;
with a modest effect size, a risk reduction of 30% (RR=0,70 ; 95% CI: [0,58 ; 0,86], p=0,0005) in the BOREAS study and of 34% (RR=0,66 ; 95% CI: [0,53 ; 0,82], p=0,0002) in the NOTUS study versus background treatment alone;
on lung function at 52 weeks, with an improvement in pre-bronchodilator FEV1 (VEMS) of +83 mL (95% CI: [38 ; 128], p<0,0001) in the BOREAS study and of +62 mL (95% CI: [11 ; 113], p=0,0182) in the NOTUS study versus background treatment alone;
of the improvement in quality of life at 52 weeks (assessed by the SGRQ score, concerning the change in total SGRQ score and the percentage of patients with a reduction in SGRQ score ≥ 4 points) in the BOREAS study versus background treatment alone;
of its acceptable safety profile;
of the unmet medical need in patients with COPD not controlled by the ICS/LABA/LAMA combination or by the LABA/LAMA combination;
but in view of:
the number of patients in the placebo group without exacerbation during the study, which reflects control of the disease with background treatment;
the low annualised rate of exacerbations during the study in patients receiving placebo (1,1 to 1,3);
an efficacy assessed on moderate and severe exacerbations, with no measured effect on each component, this endpoint having a different significance for patients (moderate and severe exacerbations have different consequences, notably for symptoms, quality of life and hospitalisation);
the effect appearing to be driven essentially by moderate exacerbations (90% of observed exacerbations);
smoking cessation and pulmonary rehabilitation implemented in a non-optimal manner;
the absence of data available beyond one year of treatment on the course of COPD, in particular on the annualised rate of exacerbations;
the absence of demonstration of quality of life in a single study;
the Transparency Committee considers that DUPIXENT (dupilumab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the COPD management strategy characterised by high blood eosinophil levels, not controlled by ICS/LABA/LAMA or by LABA/LAMA alone if ICS are not appropriate.

e71842c11973e677:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of ALECENSA (alectinib) over chemotherapy as adjuvant treatment, in a randomised, open-label study, on the risk of disease recurrence or death, a clinically relevant endpoint;
with a large effect size, with a 76 % reduction in risk (HR = 0,24 ; IC95% [0,13 ; 0,45]), p<0,0001) in patients with stage II-IIIA disease and in the stage IB-IIIA population (HR = 0,24 ; IC95% [0,13 ; 0,43], p<0,0001);
of its acceptable safety profile, with no new safety signal in this indication;
of the insufficiently met medical need in patients with ALK-positive NSCLC at high risk of recurrence after complete resection;
but in view of:
an open-label study;
a primary disease-free survival assessment performed by the investigator (but 94% of results were reviewed by an independent committee);
a smaller effect reported when disease-free survival was assessed by the independent committee than by the investigator, although the primary endpoint was an investigator assessment;
the absence of data on:
overall survival;
any recurrence after 2 years of alectinib treatment;
efficacy and safety of alectinib in combination with chemotherapy;
sufficiently robust quality of life;
resistance to alectinib;
the optimal duration of adjuvant alectinib treatment;
a study population predominantly Asian (55,6%), with no interaction test available for this criterion, so a risk of heterogeneity of effect cannot be ruled out;
an unknown heterogeneity of effect by stage, as only 26 patients with stage Ib disease were included;
the Commission considers that ALECENSA (alectinib) provides an improvement in clinical benefit provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the management strategy, as adjuvant treatment after complete resection of the tumour, of adult patients with ALK-positive non-small cell lung cancer at high risk of recurrence.

e4769e3b5c4e110e:
The service médical rendu by VIAGRA (sildenafil) 50 mg, orodispersible film, is important only in adult men with erectile dysfunction related to one of the following conditions:
Paraplegia and tetraplegia, whatever their origin;
Pelvic trauma complicated by urinary disorders;
Sequelae of surgery (aortic aneurysm; radical prostatectomy, total cystectomy and colorectal excision) or of abdominopelvic radiotherapy;
Sequelae of priapism;
Confirmed diabetic neuropathy;
Multiple sclerosis;
And in those with erectile dysfunction due to long-term treatment with an antipsychotic.

091777633db120bf:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of EYLEA 114.3 mg/mL (aflibercept), solution for injection in a prefilled syringe, is important only in adults:
for the treatment of neovascular (wet) retrofoveal age-related macular degeneration (AMD),
for the treatment of visual impairment due to diabetic macular oedema, in cases of diffuse form or leakage close to the centre of the macula, in patients with visual acuity less than or equal to 7/10 and in whom diabetes management has been optimised.

734914968ceae55a:
Given:
the demonstration in 2 phase III clinical studies (BE HEARD I and II) with the same protocol, which included adults with moderate to severe active hidradenitis suppurativa, most of whom had been treated with antibiotic therapy before inclusion (approximately 80%):
the superiority of bimekizumab (320 mg every 2 weeks) versus placebo in both studies, with a modest effect size, on HiSCR50 responses (primary endpoint; differences of the order of 19%, p = 0,006) and HiSCR75 responses (differences of 15% to 20% depending on the study, p < 0,03) and on the skin pain score (HSSDD);
the superiority of bimekizumab (320 mg every 4 weeks) versus placebo only in the BE HEARD II study, with a modest effect size, on HiSCR50 responses (difference of 21,6%, p = 0,004) and HiSCR75 responses (difference of 18,1%, p = 0,007);
the exploratory medium-term results at week 48, suggesting maintenance of the clinical responses observed at week 16 for both dosing regimens;
the short-term (16 weeks) and medium-term (48 weeks) safety profile of bimekizumab, similar to that established in the other indications of the marketing authorisation;
the absence of demonstration of a clinically relevant benefit in terms of quality of life, although this is particularly impaired in moderate to severe forms of this disease;
the absence of demonstration of an impact on the use of scheduled surgery (cancellation, delay or reduction of its extent);
the absence of a direct comparison with adalimumab (HUMIRA) in a phase III study, another treatment with a marketing authorisation in hidradenitis suppurativa with an indication wording identical to that of BIMZELX (bimekizumab);
the absence of a direct comparison with secukinumab (COSENTYX) in a phase III study, given concomitant development;
the Commission considers that BIMZELX 160 mg (bimekizumab), solution for injection in prefilled syringe and prefilled pen, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current management of active moderate to severe hidradenitis suppurativa in adults with an insufficient response to conventional systemic treatment, which includes HUMIRA (adalimumab) and COSENTYX (secukinumab).

e0a606bd72132afc:
Considering:
the unmet medical need in adults with primary IgA nephropathy and proteinuria ≥ 1.0 g/day (or a protein-to-creatinine ratio ≥ 0.75 g/g);
the results of the randomised, double-blind, phase III PROTECT study in adults with primary IgA nephropathy and proteinuria ≥ 1.0 g/day despite optimised treatment with an ACE inhibitor (IEC) and/or an angiotensin II receptor antagonist (ARA II), which showed the superiority of sparsentan over irbesartan on the primary endpoint assessing the protein-to-creatinine ratio (UPCR), with a clinically relevant effect size (change of -40.77% in the sparsentan group versus -15.05% in the irbesartan group, p < 0.001);
but taking into account:
the heterogeneous results observed on the secondary endpoints, with alpha-risk control, assessing the change in eGFR over different periods, showing a significant but clinically marginal difference (difference of 1.1 mL/min/1.73 m2), only on the chronic annualised slope of eGFR change between weeks 6 and 110 (not taking into account the initial haemodynamic effects linked to initiation of treatment), whereas the change in eGFR is the most relevant biological criterion for assessing renal function;
the absence of long-term data on the use of dialysis or renal transplantation to confirm the efficacy of sparsentan on the decline in renal function, in the absence of a clear demonstration on the change in eGFR;
the absence of a demonstrated impact on quality of life;
the absence of data in patients who are naive to standard treatment;
the absence of comparative data allowing the position of sparsentan relative to an SGLT-2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin) to be determined, owing to concurrent development in this indication (data comparing sparsentan with the combination of sparsentan and an SGLT-2 inhibitor are awaited);
the safety profile of sparsentan being comparable to that of irbesartan, but including in particular hepatic and renal risks that require special monitoring during treatment and do not allow sparsentan to be recommended in severe forms of renal and hepatic impairment;
the uncertainties regarding the tolerance of sparsentan in patients with heart failure, severe hepatic impairment, after renal transplantation and during breastfeeding, and its contraindication during pregnancy;
the numerous drug interactions limiting its use, in particular with angiotensin II receptor antagonists (ARBs), endothelin receptor antagonists (ERAs) and renin inhibitors (contraindication);
the Committee considers that FILSPARI 200 mg and 400 mg, film-coated tablets, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for primary IgA nephropathy in adults with proteinuria ≥ 1.0 g/day (or a protein-to-creatinine ratio ≥ 0.75 g/g), which includes the relevant comparators (ACE inhibitors (IEC), ARBs (ARA II), SGLT-2 inhibitors).

ce86486c59601311:
Considering:
the demonstration of the superiority of dexamethasone intravitreal implant (OZURDEX 700 µg) over sham injection on the percentage of patients with a vitreous haze score of 0 (absence) at week 8 (46,8 % versus 11,8 %, p < 0,001) in the phase III HURON study conducted in adults with inflammation of the posterior segment of the eye related to non-infectious posterior or intermediate uveitis;
the exploratory results of this study showing a percentage of patients with a gain in best corrected visual acuity (BCVA) ≥ 15 letters (ETDRS scale) of 42,9 % in the OZURDEX 700 µg group vs 6,6 % in the sham injection group;
the tolerability, characterised mainly by ocular adverse events, in particular those related to corticosteroid administration (increased intraocular pressure, cataract) and to the implant injection procedure (reduced visual acuity, vitreous detachment, vitreous floaters, blepharitis, ocular pain, photopsia, conjunctival oedema, conjunctival hyperaemia);
the data from the French observational study LOUVRE 2, which are not such as to modify the previous evaluation of OZURDEX 700 µg (dexamethasone),
the Committee considers that the improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) of OZURDEX 700 µg (dexamethasone), intravitreal implant with applicator, remains moderate (ASMR III) in the management of adults with inflammation of the posterior segment of the eye of the non-infectious uveitis type.

a29105b57b8a6479:
Given:
updated data from the ZUMA-2 study, after a median follow-up of 46,4 months, showing a median overall survival of 46,5 months in the ITTm population and 43,9 months in the ITT population;
the additional follow-up of real-world data on TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel) from the French DESCAR-T registry (27 activated centres, 152 patients treated, median follow-up of 14,2 months from eligibility);
and this, despite:
updated data from the ZUMA-2 study showing differences with respect to real-world use data;
a median overall survival of 43,9 months in the ZUMA-2 study (median follow-up 46,4 months) versus 19,8 months for the DESCAR-T registry (median follow-up 14,2 months);
a median duration of response of 28,2 months versus 12 months for the DESCAR-T registry;
uncertainties regarding the transferability of results because of the potentially selected population in the ZUMA-2 study;
uncertainties regarding the maintenance of long-term efficacy;
a safety profile marked by significant short-term toxicity;
the Transparency Committee considers that, on the basis of current data, TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in terms of efficacy compared with current management of relapsed or refractory mantle cell lymphoma (MCL) after at least two lines of systemic therapy including a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor.

d8b9892e04a2fbc5:
Given:
an unmet medical need in the treatment of flares in adolescents,
the absence of specific data in adolescents aged 12 years and over evaluating spesolimab 450 mg (intravenous route) in the treatment of flares of generalised pustular psoriasis (GPP);
but the extrapolation to adolescents that can be made on the basis of:
efficacy data in adults showing the superiority of spesolimab 450 mg (intravenous route) over placebo in the treatment of flares of generalised pustular psoriasis (GPP) on complete clearance of lesions 1 week after a single infusion only (phase II EFFISAYIL 1 study in 53 patients, randomised, double-blind, multicentre);
efficacy data in adults and adolescents showing the superiority of spesolimab 150 mg every 4 weeks (Q4W, subcutaneous route) over placebo in the preventive treatment of GPP flares on the time to onset of a first GPP flare and on the percentage of patients who had at least one GPP flare over the 48 weeks of the study (phase II EFFISAYIL 2 study in 125 patients, including 23 in the spesolimab 300 mg Q4W group, randomised, double-blind, multicentre);
data from the EFFISAYIL ON study (extension study of EFFISAYIL 1 and 2) showing a low percentage of patients with a GPP flare over the 156 weeks of follow-up;
pharmacokinetic data showing consistency of pharmacokinetic profiles between adults and adolescents;
long-term safety data (EFFISAYIL ON) comparable to those initially obtained in the treatment of flares in adults, with no particular adverse events identified in adolescents treated with spesolimab, mainly marked by infections, although uncertainties persist regarding the risk of serious or opportunistic infections, the risk of malignant tumours and peripheral neuropathies,
the Committee considers that SPEVIGO 450 mg (spesolimab), solution to be diluted for intravenous infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current management of flares of generalised pustular psoriasis in adolescents aged 12 years and over, which includes medicines used off-label (ciclosporin, acitretin, methotrexate, anti-TNF and anti-interleukins).

60d869cb49e81e96:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of ublituximab versus teriflunomide, in two phase III, randomised, double-blind studies, in a selected population predominantly with relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS) at an early stage in terms of disease duration and inflammatory activity:
on the annualised relapse rate, primary endpoint, with a clinically relevant reduction of 59,4 % (RR = 0,406 95% CI: [0,268; 0,615], p<0,0001) in the ULTIMATE-I study and of 49,1 % (RR = 0,509 95% CI: [0,330; 0,784], p<0,002) in the ULTIMATE-II study;
on the total number of Gd-enhancing T1 lesions and on the total number of new or enlarging T2 hyperintense lesions, hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints,
but in view of:
the absence of demonstration of a difference versus teriflunomide on time to onset of disability progression, the 3rd hierarchically ranked clinically relevant secondary endpoint, whereas the other anti-CD20 agents authorised in this indication, OCREVUS (ocrelizumab) and KESIMPTA (ofatumumab), demonstrated their superiority on this endpoint;
the absence of robust comparative data versus available first-line alternatives, including the other anti-CD20 agents, rituximab, ocrelizumab and ofatumumab, the latter two having been developed concurrently;
the absence of robust data versus teriflunomide on quality of life, in a disease that causes progressive disability in patients;
the absence of long-term safety data (median follow-up of 95 weeks in the studies concerned), in particular on the long-term risk of hypogammaglobulinaemia;
the Transparency Committee considers that BRIUMVI (ublituximab) 150 mg, solution for dilution for infusion:
does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management strategy for patients with early-stage RRMS in terms of disease duration and inflammatory activity;
does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management strategy for patients with highly active or severe relapsing MS.

8af72698631319de:
Considering:
the demonstration of the superiority of BALVERSA (erdafitinib) over chemotherapy (docetaxel or vinflunine), in an open-label randomised phase III study, in terms of overall survival, with HR = 0.64; 95% CI [0.47; 0.88]) and progression-free survival (HR = 0.58; 95% CI [0.44; 0.78]);
the medical need partially covered by the available alternatives;
and despite:
a toxicity profile marked by the occurrence of central serous retinopathy, dermatological and mucosal reactions (hand-foot syndrome, stomatitis and onycholysis), and severe hyponatraemia and hyperphosphataemia;
an absence of demonstrated improvement in quality of life;
the absence of robust comparative data versus enfortumab vedotin, a treatment already available in the treatment of patients with locally advanced or metastatic urothelial carcinoma who have previously received platinum-based chemotherapy and an anti-PD-1 or anti-PD-L1 agent. The absence of direct comparison is nevertheless justified at the date of this assessment given their concomitant development;
the Commission considers that BALVERSA 3 mg, 4 mg, 5 mg (erdafitinib), film-coated tablets, provides a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with chemotherapies in the treatment of adult patients with unresectable or metastatic urothelial carcinoma (UC) with sensitive FGFR3 genetic alterations who have previously received platinum-based chemotherapy and a PD-1 or PD-L1 inhibitor in the context of unresectable or metastatic treatment.

ca53f40bc22d5f57:
Given:
the results of a randomised phase 3 placebo-controlled trial conducted in children with a rare disease, demonstrating in 52 patients a clinically relevant effect in reducing pruritus (reduction in the intensity of scratching lesions);
the adverse event profile of odevixibat;
but also the absence of data on complications of cholestasis and on recourse to transplantation due to refractory pruritus,
the Commission considers that KAYFANDA (odevixibat) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the current therapeutic strategy.

05e0ea7699e3b42a:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of COLPRONE 5 mg (medrogestone) is insufficient to justify reimbursement by the national solidarity scheme in:
the other indications of the marketing authorisation in disorders related to progesterone insufficiency, particularly those observed in the pre-menopausal period: severe mastodynia associated with mastopathy;
the other indications of the marketing authorisation in functional haemorrhages and menorrhagia of fibroids in the preoperative setting;
the other indications of the marketing authorisation in endometriosis;
the artificial cycle in combination with an oestrogen;
dysmenorrhoea.

85cc05b5c8647e86:
Having regard to:
the well-established medical use of primaquine as the reference radical treatment for preventing relapse of P. vivax and P. ovale malaria, according to the current national (HCSP, SPILF) and international (WHO) recommendations;
recent data supporting its efficacy in reducing the frequency of P. vivax relapses, from the clinical study (DETECTIVE) of tafenoquine development (holding a marketing authorisation in the USA but not available in France), in which primaquine was the comparator: a proportion of patients without recurrence of 69,6 % with primaquine, higher than with placebo (27,7 %); HR for risk of recurrence versus placebo: 0,26; 95% CI = [0,18 ; 0,39];
the absence of any therapeutic alternative available in France;
the known and generally favourable safety profile, with a relative caveat regarding use in subjects with G6PD deficiency (risk of severe haemolysis; prior testing essential), justifying a contraindication in cases of severe deficiency (G6PD activity < 10%, in particular the Mediterranean B variant). In addition, its use is contraindicated in pregnant or breastfeeding women (see SmPC, "Special warnings and precautions for use");
the Transparency Committee considers that PRIMAQUINE SANOFI (primaquine phosphate) 15 mg provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) within the therapeutic strategy.

b88e46c055ecb2e8:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority in the phase III DUO-E study of durvalumab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy during the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with durvalumab monotherapy, in terms of progression-free survival assessed radiologically in the ITT population, with an HRBvsA = 0.71 (95% CI = [0.57; 0.89]; p = 0.003). The median progression-free survival was 10.2 months (95% CI = [9.7; 14.7]) in the durvalumab group versus 9.6 months (95% CI = [9.0; 9.9]) in the control group, i.e. an absolute difference of 0.6 months;
and despite:
the absence of demonstration of superiority of durvalumab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy during the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with durvalumab in combination with olaparib, in terms of overall survival, in the ITT population (including 20% of patients who are dMMR/MSI-H), as evaluated in an interim analysis;
the absence of an overall survival analysis specifically planned in the protocol for the dMMR/MSI-H subgroup of patients in the DUO-E study;
an excess of toxicity compared with the other groups, in particular for grade 3 or 4 adverse events, which were reported in approximately 50% of patients (mainly anaemia and neutropenia, but also syncope and digestive disorders);
the absence of a formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Committee considers that IMFINZI (durvalumab), concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with the combination of carboplatin and paclitaxel in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed or recurrent advanced endometrial cancer (EC) with dMMR/MSI-H tumour status.

ea1c4b4d128704a6:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) in combination with enfortumab vedotin versus platinum-based chemotherapy (gemcitabine + cisplatin or carboplatin), in an open-label randomised phase III study, in terms of overall survival (HR = 0.468; 95%CI [0.376; 0.582]), and in progression-free survival (HR = 0.450; 95%CI [0.3777; 0.538]);
and despite:
no gain in time to pain progression;
a marked toxicity profile, notably the frequent occurrence of skin disorders, peripheral sensory neuropathies, and treatment discontinuations due to adverse events (39.8% vs. 21.5%);
no demonstrated gain in quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Commission considers that KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) 25 mg/mL, concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with platinum-based chemotherapy.

8d0053d188193984:
Having regard to:
the quality of the demonstration of pembrolizumab's efficacy in the KEYNOTE-671 study, with:
a double-blind, two-group parallel design, combined with platinum-salt-based chemotherapy as neoadjuvant treatment, then continued as monotherapy as adjuvant treatment (pembrolizumab + chemotherapy/pembrolizumab) versus placebo + platinum salts as neoadjuvant treatment, then placebo alone as adjuvant treatment (placebo + chemotherapy/placebo);
the adequacy of the included population relative to that of the marketing authorisation (MA) indication;
the clinical relevance of the 2 primary endpoints (overall survival and event-free survival) and of the two secondary endpoints with alpha risk management (major pathological response and complete pathological response);
the results showing a statistically significant difference on these 4 endpoints in favour of pembrolizumab + chemotherapy/pembrolizumab versus placebo + chemotherapy/placebo:
overall survival, with an estimated difference in mean survival at 5 years showing a gain of 4,15 mois (95% CI [1,05-7,26]);
event-free survival, with a 42% reduction in the risk of events (progression/recurrence, inability to resect the tumour, or death) (HR = 0,58, 95% CI [0,46 ; 0,72], p<0,00001);
major pathological response, with a difference of 19,2 points between the 2 groups (95% CI [13,9 ; 24,7], p<0,00001);
complete pathological response, with a difference of 14,2 points between the 2 groups (95% CI [10,1 ; 18,7], p<0.00001);
however, the scope of these results is limited by the following points:
the beneficial effect on event-free survival appears to be mainly linked to a reduction in the occurrence of progressions/recurrences, whereas deaths without progression/recurrence are increased during the first 6 months; details of the effect on each component of the endpoint (in particular cumulative incidences) are not available, nor is the observed detail of the causes of early censoring;
the beneficial effect on overall survival was quantified by an HR of 0,72, of the same order as the expected 0,70, with median survival in the control group prolonged relative to the expected value (52,4 months versus 34 months);
the effect on overall survival appears delayed (p=0,004), observed after 12 months;
the value of perioperative treatment compared with neoadjuvant treatment alone is not known, whereas KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) has MA as monotherapy in the adjuvant treatment of adult patients with NSCLC at high risk of recurrence after complete resection and platinum-salt-based chemotherapy (indication not evaluated by the Committee), and has not demonstrated an overall survival gain in this setting;
a change, made during the study, to the definition of the primary endpoint (local reading of event-free survival), occurring 3 months after the end of enrolment and 4 months before the first interim analysis;
the tolerability of the product, and in particular the proportion of patients discontinuing treatment and with serious adverse events and grade ≥ 3 adverse events, was higher in the group treated with pembrolizumab;
long-term efficacy in this indication is not known;
the Committee considers that KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) relative to platinum-salt-based chemotherapy as neoadjuvant treatment.

201ba1f0e79562a3:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of PADCEV (enfortumab vedotin) in combination with pembrolizumab compared with platinum-based chemotherapies (gemcitabine + cisplatin or carboplatin), in a randomised open-label phase III study, in terms of overall survival (HR = 0,468; IC95%[0,376; 0,582]), and in progression-free survival (HR = 0,450; IC95%[0,3777; 0,538]);
and despite:
no gain in time to pain progression;
a marked toxicity profile, notably the frequent occurrence of skin disorders, peripheral sensory neuropathies, and treatment discontinuations for adverse events (39,8% vs. 21,5%);
no demonstrated gain in quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Commission considers that PADCEV (enfortumab vedotin) 20 mg and 30 mg, powder for concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with platinum-based chemotherapies.

0bcc409c87f1bd08:
Having regard to:

the demonstration of superiority in the phase III RUBY study of dostarlimab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of radiological progression-free survival (PFS), with:
in patients with dMMR/MSI-H tumour status, an HR = 0,28 (95% CI = [0,162 ; 0,495]; and a median PFS: not reached [11,8 ; NA] versus 7,7 months [5,6 ; 9,7]) in the comparator group;
in the ITT population, an HR = 0,64 (95% CI = [0,507 ; 0,800]) and a median PFS of: 11,8 months [9,6 ; 17,1] versus 7,9 months [7,6 ; 9,5] in the comparator group;
the demonstration of superiority of dostarlimab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of overall survival (OS), in the ITT population (including 23% dMMR/MSI-H patients), through an interim analysis;
the existence of a significant quantitative interaction by MMR status regarding the endpoints (PFS and OS), demonstrating a greater effect of dostarlimab in patients with dMMR tumour status than in those with pMMR tumour status;
and this despite:
the absence of an analysis of overall survival specifically planned in the protocol for the dMMR/MSI-H patient subgroup in the RUBY study;
increased toxicity, notably concerning grade ≥ 3 adverse events with 72,2 % in the dostarlimab group and 60,2 % in the placebo group, immune-mediated adverse events (58,5 % and 37,0 %), and serious adverse events (39,8 % and 28,0 %);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Committee considers that JEMPERLI (dostarlimab) in combination with carboplatin and paclitaxel in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with JEMPERLI (dostarlimab) as monotherapy, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with the carboplatin and paclitaxel combination in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed or recurrent advanced endometrial cancer (EC), with dMMR/ MSI-H tumour status.

15e4695b6d36524c:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of VABYSMO (faricimab) 120 mg/mL, solution for injection in pre-filled syringe, is important:
in the treatment of exudative age-related macular degeneration (AMD) with retrofoveolar involvement, in adult patients;
in adults, in the treatment of visual impairment due to diabetic macular oedema, in cases of diffuse form or leakage close to the centre of the macula, in patients with visual acuity of 5/10 or lower and in whom diabetes management has been optimised;
in the treatment of macular oedema secondary to retinal vein occlusions.

dad08bb7d2292017:
In view of:
the demonstration of superiority in the phase III DUO-E study of durvalumab combined with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with durvalumab combined with olaparib, in terms of radiological progression-free survival in the ITT population: HR = 0,55, (95% CI = [0,43 ; 0,69]), p < 0,0001. Median progression-free survival was 15,1 months, (95% CI = [12,6 ; 20,17]) in the durvalumab + olaparib group versus 9,6 months, (95% CI = [9,0 ; 9,9]) in the control group, i.e. an absolute difference of 5,5 months;
and despite:
the absence of demonstrated superiority of durvalumab combined with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with durvalumab combined with olaparib, in terms of overall survival, in the ITT population (including 80 % pMMR/MSS patients) assessed in an interim analysis;
the absence of an overall survival analysis specifically planned in the protocol for the pMMR/MSS subgroup of patients in the DUO-E study;
the absence, to date, of robust comparative data supporting the therapeutic contribution of olaparib in the maintenance phase and in combination with durvalumab following an induction phase with carboplatin + paclitaxel combined with durvalumab;
the submitted data from an indirect comparison between the RUBY study (pivotal study of JEMPERLI (dostarlimab)) and the DUO-E study (pivotal study of the combination IMFINZI (durvalumab) and LYNPARZA (olaparib)) do not allow this treatment sequence to be better positioned relative to dostarlimab (JEMPERLI) in combination with chemotherapy followed by dostarlimab monotherapy, due to methodological weaknesses that make interpretation of the results difficult;
a tolerability profile notably marked by grade 3 or 4 hematological and gastrointestinal adverse events, and syncope, reported in 2/3 of patients in group C (durvalumab + olaparib);
the absence of a formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the Committee considers that IMFINZI (durvalumab), concentrate for solution for infusion, and LYNPARZA (olaparib), film-coated tablet, provide a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with the combination of carboplatin and paclitaxel in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed or recurrent advanced endometrial cancer (EC), with pMMR/MSS tumour status.

063cb041ccd4dc18:
Considering, on the one hand:
the demonstration of superiority, in two double-blind phase III studies, of VYLOY (zolbetuximab) in combination with dual chemotherapy based on platinum salts and fluoropyrimidine compared with dual chemotherapy alone, in the ITT population in terms of:
overall survival: HR=0.784; 95% CI [0.644; 0.954] (SPOTLIGHT study) and HR=0.763; 95% CI [0.622; 0.936] (GLOW study);
progression-free survival: HR= 0.751; 95% CI [0.598; 0.942] (SPOTLIGHT study) and HR= 0.687; 95% CI [0.54; 0.87] (GLOW study);
but on the other hand:
methodological limitations concerning the analysis of time to confirmed deterioration (TTCD), with non-demonstration of non-inferiority in the SPOTLIGHT study and non-inferiority only on the physical function scale (first endpoint of the hierarchical sequence), despite a hierarchical analysis provided for in the protocols of both studies;
a magnitude of effect judged modest in terms of progression-free survival and overall survival;
a tolerability profile notably marked by an increase in nausea and vomiting;
the problem of transposability of the results to the French population (approximately 30% Asian patients in the SPOTLIGHT study and 60% in the GLOW study);
the Commission considers that VYLOY (zolbetuximab) 100 mg, powder for concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with chemotherapy based on fluoropyrimidine and platinum salts as first-line treatment of adult patients with locally advanced unresectable or metastatic HER2-negative adenocarcinoma of the stomach or gastro-oesophageal junction (GEJ) whose tumours are Claudin (CLDN) 18.2 positive.

081ca5f0291d76c8:
The medical benefit (service médical rendu) of NILEMDO (bempedoic acid) 180 mg, film-coated tablet, is important only in adult patients at high or very high cardiovascular risk, with proven intolerance to statins or in whom statins are contraindicated, to reduce cardiovascular risk by lowering LDL-c levels when these are not at target despite optimised lipid-lowering treatment:
in cases of high risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (primary prevention);
or in cases of proven atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (secondary prevention).

2dec0ae2da89993e:
Taking into account:
the data suggesting an objective response rate of more than 50% (varying according to tumour location) in patients treated with VITRAKVI (larotrectinib) in the non-comparative phase II study NAVIGATE;
the medical need, considered as unmet in situations where no therapeutic option is available other than supportive care, after failure of standard treatments appropriate to the tumour location, or when surgery risks leading to significant morbidity;
and despite:
the heterogeneity of the clinical situations targeted by the indication, in particular in terms of prognosis, therapeutic strategy and histology;
the results on an intermediate primary endpoint (objective partial or complete response rate) without demonstrated correlation with a clinical benefit on overall survival;
the absence of robust comparative data allowing a formal conclusion on the clinical benefit of VITRAKVI (larotrectinib) compared with supportive care;
the absence of demonstrated additional benefit in terms of quality of life;
the Committee considers that VITRAKVI (larotrectinib) 25 mg, 100 mg and 20 mg/mL does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the current therapeutic strategy for soft tissue sarcomas, salivary gland cancers and non-medullary thyroid cancers with an NTRK gene fusion.

5ce69cfb4cd39a97:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of NILEMDO (bempedoic acid) versus placebo in reducing the risk of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE 4 and MACE 3) in patients intolerant to statins and with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease or a high risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (Clear-OUTCOMES study);
the favourable tolerability profile of bempedoic acid;
the currently partially met medical need;
but in view of:
the uncertainty regarding the overall clinical effect of bempedoic acid on cardiovascular mortality, owing to the absence of a demonstrated statistically significant difference versus placebo in the reduction of stroke, cardiovascular deaths and all-cause deaths;
the absence of comparison versus an active comparator, although this was possible;
the absence of adherence or quality-of-life data;
the Committee considers that NILEMDO (bempedoic acid) 180 mg, film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic management strategy for adult patients at high risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (primary prevention) or with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (secondary prevention).

665de473b9db32f4:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of VOXZOGO (vosoritide), powder and solution for subcutaneous injection, is low within the scope of the marketing authorisation indication under evaluation:
Patients aged from 4 months to under 2 years (marketing authorisation indication extension);
Patients aged from 2 years to under 5 years (the new data provided as part of the re-evaluation are such as to modify the Committee's previous conclusions).

a7dea1da5fe5bb4f:
The service médical rendu by PHELINUN 50 mg/10 ml and 200 mg/40 ml (melphalan) is important only in the indication "A high dose of PHELINUN used alone or in combination with other cytotoxic drugs and/or total body irradiation is indicated in the treatment of the following conditions:
multiple myeloma;
malignant lymphoma (Hodgkin's lymphoma, non-Hodgkin lymphoma);
acute lymphoblastic leukaemia and acute myeloblastic leukaemia;
infantile neuroblastoma."

e3b75c4c5ff86136:
KRAZATI (adagrasib) demonstrated a statistically significant superiority in terms of progression-free survival and objective response rate, assessed by an independent review committee, in a randomised open-label phase 3 study, compared with docetaxel. However, this result is limited by:
the lack of clinical relevance of the absolute difference in median progression-free survival, of 1,65 months;
the open-label design of the study, which led to possible assessment bias, in particular regarding treatment decisions;
the absence of demonstrated effect on overall survival, in a context of advanced-stage disease with an unfavourable prognosis;
the lack of clinical relevance of the objective response rate in this context;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the adverse event profile of adagrasib, which does not appear more favourable than that of docetaxel;
the Commission considers that KRAZATI 200 mg (adagrasib), film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with docetaxel.

3d4866b4930fffab:
The actual clinical benefit of PHELINUN 50 mg/10 ml and 200 mg/40 ml (melphalan) is low only for the indication “PHELINUN in combination with other cytotoxic medicinal products is indicated as a reduced-intensity conditioning (RIC) treatment before allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (allo-GCSH) in adults with haematological malignancies. PHELINUN in combination with other cytotoxic medicinal products is indicated as a conditioning treatment before haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in haematological disorders in the paediatric population, namely:
Myeloablative conditioning (MAC) treatment for haematological malignancies;
RIC treatment for non-malignant haematological disorders.”

f61ee41f44e3a284:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of mirvetuximab soravtansine over single-agent chemotherapy, in a randomised, open-label, phase III study, in terms of:
progression-free survival in the ITT population: HR = 0,65; IC95% [0,52 ; 0,81]; p < 0,0001; with a median progression-free survival of 5,62 months in the mirvetuximab soravtansine group versus 3,98 months in the chemotherapy group;
overall survival in the ITT population: HR = 0,67; IC95%[0,50 ; 0,88]; p = 0,0046; with a median overall survival of 16,5 months in the mirvetuximab soravtansine group versus 12,7 months in the chemotherapy group.
the absence of demonstrated superiority on abdominal/gastrointestinal symptoms;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the safety profile of mirvetuximab soravtansine, judged acceptable compared with single-agent chemotherapy but marked in particular by a high incidence of ocular adverse events. This risk is qualified by the EMA as an important potential risk in the risk management plan for this medicinal product;
the Committee considers that ELAHERE 5 mg/mL (mirvetuximab soravtansine), solution to be diluted for intravenous infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with single-agent chemotherapy.

85bc7f5763541b1c:
Given:
the demonstrated vaccine effectiveness (VE) of live attenuated vaccines in reducing the incidence of laboratory-confirmed influenza cases (VE of 78%, CI95% = [59 ; 89]) and of influenza-like illness (VE of 31%, CI95% = [20 ; 40]) from the 2018 Cochrane review;
the demonstrated superiority of the trivalent live attenuated nasal vaccine over the trivalent inactivated vaccine in terms of reducing the incidence of laboratory-confirmed influenza: RR of influenza (inactivated versus live attenuated) = 0,52, CI95% = [0,22 ; 0,82] (meta-analysis by Minozzi et al. 2022);
the absence of demonstrated impact on reducing the incidence of acute otitis media, pneumonia, hospitalisations and mortality, and the lack of sufficient clinical evidence to differentiate the available quadrivalent influenza vaccines (inactivated or live attenuated) on these clinically relevant criteria (Cochrane reviews 2018 and 2017, Minozzi 2022, Stuurman 2021 and the study by Sinnathamby 2022);
the possible interest of a trivalent live attenuated nasal influenza vaccine in terms of better acceptability of this vaccine in children, owing to the absence of a needle, which could have a potential positive impact on vaccination coverage;
the potential risk of transmission of the vaccine influenza virus, with a peak incidence of vaccine virus presence 2 to 3 days after vaccination according to clinical studies;
the Commission considers that FLUENZ (trivalent live attenuated nasal influenza vaccine) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the other vaccines recommended for the prevention of seasonal influenza in children with and without comorbidities aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, in accordance with the current HAS recommendations (opinion of 12 December 2024).

43c706c37fe67985:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of RYBREVANT (amivantamab) in combination with carboplatin and pemetrexed compared with carboplatin and pemetrexed on progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee (IRC), with HR = 0,395 (95% CI = [0,296; 0,528]; p < 0,0001), in a phase III, randomised, open-label study;
a median progression-free survival of 11,4 months (95% CI = [9,79; 13,70]) in the amivantamab + carboplatin + pemetrexed (ACP) group and of 6,7 months (95% CI = [5,59; 7,33]) in the carboplatin + pemetrexed (CP) group;
an objective response rate assessed by an IRC of 73,0% (95% CI = [65,2; 79,9]) in the ACP group and of 47,4% (95% CI = [39,2; 55,6]) in the CP group, with OR = 2,97 (95% CI = [1,84; 4,79]; p < 0,0001);
the modest clinical relevance of the effect size measured on median progression-free survival and objective response rate;
and despite:
the open-label design of the study;
the beneficial effect on progression-free survival being essentially due to a reduction in progressions, while deaths without progression are increased, with no test comparing the cumulative incidences of deaths without progression between treatment groups;
the absence of demonstration of an impact on overall survival compared with chemotherapy, in the current state of the data;
an excess of short-term mortality (first 8 months);
the absence of data on brain metastases, with possible heterogeneity of effect according to their presence, and without interaction test;
the absence of robust conclusions that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the tolerability profile marked by a high frequency of grade ≥ 3 adverse events, serious adverse events and adverse events leading to permanent discontinuation of at least one study treatment, as well as by the occurrence of dermatological reactions (notably rash) and infusion-related reactions in 90% and 42% of patients respectively;
the Committee considers that RYBREVANT 350 mg (amivantamab), concentrate for solution for infusion, in combination with carboplatin and pemetrexed, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with chemotherapy with carboplatin and pemetrexed.

17b7a37720585202:
Having regard to:
its demonstrated in vitro activity against Gram-negative bacteria, such as Enterobacteriaceae, with an OXA-48-type resistance mechanism and extended-spectrum β-lactamases (BLSE);
the experience gained with cefepime, a fourth-generation cephalosporin (C4G) widely used in the treatment of severe nosocomial infections due to Gram-negative bacteria with a strong suspicion of P. aeruginosa;
the results of the phase III study (ALLIUM) demonstrating its non-inferiority to piperacillin/tazobactam, then its superiority on the composite endpoint of clinical cure and microbiological eradication at the test-of-cure visit (m-MITT population): 273/345 (79,1 %) versus 196/333 (58,9 %); i.e. a difference between the 2 groups of 21,2 % (95 % CI: [14,3 %, 27,9 %]) in adult patients aged 18 years or older, with a clinical diagnosis of complicated urinary tract infection or acute pyelonephritis caused by Gram-negative urinary pathogens;
limited clinical data in severe forms and/or infections due to multidrug-resistant bacteria;
the absence of clinical trials in nosocomial pneumonia, including ventilator-associated pneumonia, efficacy being extrapolated from PK/PD data and clinical data of cefepime alone;
the fact that the combination cefepime/enmetazobactam is one of the few currently available antibiotics active for the treatment of Enterobacteriaceae with an OXA-48-type resistance mechanism associated with BLSE, particularly within a carbapenem-sparing strategy,
the Transparency Committee considers that EXBLIFEP (cefepime/enmetazobactam) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of complicated urinary tract infections (including pyelonephritis) and nosocomial pneumonia (including ventilator-associated pneumonia), as well as the treatment of patients with associated bacteraemia, or suspected to be associated, as a last-resort treatment for patients with infections due to Enterobacteriaceae with an OXA-48-type resistance mechanism associated with BLSE, susceptible to the combination, particularly within a carbapenem-sparing strategy.

49ebf6643e382409:
Given:
the demonstration in 1 Phase III placebo-controlled, randomised, double-blind, multicentre clinical study (SPROUT) that included patients aged 6 to 17 years with moderate to severe plaque psoriasis eligible for systemic treatment or phototherapy;
the superiority of aprémilast over placebo, with a modest effect size, on sPGA 0/1 responses (primary endpoint; 33,1 % vs 11,5 %, p < 0,0001) and PASI75 (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint; 45,4 % vs 16,1 %, p < 0,0001);
the interim and exploratory longer-term results of the SPROUT-LTE extension study, at week 52 (i.e. 2 years after patients were enrolled in the SPROUT study), suggesting maintenance of the clinical responses observed in the initial study with aprémilast;
the safety profile of aprémilast, similar to that established in the adult indication, mainly marked by diarrhoea, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting;
but taking into account:
the absence of demonstration of a clinically relevant benefit on quality of life, which is nonetheless particularly impaired in the moderate to severe forms of this disease;
the low clinical relevance of the PASI 75 endpoint, now replaced by more stringent criteria such as PASI 90 and PASI 100 responses;
the absence of a direct comparison in a Phase III study with adalimumab (HUMIRA), another treatment with a marketing authorisation in chronic severe plaque psoriasis in children from 4 years of age and in adolescents with inadequate response to topical treatments and phototherapies or when these treatments are inappropriate;
the absence of a direct comparison with secukinumab (COSENTYX) in a Phase III study, given concomitant development;
the significant identified risks of serious events of depression, suicidal behaviour or suicidal ideation;
the Transparency Committee considers that the medicinal products OTEZLA (aprémilast), film-coated tablets, do not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of moderate to severe plaque psoriasis in children and adolescents from 6 years of age (weighing at least 20 kg) eligible for systemic treatment.

56ed7011601fdf49:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of RYBREVANT (amivantamab) in combination with carboplatin and pemetrexed over carboplatin and pemetrexed alone on progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee (IRC), with HR = 0,48 (95% CI = [0,36 ; 0,64]; p < 0,0001), in a phase III, randomised, open-label study;
a median progression-free survival of 6,3 months (95% CI = [5,55 ; 8,41]) in the amivantamab + carboplatin + pemetrexed (ACP) group and of 4,2 months (95% CI = [4,04 ; 4,44]) in the carboplatin + pemetrexed (CP) group;
the objective response rate assessed by an IRC of 63,8 % (95% CI = [55,0 ; 72,1]) in the ACP group and of 36,2 % (95% CI = [30,3 ; 42,3]) in the CP group, with OR = 3,10 (95% CI = [2,00 ; 4,80]; p < 0,0001);
the modest clinical relevance of the effect size measured on median progression-free survival and objective response rate;
and despite:
the open-label design of the study;
the substantial amendments to the MARIPOSA-2 trial hypotheses, in particular regarding the combination of interest and the dosage, with selection during the trial of a single combination of interest;
the beneficial effect on progression-free survival being essentially due to a reduction in progressions, while deaths without progression are increased, without a test comparing the cumulative incidences of deaths without progression between treatment groups;
the absence of demonstration of an impact on overall survival compared with chemotherapy, on the current state of the data;
the absence of any robust conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
a possible attrition bias linked to the number of subjects not treated (7,6 % of patients included in the CP group, versus 0,8 % in the ACP group) and to consent withdrawals (11,0 % of patients included in the CP group, versus 4,6 % in the ACP group);
the tolerability profile marked by a high frequency of grade ≥ 3 adverse events, serious adverse events and adverse events leading to permanent discontinuation of at least one study treatment, as well as by the occurrence of:
dermatological reactions (notably rash) and infusion-related reactions in 71 % and 59 % of patients respectively,
venous thromboembolic events in 10 % of patients;
the Committee considers that RYBREVANT 350 mg (amivantamab), solution to be diluted for infusion, in combination with carboplatin and pemetrexed chemotherapy, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with carboplatin and pemetrexed chemotherapy.

07d67c110ee056d5:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of the superiority of delgocitinib 20 mg/g over placebo in adults with moderate to severe chronic hand eczema (ECM) with a history of inadequate or inappropriate response to topical corticosteroids (DC) in 2 phase III studies (DELTA 1 and 2):
on the primary endpoint: disappearance or near-disappearance of lesions assessed by the investigator (IGA-CHE score 0 or 1 with an improvement of ≥ 2 points at week 16),
on all hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints at week 16, notably on improvement of at least 90% in a severity index of ECM (HECSI-90), and on improvement of pruritus and patient-reported pain (HESD score);
the demonstration of the superiority of delgocitinib 20 mg/g over alitretinoin in adults with severe ECM with a history of inadequate response to DC in 1 phase III study (DELTA FORCE):
on the primary endpoint: absolute change in HECSI score at week 12 from its baseline value,
on all hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints at week 12 or 24, notably the HECSI-90 response, improvement of patient-reported pruritus and pain (HESD score), or the IGA-CHE 0/1 response with an improvement of at least 2 points;
the safety profile of delgocitinib, marked mainly by application site reactions and more favourable compared with alitretinoin (teratogenic, rare psychiatric risks);
But taking into account:
the modest magnitude of effect versus placebo (DELTA 1 and 2 studies) and versus alitretinoin (DELTA FORCE):
on the IGA-CHE 0/1 response with an improvement of ≥ 2 points at S16 in the DELTA 1 (19,7 % vs 9,9 % ; p = 0,006) and DELTA 2 (29,1 % vs 6,9 % ; p < 0,001) studies, with between-group differences lower than the hypotheses formulated by at least 30%,
on the absolute change in HECSI score (score from 0 to 360 points) at S12 in the DELTA FORCE study (- 67,6 versus - 51,5 ; p < 0,001);
the absence of clinically relevant differences observed on quality-of-life endpoints (change in DLQI and HEIS) in the placebo-controlled studies (DELTA 1 and 2);
the open-label design of the DELTA FORCE study (blinded assessor only), which limits the scope of results on patient self-assessed endpoints (DLQI, HESD);
the Commission considers that ANZUPGO 20 mg/g (delgocitinib), cream, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the management strategy for moderate to severe ECM in adults for whom topical corticosteroids are inadequate or inappropriate.

aedd655489b89be3:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of the efficacy of WINREVAIR (sotatercept) versus placebo in a randomised, double-blind study in patients with pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) of WHO functional class II or III, added to standard treatment (monotherapy, dual therapy or triple therapy):
on a clinically relevant endpoint, 6-minute walk test, primary endpoint;
an effect on this endpoint of +40,8 metres, IC95% [27,5 ; 54,1], p<0,001) between the 2 groups;
an improvement observed on the hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints, notably:
of functional capacity assessed at week 24 by change in WHO functional class (29,4% of patients in the sotatercept group versus 13,8% in the placebo group, p<0,001);
of the risk of morbidity and mortality assessed at week 24, on a composite endpoint of disease improvement (38,9% of patients in the sotatercept group versus 10,1% in the placebo group, p<0,001), on time to the first clinical worsening event or death (HR: 0,163, IC95% [0,076 ; 0,347], p<0,001) and on achieving or maintaining a low risk score on the French risk score (39,5% of patients in the sotatercept group versus 18,2% in the placebo group, p<0,001);
but with the following limitations:
a lack of robust demonstration on the mortality endpoint alone;
a modest effect on quality of life;
tolerability with a risk of 20% of treatment-related events, notably dizziness, epistaxis and telangiectasia, and 3% of adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation;
patients were reported in the wrong strata at randomisation;
missing data in 19 patients (13 in the placebo group and 6 in the sotatercept group), possibly leading to overestimation of the observed differences;
limited follow-up for both efficacy and tolerability (median follow-up of 8 months for the primary analysis);
missing values assumed to be correlated with observed data, without this being verifiable;
numerous major protocol deviations related to treatment or missed visits (notably assessment of the endpoint in 14% of those included): 73 (44,8%) patients in the sotatercept group and 66 (41,3%) patients in the placebo group;
a sample size calculation based on an expected difference of 25 m between groups, whereas the minimal clinically relevant difference is 33 m (this value was established in 2023, i.e. after the start of the clinical study);
the secondary endpoint concerning improvement in WHO functional class being ranked only in 4th position, although it is the only secondary endpoint considered for controlling the risk of type II error;
the Committee considers that WINREVAIR (sotatercept) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators (cf. 5.2).

0dba0550904eafce:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of the DARZALEX (daratumumab) + bortézomib + lénalidomide + dexaméthasone combination in induction and consolidation followed by the DARZALEX (daratumumab) + lénalidomide combination in maintenance (D-VRd/D-R protocol) compared with the bortézomib + lénalidomide + dexaméthasone combination in induction and consolidation followed by lénalidomide alone in maintenance (VRd/R protocol), in an open-label randomised phase III study, in terms of progression-free survival (PFS) assessed by an algorithm, with:
an HR = 0,42 (95% CI = [0,30 ; 0,59]; p<0,0001),
median PFS not reached in either group,
and 48-month PFS rates of 84,3 % vs 67,7 %, i.e. a difference of 16,6 %;
a complete response or better (CR + sCR) rate of 87,9 % (95% CI = [84,0 ; 91,1]) in the D-VRd/D-R group and 70,1 % (95% CI = [65,0 ; 74,8]) in the VRd/R group, with an OR = 3,13 ([2,11 ; 4,65]; p < 0,0001);
an MRD-negative rate at the 10-5 threshold among patients with a complete response of 75,2 % (95% CI = [70,4 ; 79,6]) in the D-VRd/D-R group and 47,5 % (95% CI = [42,2 ; 52,8]) in the VRd/R group, with an OR = 3,40 [2,47 ; 4,69] ; p < 0,0001);
and despite:
a design and study schema that do not allow the possible therapeutic contribution of adding DARZALEX (daratumumab) to be isolated across the different sequences (induction, transplant and consolidation phases, and the maintenance phase), given the absence of randomisation before the maintenance phase. Moreover, in the context of the maintenance phase, where treatment may extend over the long term, it is important to note that the tolerability profile of the D-VRd/D-R protocol differs from that of the VRd/R protocol by a numerically higher proportion of serious adverse events (38,8 % vs 26,0 %). These SAEs were mainly infections, occurring in 22,7 % of patients in the D-VRd/D-R group versus 13,0 % in the VRd group during maintenance;
the open-label conduct of the study;
a major amendment to the statistical analysis plan concerning the hierarchical procedure of statistical tests (amendment of 30/08/2023), made after the data cut-off date (01/08/2023). These modifications are detrimental to the internal validity of the study and therefore to the quality of the evidence, as they suggest changes made in light of the data and possibly dictated by them;
possible heterogeneity of effect by age: a greater treatment effect is observed in the subgroup of patients aged under 65 years;
the comparator (VRd/R) is not the most clinically relevant given current practice, as this comparator is little used in routine practice;
the impossibility of quantifying the contribution of this combination compared with the D-VTd protocol due to concomitant development;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the descriptive analysis of overall survival, based on the current data;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life data (exploratory endpoint);
the tolerability profile of the D-VRd/D-R protocol compared with the VRd/R protocol, marked by an excess of grade 3 or 4 AEs (91,5 % versus 85,6 %) and serious AEs (57,0 % versus 49,3 %);
the Committee considers that DARZALEX 1 800 mg (daratumumab), solution for injection, in combination with bortézomib, lénalidomide and dexaméthasone as induction and consolidation treatment, followed by maintenance treatment with DARZALEX (daratumumab) and lénalidomide (D-VRd/D-R protocol), provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with bortézomib, lénalidomide and dexaméthasone as induction and consolidation treatment followed by maintenance treatment with lénalidomide alone (VRd/R protocol).

8247cd1605806c03:
Given:
the observation of an overall response rate of:
67.2% (n = 41/61; 95% CI [54.0; 78.7]) in the overall population of the LITESPARK-004 study and 83.3% (n = 15/18; 95% CI [58.6; 96.4]) in the B1 cohort population of the LITESPARK-015 study, i.e. in patients with measurable solid tumours of RCC associated with VHL disease,
48.0% (95% CI [33.7; 62.6]) and 90.9% (95% CI [70.8; 98.9]) respectively in the VHL-CNS (n = 50) and VHL-pNET (n = 22) groups of the LITESPARK-004 study,
60.0% (95% CI [26.2; 87.8]) and 66.7% (95% CI [34.9; 90.1]) respectively in the VHL-CNS (n = 10) and VHL-pNET (n = 12) groups of the LITESPARK-015 study. This, in a context of a disease involving tumours that do not have a tendency to regress spontaneously and have a highly variable course (expert opinion).
of the unmet medical need for effective and well-tolerated medicines in patients for whom localised interventions are not appropriate;
and taking into account:
the absence of comparative data due to the design of the LITESPARK-004 and LITESPARK-015 studies;
the absence of single-arm trial data with an external control arm, or of other forms of indirect comparison of good methodological quality, to assess the added value of the medicinal product;
the absence of robust data on morbidity-mortality endpoints;
the uncertainties regarding the safety profile of belzutifan due to a novel mechanism of action and limited follow-up;
the absence of quality-of-life data;
the Transparency Committee considers that WELIREG (belzutifan) 40 mg, film-coated tablet, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) within the current therapeutic strategy and for patients for whom localised interventions are not appropriate.

2d029dadb3fdcb88:
Considering:
the demonstration of superiority of BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) added to consolidation chemotherapy, compared with consolidation chemotherapy alone, in adult patients aged 30 to 70 years with newly diagnosed B-precursor ALL without BCR-ABL1 gene fusion and with MRD-negative status, in a phase III, comparative, randomised, open-label study (ECOG-ACRIN E1910), in terms of overall survival (SG), with:
an HR = 0,44 (95% CI: [0,25 ; 0,76]; p= 0,003),
medians of SG not reached in either treatment group,
and 5-year SG rates of 82,4% (95% CI [73,7 ; 88,4]) versus 62,5% (95% CI [52,0 ; 71,3]);
the medical need currently partially covered by available alternatives;
the safety profile judged acceptable to date;
and despite:
the open-label design of the study;
the exploratory nature of the secondary endpoints, in particular relapse-free survival and endpoints relating to allogeneic HSCT;
the methodological limitations regarding the collection of safety data: SAEs, AEs leading to treatment interruption or discontinuation, grade 1-2 AEs, and timing and duration of AEs were not collected or presented, which limits the interpretation of these data;
the absence of quality-of-life data;
limitations to the transferability of the population included in the study to the indication concerned, given the following:
the chemotherapy used in the consolidation phase of the study (4 cycles of chemotherapy) is less intensive than the protocols used in current French practice (6 to 8 cycles in the GRAALL and Hyper-CVAD protocols), particularly in young adults;
the notable difference in the definition of MRD-negative status: in the ECOG-ACRIN E1910 study, achieving MRD negativity at D28 is more favourable than the definition currently used in France to define the standard-risk patient group (MRD < 10-4 at D72);
the substantial proportions (25% and 29,5%) of patients with MRD-negative status who underwent transplantation, which do not reflect current practice in France (expert opinion). As a reminder, to date no benefit has been demonstrated from the contribution of HSCT in MRD-negative patients18. In addition, MRD was measured using a flow cytometry technique different from that used in France;
the absence of efficacy and safety data in adult patients aged 18 to 30 years with CD19-expressing Philadelphia chromosome-negative B-ALL in first complete remission with MRD negativity. However, it should be noted that:
the pathophysiology of MRD-negative CD19-expressing Philadelphia chromosome-negative B-ALL is comparable in young adults and adults over 30 years (expert opinion);
moreover, the results of a study conducted in patients with MRD-positive B-ALL, in first or second complete remission (study MT103-203), suggest comparable efficacy in adults aged 18 to 34 years compared with other age groups;
the Committee considers that BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) 38,5 micrograms, powder for solution for dilution and solution for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the consolidation treatment of adult patients with CD19-expressing Philadelphia chromosome-negative B-precursor acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) in first complete remission with minimal residual disease (MRD) negativity.

bc79e3882a96e8a4:
Given:
results obtained in the paediatric population aged over 1 year, with:
demonstration of superiority of blinatumomab over conventional chemotherapy,
no demonstration of an improvement in overall survival versus chemotherapy, an exploratory secondary endpoint,
a favourable tolerability profile for blinatumomab, marked by lower haematotoxicity compared with chemotherapy despite more frequent general and neurological adverse events;
an expected improvement in care conditions compared with chemotherapy, given the possibility of administering blinatumomab mainly at the patient's home;
pharmacokinetic data indicating exposure similar to that observed in paediatric patients aged 1 to under 18 years and in adults with B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (B-ALL), at the dose of 15 micrograms/m²/day;
results of the study by van der Sluis et al. suggesting efficacy of blinatumomab in infants with newly diagnosed KMT2A-rearranged B-ALL;
the partially met need at this stage of the disease;
and despite:
the absence of efficacy, tolerability and quality-of-life data in the context of consolidation treatment for children aged 1 month to 1 year with B-ALL in first high-risk relapse;
the uncertainties related to the extrapolation of results from studies in patients over 1 year;
the uncertainties related to the transposition of data obtained in children aged 1 month to 1 year with newly diagnosed B-ALL,
the Transparency Committee considers that BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) 38,5 micrograms, powder for solution to be diluted and solution for solution for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with conventional chemotherapy in the treatment of children aged 1 month to 1 year with CD19-expressing precursor B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, in the absence of the Philadelphia chromosome, in first high-risk relapse, as part of the third consolidation block.

2d63ce280612dc8c:
Having regard to:
the results obtained in the paediatric population aged over 1 year, with:
efficacy data showing a complete remission rate of approximately 38% during the first two treatment cycles of blinatumomab, maintained in approximately 18% of patients after a median follow-up of 24 months, in a non-comparative study;
uncertainties about the magnitude of effect relative to historical management, in the absence of direct comparison;
a known efficacy and tolerability profile of blinatumomab in adults;
pharmacokinetic data indicating exposure similar to that observed in paediatric patients aged 1 to under 18 years and in adults with ALL, at the dose of 15 microgrammes/m²/jour;
results of the study by van der Sluis et al. suggesting efficacy of blinatumomab in infants with newly diagnosed B-cell ALL (LAL-B) carrying a KMT2A rearrangement;
the partially met need at this stage of the disease;
and despite:
the absence of efficacy, tolerability and quality-of-life data in patients aged 1 month to 1 year with refractory B-cell ALL (LAL-B), or relapsed after at least two prior treatments, or relapsed after a prior allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation;
uncertainties linked to extrapolation of results from studies in patients older than 1 year;
uncertainties linked to transposition of data obtained in children aged 1 month to 1 year with newly diagnosed LAL-B,
the Transparency Committee considers that BLINCYTO (blinatumomab) 38,5 microgrammes, powder for solution to be diluted and solution for solution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) relative to historical management, based on chemotherapy salvage treatments, of CD19-expressing Philadelphia chromosome-negative precursor B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia that is refractory or relapsed after at least two prior treatments or relapsed after a prior allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, in children aged 1 month to 1 year.

587935b8b30a0ee2:
Given:
an adapted development plan in primary biliary cholangitis for seladelpar, comprising two randomised controlled trials (the pivotal phase 3 study and an ongoing confirmatory phase 3 study), with efficacy results in favour of seladelpar compared with placebo, on an intermediate endpoint (alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin) and on normalisation of alkaline phosphatase (the more relevant biochemical endpoint);
a demonstrated effect on pruritus (a highly disabling symptom) compared with placebo, after 6 months of treatment, but with an effect size of questionable clinical relevance;
a reassuring safety profile according to the available data;
but also:
the absence of an established effect of seladelpar compared with placebo on bilirubin levels (a relevant endpoint);
the absence of established efficacy on disease progression endpoints, in particular on clinical events or transplant-free survival (relevant assessment endpoints);
the absence of comparative data versus bezafibrate (used off-label, but recommended by experts), which is a clinically relevant comparator as second-line treatment, and versus elafibranor (IQIRVO),
the Committee considers that, on the current state of the data, LYVDELZI 10 mg, capsule (seladelpar), does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, in combination with ursodeoxycholic acid (AUDC) in adults with an inadequate response to AUDC, or as monotherapy in patients who do not tolerate AUDC.

7619fe84b31c61bf:
Given:
the marketing authorisation indication attributed to a subgroup of patients with PIK3CA/AKT1/PTEN alteration(s) (41% of patients in the CAPItello-29 study), defined after the inclusion phase, without stratification at randomisation on this factor;
a control arm (fulvestrant alone) in the CAPItello study that the Transparency Committee judged questionable, or even non-optimal, in view of the other available alternatives and the heterogeneous population included in that study;
the observed gain in progression-free survival, judged modest, with a point estimate of the absolute difference in medians of 4.2 months and an HR = 0.50, 95% CI [0.38; 0.65]; p < 0.001 in the subgroup of patients (41%) with PIK3CA/AKT1/PTEN alteration(s);
the absence of demonstration in terms of overall survival at the date of the first interim analysis (p=NS), in the subgroup of patients with PIK3CA/AKT1/PTEN alteration(s) (final analysis expected in Q1 2026);
the absence of formal demonstration of an improvement in quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the tolerability profile, marked by excess toxicity, notably with more frequent grade ≥ 3 adverse events (39.7% vs. 14.9%), serious adverse events (18.0% vs. 8.6%), adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation (14.4% vs. 2.3%) and deaths related to an adverse event (1.7% vs. 0.3%);
the Committee considers that TRUQAP 160 mg and 200 mg (capivasertib), film-coated tablets, in combination with fulvestrant, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with fulvestrant alone in the treatment of adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic oestrogen receptor (ER)-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer presenting one or more PIK3CA/AKT1/PTEN alterations following recurrence or progression during or after hormone therapy.

e6e097ff9c234a4e:
Considering:
the simplicity of its administration through its tablet and oral solution forms (oral route), allowing use of the product in outpatient settings and an age-appropriate form for young children;
the data available in adults supporting the presumption of a benefit for the patient, in particular:
short-term efficacy data from a comparative, randomised, open-label phase III study, which demonstrated superiority of ruxolitinib over the comparator treatments used on overall response rate at Day 28 (OR=2,64 [IC95% : 1,65-4,22]) and Day 56 (OR=2,38 [IC95% : 1,43-3,94]) in patients over 12 years of age with steroid-refractory grade II-IV acute GvHD (REACH 2 study);
efficacy data from a comparative, randomised, open-label phase III study, which demonstrated superiority of ruxolitinib over overall response rate at Week 24 (OR=2,99 [IC95% : 1,86-4,80]) and failure-free survival (HR=0,37 [IC95% : 0,27-0,51]) in patients over 12 years of age with moderate to severe chronic GvHD refractory or dependent on corticosteroids (REACH 3 study);
pharmacokinetic data supporting the presumption that results can be extrapolated from the population aged 12 years and over to the paediatric population aged 28 days to 11 years (acute GvHD) and 6 months to 11 years (chronic GvHD);
results from the open-label, non-comparative phase I/II and II studies (REACH 4 and REACH 5) in paediatric patients, assessing overall response rate;
tolerability data available (REACH 4 and REACH 5 studies) suggesting a safety profile in paediatric patients similar to that observed in adult patients;
pharmacokinetic modelling data supporting the presumption that results can be extrapolated from the population aged 12 years and over to the population under 2 years of age;
the insufficiently covered medical need, in particular thanks to its liquid formulation adapted to paediatric use;
and despite:
a non-comparative design of the paediatric studies allowing only a non-causal estimate of the effect of JAKAVI (ruxolitinib) +/- combination with other treatments with the same therapeutic aim, and not of its additional relative efficacy compared with the placebo effect and the natural course of the disease; moreover, this study design, which allowed administration of prophylactic treatments and systemic treatments for GvHD in addition to ruxolitinib, does not make it possible to isolate the effect of JAKAVI (ruxolitinib) alone. In accordance with the SmPC, section 4.2 "Posology and method of administration: JAKAVI may be added to corticosteroids and/or calcineurin inhibitors (CNIs)";
the conduct of these open-label studies, which may have introduced a follow-up bias, in particular for the measurement of subjective endpoints such as response criteria based on the National Institutes of Health consensus;
the absence of robust demonstration of an effect on overall survival in clinical situations involving life-threatening prognosis;
the absence of robust data on quality of life, the scope of which would nevertheless remain limited given the open-label nature of the study;
the use of different pharmaceutical forms (tablets, capsules, oral solutions) in the paediatric clinical studies;
uncertainty regarding the long-term safety profile in the paediatric population and the significant developmental toxicity risk identified in the risk management plan (PGR);
uncertainties regarding the transposability of data: no clinical data are available for patients under 2 years of age, and there are few children under 12 years who are resistant to corticosteroids or other systemic treatments;
the Transparency Committee considers that the JAKAVI 5 mg/mL oral solution (ruxolitinib) and JAKAVI 5 mg tablet (ruxolitinib) provide a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the management strategy for patients aged 28 days to under 12 years with acute graft-versus-host disease (GvHD) who have an inadequate response to corticosteroids or other systemic treatments, and for patients aged 6 months to under 12 years with chronic graft-versus-host disease (GvHD) who have an inadequate response to corticosteroids or other systemic treatments.

858542ff642d500d:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority in the phase III RUBY study of dostarlimab combined with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of radiological progression-free survival (PFS), with:
in patients with a dMMR/MSI-H tumour status, an HR = 0,28 (95% CI = [0,162 ; 0,495], p < 0,0001; and a median PFS of: not reached [11,8 ; NA] versus 7,7 months [5,6 ; 9,7]) in the comparator group;
in the ITT population, an HR = 0,64 (95% CI = [0,507 ; 0,800]) and a median PFS of 11,8 months [9,6 ; 17,1] versus 7,9 months [7,6 ; 9,5] in the comparator group;
the demonstration of superiority of dostarlimab combined with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of overall survival (OS), in the ITT population (including 23% of dMMR/MSI-H patients), through an interim analysis;
the existence of a significant quantitative interaction by MMR status for the endpoints (PFS and OS), demonstrating a greater effect of dostarlimab in patients with a dMMR tumour status than in those with a pMMR tumour status;
and this despite:
the absence of an overall survival analysis specifically planned in the protocol for the dMMR/MSI-H subgroup in the RUBY study;
an excess of toxicity, notably concerning grade ≥ 3 adverse events with 72,2 % in the dostarlimab group and 60,2 % in the placebo group, immune-mediated adverse events (58,5 % and 37,0 %), and serious adverse events (39,8 % and 28,0 %);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint).
the Commission considers that JEMPERLI (dostarlimab) in combination with carboplatin and paclitaxel in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with JEMPERLI (dostarlimab) as monotherapy, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with carboplatin and paclitaxel in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed or recurrent advanced endometrial cancer (EC), with a dMMR/MSI-H tumour status.

875cddd884dfc02d:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of superiority of the combination of RYBREVANT (amivantamab) and LAZCLUZE (lazertinib) versus osimertinib alone in a randomised study (MARIPOSA) conducted in patients with newly diagnosed locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC, with common EGFR mutation by deletion in exon 19 or L858R substitution in exon 21, in terms of:
progression-free survival assessed by an independent review committee (IRC) according to RECIST v1.1 criteria: HR = 0,70 (95% CI = [0,58 ; 0,85]; p=0,0002), with median progression-free survival of 23,7 versus 16,6 months respectively;
overall survival, with an HR of 0,75 (95% CI = [0,61 ; 0,92]; p=0,0048), a median overall survival not reached in the amivantamab + lazertinib group and 36,7 months in the osimertinib group, and overall survival rates at 42 months of 56 % and 44 % respectively;
and despite:
the open-label conduct of the MARIPOSA study for the amivantamab + lazertinib group;
a difference in progression-free survival essentially linked to a reduction in progressions while deaths without progression are increased, without a test comparing the cumulative incidences of deaths without progression between groups;
an excess of short-term mortality (first 9-12 months of treatment, before the Kaplan-Meier overall survival curves cross) observed in patients treated with the combination of RYBREVANT (amivantamab) and LAZCLUZE (lazertinib). To date, no data are available on the factors leading to this major risk in view of a prior selection of patients;
the tolerability profile of the amivantamab + lazertinib combination versus osimertinib, marked by a high frequency of grade ≥ 3 adverse events (75,1 % and 42,8 % respectively), serious adverse events (48,7 % and 33,4 % respectively) and adverse events leading to permanent discontinuation of at least one study treatment (34,9 % and 13,6 % respectively), as well as by the occurrence of:
dermatological reactions (notably rashes) and infusion-related reactions in 88,6 % and 62,9 % respectively,
and venous thromboembolic events in 37,3 % of patients;
a possible heterogeneity of effect by age, with a possibly greater treatment effect in the subgroup of patients under 65 years of age in the MARIPOSA study;
the absence of data in patients with symptomatic brain metastases, who were excluded from the study, which limits extrapolation of the results to this patient profile;
the impossibility of quantifying the contribution of this combination versus the combination of osimertinib + pemetrexed + platinum salt (cisplatin or carboplatin) due to concomitant development;
possibly informative censoring for 11% of patients in the amivantamab + lazertinib group and 9% of patients in the osimertinib group, related in particular to withdrawals of consent (15 and 8 patients respectively) and to patients who missed 2 or more consecutive assessments (11 and 8 patients respectively);
the absence of a formal conclusion regarding quality-of-life results;
the Transparency Committee considers that the combination of RYBREVANT (amivantamab) 350 mg, solution for dilution for infusion, with LAZCLUZE (lazertinib) 80 mg and 240 mg, film-coated tablets, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with TAGRISSO (osimertinib).

d8a138e5acfee954:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority in the phase III RUBY study of dostarlimab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of radiological progression-free survival, with in the ITT population an HR = 0,64 (95 % CI = [0,507 ; 0,800], p < 0,0001) and a median PFS of 11,8 months [9,6 ; 17,1] versus 7,9 months [7,6 ; 9,5] in the comparator group;
the demonstration of superiority of dostarlimab in combination with paclitaxel + carboplatin chemotherapy in the induction phase, followed by maintenance treatment with dostarlimab, in terms of overall survival, in the ITT population (including 76 % pMMR/MSS patients), through an interim analysis;
but taking into account:
the absence of risk-of-error management for the progression-free survival and overall survival analyses in the pMMR/MSS subgroup, which nonetheless represents 76 % of the patients included in the RUBY study, rendering these results exploratory;
the existence of a significant quantitative interaction by MMR status for the endpoints (PFS and OS), in favour of a smaller effect of dostarlimab in patients with a pMMR tumour status than in those with a dMMR tumour status;
excess toxicity, notably in grade ≥ 3 adverse events with 72,2 % in the dostarlimab group and 60,2 % in the placebo group, immune-mediated adverse events (58,5 % and 37,0 %), serious adverse events (39,8 % and 28,0 %);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint).
the Transparency Committee considers that JEMPERLI 500 mg (dostarlimab) in combination with carboplatin and paclitaxel in the induction phase, followed by JEMPERLI (dostarlimab) maintenance monotherapy, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with carboplatin and paclitaxel in the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed or recurrent advanced endometrial cancer (EC) with pMMR/MSS tumour status or whose status with regard to this deficiency is unknown.

ebdde08a9ee89c79:
Taking into account:
a gain in progression-free survival with HR = 0,60 (98.5% CI [0.41; 0.88]) versus a comparator (VRd regimen) that was accepted at the time the study was conducted but is no longer a treatment reference at the date of this assessment, since the marketing authorisation (AMM) of DARZALEX (daratumumab) for this indication on 31 August 2018 (superiority established versus a regimen without anti-CD38);
the absence of demonstrated benefit in terms of overall survival (OS);
the absence of formal demonstration of an improvement in quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the tolerability profile of the IsaVRd regimen versus the VRd regimen, marked by an excess of grade 3 or higher adverse events (91,6% versus 84,0%) and of serious adverse events (70,7% versus 67,4%);
the impossibility of positioning this regimen relative to those based on daratumumab,
the Transparency Committee considers that SARCLISA (isatuximab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current first-line treatment strategy for adult patients with newly diagnosed multiple myeloma who are ineligible for autologous stem cell transplantation.

264aa1bc87b55764:
In view of:
the demonstrated efficacy in reducing and normalising serum potassium, and in preventing recurrences of hyperkalaemia, versus placebo (CT opinion of 2019);
new data in favour of an effect of VELTASSA versus the non-active comparator (placebo) on potassium control in patients with heart failure with reduced LVEF treated with RAAS inhibitors, but of a modest magnitude of effect with questionable clinical relevance in the DIAMOND trial on the primary endpoint;
new data, again versus placebo, demonstrating the ability of VELTASSA to facilitate continuation of spironolactone in adult patients with chronic kidney disease and resistant arterial hypertension, after 12 weeks of treatment;
comparative palatability data versus CCP, suggesting better treatment adherence with VELTASSA compared with available alternatives (KAYEXALATE / RESIKALI);
a tolerability profile judged acceptable owing to the absence of sodium in the composition of VELTASSA, in favour of better tolerance in terms of fluid and sodium retention; however, the calcium content of the product forms part of treatment monitoring, and there are uncertainties regarding long-term tolerability data in relation to rare but serious gastrointestinal complications (possible class effect observed with other potassium binders, particularly in combination with sorbitol);
the medical need for new cation exchange resins that are effective and better tolerated;
and:
the absence of robust comparative data versus available active treatments demonstrating superior efficacy of VELTASSA;
the absence of demonstration of an impact on morbidity and mortality and on quality of life;
the Transparency Committee considers that VELTASSA 8,4 g and 16,8 g (patiromer), powder for oral suspension, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic management strategy for hyperkalaemia in adults, on a par with LOKELMA.

1f08eea130aa408b:
The actual clinical benefit (service médical rendu) of ZINNAT 125 mg/5 ml ENFANTS et NOURRISSONS (cefuroxime) remains important in the following indications:
acute otitis media;
angina documented as due to group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus.

4b0ffa48f12ebf19:
Given:
the methodological quality of the two pivotal studies (controlled, randomised, double-blind, relevant choice of endpoints including quality of life, sample sizes), but knowing that the choice of placebo is regrettable, particularly in patients naive to any biologic treatment, and that the efficacy assessment was carried out in a heterogeneous population combining patients naive and non-naive to biologic treatment;
the demonstration of superiority of guselkumab over placebo, with an effect size at best moderate on clinical remission in the overall study population;
the absence of demonstration of an effect on the need for colectomy;
the absence of comparative data versus anti-TNFα agents in patients with an inadequate response to conventional background therapy and naive to anti-TNFα, and versus vedolizumab (ENTYVIO), although these comparisons were feasible;
and the good safety profile of guselkumab,
the Commission considers that TREMFYA (guselkumab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators.

cf4a598e12c15540:
Given:
the demonstration, in a phase 3, randomised, open-label study, of the superiority of CARVYKTI (ciltacabtagene autoleucel) compared with DPd or PVd protocols (at the investigator's choice) in terms of:
progression-free survival (primary endpoint) assessed by a validated computerised algorithm under the responsibility of a laboratory blinded to the treatment allocated, and defined by IMWG criteria: HR = 0,26 (95% CI: [0,18 ; 0,38]), p-value < 0,0001 without accounting for progression events occurring in the first 8 weeks, otherwise HR = 0,40 (95% CI: [0,29 ; 0,55]);
complete response or stringent complete response rate assessed by a computerised algorithm (hierarchised secondary endpoint): OR = 10,3; 95% CI: [6,5 ; 16,4]; p < 0,0001;
overall response rate (hierarchised secondary endpoint): OR = 3,0; 95% CI: [1,8 ; 5,0]; p < 0,0001;
overall rate of negative minimal residual disease (MRD) (hierarchised secondary endpoint): OR = 8,7; 95% CI: [5,4 ; 13,9]; p < 0,0001;
overall survival (hierarchised secondary endpoint) at the second interim analysis conducted on 01/05/2024, with HR = 0,55; 95% CI: [0,39 ; 0,79]; p = 0,0009 < 0,0108;
time to symptom deterioration according to the MySIm-Q score, with 79 deteriorations observed (30 in the cilta-cel group and 49 in the control group) and HR = 0,38; 95% CI: [0,24 ; 0,61]; p < 0,0001. The median time to score deterioration was 23,7 months in the cilta-cel group and 18,86 months in the control group;
and despite:
a crossing of the progression-free survival curves, with a greater number of progressions in the cilta-cel group during the first 8 weeks before cell infusion, without a clear explanation, a crossing that is also seen on the overall survival curves, thus suggesting excess early mortality while awaiting reinfusion, before a potential delayed benefit;
a marked safety profile, notably neurotoxicity and a long-term risk of secondary haematological malignancy, requiring more data to resolve uncertainties regarding the overall longer-term safety profile of this CAR-T;
the impossibility of positioning it relative to ABECMA (idecabtagene vicleucel) in patients from their second relapse of multiple myeloma, in the absence of robust data,
the Transparency Committee considers that CARVYKTI (ciltacabtagene autoleucel) provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with DPd [daratumumab – pomalidomide – dexamethasone] and PVd [pomalidomide – bortezomib – dexamethasone] protocols in the treatment of adult patients with relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma, who have received at least one prior treatment including an immunomodulatory agent and a proteasome inhibitor, who are refractory to lenalidomide and whose disease progressed during the last treatment.

f3de4caa5cda2c95:
Given the absence of clinical study data for the population of children aged under 5 years;
the previously submitted and analysed data concerning the other populations for which evinacumab is indicated (children aged 5 to 11 years [non-comparative study CL-17100 and extrapolation data] and adults and adolescents [comparative study ELIPSE-HoFH]);
the reassuring usage data from compassionate access in children under 5 years and an extrapolation analysis of evinacumab's efficacy in this population based on a pharmacokinetic (PK) and PK/PD (pharmacodynamics) simulation model;
the absence of any new safety signal and the rather favourable tolerability profile of evinacumab;
the Commission considers that EVKEEZA (evinacumab) 150 mg/ml, concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic management strategy for children aged 6 months to 4 years with homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia.

0429be0f2e21cb2c:
Given:
limited data from the literature demonstrating the efficacy of diluted noradrenaline in restoring and maintaining peri-operative arterial pressure after hypotension induced by spinal anaesthesia or general anaesthesia in adults;
the established medical use of diluted noradrenaline in the peri-operative control of arterial pressure;
a known safety profile judged acceptable to date, noradrenaline being a treatment routinely handled by professionals working in emergency care, in the operating theatre and in intensive care/resuscitation units;
but in view of:
the modest magnitude of effect observed with diluted noradrenaline compared with alternatives, including in robust clinical trials;
the absence of efficacy and tolerability data specific to the specialty TANOREN 0.5 mg/mL (noradrenaline tartrate), solution to be diluted for injectable solution/for infusion, in particular compared with other vasopressors;
the Commission considers that TANOREN 0.5 mg/mL (noradrenaline tartrate), solution to be diluted for injectable solution/for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the other vasopressors available in France.

9f168ac330c72ffd:
Considering:
the medical need to broaden the pneumococcal vaccine offer in line with the evolving epidemiological situation of infections caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, in particular due to the emergence of new non-vaccine serotypes;
the non-inferiority of the immune response induced by CAPVAXIVE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 21-valent) compared with PREVENAR 20 (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 20-valent, adsorbed) in terms of geometric mean titres of opsonophagocytic antibodies (OPA GMT) for the 10 common serotypes covered by both vaccines;
the superiority of the immune response induced by CAPVAXIVE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 21-valent) compared with PREVENAR 20 (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 20-valent, adsorbed) in terms of OPA GMT for 10 of the 11 unique serotypes (9N, 15A, 16F, 17F, 20, 23A, 23B, 24F, 31 and 35B) covered by CAPVAXIVE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 21-valent);
the co-administration data with a quadrivalent seasonal influenza vaccine (QIV), which showed non-inferiority of concomitant administration of QIV and PCV21 compared with sequential administration of these same vaccines, in terms of HAI (haemagglutination inhibition) GMT 30 days after vaccination, for 3 of the 4 QIV strains except the A/H3N2 strain;
a favourable safety profile;
but:
a potential additional impact on the reduction of morbidity and mortality conferred by the addition of 11 further serotypes (9N, 15A, 15C, 16F, 17F, 20A, 23A, 23B, 24F, 31 and 35B) compared with PREVENAR 20, which cannot be assessed in the absence of clinical efficacy data, with no coverage of the 10 serotypes currently covered by PREVENAR 20 (4, 6B, 9V, 14, 18C, 19F, 23F, 1, 5, 15B);
the Committee considers that CAPVAXIVE (pneumococcal polysaccharide conjugate vaccine, 21-valent, non-adsorbed) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active immunisation for the prevention of invasive infections and pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae in adults aged 18 years and over.

c53bddd91e2e65f3:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of superiority of JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib) over the idélalisib + rituximab or bendamustine + rituximab regimens in terms of progression-free survival (primary endpoint; stratified HR = 0,583; 95% CI [0,38; 0,89]; p=0,0105), shown in the BRUIN 20020 study with median follow-up of 8,3 months in the pirtobrutinib group and 6,1 months in the IdelaR or BendaR group in patients with CLL previously treated with a BTKi, but of a magnitude of effect judged to be modest;
the absence of demonstrated superiority in overall survival between the two groups in clinical situations involving life-threatening prognosis;
the exploratory nature of the quality-of-life analyses;
the safety profile of JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib), marked by a similar proportion of serious adverse events (47,4 %) compared with the idélalisib + rituximab and bendamustine + rituximab regimens (46,8 %), and despite a lower proportion of adverse events of grade ≥ 3 (57,7 % vs 73,4%);
the sensitivity analysis of overall survival, in which patients in the IdelaR or BendaR groups who switched to the pirtobrutinib group were censored, and which did not allow an exploratory determination of whether the absence of effect found on OS could be explained by dilution of the effect due to switching. Consequently, the hypothesis of a true absence of treatment effect cannot be excluded;
the number of deaths in the pirtobrutinib group, higher than that observed in the IdelaR or BendaR group (32 % versus 27 %) in the ITT population;
the presence in the study of a subgroup of "doubly exposed" patients (n = 60 per group), which approximates the reimbursement scope ("doubly refractory") without corresponding to it exactly, with the observation, however, of 26 deaths (43 %) in the pirtobrutinib group versus 19 deaths (31 %) in the IdelaR or BendaR group,
the Committee considers that JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib) 100 mg, film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the comparators bendamustine + rituximab and idélalisib + rituximab.

cfcdcae48ee1f057:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of OPDIVO YERVOY (nivolumab/ipilimumab) over standard chemotherapy ± targeted therapy, in terms of progression-free survival in first-line treatment: (HR=0,21 IC95% [0,14 ; 0,32] ; p<0,0001) in a randomised phase III study, with multiple substantial amendments made during the course of the study;
the absence of data available on overall survival and on the added value of YERVOY (ipilimumab) to OPDIVO (nivolumab) compared with OPDIVO (nivolumab) alone in terms of progression-free survival in first-line treatment exclusively;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
a safety profile marked by a lower frequency of grade 3-5 adverse events and serious adverse events, with nevertheless a higher frequency of immune-mediated adverse events (identified as important risks in the RMP);
the Committee considers that the combination of OPDIVO (nivolumab) & YERVOY (ipilimumab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with chemotherapy ± targeted therapy in the first-line treatment of adult patients with metastatic colorectal cancer with high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) or mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) that is unresectable upfront.

3d75705aa50b9d1b:
Given:
the data previously assessed at initial listing and at re-evaluation (CT opinions of 21 avril 2021 and 1er février 2023), which demonstrated the non-inferiority of switching from conventional triple therapy (oral triple therapy) to monthly long-acting injectable dual therapy with cabotegravir + rilpivirine in adult patients who were virologically suppressed and had no history of treatment failure or resistance, in terms of virological failures and immunovirological response at 48 weeks;
new pharmacokinetic data (MOCHA clinical study) showing:
treatment exposure equivalent between adolescents and adults, allowing extrapolation of adult efficacy data to adolescents, in line with European recommendations on the clinical development of medicines for the treatment of HIV,
a long half-life of the products allowing spaced dosing,
a relatively favourable tolerability profile, although marked by a high frequency of injection-site reactions,
a relatively low genetic barrier, particularly compared with rilpivirine, with a risk of emergence of resistance (>50%) to the drugs of the combination in case of virological failure, and cross-resistance across the 2 therapeutic classes (INI and INNTI);
the absence of demonstrated impact of this simplification strategy in terms of improved morbidity and mortality, quality of life or adherence compared with continuing conventional oral triple or dual therapy;
the absence of clinical data to assess the benefit of this dual therapy in patients with adherence difficulties who, in theory, could benefit from this strategy;
the Commission considers that VOCABRIA (cabotegravir) 30 mg, tablet, and VOCABRIA (cabotegravir) 600 mg, injectable suspension, do not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of adolescents (aged 12 ans et plus, and weighing at least 35 kg) infected with HIV-1, virologically suppressed (viral load < 50 copies/mL) on stable antiretroviral treatment for at least 6 mois, with more than 200 CD4/mm3, with no evidence of current or past resistance and no history of virological failure on non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI) and integrase inhibitors (INI), compared with the available alternatives (oral triple and dual therapies, see section 2.2).

e1474495c0822d55:
Given:
an insufficiently covered medical need in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (DEB) from birth, involving only nursing skin care, dressings and interfaces (medical devices);
the demonstration of superiority, with a large effect size, in a Phase III, randomised (intra-patient), double-blind study versus placebo (excipient gel) in 31 patients with DEB with one or more genetically confirmed mutation(s) of the COL7A1 gene, on the percentage of patients with complete wound closure confirmed over 2 consecutive weeks at 6 months (primary endpoint, ∆ = 45,8 %) and 3 months (∆ = 51,0 %);
a tolerance profile marked mainly by chills and pruritus;
despite:
the absence of robust data on the assessment of pain associated with dressing changes at the main wounds, assessed exploratorily by visual analogue scale (VAS) or by the FLACC-R score (children < 6 years);
the lack of efficacy data for beremagene geperpavec in dominant DEB (only 1 patient included in the GEM-3 study) and in the most severe generalised forms of DEB, as these patients almost all have a history of squamous cell carcinoma (exclusion criterion);
the demonstration of efficacy in the population from birth relying on data from patients ≥ 1 year included in the clinical study, compared with real-world data from patients < 1 year who received beremagene geperpavec under compassionate use in Europe or after marketing in the USA;
the Committee considers that VYJUVEK (beremagene geperpavec), suspension and gel for cutaneous use, provides a moderate improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the management of wounds in patients with dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (DEB) presenting one or more mutations of the gene for the alpha 1 chain of type VII collagen (COL7A1), from birth.

f0d8077be9c7b92c:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of superiority of adding DARZALEX (daratumumab) to the VRd regimen in terms of rate of negative minimal residual disease (OR = 2,07 ; IC95% = [1,38 ; 3,10]) and progression-free survival (HR = 0,61 ; IC95% = [0,42 ; 0,90]); it should be noted, however, that the comparator (VRd regimen) was accepted at the time the CEPHEUS study was conducted, but is no longer a reference treatment at the date of this assessment, since DARZALEX (daratumumab) obtained marketing authorisation (AMM) in the D-VMP (31/08/2018) and D-Rd (19/11/2019) regimens;
uncertainties regarding the transferability of the CEPHEUS study results to the indication concerned by the request (in patients ineligible for autologous stem cell transplantation): this study included patients eligible for autologous stem cell transplantation who chose to defer it (26,8 % of the patients included in the CEPHEUS study);
uncertainties regarding the capacity of the surrogate endpoint (surrogacy) of the CEPHEUS primary endpoint (rate of negative minimal residual disease) to predict overall survival;
the absence of demonstrated benefit in terms of overall survival;
the absence of robust quality-of-life data;
the tolerability profile of the experimental arm, marked by excess haematological toxicity (D-VRd: 82,7 %; VRd: 64,6 %) and infectious toxicity (D-VRd: 91,9 %; VRd: 85,6 %), as well as an increase in deaths related to adverse events (D-VRd: 16,8 %; VRd: 10,8 %);
and the impossibility of positioning the D-VRd regimen relative to the D-Rd, D-VMP and Isa-VRd regimens, in the absence of sufficiently robust direct or indirect comparative data;
the Transparency Committee considers that DARZALEX 1 800 mg (daratumumab), injectable solution, in combination with bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone (D-VRd regimen), does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current first-line treatment strategy for adults with newly diagnosed multiple myeloma who are ineligible for autologous stem cell transplantation.

0804552470a270b8:
Given:
a suggested difference in event-free survival (EFS) with HR = 0.64 (two-sided 98.33% CI [0.43; 0.95]) in a post-hoc analysis of ibrutinib versus autograft, based on a hypothesis reversed from the initial hypothesis pre-specified in the protocol, and a reformulation of the test (multiple analyses performed a posteriori);
a maintenance treatment with rituximab that was introduced during the study (without stratification at randomisation);
the absence of demonstration on progression-free survival and overall survival (exploratory endpoints);
the absence of quality-of-life assessment;
a toxicity profile marked by serious adverse events (AEs) more frequent in the ibrutinib [I] group compared with the autograft [A] group (64.5% vs. 45.9%), including grade ≥ 3 AEs (55.1% vs. 39.2%), and a lower frequency of AEs leading to death in group I (2.3%) compared with group A (4.1%),
the Committee considers that IMBRUVICA (ibrutinib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with autologous stem cell transplantation.

1f71ef2c533fe4b1:
Given:
the data previously assessed at initial listing and at re-evaluation (Transparency Committee opinions of 21 avril 2021 and 1er février 2023), which demonstrated the non-inferiority of switching from a conventional triple therapy (oral triple therapy) to the long-acting monthly injectable dual therapy cabotegravir + rilpivirine in virologically controlled adult patients with no history of treatment failure or resistance, in terms of virological failures and immunovirological response at 48 weeks;
new pharmacokinetic data (MOCHA clinical study) which showed:
equivalent drug exposure between adolescents and adults, allowing adult efficacy data to be extrapolated to adolescents, in line with European recommendations on the clinical development of medicines for the treatment of HIV,
a long half-life of the products allowing dosing intervals to be extended,
a relatively favourable tolerability profile, although marked by a high frequency of injection-site reactions,
a relatively low genetic barrier, particularly compared with rilpivirine, with a risk of resistance emergence (>50%) to the drugs of the combination in case of virological failure and cross-resistance to the 2 therapeutic classes (INI and NNRTI);
the absence of demonstrated impact of this simplification strategy in terms of improved morbidity-mortality, quality of life or adherence compared with continuing conventional oral triple or dual therapy;
the absence of clinical data allowing assessment of the value of this dual therapy in patients with adherence difficulties who, in theory, could benefit from this strategy;
the Commission considers that REKAMBYS (rilpivirine) 900 mg, injectable suspension, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of adolescents (aged 12 years and over, and weighing at least 35 kg) infected with HIV-1, virologically controlled (viral load < 50 copies/mL) on stable antiretroviral treatment for at least 6 months, with more than 200 CD4/mm3, without evidence of current or past resistance and without history of virological failure to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI) and integrase inhibitors (INI) class agents, compared with available alternatives (oral triple and dual therapies, see section 2.2).

ade1025cd0cd6ad8:
Given:
the low level of evidence provided by the BRUIN-18001 study, a phase I/II, non-comparative study with limited sample size, whose results suggest a signal of efficacy of pirtobrutinib without allowing a conclusion of demonstrated clinical benefit compared with available alternatives in adult patients with relapsed or refractory MCL who have previously been treated with an iBTK and are ineligible for treatment with TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel);
the absence of sufficiently robust comparative data, preventing demonstration of relative efficacy compared with therapeutic alternatives or placebo effect;
a development programme concomitant with TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel), with which no direct comparison was expected at the date of this assessment, but for which the absence of an indirect comparison carried out by the laboratory is regrettable;
the exploratory nature of the quality-of-life analyses;
the safety profile of JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib), marked by serious adverse events in 38,6% of patients;
a partially met medical need, with interest in having an additional alternative for patients who have relapsed or are refractory after treatment with an iBTK and are ineligible for treatment with TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel);
and pending the results of the phase III study comparing pirtobrutinib versus covalent iBTK in patients with MCL, pretreated and iBTK-naive,
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, and pending the results of study BRUIN-MCL-321, JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib) 100 mg, film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for patients who have relapsed or are refractory, previously treated with an iBTK and ineligible for treatment with TECARTUS (brexucabtagene autoleucel). The Committee considers that the development plan for JAYPIRCA (pirtobrutinib) is such as to provide the data needed to update its assessment. Results are expected by the Committee before 31/12/2026, which will re-evaluate this medicinal product in light of these data in 2027.

23266115d90a88df:
Considering:
the efficacy data from a non-comparative phase 2 study, suggesting an overall response rate of 97.0% (95% CI [91.6%; 99.4%]), of which 94.1% (95% CI [87.5%; 97.8%]) complete response;
the uncertainty surrounding the estimates of the relative efficacy of this treatment given the absence of a direct comparison provided, and the methodological weakness of the indirect comparisons carried out, in a context where a direct comparison with an available alternative was possible;
the safety profile marked by significant short- and medium-term toxicity;
the uncertainties regarding the long-term clinical efficacy and tolerability given the limited follow-up of nearly 42 months;
the Commission considers that BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel), 1,1-70 x 106 cellules/mL / 1,1-70 x 106 cellules/mL, dispersion pour perfusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of adult patients with relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma (FL) after at least two lines of systemic therapy.

f34c3945abb27861:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of serplulimab combined with chemotherapy (carboplatin + etoposide) over chemotherapy alone, in a randomised double-blind study, in terms of overall survival in the ITT population, with an HR of 0,63; IC95% [0,49 ; 0,82], with median OS of 15,4 months (IC95%: [13,27 ; NA]) in the serplulimab group versus 10,9 months (IC95%: [9,96 ; 14,32]) in the placebo group;
and despite:
the control arm (carboplatin/etoposide + placebo) being considered debatable in relation to current therapeutic practice;
the uncertainty regarding the representativeness of the study population with respect to the European population, owing to a majority of Asian patients (69 %) and low representation of European patients;
the absence of robust data on progression-free survival and quality of life, considered exploratory because no alpha-risk management method was applied;
unblinding that occurred after the first interim analysis;
the non-inclusion of patients with an ECOG score > 1;
the difficulty in distinguishing the portion attributable to treatment during the induction phase in combination with chemotherapy from that during maintenance treatment with serplulimab alone;
the safety profile marked by more frequent immune-mediated adverse events (38% vs 19%);
the medical need being only partly met by the existence of other therapeutic alternatives, notably PD-L1 inhibitors;
the Transparency Committee considers that HETRONIFLY 10 mg/mL (serplulimab), solution for dilution for infusion, like TECENTRIQ (atezolizumab) and IMFINZI (durvalumab), provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with chemotherapy alone (carboplatin and etoposide) as first-line treatment of extensive-stage small cell lung cancer (SCLC).

cbe6cbac464c7902:
Given:
- the available immunogenicity data:
- a predictive protection threshold set at a neutralising antibody (ANS) titre against CHIKV ≥ 100, a conservative threshold agreed with the FDA and the EMA;
- the seroresponse rate obtained in 97,8 % (95 % CI [97,2 ; 98,3]) of individuals aged 12 to 64 years, and in 87,3 % (95 % CI [81,8 ; 91,3]) of individuals aged 65 years and over, with anti-CHIKV ANS levels greater than or equal to the predictive protection threshold 22 days after a single dose of VIMKUNYA in two phase III trials;
- the GMT at D22 after vaccination of 1 618,05 [95 % CI: 1 522,11 ; 1 720,04]) in individuals aged 12 to 64 years, and 723,93 [584,13 ; 897,20]) in individuals aged 65 years and over;
- a decline in the immune response over the 6-month study follow-up, with:
- seroresponse falling from 97,8 % (D22, peak) to 85,5 % (D183);
- GMTs 2 times (65 years and over) or 3 times (12 to 64 years) higher than the predictive protection threshold at 6 months post-vaccination (versus 16 times higher at D22);
- a less pronounced immune response in individuals aged 65 years and over compared with individuals aged 12 to 64 years;
- available tolerability data showing an acceptable safety profile;
- but:
- the absence of data on the clinical efficacy of the vaccine against infections, symptomatic forms, hospitalisations and deaths;
- uncertainties regarding the duration of vaccine protection of VIMKUNYA (chikungunya vaccine (recombinant, adsorbed)) beyond 6 months;
- the Transparency Committee considers that VIMKUNYA (chikungunya vaccine (recombinant, adsorbed)) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the therapeutic strategy according to the vaccination recommendations in force from the HCSP (Recommandations sanitaires aux voyageurs 2025).

c4923d1bff822361:
Given:
the persistent medical need for vaccines in the prevention of SARS-CoV-2 infections, depending on the evolution of the epidemiological situation and the regular emergence of new variants;
the data taken into account by the HAS's current recommendations, namely:
the placebo-controlled demonstration of the efficacy of the original COMIRNATY vaccine (mRNA vaccine (nucleoside-modified) against COVID-19) in reducing the number of symptomatic COVID-19 cases, including in patients over 65 years of age, with a vaccine efficacy of 95 % [95 % CI: 90,3-97,6] in primary vaccination in 36 621 adult subjects (phase III study C4591001), and immunogenicity data obtained with the various variants in primary vaccination and booster, taken into account in establishing the current vaccination recommendations;
observational data from real-world studies that corroborate the results in terms of hospitalisations and deaths in primary vaccination and booster, notably in France;
new data included in the file which, to date, have not led to an update of the vaccination recommendations;
an acceptable safety profile confirmed by pharmacovigilance and pharmacoepidemiology data;
and despite:
the absence of new clinical efficacy data in addition to the immunogenicity data available;
long-term uncertainties, notably regarding the duration of protection conferred,
the Committee considers that – like SPIKEVAX (mRNA vaccine (nucleoside-modified) against COVID-19) and NUVAXOVID (SARS-CoV-2 Spike protein and Matrix-M adjuvant) – COMIRNATY (mRNA vaccine (nucleoside-modified) against COVID-19) provides an important improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR II) for active immunisation to prevent COVID-19 caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus in persons aged 12 years and over, in accordance with the HAS's current recommendations.

9d533efd986d7f9d:
Given the low level of evidence from the efficacy studies and the small effect observed versus placebo in diabetic patients;
given the absence of comparative data versus other treatments, in particular local-action treatments available for peripheral neuropathy in these patients;
given the frequency of adverse events observed with application of the capsaicin 8% patch;
the Commission considers that QUTENZA 179 mg (capsaicin), cutaneous patch, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the management of peripheral neuropathic pain of diabetic origin, either alone or in combination with other analgesic medicines.

0b74ca0d27825efa:
Given:
the insufficiently met medical need;
the efficacy data in adults that can be extrapolated to children on the basis of similar pharmacokinetic data in adults and children for the intravenous route;
the absence of data in patients with severe neurological impairment, although these constitute an important prognostic factor in the course of juvenile SLE;
the absence of robust data on quality of life;
the short-term safety profile (1-year follow-up) overall similar to that in adults, but with concerns about long-term safety in children because of the significant identified risks, such as systemic hypersensitivity reactions at injection that may occur in a delayed manner, and potential significant risks such as serious infections (in particular progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy), malignancies and psychiatric disorders (depression and suicidal behaviour);
the absence of data on children's growth and development;
the Committee considers that the medicinal products BENLYSTA 120 mg and 400 mg (belimumab), powder for solution for infusion, provide, as in adults, a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the management strategy for children aged 5 years and above with active systemic lupus erythematosus with the presence of autoantibodies and high disease activity (defined, for example, by the presence of native anti-DNA antibodies and low complement) despite standard treatment, in addition to usual treatment.

c80e323183d7f873:
Given:
the insufficiently covered medical need;
efficacy data in adults that can be extrapolated to children on the basis of pharmacokinetic data, similar in adults and children for the intravenous route and similar in children for the intravenous and subcutaneous routes;
the absence of data in patients with severe neurological impairment, although these constitute an important prognostic factor in the course of juvenile SLE;
the absence of robust quality-of-life data;
the short-term safety profile (1-year follow-up) overall similar to that in adults, but with concerns about long-term safety in children because of important identified risks such as systemic hypersensitivity reactions on injection that may occur with a delay, and important potential risks such as serious infections (notably progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy), malignancies and psychiatric disorders (depression and suicidal behaviour);
the absence of data on children's growth and development;
the Commission considers that the medicinal product BENLYSTA 200 mg (belimumab), solution for injection in a prefilled pen, is a range extension that does not provide a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the presentations already listed (BENLYSTA 120 mg and 400 mg, powder for solution for infusion to be diluted) in the treatment of children aged 5 years and over with active systemic lupus with presence of autoantibodies and high disease activity (defined for example by the presence of native anti-DNA antibodies and low complement) despite standard treatment, in addition to usual treatment.

f0671de353887efa:
Given:
the absence of a study specifically evaluating clopidogrel in patients with STEMI managed by primary PCI, and in particular versus CCP;
the low quality of the evidence, given the major methodological limitations of the comparative data versus placebo submitted by the laboratory (PCI-CLARITY analysis and CREDO trial), in particular due to:
an inadequate match between the population included and the requested indication: patients with STEMI managed only by delayed PCI (post-fibrinolysis) in the PCI-CLARITY analysis, reflecting an evident temporal bias linked to changes in management, and non-inclusion of patients with STEMI in the CREDO trial;
the subgroup, post-hoc and open-label design of the PCI-CLARITY analysis, whose results can only be considered exploratory for the 28-day evaluation;
only exploratory results on the secondary composite endpoint (all-cause death, MI +/- Q wave, and stroke) at 1 year in the CREDO study, and non-significant results on the primary endpoint at 28 days and on the key secondary composite endpoint at 1 year as defined by the CSR;
a body of evidence of modest level evaluating the 600 mg clopidogrel loading dose in the indication considered;
tolerability data in favour of clopidogrel in certain populations at high bleeding risk, to be considered in the context of individual benefit-risk assessment;
the medical need partially covered by the available alternatives;
the Transparency Committee considers that PLAVIX 75 mg and 300 mg (clopidogrel), film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of adult patients with acute ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction, in combination with acetylsalicylic acid, and managed by percutaneous coronary intervention (including stent placement), which includes the relevant comparators: generics of PLAVIX 75 mg (clopidogrel), BRILIQUE 90 mg (ticagrelor), EFIENT (prasugrel).

37bc1dfa79819026:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of superiority of durvalumab versus placebo as maintenance after chemoradiotherapy, in a randomised, double-blind study, in terms of overall survival (OS, primary endpoint) in the ITT population with HR = 0,73 ; 98,32% CI (0,54 ; 0,98), with a median OS of 55,9 months (95% CI [37,3–NR]) versus 33,4 months (95% CI [25,5–39,9]) in the placebo group;
and despite:
the uncertainties regarding the robustness of the results for PFS, given the absence of significance initially under allocation of a more conservative alpha risk;
the imprecision of the median survival estimates, linked to data from interim analyses and to a median follow-up limited to 30 months;
the absence of efficacy and tolerability data beyond 30 months in this indication;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life (exploratory endpoint);
the non-inclusion of patients with an ECOG score >1;
a tolerability profile marked by the occurrence of adverse events (AEs) leading to treatment discontinuation, reported in 12% of patients in the IMFINZI group and 6% in the comparator group, and by more frequent immune-mediated AEs, mainly hypothyroidism (13,7% versus 3,4%) and pneumonitis (11,8% versus 3%);
the Transparency Committee considers that IMFINZI (durvalumab) 50 mg/ml, solution to be diluted for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the current therapeutic strategy for limited-stage small-cell lung cancer (LS-SCLC), in adult patients whose disease has not progressed after platinum-based chemoradiotherapy.

a1d5a826836d7473:
The service médical rendu by TEPEZZA 500 mg, powder for concentrate for solution for infusion, is insufficient to justify reimbursement by the national solidarity scheme in the other situations covered by the marketing authorisation, namely:
in active forms of moderate to severe thyroid eye disease (TED) in adults without exophthalmos and without diplopia;
in chronic forms of moderate to severe thyroid eye disease (TED) in adults.

78f80d14b700a358:
Given:
pharmacokinetic data showing steady-state exposure values (ASCτ,ss) with the 150 mg twice-daily dosing regimen in children and adolescents comparable to those in adults, with geometric mean steady-state area under the concentration-time curve (ASCτ,ss) of 175 ng/mL·h and 170 ng/mL·h in children and adolescents respectively, and 181ng/mL·h in adults. These data came from a Phase III study (InPedILD), comparative versus placebo, randomised, double-blind, followed by open-label treatment with nintedanib, in children and adolescents aged 6 to 17 years with clinically significant fibrosing ILD (PID), whose primary objective was to assess the exposure dose and safety of nintedanib versus placebo;
extrapolation of adult data to children, associated with a comparable safety profile. Efficacy and safety were assessed in adults with progressive fibrosing ILD (PID) in the INBUILD study. Results showed that nintedanib slowed the decline in lung function, with an adjusted mean annual rate of decline in forced vital capacity (FVC, CVF) over 52 weeks of -80,8 mL/an versus -187,8 mL/an in the placebo group, i.e. a mean difference of 107,0 ml/an (IC95 % [65,4 ; 148,5]; p<0,001). Furthermore, efficacy and safety were assessed in adults with ILD (PID-ScS) associated with systemic sclerosis in the SENSCIS study. Results showed a statistically significant difference in favour of nintedanib versus placebo: adjusted mean difference between the 2 groups of 40,95 mL/an in FVC (CVF), i.e. an average reduction of approximately 43,8% in the annual decline in FVC (CVF) under nintedanib versus placebo;
a safety profile in children characterised mainly by gastrointestinal toxicity;
the unmet medical need in this rare disease in the absence of any therapeutic alternative with marketing authorisation (AMM);
but also the following limitations:
the absence of robust data on a relevant clinical endpoint and quality of life in a paediatric population in whom the impact of ILD (PID) is particularly important;
the high heterogeneity of ASCτ,ss values and the absence of an assessment of the standard deviation;
the absence of an a priori definition of the reference ASCτ,ss value in adults in the protocol;
limited extrapolation of adult clinical data to children, related to the clinical heterogeneity of ILD (PID) between adults and children;
the data concerning children with ILD-ScS (PID-ScS) in this study are limited, as only 7 patients with a fibrosing and clinically progressive phenotype associated with scleroderma were included in the study;
uncertainties regarding the risks related to growth and dental development;
the Committee considers that OFEV (nintedanib) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) within the current last-resort therapeutic strategy for the treatment of children and adolescents aged 6 years and over with:
progressive, clinically significant fibrosing interstitial lung diseases (PID);
interstitial lung disease associated with systemic sclerosis (PID-ScS).

a1465a9c2abd775d:
Taking into account:
the results of a Phase III, open-label, randomised study showing the superiority of adding durvalumab to the perioperative treatment regimen (neoadjuvant then adjuvant), with gemcitabine/cisplatin for the neoadjuvant phase, on one of the two primary endpoints: event-free survival (EFS), ITT population: median not reached in the D + G + C group versus 46,1 months in the G + C group (HR = 0,68 ; IC95%[0,558 ; 0,817] ; p< 0,0001). Nevertheless, no difference was observed in pathological complete response (pCR);
the analysis of overall survival (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint) showed superiority with HR = 0,75 ; IC95%[0,594 ; 0,934] ; p = 0,0106 and medians not reached in the compared groups. Despite statistical significance, there is uncertainty about the long-term maintenance of the overall survival results. Furthermore, the 5-year overall survival analysis planned in the protocol is expected in 2027;
the absence of available data versus nivolumab, which is recommended as adjuvant treatment in patients whose tumour cells express PD-L1 at a threshold of ≥ 1%. The majority of patients (73%) in the NIAGARA study had tumours with high PD-L1 expression (threshold >25%);
an excess of toxicity in the IMFINZI group, notably marked by serious adverse events (AEs) (61,5% versus 54,6%) and immune-mediated AEs (20,9% versus 3,0%);
the Transparency Committee considers that, in the current state of the file, IMFINZI (durvalumab) administered neoadjuvantly (in combination with gemcitabine and cisplatin) then adjuvantly (as monotherapy) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with gemcitabine/cisplatin administered neoadjuvantly alone, in the treatment of adult patients with resectable muscle-invasive bladder cancer (TVIM).

f70421b469e7389e:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of the superiority of teprotumumab over placebo in adult patients with moderate to severe active thyroid eye disease in two clinical studies, including a phase II study and a phase III study (OPTIC), both randomised, double-blind, multicentre, of 24 weeks' duration, on clinically relevant endpoints such as disease activity, proptosis and diplopia, with a clinically relevant magnitude of effect, notably: 78 % vs 7,1 % (p < 0,001) responders for the composite endpoint of proptosis (reduction ≥ 2 mm) and disease activity (reduction ≥ 2 points on the CAS), and a mean difference of -2,28 mm (p < 0,001) in the mean change in proptosis (clinical relevance threshold: 2 mm) in the OPTIC study;
the demonstration of the superiority of teprotumumab over placebo also in these two studies on the total GO-QOL score (disease-specific), with a clinically relevant magnitude of effect: improvement of 13,79 points vs 4,43 points, i.e. a mean difference of 9,36 points (p < 0,001) (clinical relevance threshold: 6 points);
exploratory results in the subgroup of patients with moderate to severe active thyroid eye disease associated with proptosis and/or diplopia, representing 91 % of the population of the phase II study and 84 % of that of the OPTIC study, which are consistent with those obtained in the overall population;
a marked safety profile, notably hearing disorders (12,2 % vs 0 % in the placebo group), such as deafness, hypoacusis and tinnitus, occurring mainly in patients with hearing disorders pre-existing before initiation of teprotumumab treatment, and hyperglycaemia (4,9 % vs 0 %), considered acceptable given the risk minimisation measures in place (laboratory monitoring for hyperglycaemia, management of hyperglycaemia, audiometry before, during and after treatment, and use of effective contraception);
the observation of relapse after treatment discontinuation in approximately 30 % of initial responders (10/34), but considering that 19/21 evaluable initial responders had maintained a response for proptosis at week 72;
the absence of data on retreatment modalities in patients who relapse;
the Committee considers that TEPEZZA 500 mg (téprotulumab), powder for concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) in the management of moderate to severe active thyroid eye disease in adults with proptosis and/or diplopia.

9841779f6b1387b4:
Given:
the proven efficacy of contraception with the etonogestrel-based subcutaneous implant, which is the active metabolite of desogestrel;
that the ANSM considered that, pharmacologically, the risk of meningioma observed with the desogestrel 75 μg progestogen-only oral contraceptive also applies to the NEXPLANON contraceptive implant, etonogestrel being the active metabolite of desogestrel;
the safety profile of desogestrel, with an excess risk of meningioma identified in a pharmacoepidemiological study EPI-PHARE on a French cohort drawn from the SNDS on the risk of intracranial meningioma associated with prolonged use of desogestrel-based oral contraception (OR = 1,25 [1,10-1,42]), and the increase in risk with duration of treatment and with the woman's age, the risk being increased from the age of 45 years;
the magnitude of the risk of meningioma associated with desogestrel 75 μg being much lower than that found in the study of prolonged use of other progestogens used at high doses, such as cyproterone acetate, chlormadinone, nomegestrol, medroxyprogesterone, medrogestone and promegestone;
the Committee considers that NEXPLANON 68 mg (etonogestrel), implant for subcutaneous use, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators (see section 5.2).

4ac5c22c1cc147f0:
Considering:
an insufficiently covered medical need in hirsutism, particularly in postmenopausal women and in forms of tumour origin, for which drug treatment options are limited and management relies mainly on surgical approaches;
the low level of evidence for therapeutic alternatives, notably cyproterone acetate, and the toxicity of the latter;
But:
the demonstration of efficacy of spironolactone rests solely on descriptive studies suggesting a reduction in the severity of hirsutism of a magnitude comparable to that observed with cyproterone acetate alone or combined with ethinylestradiol, assessed by improvement in the Ferriman-Gallwey score;
the absence of demonstrated impact on quality of life (exploratory data only), whereas the latter is particularly impaired by hirsutism, in particular the psychological, emotional and social well-being of patients, notably in moderate to severe forms;
the significant risk of hyperkalaemia identified, requiring regular biological monitoring, particularly in patients at risk (diabetes, impaired renal function, etc.);
the Committee considers that the specialties ALDACTONE 25 mg, 50 mg and 75 mg (spironolactone), scored tablet, and SPIRONOLACTONE PFIZER 25 mg, 50 mg and 75 mg, scored tablet, do not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current management strategy for hirsutism.

3c4b81a96e267688:
Given:
the data available demonstrating efficacy versus placebo in the induction and maintenance treatment of UC;
the absence of demonstrated superiority over an active comparator in the induction and maintenance treatment of UC;
the absence of demonstrated benefit on patients' quality of life in patients with mild to moderate UC;
the tolerability profile already known for medicines of the 5-ASA class,
the Commission considers that MAZALVIX (mesalazine) 500 mg and 1000 mg, gastro-resistant tablets, do not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with the available comparators (other mesalazine- or olsalazine-based medicines) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of mild to moderate ulcerative colitis in adults.

5f8de922be420147:
The clinical benefit (SMR) of XALKORI (crizotinib) 20, 50 and 150 mg, granules in hard capsules, is important in the following indications:
in the treatment of paediatric patients (aged ≥ 1 to < 18 years) with relapsed or refractory systemic anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK)-positive anaplastic large cell lymphoma (ALCL);
in the treatment of paediatric patients (aged ≥ 1 to < 18 years) with unresectable relapsed or refractory anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK)-positive inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour (IMT).

ecd5c723bbc94566:
Considering:
the lack of robust demonstration of non-inferiority (discrepancy between PP and ITT results) of mesalazine administered as a rectal foam compared with administration as a rectal enema in the treatment of the acute phase of UC;
the lack of clinical data for rectal foam mesalazine on maintenance of remission of UC;
the lack of data versus the suppository form, whose benefit is best established according to experts;
the lack of quality-of-life data;
the known safety profile of the 5-ASA class of drugs;
the fact that the choice of pharmaceutical form (suppository, enema or rectal foam) will depend on the location of the disease and the patient's preference for one form or the other,
the Transparency Committee considers that MAZALVIX (mesalazine) 1 000 mg, rectal foam, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for the treatment of mild to moderate UC and only in cases of rectosigmoid involvement.

02115c7db3b1011a:
Given:
the low level of evidence of the efficacy of oseltamivir treatment in reducing mortality and complications of influenza in seasonal epidemics;
the minimal benefit demonstrated in reducing the duration of symptoms, by an average of 18 hours [14-22] with oseltamivir in patients without a particular severity factor;
the low level of evidence of the effect of oseltamivir in reducing the incidence of suspected or confirmed influenza in the general population and in elderly communities;
the absence of any new tolerability signal since the Committee's previous opinion;
and despite the potential interest of the treatment in the event of an influenza pandemic;
the Committee considers that TAMIFLU (oseltamivir) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current strategy for managing influenza, both curative and preventive. In light of the results of the RECOVERY study (results expected in 2026), the Committee will decide whether it is appropriate to re-evaluate the medicinal product.

bbe0ef3ebf99e7c7:
Given:
the partially covered medical need in patients with primary IgA nephropathy with proteinuria ≥ 1.0 g/day (or urinary protein/creatinine ratio ≥ 0.8 g/gram), after optimisation of concomitant treatment with ACE inhibitor or ARB II;
the results of the phase III NefIgArd trial, a randomised, double-blind study conducted in patients with primary IgAN at risk of progression to end-stage chronic kidney disease, treated with renin-angiotensin system inhibitors, which demonstrated the superiority of budesonide versus placebo:
on the reduction of the urinary protein/creatinine ratio at 9 months of treatment,
on the gain in eGFR, obtained essentially during the first 3 months of treatment and maintained at the end of 2 years (9 months of treatment and 15 months of observational follow-up);
But in view of:
the absence of a sustained clinical response in terms of eGFR stabilisation, the eGFR decline continuing in a manner superimposable on the progression of kidney failure in the placebo group after the initial effect of budesonide;
the absence of controlled multiplicity for secondary analyses, including quality of life, considered exploratory;
a composite endpoint combining a 30% reduction in eGFR or confirmed end-stage renal disease (defined as dialysis ≥ 1 month, kidney transplantation, an eGFR < 15 mL/min/1.73 m² sustained for ≥ 1 month, or death attributable to a renal cause), which must be considered exploratory (not included in the final statistical analysis plan and not listed as a prespecified confirmatory test in the alpha risk control sequence);
the absence of comparative data versus clinically relevant comparators, in particular systemic corticosteroid therapy;
the absence of specific data on co-administration or interaction with gliflozins, due to the concurrent development of gliflozins in chronic kidney disease;
the absence of robust comparative data allowing estimation of the difference in corticosteroid-induced toxicity between budesonide and systemic corticosteroid therapy in this indication;
a follow-up duration limited to 2 years, reflecting uncertainty regarding long-term efficacy and tolerability data;
the Commission considers that KINPEYGO 4 mg (budesonide), modified-release capsule, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in association with optimised treatment with ACE inhibitor or ARB II, versus optimised treatment with ACE inhibitor or ARB II alone.

b2ad94d42d0ed357:
Given the available data demonstrating efficacy versus placebo in the treatment of acute exacerbations of UC;
given the absence of clinical data on maintenance of remission of UC;
given the absence of quality-of-life data;
given the known safety profile of the 5-ASA class of medicines;
given that the choice of pharmaceutical form (suppository, enema or rectal foam) will depend on the location of the disease and the patient's preference for one form or another,
the Commission considers that MAZALVIX (mesalazine) 1,000 mg, suppository, does not provide an improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the treatment of mild or moderate distal ulcerative colitis, and only in cases of involvement of the rectosigmoid.

ceb8db7542233bca:
Given:
the data from the phase I-II studies ADVL0912 and A8081013 suggesting antitumour activity of crizotinib in paediatric relapsed or refractory ALK-positive ALCL, with respective overall response rates of 88.5% (n=23/26) and 56.3% (n=9/16) and respective complete response rates of 80.8% (n=21/26) and 50.0% (n=8/16);

Wait — the instructions require copying numbers exactly, so I must keep the decimal commas.

Let me produce the correct translation:

Given:
the data from the phase I-II studies ADVL0912 and A8081013 suggesting antitumour activity of crizotinib in paediatric relapsed or refractory ALK-positive ALCL, with respective overall response rates of 88,5 % (n=23/26) and 56,3 % (n=9/16) and respective complete response rates of 80,8 % (n=21/26) and 50,0 % (n=8/16);
the unmet medical need partially covered in second-line and beyond treatment of paediatric ALK-positive ALCL;
the expected but unsubstantiated improvement in care conditions compared with chemotherapy, given the oral administration of crizotinib (capsule) at home;
and despite:
the absence of comparative data in the target paediatric population, whose results do not allow a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of crizotinib compared with available alternatives;
limited tolerance data in children in terms of duration of follow-up, with a median follow-up of less than 1 year in the studies provided;
the potential occurrence of grade 3-4 ocular toxicity and neutropenia, known adverse events of crizotinib occurring more frequently in children and requiring enhanced monitoring in the paediatric population;
the Committee considers that XALKORI (crizotinib), capsule, at doses of 250 mg and 200 mg, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the therapeutic strategy in second-line and beyond treatment of systemic relapsed or refractory ALK-positive anaplastic large cell lymphoma in paediatric patients (aged ≥ 1 year to < 18 years).

69d3cfdf3ffa8365:
Given:
the data from the phase I-II studies provided, which suggest antitumour activity of crizotinib in unresectable, relapsed or refractory ALK-positive IMTs, in particular the results of study ADVL0912, which reported, among the 12 paediatric patients treated at the dose of 280mg/m² twice daily (the marketing authorisation dose): 5 patients with a complete response, 6 patients with a partial response and 1 patient with stable disease, and a median duration of response of 14,8 months (min./max.: 2,8-48,9 months);
the unmet medical need in the treatment of unresectable, relapsed or refractory ALK-positive IMTs in children, due to the absence of a standard and/or authorised treatment for IMTs that are not conservatively operable or that are metastatic;
the expected but unsubstantiated improvement in care conditions compared with chemotherapy, given that crizotinib is administered orally (capsule) at home;
and despite:
the absence of comparative data in the targeted paediatric population, the results of which do not allow a robust conclusion on the therapeutic contribution of crizotinib compared with available alternatives;
limited tolerability data in children in terms of follow-up, with a median follow-up of less than 1 year in the studies provided;
the potential occurrence of ocular toxicity and grade 3-4 neutropenia, known adverse events of crizotinib that occur more frequently in children and require enhanced monitoring in the paediatric population;
the Transparency Committee considers that XALKORI (crizotinib), capsules, at the 250 mg and 200 mg strengths, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the therapeutic strategy for relapsed or refractory unresectable anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK)-positive inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours (IMTs) in paediatric patients (aged ≥ 1 to < 18 years).

e6d34aafdc92e17d:
Having regard to:

the demonstration of non-inferiority of isoflurane, in paediatric patients aged over 3 years under mechanical ventilation in intensive care, versus midazolam, the clinically relevant comparator, in a phase III study, on a clinically relevant primary endpoint, namely the proportion of time spent within the initially prescribed target sedation depth, in the randomised FAS population, with a mean difference of 6,57 % (IC95 % [-8,99 ; 22,13]) in favour of isoflurane, above the non-inferiority margin set at -9,36 % units;

the uncertainty of the conclusions of this study given the absence of demonstrated superiority of isoflurane versus midazolam and of non-inferiority in the PP population, and given that the sensitivity analyses were heterogeneous, with non-inferiority not demonstrated across all analyses;

the absence of robust data on secondary endpoints (exploratory analyses), in particular on consumption of morphine-based analgesics, or on time to awakening after discontinuation of sedation, the number of ventilator-free days (a clinically relevant endpoint in intensive care), or the proportion of spontaneous breathing during sedation;

a safety profile of isoflurane in paediatric patients that is broadly consistent with that observed in adults, but with the excess toxicity observed in the isoflurane group compared with the midazolam group, with AEs in 83,6 % versus 63,6 %, SAEs in 31,1 % versus 24,2 %, AEs leading to treatment discontinuation in 4,9 % versus none, and AEs of special interest in 49,2 % versus 27,3 %, respectively;

the unmet medical need already partly covered in the paediatric population by several therapeutic alternatives;

and taking into account the demonstration of non-inferiority of isoflurane in adults in a phase III study (SED001) on the proportion of time spent within the target sedation depth (RASS -1 to -4) versus propofol,

the Transparency Committee considers that CEDACONDA (isoflurane), inhalation vapour, liquid, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, which includes midazolam.

eac2c47f31d30562:
Given:
the superiority of brensocatib 25 mg over placebo in patients with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis aged 12 years and older who had experienced at least two exacerbations in the previous 12 months, in the ASPEN study on:
the annualised rate of pulmonary exacerbations (primary endpoint) was 1,036 (95% CI: [0,927; 1,157]) in the brensocatib 25 mg group and 1,286 (95% CI: [1,158; 1,428]) in the placebo group (RR=0,806; 95% CI: [0,694; 0,936], adjusted p=0,0048);
and that the annualised rate of pulmonary exacerbations is of good clinical relevance;
and the hierarchically ordered secondary endpoints have also shown:
a prolongation of the time to first pulmonary exacerbation of 17,5% versus placebo (HR: 0,825; 95% CI [0,703; 0,968]; adjusted p = 0,0364), with a median value of 50,7 weeks in the brensocatib 25 mg group and 36,7 weeks in the placebo group;
an increase in the percentage of patients without pulmonary exacerbations versus placebo (48,5% versus 40,3%), corresponding to an odds ratio of 1,400 (95% CI [1,095; 1,792], adjusted p=0,0364);
a stabilisation of post-bronchodilator FEV1 of +38 mL (95% CI: [0,011; 0,065], adjusted p <0,0001) versus placebo;
furthermore, the results of the phase II WILLOW study (n = 256 patients) demonstrated the superiority of brensocatib 25 mg over placebo in terms of prolongation of the time to first pulmonary exacerbation. The median time to first pulmonary exacerbation was not reached (90% CI: [NE; NE]) in the brensocatib 25 mg group and was 189 days (90% CI: [141; NE]) in the placebo group.
with acceptable tolerability of brensocatib.
However, the scope of the results of both studies is limited by the following points:
the magnitude of the effect of the difference between the brensocatib 25 mg group and the placebo group is small, and its clinical relevance is not assured on the annualised rate of pulmonary exacerbations (primary endpoint) of the pivotal study;
the absence of demonstration of a reduction in the risk of severe pulmonary exacerbations;
the absence of robust quality-of-life data;
the absence of investigation of an effect on mortality, and of any disease-modifying effect;
a small sample of adolescents (n=40 in the ASPEN study, i.e. 2,3% of the total population), which hardly allows the efficacy and specific tolerability of brensocatib in this population to be assessed, all the more so as selection bias and attrition bias on criteria cannot be ruled out;
the medical need is only partially covered by the available alternatives.
the Committee considers that BRINSUPRI (brensocatib) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators (see 2.2 Current management).

8aaa51c9e86403d1:
Taking into account:
the demonstrated superiority of continuing baricitinib, with or without methotrexate, over switching to placebo in terms of time to flare in children (2 years to < 18 years) with juvenile idiopathic arthritis (extended oligoarticular, enthesitis-related, psoriatic, or polyarticular RF+ or RF-) who had an inadequate response to or intolerance of at least one other conventional or biologic disease-modifying treatment (cDMARD and bDMARD) and were PedACR30 responders at the end of an initial 12-week period of baricitinib treatment;
the safety profile consistent with that established in adult patients;
But also:
the absence of robust data on quality of life and on reduction of disease activity (JADAS score), as well as on structural efficacy;
the absence of comparison with available alternatives, such as anti-TNFs, although a direct comparison was feasible;
the excess risk related to safety (major cardiovascular events, cancer, venous thromboembolism, serious infections, death) compared with anti-TNFs, identified during the PRAC re-evaluation;
the Transparency Committee considers that OLUMIANT 2 mg and 4 mg (baricitinib), film-coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy for polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis (rheumatoid factor-positive [RF+] or negative [RF-] polyarthritis), extended oligoarticular arthritis, enthesitis-related arthritis and juvenile psoriatic arthritis, in patients aged 2 years and over with an inadequate response to or intolerance of one or more disease-modifying treatments (DMARDs), including at least one anti-TNF where possible, which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 5.2.

00348097e73027ea:
Considering:
the consideration of two phase II studies (NP30179 and BiCAR) whose results do not allow conclusions to be drawn on the therapeutic contribution of glofitamab compared with available alternatives in adult patients with DLBCL relapsed or refractory after at least 2 lines of systemic treatment;
the uncertainty regarding the relative efficacy of this treatment given the absence of a direct comparison and the methodological weakness of the indirect comparison provided, in a context where a direct comparison with an available therapeutic alternative using a robust methodology was feasible;
a safety profile notably marked by the frequent occurrence of cytokine release syndromes (CRS), grade 3-4 neutropenias and severe infections;
a partially met medical need, with an interest in having an additional alternative for patients relapsed or refractory after at least two lines of treatment for DLBCL,
the Committee considers that COLUMVI (glofitamab) as monotherapy does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), after at least two lines of systemic treatment and who have failed or are ineligible for CAR-T cell-based medicinal products.

e57ebef42ed46ac2:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of canakinumab over placebo in the CLUSTER study (3 cohorts studied over 4 periods with the same two-component endpoint at the same time point, although this is not appropriate for one of the components, namely resolution of the initial flare, notably for HIDS/MKD and FMF), previously evaluated by the Committee;
the absence of demonstration of prevention of secondary amyloidosis, a major complication liable to threaten vital prognosis, but the difficulty of carrying out this demonstration within the framework of this study;
the medical need in the severe forms of these three periodic fever syndromes;
the new observational data submitted, which do not reveal any misuse of canakinumab in clinical practice;
the absence of a clinical comparison of canakinumab versus anakinra, which does not allow, in the current state of the available data, to position one relative to the other;
the Committee considers that its previous conclusions are not likely to be modified. The Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, ILARIS 150 mg/mL (canakinumab), solution for injection, provides a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the therapeutic management strategy for these three inflammatory syndromes (TRAPS, HIDS/MKD and colchicine-resistant FMF).

c91be5eb8b798559:
Given the demonstration of superiority of LOQTORZI (toripalimab) in combination with gemcitabine + cisplatin followed by maintenance treatment, compared with gemcitabine + cisplatin alone, in a randomised, double-blind phase III study in terms of:
progression-free survival (primary endpoint) with an HR=0,52 (IC95% [0,36 ; 0,74]), (median of 11,7 months [IC95% [11,04 ; NE]) in the toripalimab + G + C group versus 8,0 months (IC95% [6,97 ; 9,53]) in the placebo + G + C group;
overall survival (hierarchically tested secondary endpoint) with an HR= 0,63 [IC95% : 0,446 ; 0,891] and median overall survival not reached in the toripalimab + G + C group versus 33,7 months in the placebo + G + C group;
and despite:
a problem with the transposability of the results to the French population (under-representation of the keratinising nasopharyngeal carcinoma subtype in the pivotal study, which enrolled an exclusively Asian population);
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results (exploratory endpoint);
the safety profile of LOQTORZI (toripalimab), notably marked by a greater number of adverse events (AEs) in the toripalimab group than in the control group, in particular those leading to treatment discontinuation (11,6% versus 4,9%), and immune-mediated AEs (54,1% versus 21,7%),
the Commission considers that LOQTORZI (toripalimab) 240 mg, solution for dilution for infusion, in combination with gemcitabine + cisplatin chemotherapy provides a moderate improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR III) compared with gemcitabine + cisplatin alone as first-line treatment of patients with metastatic or recurrent nasopharyngeal carcinoma not eligible for treatment by surgery or radiotherapy.

acf432ad00b2da21:
Having regard to:
the partially met medical need in the symptomatic treatment of acute HAE attacks in adult and adolescent patients aged 12 years and over;
the results of the phase III KONFIDENT study, randomised, cross-over, double-blind, which demonstrated the superiority of sebetralstat over placebo in the symptomatic treatment of HAE attacks in adults and adolescents ≥ 12 years, according to a hierarchical sequence, on:
time to symptom relief within 12 hours of the first administration of treatment,
time to reduction in severity and time to complete resolution of the attack, respectively within 12 hours and within 24 hours of the first administration of treatment;
the availability of an oral treatment, likely to improve treatment acceptability and to facilitate early self-administration in an emergency setting in educated patients;
a safety profile of sebetralstat that appears overall acceptable, but is notably marked by frequently reported headache and digestive disorders, a risk of QT interval prolongation and drug interactions;
but in view of:
the absence of direct or indirect comparative data allowing confirmation or not of an additional clinical benefit of sebetralstat compared with icatibant or human C1-esterase inhibitors, in the absence of a concomitant development context;
the absence of demonstrated efficacy in laryngeal involvement at the marketing authorisation dose, given the very small number of laryngeal attacks included and the non-eligibility of severe laryngeal attacks for treatment in the pivotal phase III KONFIDENT trial;
the limitations of the oral route in abdominal attacks if associated with a sub-occlusive syndrome (vomiting) or other disorders of drug absorption;
the results of subgroup analyses suggesting a smaller amount of effect of sebetralstat versus placebo in patients on long-term prophylaxis (berotralstat, lanadelumab, C1-inhibitor) than in patients treated on demand only, but with limited transposability given small patient numbers and the absence of an interaction test, not allowing a robust conclusion on the efficacy of sebetralstat in the "long-term prophylaxis" subgroup;
an additional impact compared with comparators:
not demonstrated on morbidity and mortality,
remaining to be established, in the absence of available data, on quality of life and care pathway / care organisation, in view of the practicality of the oral mode of administration;
limited efficacy and safety data in adolescents, due to the small number of patients included;
the lack of long-term follow-up at the marketing authorisation dose;
the Commission considers that EKTERLY 300 mg (sebetralstat), coated tablet, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 2.2.

f6ee8d5a494e29a3:
Given:
the demonstration in the MT-12 trial of the superiority of a standardised house dust mite allergen extract 12 SQ-HDM over placebo, in children aged 5 to 11 years with house dust mite allergic rhinitis after one year of treatment, on the reduction of symptoms and of use of symptomatic rhinitis medication as assessed by the TCRS score (primary endpoint): 3,4 in the treated group versus 4,4 in the placebo group (p < 0,0001);
the demonstration corroborated by the results on the secondary endpoints, components of the composite primary endpoint:
daily rhinitis symptom score (DSS): 1,5 in the treated group versus 1,9 in the placebo group (p < 0,0001),
daily rhinitis medication score (DMS): 1,4 in the treated group versus 1,9 in the placebo group (p = 0,0016),
total combined rhinoconjunctivitis score (TCS): 4,0 in the treated group versus 5,2 in the placebo group (p < 0,0001),
an acceptable tolerability profile, marked mainly by oral and laryngeal irritation effects;
of the demonstration of a reduction in the impact of rhinoconjunctivitis on patients' quality of life, estimated from the PRQLQ score: 0,8 in the treated group versus 1,0 in the placebo group (p < 0,0001);
but also of the following limitations:
the small magnitude of clinical effect estimated by the mean differences in the various scores between the Acariens 12 SQ-HDM group and the placebo group:
1 point on a 24-point scale for the TCRS score,
0,4 point on a 12-point scale for the DSS score,
0,5 point on a 12-point scale for the DMS score,
1,1 points on a 38-point scale for the TCS score;
the small magnitude of effect on improvement in quality of life: 0,2 point difference for the PRQLQ score on a 6-point scale;
the absence of comparative data versus APSI;
insufficient data to assess the optimal duration of treatment;
the absence of long-term efficacy and tolerability data (beyond one year);
the Transparency Committee considers that ACARIZAX 12 SQ-HDM, oral lyophilisate, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in children (aged 5 to 11 years) who, based on a diagnosis combining a suggestive clinical history and a positive test for sensitisation to house dust mites (skin prick test and/or presence of specific IgE), have moderate-to-severe persistent house dust mite allergic rhinitis insufficiently controlled by symptomatic treatments compared with the relevant comparators (APSI).

35e1697d49f9af9a:
The service médical rendu by BRUKINSA 160 mg (zanubrutinib), film-coated tablet is important:
as monotherapy for the treatment of adult patients with marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) who have received at least one prior anti-CD20 antibody-based treatment;
as monotherapy for the treatment of previously untreated adult patients with chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL), only in patients without 17p deletion or TP53 mutation who are ineligible for fludarabine-based treatment, or in the presence of 17p deletion and/or TP53 mutation;
as monotherapy for the treatment of adult patients with chronic lymphocytic leukaemia who have received at least one prior treatment.

21afb971523651b2:
Having regard to:
the demonstration of the superiority of the combination SARCLISA (isatuximab) with bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone in the induction phase (IsaVRd) over the combination of bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone (VRd), in part 1 (induction) of the phase III, randomised (double randomisation), open-label GMMG-HD7 study, on:
the rate of negative MRD at the 10-5 threshold at the end of induction, primary endpoint, with a rate of 50,5 % in the IsaVRd group versus 35,6 % in the VRd group, i.e. an absolute difference of 14,8 percentage points (95% CI [7,32 ; 22,28]);
PFS, counted from the 1st randomisation, independently of the 2nd randomisation (IsaVRd-R or IsaVRd-IsaR versus VRd-R or VRd-IsaR), first hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint, after a median follow-up of 49,31 months, with an HR = 0,70 ; (95% CI [0,52 ; 0,95] ; one-sided p = 0,0113), as well as PFS by induction regimen in patients who received maintenance treatment with lenalidomide alone (IsaVRd-R versus VRd-R), estimated by an inverse probability weighting method, second hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint, with an HR = 0,62 ; (95% CI [0,42 ; 0,91] ; p = 0,0066);
and despite:
the choice of negative MRD rate at the end of induction as primary endpoint, corresponding to an intermediate endpoint assessed at an early stage of patient management, without established demonstration of its validity as a surrogate for a clinically relevant endpoint;
the numerous substantial amendments to the protocol and the statistical analysis plan, some of which were made after the end of enrolment;
the open-label design of the study;
the presence of an imbalance in the nature of events observed between the groups for PFS counted from the first randomisation, with a cumulative incidence of death without progression higher in the IsaVRd group (34,7 %) than in the VRd group (12,8 %), p = 0,038, Gray's test;
the comparator (VRd) not being the most clinically relevant in light of current practice, as this regimen is little used in routine clinical practice;
the impossibility of quantifying the contribution of this combination relative to the DVRd and DVTd regimens due to concurrent development;
the absence of demonstrated benefit on overall survival counted from the first randomisation;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life data;
the safety profile marked by a high frequency of grade ≥ 3 adverse events, broadly comparable between groups (63,6 % in the IsaVRd group versus 61,3 % in the VRd group), with nevertheless a higher proportion of neutropenia in the IsaVRd group (16,1 % versus 4,0 %);
the Transparency Committee considers that SARCLISA 20 mg/ml (isatuximab), concentrate for solution for infusion, in combination with bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone in the induction phase (IsaVRd regimen), provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with the combination of bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone as induction treatment (VRd regimen).

772146fe430213c2:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of BRUKINSA 160 mg (zanubrutinib), film-coated tablet, is insufficient to justify reimbursement by the national solidarity scheme:
as monotherapy in first-line treatment of adult patients with Waldenström's macroglobulinaemia (WM) who are ineligible for chemo-immunotherapy;
as monotherapy for the treatment of adult patients with previously untreated chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL), in patients without 17p deletion and/or TP53 mutation and eligible for fludarabine-based treatment;
in the treatment of adult patients with relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma who have received at least two prior systemic treatments.

e863a118613b4205:
Given:
non-comparative efficacy data from one of two cohorts of a non-comparative study that enrolled 28 paediatric patients with Ph+ CML in chronic phase, who had failed prior treatment including imatinib, dasatinib and nilotinib, and who were treated with different doses of bosutinib;
the unmet medical need in these patients;
the absence of data on long-term efficacy and tolerability, with median follow-up durations ranging from 14 to 33 months across all patients;
the absence of robust data on overall quality of life;
the Transparency Committee considers that BOSULIF (bosutinib) 50 and 100 mg, capsules, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the treatment of paediatric patients aged 6 years and over with Ph+ CML, previously treated with one or more tyrosine kinase inhibitors and for whom imatinib, nilotinib and dasatinib are not considered appropriate treatments.

f9a976dbc7c18cb9:
Given:
the uncertainties regarding the additional benefit of this treatment relative to the available alternatives, considering the absence of comparative data in patients previously treated with a single TKI;
the medical need partially covered by the existence of second-line treatment alternatives;
the Transparency Committee considers that, in the current state of the data, SCEMBLIX (asciminib) 20 and 40 mg, film-coated tablets, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the treatment of patients with Ph+ CML-CP previously treated with a single TKI.

