f7de63cfe69e1341:
Given:
the implementation of standardisation norms and procedures established by the ERS/ATS international recommendations based on a reference method for measuring DLCO;
the recommendations published by the SPLF and the HAS, including the PNDS, relating to the use of DLCO in various clinical indications within the framework of respiratory function tests (EFR);
the data available in the literature confirming the value of DLCO for assessing severity and prognosis, particularly in interstitial lung diseases, COPD, pulmonary hypertension and lung cancer;
the well-established use of these gases in clinical practice for several decades;
the non-invasive, painless, short-duration nature of a DLCO measurement examination;
the absence of tolerance signals as stated in the product's SmPC (RCP);
But also the following limitations:
the lack of direct or indirect comparative data on the various gases used for the assessment of DLCO;
the data from the literature and provided by the laboratory present significant methodological biases:
small sample sizes;
highly selected patient populations;
the non-randomised or retrospective nature of studies, which may lead to various methodological biases, including confounding and selection biases;
the absence of evidence on the tolerance of gases in diagnostic use;
The Committee considers that AELIORA does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with other medical gases for diagnostic use in respiratory function tests (EFR) (mainly for measuring lung diffusing capacity and, incidentally, for measuring lung volume).

11723bb4b02d486b:
Given:
the demonstration of non-inferiority of methenamine hippurate compared with antibiotic prophylaxis (trimethoprim or nitrofurantoin or cefalexin) on the incidence of symptomatic urinary tract infections over 12 months;
the non-specific antiseptic activity of methenamine hippurate, subject to maintenance of an acidic urinary pH, administration in two doses of the product, with a potential rebound effect reported in the literature after discontinuation of the medicinal product, the conditions of discontinuation of which are not defined, the uncertainty regarding the resistance profile, and the absence of robust data on a possible impact on reducing antibiotic resistance;
the Committee considers that ALTAROMIN 1 g (methenamine hippurate) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy for prophylaxis of recurrent cystitis solely in adult women (with the exception of pregnant women).

dd90768f716b634c:
Given:
the initial data already considered by the Transparency Committee on:
vaccine efficacy (VE) demonstrated with live attenuated vaccines in reducing the incidence of laboratory-confirmed influenza cases (VE of 78 %, 95% CI [59 ; 89]) and influenza-like illness (VE of 31 %, 95% CI [20 ; 40]) from the 2018 Cochrane review;
the demonstrated superiority of the trivalent live attenuated nasal vaccine over the trivalent inactivated vaccine in terms of reducing the incidence of laboratory-confirmed influenza: influenza RR (inactivated versus live attenuated) = 0,52, 95% CI [0,22 ; 0,82] (meta-analysis by Minozzi et al. 2022);
new data available since the CT's last review in February 2025:
two meta-analyses covering LAIV vaccine efficacy, including that of Stuurman 2025 of good methodological quality, which suggested considerable variability in efficacy estimates, depending on season and viral subtype, making it difficult to identify a robust clinical benefit of LAIV over inactivated vaccines;
recent observational studies, notably Kitano 2026, which did not demonstrate a clinical superiority of the live attenuated vaccine over inactivated vaccines, despite favourable results depending on year and country;
the impact yet to be demonstrated on:
vaccination coverage in France, these data not being transposable from one country to another given the different circulating strains on the one hand and vaccination programmes on the other;
the reduction in the incidence of hospitalisations and mortality, and without sufficient clinical evidence to differentiate the trivalent influenza vaccines available (inactivated or live attenuated vaccines);
the possible interest of a trivalent live attenuated nasal influenza vaccine regarding its better acceptability in children owing to the absence of needles, which could have a potential positive impact on vaccination coverage;
the potential risk of transmission of the vaccine influenza virus, with a peak incidence of vaccine virus shedding 2 to 3 days after vaccination according to clinical studies, in situations where contact with severely immunocompromised patients cannot be avoided,
the Commission considers that, pending the data requested in its opinion of 26 March 2025, FLUENZ (trivalent live attenuated nasal influenza vaccine) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with other vaccines recommended for the prevention of seasonal influenza in children aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, with and without comorbidities, in accordance with the current HAS recommendations (opinion of 12 December 2024).

