f46f5b0559630e04:
Having regard to:
updated data from the phase III ZUMA-7 study, at the new data cut-off for the final overall survival (OS) analysis (median follow-up of 47,2 months, i.e. 22,3 additional months), with a statistically significant superiority in OS in favour of YESCARTA (HR = 0,73 ; 95% CI [0,54 ; 0,98] ; p = 0,0168) compared with standard of care (salvage chemotherapy, possibly followed, in case of response, by intensification and autologous stem cell transplantation);
updated data from the non-comparative ALYCANTE study with a median follow-up of 25,4 months and a complete metabolic response rate at 3 months of 71 % (95% CI [58,1 % ; 81,8 %]), similar to the previous 2023 assessment;
additional follow-up on real-world data for YESCARTA (axicabtagene ciloleucel) from the French DESCAR-T registry (34 active centres, 876 patients enrolled, median follow-up of 10,6 months from eligibility);
and this, despite:
in the ZUMA-7 study:
a statistically significant superiority in OS in favour of YESCARTA, confirming the magnitude of the effect estimated at the previous data cut-off but still with an upper bound of the confidence interval close to 1;
a non-negligible proportion of patients in the standard-of-care arm having received CAR-T therapy at third line after progression or insufficient response (57 % of patients in the standard group received subsequent treatment with CAR-T cells);
uncertainties regarding the transposability of the results due to the absence of bridging therapy (other than corticosteroids);
in the DESCAR-T registry:
descriptive data, with short-term follow-up at the data extraction of 17 February 2025 (median follow-up from eligibility of 10,6 months [95% CI: 9,2 ; 12,9]), whereas early access was granted in July 2022;
in the context of comparative data available from a randomised controlled trial (ZUMA-7 study) with a median follow-up of 47,2 months;
and which present substantial missing data (up to approximately 20% for certain patient and disease characteristics, and beyond that depending on the variables recorded) and a registry completeness that is not assured;
the Committee maintains a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) for YESCARTA (axicabtagene ciloleucel), 0,4 – 2 x 108 cells, dispersion for infusion, in the management of adult patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) and high-grade B-cell lymphoma (HGBCL) who relapsed within 12 months after the end of first-line chemoimmunotherapy, or who are refractory to it.

8c2d058723775248:
Having regard to:
updated data from the phase III TRANSFORM study, at the new data cut-off for the final overall survival analysis (median follow-up of 33,9 months, i.e. 16,4 additional months), showing no statistically significant difference (HR=0,76 ; IC95% [0,48 ; 1,19]) for BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel) in overall survival compared with standard treatment (salvage chemotherapy possibly followed, in case of response, by intensification and autologous stem cell transplantation);
updated data from two non-comparative studies, TRANSCEND PILOT and TRANSCEND-WORLD, with median follow-up of 18,2 months and 16,8 months respectively, which showed overall response rates of 80,3 % (IC95% : 68,2 % - 89,4 %) and 63 % (IC95% : 42,4 % - 80,6 %), similar to the previous 2023 assessment;
additional follow-up on real-world data for BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel), mainly from the French DESCAR-T registry (16 active centres, 259 patients enrolled, median follow-up of 5,7 months from eligibility);
and despite:
in the TRANSFORM study:
a non-negligible proportion of patients in the standard-treatment arm who received a CAR T-cell therapy as third-line treatment after progression or insufficient response (protocol switch 63 %);
in the DESCAR-T registry:
descriptive data, with short-term follow-up at data extraction on 17 février 2025 (median follow-up from eligibility of 5,7 months [IC95% : 5,3 ; 7,3], whereas early access was granted in septembre 2022);
in the context of comparative data available from a randomised controlled trial (TRANSFORM study) with median follow-up of 33,9 months;
and which present substantial missing data (up to approximately 20 % for patient and disease characteristics, and more for other recorded variables) and a registry whose completeness is not assured;
indirect comparisons that do not permit formal conclusions to be drawn;
the Transparency Committee maintains a moderate improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR III) for BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel), 1,1-70x10^6 cellules/mL / 1,1-70x10^6 cellules/mL, dispersion pour perfusion, in the management of adult patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (LDGCB), high-grade B-cell lymphoma (LHGCB), primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma (LMPGCB) or grade 3B follicular lymphoma (LF3B), relapsing within 12 months of completing first-line immunochemotherapy or refractory to this first-line treatment.

d88eeabaddb251b2:
Given:
the superiority of cytisinicline over placebo, reported by the meta-analysis of Ofori et al 2023, in adult daily smokers wishing to quit smoking, on biochemically confirmed continuous abstinence at 6 months (abstinence rate of 24,8 % vs 19,7 %; RR = 2, 25 (IC95% = [1,13 ; 4,47])) and on abstinence over the longest follow-up period (25,6 % vs 14,6 %; RR = 2,78 (IC95% = [1,64 ; 4,70]));
a tolerability profile that appears more favourable compared with varenicline;
But:
the non-optimal level of evidence of the demonstration (substantial heterogeneity of results and GRADE level of evidence low to moderate according to the authors);
the absence of demonstration of superiority or non-inferiority versus varenicline;
the medical need partially covered by the existence of an alternative (varenicline) having demonstrated efficacy on the 6-month abstinence rate with a high level of evidence;
the Commission considers that LIBEROSTIN (cytisinicline) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy.

9bd87f6d62591c00:
Having regard to:
the demonstration in a phase III study (ASTRO) of the superiority of guselkumab over placebo as SC induction treatment, with a moderate magnitude of effect on clinical remission in the overall study population;
the methodological quality of the phase III study (controlled, randomised, double-blind, based on relevant endpoints, although quality of life was assessed only as an exploratory endpoint, and including appropriately sized samples); however, the use of a placebo is regrettable, particularly in patients naive to any biologic treatment, efficacy having been assessed in a heterogeneous population including both biologic-naive and non-biologic-naive patients;
the absence of clinical data comparing intravenous and subcutaneous guselkumab as induction treatment of UC in adults;
the absence of comparative data versus anti-TNFα agents in patients with an inadequate response to conventional background therapy and naive to anti-TNFα agents, and versus vedolizumab (ENTYVIO), although these comparisons were feasible;
and the good safety profile of guselkumab,
the Transparency Committee considers that TREMFYA 200 mg, solution for injection in pre-filled syringe or pre-filled pen (guselkumab) as SC induction treatment of UC does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the current therapeutic strategy.

c1ede6348ae7eff9:
Given:
the demonstration of superiority of mirvetuximab soravtansine over single-agent chemotherapy, in a randomised, open-label phase III study, in terms of:
progression-free survival in the ITT population: HR = 0,65; 95% CI [0,52; 0,81]; p < 0,0001; with a median progression-free survival of 5.62 months in the mirvetuximab soravtansine group versus 3.98 months in the chemotherapy group;
overall survival in the ITT population: HR = 0,68; 95% CI [0,54; 0,84], with a median overall survival of 16,85 months in the mirvetuximab soravtansine group versus 13,34 months in the chemotherapy group. At 33 months, 36 months and 40 months, the restricted mean survival time (RMST) differences are +3,29 months, +3,64 months and +4,11 months respectively;
the absence of demonstrated superiority on abdominal/gastrointestinal symptoms;
the absence of any formal conclusion that can be drawn from the quality-of-life results;
the safety profile of mirvetuximab soravtansine, considered acceptable compared with single-agent chemotherapy but marked in particular by a high incidence of ocular adverse events. This risk is qualified by the EMA as an important potential risk in the risk management plan for this medicine;
the Committee considers that ELAHERE 5 mg/mL (mirvetuximab soravtansine), concentrate for solution for infusion, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with single-agent chemotherapy.

40237a0111629351:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) is important in:
the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants with or without risk factors as defined by national recommendations, and not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season,
the marketing authorisation (AMM) indication in the prevention of severe RSV-related lower respiratory tract infections requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection, and eligible for palivizumab, during their first and second RSV circulation seasons:
Children born at 35 weeks gestational age or less and under 6 months old at the start of the seasonal RSV epidemic;
Children under 2 years old who required treatment for bronchopulmonary dysplasia in the last 6 months;
Children under 2 years old with haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease.

022433d58cafbc08:
Having regard to:
statistically significant improvement (p<0,001) in favour of the tézépélumab group at week 52 on the 2 co-primary endpoints and on the first 5 hierarchically ranked secondary endpoints;
mean reduction in the NPS score (primary endpoint) of -2,46 points in the tézépélumab group versus -0,38 in the placebo group, i.e. a difference of –2,08; 95 % CI (–2,40 ; –1,76) [co-primary endpoint];
reduction in the NCS score (primary endpoint) of –1,74 in the tézépélumab group versus –0,70 with placebo, corresponding to a mean difference of –1,04; 95 % CI (-1,21 ; -0,87) [co-primary endpoint];
significant improvement in loss of smell, with a mean weekly change in the "difficulty with smell" item of the NPSD of –1,01, 95 % CI (-1,18 ; -0,83) in favour of the tézépélumab group;
improvement in quality of life measured by the SNOT-22 score, with a mean difference of –27,44 versus the placebo group, 95 % CI (–32,51 ; –22,37);
decrease in the LMK score of –5,70, 95 % CI (–6,37 ; –5,03), in favour of the tézépélumab group;
use of surgery and/or CSS, which is significantly reduced in the tézépélumab group compared with the placebo group: HR = 0,08, 95 % CI (0,03 ; 0,16), with 5,7 % of patients in the tézépélumab group having required it versus 31,4 % in the placebo group;
improvement in the total NPSD score with a difference of –6,96, 95 % CI (–8,09 ; –5,83); of the clinical relevance of these endpoints and of the results showing a large effect size versus placebo;
of the acceptable safety profile of tézépélumab in nasal polyposis (PNS), similar to that in its approved indication for severe asthma.
However, the scope of these results is limited by the following:
results obtained in the marketing authorisation (AMM) population, and not in the population for which reimbursement is sought (treatment of severe PNS when treatment with systemic corticosteroids AND surgery does not provide satisfactory disease control);
absence of comparative data versus dupilumab and mepolizumab, which are indicated in a similar indication;
uncertainty about the optimal duration of treatment in the absence of long-term efficacy and tolerability data;
a high number of major protocol amendments concerning the definition or analysis of the co-primary and secondary endpoints, made in response to FDA requests;
a higher number in the control group of patients with polyp surgery or post-randomisation systemic corticosteroid use, and missing data leading to imputations using different methods, some assigning the minimum score value, which may penalise it while reducing variability in the size of the effect;
unknowns regarding predictors of response to treatment, the possible efficacy of combining surgery with tézépélumab, and the risk of therapeutic escape;
the medical need is only partially covered by available alternatives;
the Transparency Committee considers that TEZSPIRE (tézépélumab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators (see 2.2 Current management).

881f4068d4073da7:
Given:
the unmet medical need in preventing RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease in individuals aged 75 years and older, and in individuals aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory disease (particularly COPD) or cardiac disease (particularly heart failure);
the demonstration of superiority of the mRESVIA vaccine over placebo in 35 088 subjects (study P301) in reducing RSV-LRTD with ≥ 2 symptoms and RSV-LRTD with ≥ 3 symptoms (vaccine efficacy (VE) decreasing from 83,7 % IC95% =[66,0 ; 92,2] to 44,3 %, IC95% = [32,4 ; 54,1] and from 82,4 % IC95% = [34,8 ; 95,3] to 50,8 %, IC95% = [33,2 ; 63,8] between the primary analysis (median follow-up of 3,7 months) and the final analysis (median follow-up 24,1 months);
a favourable tolerability profile for the mRESVIA vaccine, marked by adverse events (AEs) that are mostly grade 1 (mild) or grade 2 (moderate), injection-site pain (55,9 %), fatigue (30,8 %), headache (26,7 %), myalgia (25,6 %) and arthralgia (21,7 %);
But:
the absence of demonstration, in the mRNA P301 study, of vaccine efficacy in subjects aged 80 years and older for preventing over 24 months the first episode of RSV-LRTD with ≥ 2 symptoms (VE = 24,5 % [IC95% : -37,1 ; 58,4]) and RSV-LRTD with ≥ 3 symptoms (VE = 40,3 % [IC95% : -64,2 ; 78,3]). Vaccine efficacy in subjects aged 70 to 79 years is, however, 50,1 % [28,6 ; 65,2] and 62,6 % [30,8 ; 79,8] respectively for the reduction of RSV-LRTD with ≥ 2 symptoms and RSV-LRTD with ≥ 3 symptoms (final analysis; median follow-up 24,1 months);
a decrease in vaccine efficacy in frail and vulnerable subjects with a frailty score of 4 or more, at 24 months, with VE on RSV-LRTD with ≥ 2 symptoms of 48 % [IC95% : 10,9 ; 69,7] and on RSV-LRTD with ≥ 3 symptoms of 41,3 % [IC95% : -23,4 ; 72,1]);
limited real-world data in the populations at highest risk of decompensation during an RSV infection (subjects aged 75 years and older and subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory disease (particularly COPD) or cardiac disease (particularly heart failure)), with only one test-negative design case-control study having reported a VE against hospitalisations in subjects aged 50 years and older of 87 % [IC95% : 47 ; 97], with the limitations inherent to this type of study reducing their level of evidence;
the absence of data in immunocompromised subjects (SmPC change under evaluation by the EMA);
the Committee considers that, given the current state of the data, mRESVIA (Messenger RNA Respiratory Syncytial Virus Vaccine (nucleoside-modified)) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in active immunisation for the prevention of lower respiratory tract disease caused by RSV in subjects aged 75 years and older and in subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory disease (particularly COPD) or cardiac disease (particularly heart failure) at risk of decompensation during an RSV infection.

7a5a2f12c8c13669:
Given:
the demonstration, in a randomised, double-blind phase III study, of superiority of GAZYVARO (obinutuzumab) in combination with standard treatment (including corticosteroids and MMF) versus placebo (standard treatment alone), on complete renal response (RRC) at week 76 (18 months), on RRC with prednisone-equivalent dose ≤ 7,5 mg/j between S64 and S76, and on response in terms of proteinuria, in the treatment of adult patients with active class III or IV lupus glomerulonephritis, ± associated class V;
of a medical need that remains partially covered by available alternatives;
but in view of:
a low quality of the demonstration, due to poorly documented histological data at baseline (activity and chronicity scores unknown), heterogeneity of the included population, a debated optimisation of standard treatment, and the absence of direct comparison with other combination therapies (tacrolimus, in the absence of concomitant development; or belimumab, given concomitant development);
a modest magnitude of effect, without complete renal response being achieved for the majority of patients, and associated with uncertainties about the true size of the benefit (lower than the effect size planned in the study sample size calculation, imprecision of confidence intervals, possible dilution of the effect linked to the two different treatment regimens combined in the obinutuzumab group, debated optimisation of standard treatment, impact of missing data);
the absence of a significant difference in mean change in eGFR (DFGe) at 18 months versus the control group, the absence of robust demonstration of benefit on hard long-term renal endpoints (sustained eGFR decline, end-stage renal disease, dialysis and/or transplantation), and the absence of assessment of lupus nephritis flares;
uncertainties about efficacy in the presence of a history of lupus nephropathy or eGFR (DFGe) between 30 and 60 ml/min/1,73 m2 at baseline, based on exploratory, non-stratified subgroup analyses with small numbers and without interaction tests, which do not allow any robust conclusion on the efficacy of obinutuzumab in these subpopulations, together with the limits of using an endpoint based on proteinuric response because of possible residual chronic lesions;
the absence of robust data on quality of life: FACIT-F score (last key secondary endpoint of the interrupted hierarchical testing sequence), exploratory;
the absence of robust data on medium- and long-term efficacy and tolerability, having evaluated the administration regimen beyond 18 months;
a safety profile including serious adverse events that may be life-threatening, in particular infectious complications, neutropenias and infusion-related reactions; with long-term uncertainties linked to profound and prolonged B-cell depletion (long-term safety included in the RMP risk table as missing information, extension phase of the REGENCY study ongoing in connection with additional pharmacovigilance activities required by the EMA);
a debated transposability of the therapeutic effects to our health system (recruitment mainly in Latin America and the Caribbean);
the Transparency Committee considers that GAZYVARO 1 000 mg (obinutuzumab), solution to be diluted for infusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators cited in paragraph 2.2.

27995141a24cd3f8:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of ENFLONSIA (clesrovimab) is important in:
the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants with or without risk factors as defined by national recommendations, and not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season,
the marketing authorisation indication in the prevention of severe lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection, and eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season:
Children born at 35 weeks of gestational age or less and under 6 months of age at the start of the seasonal RSV epidemic;
Children under 2 years of age who have required treatment for bronchopulmonary dysplasia in the past 6 months;
Children under 2 years of age with haemodynamically significant heart disease.

55087946edb43104:
Considering:
the medical need for prevention of lower respiratory tract infections caused by RSV (RSV-LRTI, IVRI-VRS) in newborns and infants, particularly in terms of reducing hospitalisations and associated complications;
the data already considered by the Transparency Committee (CT opinions of 19 July 2023 and 23 October 2024):
the demonstration of superiority of nirsevimab (BEYFORTUS) over placebo in low-risk subjects, in terms of reducing the incidence of RSV-LRTI within 150 days after administration, with a statistically significant relative risk reduction in the phase IIb studies (2,6 % versus 9,5 %, i.e. an RRR versus placebo = 70,1 % [52,3 ; 81,2]) and in the phase III MELODY study (1,2 % versus 5,0 % (25/496), RRR versus placebo = 74,5 % [49,6 ; 87,1]);
an expected effect on RSV-related hospitalisations, but with heterogeneous results (significant in phase IIb, not significant in MELODY);
the result of the open-label pragmatic HARMONIE study, suggesting a reduction in hospitalisations versus no intervention (0,3 % versus 1,6 %, RRR = 84,0 % [69,5 ; 92,4]);
a safety profile of nirsevimab (BEYFORTUS) characterised by adverse events (AEs) mostly of grade 1–2 (mild to moderate), such as upper respiratory tract infections, nasopharyngitis, pyrexia or gastroenteritis, and AEs of special interest such as rash reported in clinical studies and consistent with the information in the SmPC, as well as the absence of any important identified or potential risk within its European risk management plan (RMP) to date;
the monotherapy use and the long half-life of nirsevimab, which may raise concerns about selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacological properties of the SmPC);
the simplicity of its use through single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of nirsevimab);
French observational data (case-control studies), suggesting a relative risk reduction for hospitalisation for RSV bronchiolitis of between 73 % [61 ; 84] and 83,0 % [73,4 ; 89,2] in favour of nirsevimab (Institut Pasteur and ENVIE study), and a relative risk reduction for RSV bronchiolitis cases admitted to intensive care units of 75,9 % [48,5 ; 88,7] (Santé Publique France study);
new international and French observational data from the French National Health Data System (SNDS), suggesting a difference in hospitalisations for RSV-LRTI in favour of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) versus ABRYSVO (respiratory syncytial virus vaccine (bivalent, recombinant)), with a relative risk reduction of 22 % in the Valtuille 2026 study and 26 % in the EPI-PHARE – Jabagi 2026 study; although small in terms of absolute risk reduction in both studies, respectively -0,13 % and -0,3 %;
the absence of robust data on severity endpoints (length of hospitalisation, intensive care use, mortality);
the lack of follow-up on long-term impact (over several seasons) regarding the course of respiratory tract infections in populations treated during their first years of life, and the reduction of RSV-related complications;
the Committee considers that BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) retains a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the prevention of RSV lower respiratory tract infections in newborns and infants with or without risk factors who are not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season.

c4d1eac8d4008529:
Having regard to:
the partially met medical need in the prevention of IVRI-VRS (RSV lower respiratory tract infection) in newborns and infants;
the data already reviewed by the Transparency Committee (CT opinions of 19 juillet 2023 and 23 octobre 2024):
the absence of demonstrated superiority or non-inferiority of nirsévimab versus palivizumab (the clinically relevant comparator in infants at high risk of severe forms) in terms of clinical efficacy, although this comparison was feasible;
the limited clinical data in patients eligible for palivizumab, based on the MEDLEY study, whose objective was to determine the safety and pharmacokinetic profile of nirsévimab versus palivizumab in high-risk infants (premature infants or those with chronic lung disease or haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease);
the efficacy of nirsévimab in infants at higher risk of severe RSV infection, extrapolated from the efficacy of nirsévimab in studies D5290C00003 and MELODY (primary cohort) on the basis of pharmacokinetic exposure;
the safety profile of nirsévimab, after a first and/or second season of RSV exposure, considered acceptable and comparable to that of palivizumab; the adverse events were predominantly grade 1–2 (mild to moderate), mainly ENT infections, nasopharyngitis, fever and gastroenteritis, with skin rashes consistent with the SmPC; however, a higher frequency of serious AEs was observed in the nirsévimab-exposed groups: 12,8 % (NIRS/NIRS) and 10,0 % (PALI/NIRS) versus 4,8 % (PALI/PALI) in the MEDLEY study;
the absence of robust data on severity endpoints (duration of hospitalisation, use of intensive care, mortality);
the use in monotherapy and the long half-life of nirsévimab, which may raise concerns about selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacological properties of the SmPC);
the simplicity of use through single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of nirsévimab);
the absence of new data supporting an impact in this population of interest;
the Committee considers that BEYFORTUS (nirsévimab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the prevention of severe RSV-related lower respiratory tract infections requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection and eligible for palivizumab, during their first and second RSV circulation seasons.

958232411bed2e10:
Having regard to:
the partially met medical need in the prevention of RSV lower respiratory tract infections (IVRI-VRS) in newborns and infants;
the absence of demonstration of superiority or non-inferiority of clesrovimab versus palivizumab (the clinically relevant comparator in infants at high risk of severe disease) in terms of clinical efficacy, although this comparison is feasible;
the limited clinical data in patients eligible for palivizumab, based on the SMART study, whose objective was to determine the safety and pharmacokinetic profile of clesrovimab versus palivizumab in high-risk infants (preterm infants, or infants with chronic lung disease or hemodynamically significant congenital heart disease);
the efficacy of clesrovimab in infants at higher risk of severe RSV infection is extrapolated from the efficacy of clesrovimab in the CLEVER study on the basis of pharmacokinetic exposure;
the safety profile of clesrovimab appears comparable to that of palivizumab. The incidence of serious adverse events was comparable (22,2 % versus 24,4 %), deaths were more frequent (1,8 % versus 0,9 %), but no serious adverse event and no death was considered related to treatment;
the absence of robust data on severity criteria (length of hospital stay, intensive care use, mortality);
the monotherapy use and long half-life of clesrovimab may raise concerns about the selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacological properties of the SmPC (RCP)), as with nirsevimab;
the simplicity of use through single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of clesrovimab);
the Transparency Committee considers that ENFLONSIA (clesrovimab) does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) in the prevention of severe RSV-related lower respiratory tract infections requiring hospitalisation in newborns and infants at high risk of RSV infection who are eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season.

435608068c86bdd3:
Having regard to:

the medical need for prevention of lower respiratory tract infections caused by RSV (RSV-LRTI) in newborns and infants, particularly in terms of reducing hospitalisations and associated complications;

the magnitude of effect of ENFLONSIA (clesrovimab) in reducing the incidence of RSV-LRTI within 150 days after administration in newborns and infants at low risk of severe RSV infection (no comorbidities such as immunodeficiency and not eligible for palivizumab, these being the exclusion criteria of the clinical studies), which was statistically significant in the CLEVER study: 2,5 % (60 of 2 398) of infants in the clesrovimab group versus 6,2 % (74 of 1 201) of infants in the placebo group, corresponding to a relative risk reduction of 60,4 % (IC95 % [44, ; 71,9]);

the magnitude of effect in terms of reduction in the incidence of hospitalisations related to RSV-LRTI in the CLEVER study (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint): 0,38 % (9 of 2 398) infants versus 2,33 % (28 of 1 201) infants in the placebo group within 150 days after administration, corresponding to a relative risk reduction of 84,2 % (IC95 % [66,6 ; 92,6]);

the absence of robust data on severity endpoints (length of hospital stay, intensive care use, mortality);

a safety profile of ENFLONSIA (clesrovimab) characterised mainly by adverse events of grade 1 (mild) or grade 2 (moderate), such as upper respiratory tract infections (14,8 % versus 15,7 %), irritability (21,5 % versus 23,1 %) and somnolence (13,1 % versus 14,8 %) in the CLEVER study, and by adverse events of special interest such as rash reported in the clinical studies and consistent with the information in the SmPC, as well as the absence of any important identified or potential risk to date under its European risk management plan;

the monotherapy use and long half-life of clesrovimab, which may raise concerns about selection of resistant variants (see section 5.1 Pharmacodynamic properties of the SmPC);

the simplicity of use, with single-dose intramuscular administration (long half-life of clesrovimab);

the lack of long-term perspective (across several seasons) on the impact on the evolution of respiratory tract infections in populations treated during their first years of life, and on the reduction of RSV-related complications;

the Transparency Committee considers that ENFLONSIA (clesrovimab) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in newborns and infants with or without risk factors and not eligible for palivizumab, during their first RSV circulation season.

3ec18af8eb3683a4:
Having regard to:

the insufficiently covered medical need in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in subjects aged 75 years and older and in subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions;
the demonstration of superiority of the AREXVY vaccine over placebo in 24 966 subjects (study RSV OA=ADJ-006) in terms of reduction of RT-PCR-confirmed RSV-associated LRTD over 3 seasons (VE of 62,91 % [46,74 ; 74,79], 15 days after administration of a single dose of vaccine, primary endpoint) and in terms of reduction of severe RSV-associated LRTD (VE of 67,38 % (95% CI [42,43 ; 82,68]), secondary endpoint);
vaccine efficacy against deaths, hospitalisations and emergency department visits, reported by real-world studies and estimated between 61,6% [95% CI: 9,1 ; 83,7] and 83 % [95% CI: 73 ; 89] across all studies, in subjects aged 75 years and older, subjects aged 65 years and older with comorbidities and immunocompromised subjects (vaccine efficacy reduced in immunocompromised subjects);
a tolerability profile for the AREXVY vaccine that remains favourable, marked by adverse events mostly of grade 1 (mild) or 2 (moderate), of the type pain (60,9 %), fatigue (33,6 %) and myalgia (28,9 %); despite a signal for Guillain-Barré syndrome (added to the list of very rare adverse events);
However:
the non-optimal level of evidence from real-world vaccine efficacy data on hospitalisations and emergency department visits, due to the methodological limitations inherent in the design of these observational studies (mainly test-negative design case-control studies, but also the matched exposed-unexposed cohort study);
the Transparency Committee considers that AREXVY (recombinant respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) vaccine, with adjuvant) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in active immunisation for the prevention of lower respiratory tract disease (LRTD) due to respiratory syncytial virus in subjects aged 75 years and older and subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions liable to decompensate during an RSV infection.

ab2e6be90d705296:
Considering:
the medical need for prevention of lower respiratory tract infections caused by RSV (RSV-LRTI) in newborns and infants, particularly in terms of reducing hospitalisations and associated complications;
the initial data already considered by the Transparency Committee (CT opinion of 10 July 2024):
the demonstration of superiority of the ABRYSVO (RSVpreF) vaccine over placebo in 6,975 infants born to mothers vaccinated during pregnancy, in terms of reduction of severe RSV-LRTI cases up to D180 (0.5% in the ABRYSVO (RSVpreF) group vs 1.8% in the placebo group), i.e. a VE of 69.4% [44.3; 84.1];
a non-significant reduction in the incidence of RSV-LRTI cases (co-primary endpoint): 0.7% in the ABRYSVO (RSVpreF) group versus 1.6% in the placebo group, i.e. a vaccine efficacy (VE) of 57.1% [14.7; 79.8]; statistical significance was not retained because the lower bound of the CI was below 20% within the 90 days following birth;
a reduction in hospitalisations (secondary endpoint): VE of 67.7%, CI 99.17% = [15.9; 89.5] reported at D90 after birth, before decreasing over time to reach values of 59.5% [8.3; 83.7], 56.4% [5.2; 81.5], 56.8% [10.1; 80.7], 33.3% [-17.6; 62.9] at D120, D150, D180 and D360 after birth respectively;
the tolerability profile of the ABRYSVO (RSVpreF) vaccine, characterised by adverse events (AEs) mostly of grade 1 (mild) or grade 2 (moderate), namely pain at the injection site (41%), headache (31%) and myalgia (27%). However, continued pharmacovigilance monitoring remains necessary because of a potential excess risk of preterm birth, although the data are reassuring within the scope of the current HAS vaccination recommendations (between 32 and 36 weeks of gestation);
new data available:
the results, for descriptive purposes, of the final analysis of the MATISSE study, which confirmed a relative reduction in the risk of severe forms of RSV-LRTI in infants up to 180 days after birth: VE of 81.8% (CI 99.5% [40.6 – 96.3]) at 90 days and VE of 69.4% (CI 97.58% [44.3 – 84.1]) at 180 days;
French observational studies from the French National Health Data System (SNDS) suggesting:
no significant difference on the endpoints (including preterm births and intrauterine fetal deaths) in favour of an excess risk of events in women vaccinated from 33–36 weeks of gestation, corresponding to the recommended period (EPI-PHARE study – Gabet 2026);
a difference in hospitalisations for RSV-LRTI in favour of BEYFORTUS (nirsevimab) versus ABRYSVO (bivalent, recombinant respiratory syncytial virus vaccine), with a relative risk reduction of 22% in the Valtuille 2026 study and 26% in the EPI-PHARE – Jabagi 2026 study; although small in terms of absolute risk reduction in both studies, namely -0.13% and -0.3% respectively;
new international observational data suggesting a vaccine efficacy against RSV-related hospitalisations for LRTI estimated between 58% (95% CI: 28–75) and 83.7% (95% CI: 78.6–87.6), across all studies, in infants under 6 months of age hospitalised for LRTI;
the absence of robust data on severity criteria (length of hospital stay, admission to intensive care, mortality);
the value of having a protection that is not based on a single monoclonal antibody as an alternative to immunisation at birth with a monoclonal antibody;
the lack of long-term follow-up data on the impact of this strategy (in particular because of the known risk of Guillain-Barré syndrome in elderly persons);
the Committee considers that ABRYSVO (bivalent, recombinant respiratory syncytial virus vaccine) retains a minor improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in the active immunisation of pregnant women to protect infants, from birth up to 6 months of age, against lower respiratory tract disease caused by respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) only between 32 and 36 weeks of gestation.

0dfe76b2f734e6f6:
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab) in neoadjuvant treatment, then in adjuvant (post-surgery) treatment in combination with radiotherapy with or without cisplatin followed by monotherapy, at the data cut-off of 25/07/2024, versus adjuvant radiotherapy alone or combined with cisplatin chemotherapy, on event-free survival and major histological response rate in a randomised open-label phase III study;
the absence of demonstrated benefit on overall survival (at a first interim analysis);
exploratory quality-of-life data ?; the toxicity profile marked by an increase in toxicity, notably in serious adverse events (49,6 % versus 36,8 %) and immune-mediated AEs (49,8 % versus 10,8 %),
the Transparency Committee considers that KEYTRUDA (pembrolizumab), 25 mg/ml, concentrate for solution for infusion (IV route), as monotherapy in neoadjuvant treatment, continued as adjuvant treatment in combination with radiotherapy with or without concomitant cisplatin then as monotherapy, provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) compared with the combination of radiotherapy +/- cisplatin as adjuvant treatment in patients with locally advanced resectable squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck, excluding the hypopharynx, whose tumours express PD-L1 with a CPS ≥ 1.

c055568987045d36:
Taking into account:
- non-comparative data from the multi-cohort study TRANSCEND NHL 001, an open-label early-phase study (phase I "seamless"), initially designed as a dose-finding study, with a mantle cell lymphoma cohort selected post hoc, leading to uncertainty in the estimate of the observed effect;
- a demonstration on an intermediate endpoint, with an overall response rate assessed by an independent review committee (primary endpoint) of 86,5 % (64/74) (IC95% [76,5 ; 93,3]), including 55 complete responses, without demonstration of a survival benefit;
- the absence of formal demonstration on quality of life;
- the safety profile marked by significant short-term toxicity, with unknown long-term tolerability;
- uncertainties about the transferability of the results, related to the potentially selected nature of the population included in the study;
- uncertainty about the relative efficacy of this treatment, due to the absence of direct comparison and the methodological weakness of the indirect comparisons provided;

the Transparency Committee considers that BREYANZI (lisocabtagene maraleucel), 1,1-70 x 106 cellules/mL / 1,1-70 x 106 cellules/mL, dispersion pour perfusion, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) within the current therapeutic strategy, which includes the relevant comparators (see section 5.2 of this opinion).

343bb6dba8cce62c:
Having regard to:
the insufficiently met medical need in the prevention of lower respiratory tract infections due to RSV in subjects aged 75 years and older and in subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions;
the demonstration of superiority of the ABRYSVO vaccine over placebo in 32 223 subjects at the end of season 2, in terms of reduction of RSV-LRTI with ≥ 2 symptoms (VE 56 % (95%CI [34 ;70])) and of RSV-LRTI with ≥ 3 symptoms (VE 78 % (95%CI [51 ;91]);
vaccine effectiveness against hospitalisations and emergency department admissions, reported by real-world studies and estimated between 69 % [95% CI: 52 ; 81] and 88,9 % [95% CI: 77,9 ; 95,7], across all studies, in subjects aged 75 years and older, subjects aged 65 years and older with comorbidities, and immunocompromised subjects (vaccine effectiveness reduced in immunocompromised subjects);
a safety profile of the ABRYSVO vaccine that remains favourable, characterised by AEs mostly of grade 1 (mild) or grade 2 (moderate), such as headache (20 %), injection-site pain (19 %), fatigue (23 %) and myalgia (16 %); despite a signal for Guillain-Barré syndrome (added to the list of very rare adverse events);
However:
the suboptimal level of evidence of real-world vaccine effectiveness data on hospitalisations and emergency department admissions, owing to the methodological limitations inherent in the design of these observational studies (mainly case-control studies using a "test-negative design", but also the matched exposed-unexposed cohort study);
the Transparency Committee considers that ABRYSVO (respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) vaccine (bivalent, recombinant)) provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV) in active immunisation for the prevention of lower respiratory tract disease caused by RSV in subjects aged 75 years and older and in subjects aged 65 years and older with chronic respiratory (particularly COPD) or cardiac (particularly heart failure) conditions likely to decompensate during an RSV infection.

aa40e660d5406fd0:
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of BORTEZOMIB EVER PHARMA 2,5 mg/mL (bortézomib), solution injectable en 5 flacons en verre de 1,4 mL (3,5 mg/1,4 mL), is important in:
the marketing authorisation (AMM) indication “as monotherapy or in combination with dexamethasone, for the treatment of adult patients with progressive multiple myeloma who have received at least 1 prior therapy and who have already received or are ineligible for a haematopoietic stem cell transplant”;
newly diagnosed multiple myeloma in patients:
ineligible for intensive chemotherapy followed by haematopoietic stem cell transplant, in combination with melphalan and prednisone;
eligible for intensive chemotherapy followed by haematopoietic stem cell transplant as induction treatment, in combination with dexamethasone, or with dexamethasone and thalidomide;
the marketing authorisation (AMM) indication “in combination with rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin and prednisone, for the treatment of adult patients with previously untreated mantle cell lymphoma for whom a haematopoietic stem cell transplant is unsuitable”.

6d55734e637b8555:
Le service médical rendu (SMR) de SARCLISA (isatuximab) 1 400 mg/10 mL, solution pour injection sous-cutanée, est important dans les indications :
« en association avec le pomalidomide et la dexaméthasone pour le traitement des patients adultes atteints de myélome multiple en rechute et réfractaire, qui ont reçu au moins deux traitements antérieurs incluant le lénalidomide et un inhibiteur du protéasome et dont la maladie a progressé lors du dernier traitement » ;
