4caa17c370921663:
Low therapeutic value in acute rhinitis and sinusitis
These vasoconstrictors have marketing authorisation (AMM) for short-term symptomatic treatment of acute congestive states during acute rhinitis and sinusitis, from the age of 15 years. They are administered topically (oxymetazoline) or orally (pseudoephedrine).
Because of the risk of serious but rare cardiovascular adverse events associated with vasoconstrictors (myocardial infarction, arrhythmias, transient ischaemic attacks, ischaemic strokes, etc.):
these medicines are contraindicated before 15 years of age;
their use must be of short duration (3 to 5 days);
they must not be combined with each other, whatever their route of administration;
they must be stopped in the event of cardiovascular manifestations.
Their value is low, given insufficiently established efficacy and the cardiovascular risk.
For more information, download the summary or full opinions ATURGYL, PERNAZENE,, SUDAFED.

f708e9c1f8496c56:
Weak therapeutic benefit in acute rhinitis and sinusitis
These vasoconstrictors have marketing authorisation (AMM) for short-term symptomatic treatment of acute congestive states during acute rhinitis and sinusitis, from the age of 15 years. They are administered topically (oxymetazoline) or orally (pseudoephedrine).
Because of the risk of serious but rare cardiovascular adverse events associated with vasoconstrictors (myocardial infarction, rhythm disorders, transient ischaemic attacks, ischaemic strokes, etc.):these medicines are contraindicated before 15 years of age;
their use must be of short duration (3 to 5 days);
they must not be combined with one another, whatever their route of administration;
they must be stopped in the event of cardiovascular manifestations.
Their benefit is low, given insufficiently established efficacy and the cardiovascular risk.
For more information, download the summary or the full opinions for ATURGYL, PERNAZENE, SUDAFED.

962469f9f1b66d0f:
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement due to insufficient clinical interest in the treatment of COPD
DAXAS has the marketing authorisation for continuous treatment of severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) (post-bronchodilator FEV1 < 50 % of the predicted value) associated with chronic bronchitis in adults with a history of repeated exacerbations, as an add-on to bronchodilator treatment.
Due to: of little clinical relevance in terms of reducing moderate to severe exacerbations, with no data on dyspnoea or quality of life;
uncertainties about the risks associated with weight loss, depression and suicidal ideation in a frail population;
a poorly defined place in the therapeutic strategy;
this medicine has no demonstrated clinical value in the management of COPD.
For more information, download the summary or the full DAXAS opinion.

86d399a45eb484da:
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the absence of demonstrated clinical interest.
ARIXTRA 1.5 mg/0.3 mL has a marketing authorisation in adults for venous thromboembolism prevention:
– in major orthopaedic surgery of the lower limb (hip fracture, hip replacement, major knee surgery);
– in abdominal surgery, in patients considered at high risk of thromboembolic complications (abdominal surgery for cancer);
– in patients considered at high risk of venous thromboembolic complications and bedridden for an acute medical condition (heart failure, acute respiratory disorder, infectious or inflammatory disease);
– and in the treatment of acute symptomatic spontaneous superficial venous thrombosis of the lower limbs, without associated deep venous thrombosis.
The place of ARIXTRA 1.5 mg in the therapeutic strategy cannot be established in the absence of robust data on thromboprophylactic efficacy in cases of renal insufficiency and in subjects of low weight or aged ≥ 75 years.

e8e27bafb382acd6:
In atopic dermatitis, unfavourable opinion on reimbursement:
– before 16 years of age, regardless of flare severity;
– from 16 years of age, in moderate flares;
– in children and adults, as maintenance treatment
Low clinical interest, from 16 years of age, in severe flares failing topical corticosteroids
PROTOPIC has marketing authorisation (AMM) in moderate to severe atopic dermatitis:
– for treatment of flares in adults and adolescents over 16 years of age in case of intolerance or inadequate response to conventional treatments, in particular topical corticosteroids, and in children (from 2 years of age and older) who have not responded adequately to conventional treatments such as topical corticosteroids;
– as maintenance treatment in case of very frequent exacerbations (≥ 4/year) in patients who had an initial response to tacrolimus ointment (twice daily for up to 6 weeks)
Due to efficacy and tolerability data, PROTOPIC no longer has clinical interest in children or as maintenance treatment.
It may be used in adolescents aged 16 years or older and in adults, in case of severe atopic dermatitis after failure of well-used corticosteroid treatment.

6910c776e5279e62:
Minor therapeutic progress, like DAKLINZA (daclatasvir) and OLYSIO (siméprévir), in the management of adults infected with hepatitis C virus (HCV) genotypes 1 and 4
VIEKIRAX, like EXVIERA, has marketing authorisation for the treatment of chronic hepatitis C, in combination with other medicines, in adults infected with HCV genotype 1.
VIEKIRAX also has marketing authorisation in adults infected with HCV genotype 4.
VIEKIRAX and EXVIERA could achieve cure in most patients with hepatitis C, with or without extra-hepatic manifestations. They should preferably be offered first to all patients whose liver disease is at fibrosis stage F3 or F4, as well as to certain specific populations. A specific mention concerns patients infected with genotype 3 virus, for whom early treatment is desirable. Patients at fibrosis stage F2 should also benefit from new treatments within short timeframes. For stages F0 or F1, treatment could be deferred depending on disease progression and the clinical context.
They provide minor therapeutic progress, like DAKLINZA and OLYSIO, in the management of chronic hepatitis C genotypes 1 and 4, given:
their high virological efficacy, similar to that observed with the sofosbuvir-based combinations available (sofosbuvir/daclatasvir, sofosbuvir/siméprévir and sofosbuvir/lédipasvir), with a good level of evidence;
their satisfactory safety profile;
the significant risk of cross-resistance developing in case of treatment failure;
the potential for drug interactions;
the current treatment strategy, which mainly comprises a sofosbuvir-based combination, without interferon.

974ae6d58958c4f0:
Therapeutic value unchanged in the treatment of asthma, except in children aged 6 to 24 months, where it is low
SINGULAIR, available in 3 strengths (4 mg, 5 mg and 10 mg), has marketing authorisation (AMM):
- As add-on to corticosteroid therapy in patients with mild to moderate persistent asthma insufficiently controlled by inhaled corticosteroids and in whom fast-acting, short-acting beta-2 mimetics administered "on demand" do not provide sufficient clinical control;
- As preventive treatment of exercise-induced asthma;
- As an alternative to low-dose inhaled corticosteroids in patients with mild persistent asthma without a recent history of severe asthma attacks requiring oral corticosteroid therapy, and whose inability to adhere to inhaled corticosteroid treatment has been demonstrated (SINGULAIR 4 mg only for children aged 6 months to 5 years and 5 mg for children and adolescents aged 6 to 14 years).
Its therapeutic value remains important across all its indications, except in children aged 6 to 24 months, where it remains low.

b3a43a60197e22f9:
There is no clinical interest in a hypotonic injectable glucose solution in paediatrics, apart from its use as a vehicle for therapeutic administration, because of the risk of severe, even fatal, hyponatraemia.
Glucose-based medicines administered by injection have a marketing authorisation (AMM):
for the treatment of rehydration when water loss exceeds that of sodium chloride and other osmoles,
for the prevention of intra- and extracellular dehydration,
for the treatment and prevention of ketosis in malnutrition,
as a vehicle for therapeutic administration in the preoperative, intraoperative and immediate postoperative period.
The main interest of a 5 % (hypotonic) glucose injectable solution lies in its use as a vehicle for injectable drug administration.
Administration of a hypotonic glucose solution should be avoided in paediatrics to limit the occurrence of encephalopathy due to dilutional hyponatraemia.

0b3486d2b012c350:
Actual clinical benefit (SMR) of CINQAERO is important only as add-on therapy in severe refractory eosinophilic asthma in adults meeting the following criteria:
a blood eosinophil count = 400 cells/µL on corticosteroids at treatment initiation;
AND
at least two asthma exacerbation episodes requiring oral corticosteroid treatment (= 3 days each) within the past 12 months despite maintenance treatment combining high-dose inhaled corticosteroids and a long-acting bronchodilator (GINA step 4/5);
OR oral corticosteroid therapy for at least 6 months during the past 12 months.
Patients whose asthma is not controlled due to inadequate maintenance treatment, adherence problems, comorbidities or unmanaged aggravating risk factors do not fall within this scope.
The actual clinical benefit (SMR) of CINQAERO is insufficient in other situations.
Taking into account:
the demonstration in two placebo-controlled studies of a reduction in asthma exacerbations in patients with uncontrolled severe asthma;
the reduction in asthma exacerbations requiring systemic corticosteroid therapy for at least 3 days compared with placebo;
a modest effect on quality of life, FEV1 and asthma control assessed by the ACQ questionnaire;
data suggesting less use of oral corticosteroids to manage exacerbations in the reslizumab group;
the unmet therapeutic need in patients with uncontrolled severe asthma and the risks associated with exacerbations, their impact on quality of life and the healthcare consumption they generate;
the absence of clear diagnostic criteria for eosinophilic asthma and the limitations associated with blood eosinophil measurement;
the uncertainties regarding the criteria for identifying, in clinical practice, the patients most likely to benefit from reslizumab;
the absence of data in omalizumab failure;
CINQAERO provides a minor improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR IV), as NUCALA, in the management of severe eosinophilic asthma insufficiently controlled by high-dose inhaled corticosteroids combined with another drug for continuous maintenance treatment.

b81c43d056de4ea5:
Clinical benefit still insufficient in thromboprophylaxis in the absence of robust efficacy and tolerance data in patients with renal impairment.
ARIXTRA 1.5 mg/0.3 mL has marketing authorisation in adults for the prevention of venous thromboembolism:
-  in major orthopaedic surgery of the lower limb (hip fracture, hip replacement, major knee surgery);
-  in abdominal surgery, in patients considered at high risk of thromboembolic complications (abdominal surgery for cancer);
-  in patients considered at high risk of venous thromboembolic complications and bedridden due to an acute medical condition (heart failure, acute respiratory disorder, infectious or inflammatory disease);
-  and in the treatment of acute symptomatic spontaneous superficial vein thrombosis of the lower limbs, without associated deep vein thrombosis.
This lower dose has marketing authorisation as a dose adjustment from 2.5 mg only in patients with moderate to severe renal impairment (CrCl between 20 and 50 mL/min).
In the absence of robust efficacy and tolerance data in renal impairment, its place in the therapeutic strategy cannot be established.

4f19b96dfb528488:
The clinical benefit (service médical rendu) of MYLOTARG is important only in patients aged 15 years and over with de novo CD33+ AML, treatment-naïve, in good general condition (ECOG 0 or 1), with the exception of AML with a FLT3 gene mutation eligible for treatment with midostaurin (RYDAPT) and of acute promyelocytic leukaemia.
Given:
the demonstration of the superiority of adding MYLOTARG to the standard daunorubicin and cytarabine chemotherapy regimen compared with this same regimen during the induction and consolidation phases of treatment of de novo CD33+ AML, in terms of event-free survival, with a clinically relevant gain (+ 7,8 months; HR = 0,562; 95%CI [0,415; 0,762]; p = 0,0002), in treatment-naïve patients aged 50 to 70 years;
a possible measurement bias on the primary endpoint (event-free survival), which was assessed by the investigator in an open-label study;
the demonstration of the absence of benefit of adding MYLOTARG to the standard chemotherapy regimen on overall survival (hierarchically ranked secondary endpoint) in a context where patients' vital prognosis is threatened in the short or medium term, which limits the clinical relevance of the effect observed on event-free survival;
the absence of data collection on quality of life;
the tolerability profile of MYLOTARG, marked by a higher number of serious adverse events from all causes (67,2% versus 55,5%);
and treatment discontinuations due to adverse events, observed in 31,1% of patients treated with MYLOTARG (versus 7,3% in the standard chemotherapy group);
the Committee considers that MYLOTARG, in combination with daunorubicin and cytarabine, in the treatment of patients aged 15 years and over with de novo, treatment-naïve, CD33-positive acute myeloid leukaemia, does not provide an improvement in clinical benefit (ASMR V) compared with daunorubicin and cytarabine combination.

9708e67e4bebe005:
Considering:
that certain conditions or symptoms are benign and self-limiting, of no serious consequence, for which no identified medical need exists and for which the use of medicines (including homeopathy) is not necessary;
that there is no demonstration of efficacy (in terms of morbidity and/or quality of life) of homeopathic medicines in the conditions/symptoms for which data were found in the literature (non-significant data and/or methodological weaknesses that do not allow a conclusion of superiority over placebo or an active comparator, or absence of comparison with clinically relevant comparators);
that there is no demonstration of their impact on public health, in particular of their interest in reducing the consumption of other medicines;
that homeopathic medicines have no defined place in the therapeutic strategy for the conditions/symptoms for which data were found in the literature;
that there are no data for other conditions/symptoms (not found in the literature) for which homeopathy is used in current practice, and therefore no place for it in these situations;
and despite:
the severity and/or potential impact on patients' quality of life of certain symptoms/conditions studied, for which there is a medical need for therapeutic alternatives or complementary medicines;
the very good tolerability and safety profile of homeopathic medicines;
the Committee gives an unfavourable opinion on maintaining reimbursement by the health insurance system (assurance maladie) of homeopathic medicines falling, or intended to fall, under the registration procedure provided for in Article L. 5121-13 of the Public Health Code.

33aeb7f50207db3b:
Key points
Opinion favourable to maintaining reimbursement in the indications:
“GRANOCYTE is indicated in adults, adolescents and children over 2 years of age in:
the reduction of the duration of neutropenia in patients (with non-myeloid neoplasia) receiving myelosuppressive therapy followed by bone marrow transplantation and at increased risk of severe and prolonged neutropenia;
the reduction of the duration of severe neutropenia and associated complications in patients undergoing established chemotherapy known to be associated with a significant incidence of febrile neutropenia;
the mobilisation of haematopoietic stem cells into peripheral blood (PBPCs: Peripheral Blood Progenitor Cells) in patients and healthy donors.”

3edc409cfdd1f46d:
L'essentiel
Favourable opinion for reimbursement only in the indications currently reimbursable for the reference medicine (princeps), namely:
Angina pectoris;
Arterial hypertension;
Long-term prevention of recurrent myocardial infarction after recovery from acute myocardial infarction;
Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy;
Supraventricular cardiac arrhythmia;
Ventricular cardiac arrhythmias;
Hyperthyroidism and thyrotoxicosis;
Migraine;
Prevention of upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage in patients with portal hypertension and oesophageal varices;
Essential tremor.
What progress?
No progress compared with the princeps AVLOCARDYL 40 mg, comprimé sécable (scored tablet).

47d58fbd93d9a260:
First evaluation.
Favourable opinion for reimbursement in the prevention of influenza in adults and children aged 9 years and over for whom influenza vaccination is recommended.
What progress?
No progress in patient management.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
In France, seasonal influenza vaccination is recommended for all persons aged 65 years and over and for the following specific populations (see vaccination schedule):
pregnant women, whatever the trimester of pregnancy;
persons from the age of 6 months with diseases that predispose to serious complications of influenza;
obese persons;
persons staying in a post-acute care facility or in a residential medico-social establishment;
the close contacts of infants under 6 months of age with risk factors for severe influenza, and the close contacts of immunocompromised persons;
professionals in regular and prolonged contact with persons at risk of severe influenza, in particular healthcare workers and flight crew.
Place of the medicinal product
FLUCELVAC TETRA may be used in accordance with its marketing authorisation (AMM) within the framework of current vaccination recommendations, aimed at preventing severe forms and deaths related to seasonal influenza.

40614897db78862f:
Insufficient clinical benefit to justify its reimbursement in the symptomatic treatment of erectile dysfunction in adult men
INVICORP has the marketing authorisation for the symptomatic treatment of erectile dysfunction in adult men of neurogenic, vascular, psychogenic or mixed origin.
INVICORP has no established place in the therapeutic strategy given the available alternatives, on the basis of the clinical data available:
efficacy data versus placebo of low level of evidence, placebo not being clinically relevant, including in the sub-population of patients who failed sildenafil for whom other alternatives are available;
limited interest of the data from one study comparing INVICORP with intracavernous alprostadil injections, given its open-label design and the exploratory nature of the secondary efficacy endpoints, the primary endpoint being a tolerance endpoint;      .
no data versus phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors, first-line treatment of erectile dysfunction, and versus alprostadil cream or urethral stick, second-line treatment;
no data in cases of erectile dysfunction of non-organic origin;
rare but serious risks of penile fibrosis or priapism.

39bb9d054eb63c2f:
New indication.
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in combination with bortezomib and dexamethasone for the treatment of previously untreated multiple myeloma in adult patients not eligible for transplant.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
In symptomatic patients, first-line treatment depends on whether or not the patient is eligible for intensive chemotherapy combined with autologous peripheral blood stem cell transplantation (ACSP).
When patients are not eligible for transplant (in particular due to their age, comorbidities and/or general condition), the therapeutic strategy is based on a chemotherapy regimen, which may be combined with an anti-CD38 monoclonal antibody:
chemotherapy either with bortezomib (VELCADE) combined with melphalan and prednisone (MPV regimen) for a fixed duration, or with lenalidomide (REVLIMID) combined with low-dose dexamethasone (Rd regimen) until disease progression; the Committee had considered that, in the absence of data comparing the Rd and MPV regimens, the choice between one or the other could be made according to the patient's tolerability profile.
the combination of the anti-CD38 monoclonal antibody DARZALEX (daratumumab) with the MPV regimen demonstrated superiority until disease progression over the MPV regimen alone in patients not eligible for transplant. In addition, DARZALEX (daratumumab) has recently obtained a marketing authorisation (AMM) in combination with the Rd regimen in patients ineligible for transplant (lenalidomide-dexamethasone).
Place of the medicinal product
In view of:
the demonstration of superiority of the combination VELCADE (bortezomib) plus REVLIMID (lenalidomide) plus dexamethasone (VRd regimen) compared with the Rd regimen given alone in terms of progression-free survival (median gain of +12,6 months), but without demonstration of a benefit in terms of overall survival (exploratory secondary endpoint);
the impossibility of quantifying the contribution of REVLIMID (lenalidomide) in terms of efficacy within the triple combination VRd, given the design of the study evaluating the addition of VELCADE (bortezomib) for a fixed duration of 8 cycles to the Rd regimen until progression;
the higher frequency of certain grade 3 or 4 adverse events (AEs) with the VRd regimen compared with the Rd regimen, in particular peripheral sensory neuropathies (22,9% versus 2,7%), as well as more frequent treatment discontinuations following the occurrence of an AE (37,0% versus 25,0%);
limitations in the transposability of the study results to the population defined by the marketing authorisation (AMM) (patients ineligible for transplant), given the inclusion in the study of patients eligible for transplant (for whom transplant was reserved for a later line) and in the absence of robust data available in patients ineligible for transplant (whose characteristics in terms of age, comorbidities and/or general condition are different);
the unproven impact on quality of life in the absence of data;
the introduction into the therapeutic strategy, since the study was carried out, of regimens including DARZALEX (daratumumab) (combined with MPV or Rd) in first line in patients ineligible for transplant, and the impossibility of defining the place of the proposed regimen in the current therapeutic strategy in the absence of comparative data;
the Committee considers that REVLIMID (lenalidomide) in combination with bortezomib and dexamethasone has no place in the treatment of previously untreated multiple myeloma in adult patients not eligible for transplant.

3841f50120f0e1c8:
First assessment.
Reimbursement application withdrawn by the laboratory.
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in combination with bendamustine and rituximab, for the treatment of adult patients with relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (LDGCB) who are not candidates for haematopoietic stem cell transplantation.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
First-line treatment of patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (LDGCB) involves a combination of chemotherapy with an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody, namely the R-CHOP regimen. In cases of primary refractoriness or relapse, the treatment of choice is salvage chemotherapy followed by consolidation with autologous transplantation in cases of chemosensitive disease.
In patients not eligible for transplantation, due to an insufficient response to salvage chemotherapy or due to the patient's condition, treatment is based on platinum- and/or gemcitabine-based immunochemotherapy (in particular the R-GemOx or R-DHAP regimens not followed by intensification and autologous transplantation).
Patients who relapse or are refractory to second-line salvage treatment may be considered for third-line chemotherapy. Treatment with CAR-T cells (axicabtagene ciloleucel or tisagenlecleucel) is recommended in patients whose life expectancy is compatible with the production timelines. The following other options may be considered depending on the clinical situation:
for patients in relapse (excluding refractory patients): a new intensive chemotherapy aimed at performing allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation if the patient is eligible; where appropriate, autologous transplantation. Allogeneic transplantation is generally considered in patients under 70 years of age, in the absence of significant comorbidity, in the presence of a donor (graft availability) and if a complete response (or very good partial response) is obtained at the end of the chemotherapy regimen;
other chemotherapies;
initiation of palliative care.
Place of the medicinal product
Given:
the methodological weakness of the clinical data from a multi-cohort phase I/II study including an exploratory randomised phase (without superiority or non-inferiority hypothesis), conducted with a liquid formulation of polatuzumab vedotin (which does not have marketing authorisation) and in a limited number of patients heterogeneous in terms of prior lines of treatment (min: 1; max: 7);
the lack of clinical relevance of the comparator chosen for this randomised phase (rituximab + bendamustine - BR), which is little used in France, as well as that of the primary endpoint (namely the percentage of complete responses) in this clinical situation, where overall survival and quality of life data would have been more appropriate;
the uncertainty regarding this percentage of complete responses, estimated at 40% in patients treated with polatuzumab vedotin + BR versus 17,5% in patients treated with BR, given the imbalances observed between these two groups that may favour the polatuzumab vedotin + BR group (in particular regarding reasons for ineligibility for transplantation and prognostic factors of the disease);
the absence of robust demonstration of an overall survival gain compared with BR;
the absence of quality of life data collected, which is regrettable as certain adverse events of polatuzumab vedotin could affect it (in particular peripheral neuropathies);
the uncertainties with the lyophilised formulation that has marketing authorisation, insofar as the data are very limited for this formulation (non-comparative cohort of 42 patients with efficacy results that appear inferior to those observed in the randomised phase with the liquid formulation);
and indirect comparisons (MAIC) submitted by the laboratory that do not allow quantification of the contribution of polatuzumab vedotin versus reference treatments (polychemotherapy regimens such as R-GemOx or CAR-T cells);
the Transparency Committee considers that POLIVY (polatuzumab vedotin) in combination with bendamustine and rituximab has no place in the treatment of adult patients with relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (LDGCB) who are not candidates for haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, in view of the alternatives and pending any possible data from future clinical development.

0873b0481f8b7577:
New indication
Favourable opinion for reimbursement in the add-on treatment to intranasal corticosteroids in adults with severe nasosinus polyposis (PNS) insufficiently controlled by systemic corticosteroids and by surgery.
What progress?
A therapeutic advance in patient management.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Topical corticosteroids constitute the reference treatment for PNS regardless of symptom severity.
When severity is high and symptoms are very marked, a short course of systemic corticosteroid therapy may be considered. Antibiotics are used only in cases of superinfection.
If symptoms remain insufficiently controlled after at least 1 to 2 courses of CSS, surgery is proposed for disabling PNS that is resistant to well-conducted, well-monitored and sufficiently prolonged medical treatment. It may be repeated.
The European Position Paper on Rhinosinusitis and Nasal Polyps (EPOS) has retained 5 criteria for the use of biologic therapies:
the use of CSS within the last 2 years;
evidence of type 2 inflammatory disease;
a significant deterioration in quality of life;
a significant loss of smell;
a diagnosis of associated asthma.
It considers that, to be eligible for biologic therapy:
3 of the 5 criteria must be met for patients with a history of surgery for PNS;
4 of the 5 criteria must be met for patients with no history of surgery for PNS.
After 16 weeks of treatment, and in the absence of response against these criteria, biologic therapy must be discontinued. Otherwise, treatment is continued and reassessed after 1 year. Response to biologic therapies should be assessed on the following criteria:
reduction in the size of nasal polyps;
reduction in the use of CSS;
improvement in quality of life;
improvement in smell;
reduction in the impact of comorbidities.
Place of the medicinal product
DUPIXENT (dupilumab) may be used, as a biologic therapy, in accordance with the summary of product characteristics (RCP), as add-on treatment to intranasal corticosteroids for adult patients with severe nasosinus polyposis insufficiently controlled by systemic corticosteroids and by surgery.
Uncertainties remain, due to the lack of evaluation of the efficacy and tolerability of dupilumab beyond one year in the available clinical studies.

144ff50f3ffb4863:
Extension of indication.
Favourable opinion on reimbursement in the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection in children aged 2 to 6 years pretreated with antiretrovirals.
What progress?
A therapeutic advance in patient management.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
In case of virological failure, treatment should preferably combine a ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor (active IP/r, essentially darunavir/r; exceptionally tipranavir/r), the combination of two IPs not being recommended, with two other active antiretrovirals to be chosen from:
étravirine (which frequently remains active even in case of resistance to éfavirenz and/or névirapine, whereas cross-resistance with rilpivirine exists);
raltégravir (in particular in combination with darunavir/r and étravirine);
dolutégravir, which generally remains active at 50 mg twice daily in case of resistance mutations to raltégravir or elvitégravir. The combination of dolutégravir with étravirine should not be used, except in combination with an IP/r to compensate for the enzyme-inducing effect of étravirine on dolutégravir metabolism;
maraviroc, if no virus using the CXCR4 coreceptor is present;
enfuvirtide (but its prolonged use is limited by its injectable form);
one or more NRTIs. In case of NRTI multiresistance (≥ 3 TAMs + M184V), residual activity of abacavir and ténofovir may persist. However, maintaining NRTIs in case of multiresistance to this class of antiretrovirals is not justified when at least three other fully active antiretrovirals are available.
Place of the medicinal product
INTELENCE (étravirine) is an NNRTI frequently active against viruses resistant to first-generation NNRTIs (éfavirenz and/or névirapine). In accordance with its marketing authorisation, INTELENCE (étravirine) is not recommended in antiretroviral-naive patients.
In combination with a boosted protease inhibitor and other antiretroviral medicinal products, it constitutes, as in adults, adolescents and pretreated children aged 6 to < 18 years, a therapeutic option for the treatment of HIV-1 infection in children aged 2 to 6 years. These patients must have a detectable viral load on current antiretroviral treatment and carry viral strains with resistance mutations to NNRTIs and protease inhibitors. As cross-resistance with other NNRTIs is possible, before prescription the absence of 3 or more mutations reducing viral sensitivity to this molecule should be verified by reverse transcriptase genotyping.
Very rare cases of severe rash, including Lyell's syndrome and DRESS syndrome or severe hypersensitivity (rash, eosinophilia and more or less associated lymphadenopathy, hepatitis, interstitial nephritis, interstitial lung disease), have been reported within 3 to 6 weeks after the start of étravirine, reinforcing the monitoring precautions required at treatment initiation. Prescribers must be informed that the incidence of skin rashes was higher in female patients (see SmPC).

a6673bb26b4be114:
Re-evaluation.
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in adjunctive treatment to mechanical ventilation in the most severe cases of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) with hypoxaemia and hypercapnia refractory despite optimised respiratory assistance (protective ventilation, prone positioning, etc.), and/or in the absence of another suitable accessible respiratory support device (examples: CO2 removal, veno-venous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, …)
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Management of ARDS mainly comprises mechanical ventilation and, where mechanical ventilation is necessary or has failed, for the most severe cases, adjunctive therapies. These are non-pharmacological (prone positioning, high-frequency ventilation and extracorporeal oxygenation techniques) or pharmacological (mainly inhaled nitric oxide [NO], although used outside the marketing authorisation).
Place of the medicinal product
Given:
the absence of new robust and recent data demonstrating efficacy of almitrine in the recently modified indication of the marketing authorisation;
its marked tolerability profile, notably peripheral neuropathies (significant potential risk with the injectable form), and its mechanism of action involving potential deleterious effects on the pulmonary circulation;
current French recommendations, which no longer mention this molecule in the management of ARDS;
the Commission considers that VECTARION (almitrine) no longer has a place in the therapeutic strategy in view of the alternatives.

d498fbfc4ac644e9:
Making a new presentation available.
Opinion favourable to reimbursement in:
Urinary tract infections (for further details, see the marketing authorisation (AMM));
Bacillary dysentery;
Typhoid and paratyphoid fever;
Curative and prophylactic treatment of infections caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii (for further details, see the AMM).
Opinion unfavourable to reimbursement in acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis.
What progress?
No progress of the new oral suspension presentation compared with the presentations already available.

3415d80be054ff93:
First assessment.
Favourable opinion on reimbursement in the treatment of 5q spinal muscular atrophy in patients aged 2 months and older with a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 1, 2 and 3.
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in patients with SMA with a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 4 and in presymptomatic patients.
What progress?
A therapeutic advance in the management of patients with 5q spinal muscular atrophy with a clinical diagnosis of SMA:
type 1, on a par with SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec),
type 2, on a par with SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and,
type 3 non-ambulatory.
No progress in the management of symptomatic patients with SMA type 3, ambulatory.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
According to the management recommendations, it should be started as early as possible, if possible at the presymptomatic stage, in order to anticipate complications, particularly respiratory ones. Current management includes:
Symptomatic treatment, with a multidisciplinary approach, aimed at improving quality of life. It notably includes physiotherapy, occupational therapy and respiratory support with, in some cases, non-invasive ventilation and gastrostomy.
Treatments with curative intent currently available:
ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec), a viral vector using the capsid of a non-replicating recombinant adeno-associated virus serotype 9 whose DNA has been replaced by the cDNA of the human survival motor neuron (SMN) gene and allowing expression of the deficient SMN protein, as a single intravenous injection;
SPINRAZA (nusinersen), an antisense oligonucleotide administered intrathecally which increases production of functional SMN protein by acting on splicing of the SMN2 gene, and
now EVRYSDI (risdiplam), an oral SMN2 gene splicing modifier which has had a European marketing authorisation since 26 March 2021.
Based on the Committee's opinions of January 2018 and July 2020 for SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and December 2020 for ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec):
In SMA type 1 and in presymptomatic patients with up to 3 copies of the SMN2 gene, SPINRAZA and ZOLGENSMA are first-line treatments. In the absence of a robust comparison, the choice between these two treatments must be made with regard to the age, clinical condition and comorbidities of patients, the modalities of administration and the choice of families, as well as the available data and their level of evidence. For patients with severe SMA type 1 who started before the age of 3 months, the decision to prescribe SPINRAZA must be discussed on a case-by-case basis, taking into account in particular the presence of severe respiratory restrictive syndrome. It should be noted that experts currently recommend treating patients diagnosed at the presymptomatic stage with 4 copies of the SMN2 gene;
In SMA type 2, SPINRAZA is the preferred treatment, while ZOLGENSMA is a therapeutic option pending data in these patients;
In SMA type 3, SPINRAZA is the only therapeutic option available, the decision to prescribe to be discussed on a case-by-case basis, taking into account the date of symptom onset and walking ability. The Committee considered that ZOLGENSMA had no place in the therapeutic strategy for these patients;
In SMA type 4, no treatment is currently recommended. ZOLGENSMA is indeed not indicated in this clinical type, and the Committee considered that SPINRAZA had no place in the therapeutic strategy.
Place of the medicinal product
Given:
the medical need for therapeutic alternatives, mainly in patients with a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 1 and 2 but also in patients with type 3 (including ambulatory patients) because of the modalities of administration, as reported by patient and user associations and experts;
the efficacy of risdiplam administered daily orally, demonstrated in 2 phase II/III clinical studies (FIREFISH and SUNFISH) in patients with a biological diagnosis of SMA and a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 1, type 2 and type 3 (non-ambulatory patients);
exploratory results suggesting a substantial improvement compared with the natural history of the disease at 2 years in symptomatic patients, but without cure (persistence of motor and respiratory disability), and with uncertainties regarding the maintenance of the treatment effect and the course of these patients over the longer term;
the major methodological limitations of the adjusted indirect comparisons performed against SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec), owing to concomitant developments, in patients with SMA type 1 and 2, which do not allow the exact place of EVRYSDI (risdiplam) in the therapeutic strategy to be known; and
an efficacy judged extrapolable to ambulatory patients with a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 3, given the clinical continuum and the pathophysiology of the disease, the mechanism of action of the medicinal product, and despite the absence of data in these patients;
EVRYSDI (risdiplam) is a first-line treatment, to be used:
in symptomatic patients with SMA type 1, on a par with SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec),
in patients with SMA type 2 and 3, on a par with SPINRAZA (nusinersen).
In the absence of data and given an efficacy that is difficult to extrapolate, EVRYSDI (risdiplam) has no place in the therapeutic strategy for presymptomatic patients with SMA with up to 4 copies of the SMN2 gene.
Because of the complexity of managing this disease, the decision to treat with EVRYSDI (risdiplam) must be taken on a case-by-case basis during multidisciplinary consultation meetings within the reference and expert centres for neuromuscular diseases of the FILNEMUS network, and, in accordance with the SmPC, treatment must be initiated by a physician experienced in the management of patients with SMA.
In the absence of a robust comparison (direct or indirect) with SPINRAZA (nusinersen) and ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec), and pending the results from the national SMA registry (see paragraph 10 of this opinion), the Committee specifies that the choice between the available treatments must be made taking into account:
the age of patients, in a context where the Committee recalls the need to start treatment as early as possible and, if possible, in presymptomatic patients;
the clinical condition of patients;
the comorbidities of patients with regard to the safety profile of each treatment and in particular the serious risk of TMA with ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec);
the different modalities of administration of these medicinal products, insofar as the Committee emphasises that the daily oral administration of EVRYSDI (risdiplam) may be an interesting option compared with the other available modalities of administration but that it does not necessarily represent an advantage in young children for reasons of treatment compliance;
the data available with these three medicinal products and their level of evidence;
as well as the choice of families and patients.
The Committee also emphasises that to date, no data are available with EVRYSDI (risdiplam) in:
patients with SMA type 1 having 1 or 3 copies of the SMN2 gene;
ambulatory patients with a clinical diagnosis of SMA type 3;
and efficacy data in patients who have previously been treated with SPINRAZA (nusinersen) or ZOLGENSMA (onasemnogene abeparvovec).
As for the alternatives previously analysed by the Committee, longer-term follow-up of patients within a registry is essential to assess, at medium and long term, the effect of EVRYSDI (risdiplam) on respiratory, motor and cognitive functions, on mortality and quality of life, as well as its tolerability.
Specific recommendations
Because of the complexity of managing this rare disease, the Committee recommends that the treatment decision be taken on a case-by-case basis during multidisciplinary consultation meetings within the reference and expert centres for neuromuscular diseases of the FILNEMUS network, and that use of this medicinal product be reserved for physicians specialised in SMA.

160dbae6a1f21644:
Early access authorisation refused on 29 September 2021 for the combination OPDIVO 10mg/mL and YERVOY 5mg/mL (nivolumab+ipilimumab) from the laboratory BRISTOL MYERS SQUIBB, in the indication "OPDIVO is indicated in combination with ipilimumab for the treatment of adult patients with metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC) with deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) or high microsatellite instability (MSI-H), after a prior fluoropyrimidine-based chemotherapy combination, who have not received prior immunotherapy and who have an ECOG score of 0 or 1. YERVOY is indicated in combination with nivolumab, for the treatment of adult patients with metastatic colorectal cancer with dMMR or MSI-H, after a prior fluoropyrimidine-based chemotherapy combination, who have not received prior immunotherapy and who have an ECOG score of 0 or 1".
Transparency Committee (CT) opinion
The Transparency Committee (CT) considered that:
The indication targeted by the application constitutes a serious, rare and disabling disease, given that the MSI-H/dMMR mutation is observed in approximately 5% of mCRC cases, that the median survival of patients with metastatic colorectal cancer is approximately 30 months, and that the disease impairs patients' quality of life;
There are appropriate treatments, considering:
the existence of chemotherapy protocols recommended at the same level of the therapeutic strategy for MSI-H/dMMR metastatic colorectal cancer as the OPDIVO-YERVOY combination;
the accessibility of chemotherapy protocols in routine practice, with coverage by national solidarity in this indication;
the absence of comparative efficacy and tolerability data does not allow the place of the OPDIVO-YERVOY combination relative to chemotherapy in second-line treatment of MSI-H/dMMR metastatic colorectal cancer to be specified;
Treatment cannot be deferred given the rapidly progressing life-threatening prognosis at an advanced stage;
This medicine is not presumed innovative (with respect to clinically relevant comparators) as it notably lacks an adapted development plan.
HAS decision
The College decides to adopt the CT's reasoning on the first, second and fourth points.
However, on the third point, the College considers that, due to the existence of an appropriate treatment, implementation of the treatment can be deferred.
In light of all these elements, the College concludes that the criteria referred to in Article L. 5121-12 of the French Public Health Code are not met.
Consequently, the early access authorisation is refused.

bb0710c8072701f4:
Extension of indication.
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement, in monotherapy treatment of adult patients with advanced, unresectable, recurrent or metastatic oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma (CEO), after prior combined fluoropyrimidine and platinum-salt chemotherapy.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Therapeutic strategies for oesophageal carcinoma are defined according to histological type (squamous cell/adenocarcinoma) and tumour stage. In patients with advanced, unresectable, metastatic squamous cell carcinoma of the oesophagus, systemic treatment alone (palliative chemotherapy) is the reference treatment.
The main chemotherapy drugs used in combination are cisplatin and 5 fluorouracil. Other regimens such as the FOLFOX regimen combining fluorouracil, oxaliplatin and folinic acid are sometimes used as a treatment option.
In certain metastatic adenocarcinomas of the oesogastric junction (JOG) overexpressing HER 2, targeted therapy (trastuzumab) may also be proposed in combination with chemotherapy.
Apart from nivolumab (the subject of this evaluation), no other medicine currently has a marketing authorisation specifically for second-line treatment of advanced, unresectable, recurrent or metastatic oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma after first-line chemotherapy. However, the European ESMO recommendations (2016) and the American NCCN recommendations (2021) recommend in this situation, in particular, taxanes (docetaxel or paclitaxel) as monotherapy in eligible patients. Patients in poor general condition are managed with supportive care.
Place of the medicine
Taking into account, on the one hand:
the demonstrated superiority of nivolumab versus taxane monotherapy (docetaxel or paclitaxel) on overall survival (primary endpoint) in a phase III, randomised, open-label study (ATTRACTION-III), with a modest absolute gain of 2,5 months and crossing survival curves (additional effect size difficult to interpret);
the more favourable tolerability profile of nivolumab compared with taxanes (grade 3-4 adverse events: 38,3% vs 70,7%; serious adverse events: 32,5% vs 63,2%; adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation: 13,9% vs 15,9%), with however grade 5 adverse events more frequent with nivolumab: 3,3% versus 2,4%);
But also taking into account:
concerns about the excess mortality observed with nivolumab compared with chemotherapy within the first 5 months after randomisation, in a context where no specific factor associated with these early deaths could be identified;
the lack of assurance that the results are transferable to French patients (mostly patients originating from Asia [95,7%]) and the exclusion of patients in poor general condition (ECOG score ≥ 2), who are nevertheless the most represented in routine practice at this stage of the disease;
the impossibility of drawing any conclusion on progression-free survival due to the interruption of the hierarchical sequence upstream;
the absence of a formal conclusion on quality of life (exploratory endpoint and open-label study);
the Transparency Committee considers that OPDIVO (nivolumab), in monotherapy, currently has no place in the therapeutic strategy for oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma (CEO) in adult patients with advanced, unresectable, recurrent or metastatic CEO, after prior combined fluoropyrimidine- and platinum-salt-based chemotherapy.

2e14ca11e8a55235:
1. New indication.
2. Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement, in adult patients with moderate to severe major depressive episode, as short-term acute treatment, for the rapid reduction of depressive symptoms, constituting a psychiatric emergency according to clinical assessment, in co-administration with an oral antidepressant.
3. What place in the therapeutic strategy?
4. Management of major depressive episode varies according to the severity of the episode:
5. mild episodes: first line, supportive psychotherapy with reassessment of management after 4 to 8 weeks;
6. moderate episodes: first line, supportive psychotherapy combined if needed with an antidepressant (AD), with reassessment of management after 4 to 8 weeks;
7. severe episodes: immediate initiation of AD treatment with rapid referral to a psychiatrist for management combining psychotherapy and an AD. The effectiveness of management will be assessed after 4 to 8 weeks.
8. Several AD classes with specific pharmacological mechanisms of action are currently available in France, with no clinical efficacy difference demonstrated between them. The choice of an AD as first line is based mainly on the tolerability profile, with the treatments recommended as first line being a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI), a serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (SNRI), or a drug from the "other AD" class, with the exception of tianeptine and agomelatine.
9. Hospitalisation is the most frequent response to a psychiatric emergency in clinical practice. Within the general primary care management guide for depression, the latest HAS recommendations (October 2017) specify that hospitalisation should be considered either immediately or during the course of a generalised depressive episode, in the following situations:
10. patient with an imminent, well-constructed suicidal scenario;
11. patient with an immediate risk of self-harm;
12. patient with a potential for violence;
13. certain severe forms of depression, in the presence of severe psychotic or somatic symptoms;
14. presence of marked anxious agitation with loss of emotional control or impulsivity;
15. psychoactive substance withdrawal;
16. whenever a particular situation requires it.
17. According to the 2017 national consensus of the AFPBN and the FondaMental foundation, no expert consensus has been established regarding the use of adjunctive treatment to antidepressant therapy in case of risk of self-harming acts. The drug options cited as examples by the experts are: hydroxyzine, a benzodiazepine, a second-generation antipsychotic or lithium, within off-label use. The antidepressants cited as first line in case of significant suicide risk are SSRIs and SNRIs. Electroconvulsive therapy constitutes an alternative to drug treatments in case of a severe presentation associated with suicide risk.
18. Place of SPRAVATO (esketamine) in the therapeutic strategy
19. SPRAVATO (esketamine) in combination with an AD alone or with a potentiation agent was evaluated in two randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled studies, including adult patients (<65 years) with severe depression, hospitalised for a psychiatric emergency situation (either due to a suicide attempt or an imminent risk of suicide).
20. Considering:
21. the modest magnitude of effect on the change in MADRS score at 24 hours versus placebo (primary endpoint);
22. the absence of difference between SPRAVATO (esketamine) and placebo on the reduction of suicidal risk 24 hours after administration of the first dose, a hierarchised secondary endpoint, relevant in this psychiatric emergency context;
23. the absence of robust data on efficacy in terms of reduction of depression severity at 4 weeks (25 days), as this endpoint was not hierarchised and this treatment duration corresponds to the dosage currently validated by the SmPC (RCP) of SPRAVATO (esketamine);
24. the absence of a comparative study versus clinically relevant comparators, which does not allow positioning esketamine relative to these (notably electroconvulsive therapy);
25. the tolerability data for esketamine reporting a high frequency of serious adverse events (SAEs) of the "psychiatric disorders" type (suicide attempt, suicidal depression, suicidal ideation, depression) in both treatment groups (11,9% and 11,6% respectively), and one suicide in the esketamine group during the follow-up phase, whereas no death was reported in the placebo group; this death was considered by the investigator as not related to treatment;
26. the Commission considers that SPRAVATO (esketamine) has no place in the therapeutic strategy in co-administration with an oral antidepressant in adult patients with moderate to severe major depressive episode, as short-term acute treatment, for the rapid reduction of depressive symptoms, constituting a psychiatric emergency according to clinical assessment.
27. Furthermore, the Commission highlights the various aspects relating to the integration of SPRAVATO (esketamine) into current management of patients in psychiatric emergency situations, with:
28. the need for SPRAVATO (esketamine) to be prescribed by a psychiatrist;
29. the need for a clinical setting where appropriate resuscitation equipment and healthcare professionals trained in cardiopulmonary resuscitation are available for patients with clinically significant or unstable cardiovascular or respiratory disease.

