149dd85b27d9d6eb:
First assessment.
Key points
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in adult patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) harbouring a mutation that causes skipping of exon 14 in the mesenchymal-epithelial transition factor (MET) gene (METex14), who require systemic treatment after prior immunotherapy and/or platinum-based chemotherapy.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
The current therapeutic management of NSCLC in second line (after first-line chemotherapy and/or immunotherapy) is identical for patients with and without a MET exon 14 skipping mutation (METex14), including in particular:
platinum-based chemotherapy, for patients who received immunotherapy monotherapy in first line (pembrolizumab if PD-L1 ≥ 50%),
single-agent chemotherapy such as docetaxel (regardless of histology) or pemetrexed (non-squamous only), for patients who previously received immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy in first line;
immunotherapy (pembrolizumab if PD-L1 ≥ 1%, nivolumab or atezolizumab) in the absence of a contraindication to immunotherapy, for patients who previously received a chemotherapy doublet without immunotherapy in first line.
In France, in addition to TABRECTA (capmatinib), another MET inhibitor has held a marketing authorisation (MA) since February 2022, namely TEPMETKO (tepotinib), not yet reviewed by the Transparency Committee, for the treatment of adult patients with advanced NSCLC harbouring a METex14 mutation who require systemic treatment after prior immunotherapy and/or platinum-based chemotherapy. Crizotinib (XALKORI), a non-specific multikinase inhibitor with MET activity, is available under compassionate use in this setting (off-label).
In thoracic oncology, the 2022 recommendations of the United States National Comprehensive Cancer Nettwork (NCCN) advocate systematic testing for the METex14 mutation in patients with metastatic NSCLC. After a first systemic treatment, they recommend capmatinib or tepotinib as preferred second-line treatments. Crizotinib is considered an option in certain circumstances.
The 2022 French Auvergne Rhône Alpes (AURA) recommendations call for systematic testing for the METex14 mutation in second line and beyond, in patients with advanced-stage NSCLC. These AURA recommendations specify that patients with this mutation should be referred to a clinical trial or, optionally, offered capmatinib in second line.
Place of TABRECTA (capmatinib) in the therapeutic strategy
Taking into account:
the low level of evidence, given the purely descriptive nature of the results (study design), with efficacy results derived mainly from a non-comparative, multi-cohort phase II study;
the available indirect comparison data, which do not allow the therapeutic contribution of this product relative to available alternatives to be quantified, due to major biases identified during its analysis;
limited data on the prognostic and predictive value of the METex14 mutation;
the tolerability profile, which differs from available treatments, notably marked by a proportion of grade ≥3 adverse events of 70%, and serious adverse events in about half of patients (53,1%);
the premature interruption of enrolment in the phase III, multicentre, randomised, comparative study versus chemotherapy (docetaxel), with only 20 patients randomised out of the 90 planned (although a significant number of patients could be recruited in an early access (ATU) cohort in France);
the Committee considers that, in the current state of the file, TABRECTA (capmatinib) has no place in the therapeutic strategy for the treatment of adult patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) harbouring a mutation that causes skipping of exon 14 in the mesenchymal-epithelial transition factor (MET) gene (METex14), who require systemic treatment after prior immunotherapy and/or platinum-based chemotherapy.

