87db80eede82f539:
Initial listing.
Key points
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement for "long-term enzyme replacement therapy in adult patients with Fabry disease (alpha-galactosidase deficiency) whose diagnosis has been confirmed".
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Given:
the results of the BALANCE study, which demonstrated non-inferiority of ELFABRIO versus agalsidase beta at 24 months (final analysis) but not at 12 months (interim analysis) in patients pretreated with agalsidase beta, with a questionable non-inferiority margin and no placebo arm, which limits interpretation of the results;
the uncertainty about efficacy, which does not allow a loss of chance for patients to be ruled out, with no other benefit provided (in particular in terms of tolerability or ease of use) by ELFABRIO (pegunigalsidase alfa) compared with other available treatments;
the non-comparative BRIDGE study, conducted in a small number of patients and over a shorter duration (12 months) than the BALANCE study, which does not resolve doubts about the efficacy of ELFABRIO (pegunigalsidase alfa);
indirect comparisons that cannot be used because of numerous methodological limitations;
ELFABRIO (pegunigalsidase alfa) has no place in the therapeutic strategy for Fabry disease.

d4148de21beab5b1:
New indication and re-evaluation.
Key points
Favourable opinion for reimbursement in the prevention of seasonal influenza in children with and without comorbidities aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, in accordance with the current HCSP recommendations of 10 July 2014 and the HAS recommendations of 2 February 2023.
What progress?
No therapeutic progress in the management strategy.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Children with comorbidities
According to the current vaccination schedule, vaccination is recommended for people aged 65 and over and for people at risk of severe or complicated influenza, namely:
pregnant women, regardless of trimester of pregnancy;
people, including children from 6 months of age, with the following conditions:
chronic broncho-pulmonary conditions meeting the criteria for ALD 14 (asthma and COPD);
chronic obstructive or restrictive respiratory insufficiency regardless of cause, including neuromuscular diseases at risk of respiratory decompensation, malformations of the upper or lower airways, pulmonary malformations or malformations of the thoracic cage;
chronic respiratory diseases not meeting ALD criteria but liable to be aggravated or decompensated by influenza, including asthma, chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, bronchial hyperreactivity;
bronchopulmonary dysplasia;
cystic fibrosis;
cyanogenic congenital heart disease or with pulmonary arterial hypertension (HTAP) and/or heart failure;
severe heart failure;
severe valvular heart disease;
severe arrhythmias requiring long-term treatment;
coronary artery disease;
history of stroke;
severe forms of neurological and muscular conditions (including myopathy, poliomyelitis, myasthenia, motor neurone disease [Charcot disease]);
paraplegia and tetraplegia with diaphragm involvement;
severe chronic nephropathies;
nephrotic syndromes;
sickle cell disease, homozygotes and S/C double heterozygotes, thalassaemia-sickle cell disease;
type 1 and type 2 diabetes;
chronic liver disease with or without cirrhosis;
primary or acquired immune deficiencies:
oncological and haematological conditions, solid organ and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, inherited immune deficiencies,
inflammatory and/or autoimmune diseases receiving immunosuppressive treatment,
people living with HIV regardless of age and immunovirological status;
obese people with a BMI of 40 kg/m2 or more, without associated conditions or with a condition other than those listed above;
people residing in a post-acute care facility or a residential medico-social facility regardless of age;
the family members of infants under 6 months of age presenting risk factors for severe influenza as defined: premature infants, in particular those with sequelae of bronchopulmonary dysplasia, and children with congenital heart disease, congenital immune deficiency, pulmonary, neurological or neuromuscular disease, or a long-term condition (ALD);
as well as the contacts of immunocompromised people.
Children without comorbidities
At the end of its evaluation, the HAS recommends that annual seasonal influenza vaccination may be offered to children without comorbidities aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, without being made mandatory. In this age group, the HAS recommends preferential use of the intranasal vaccine FLUENZ TETRA, given its better acceptability in children due to the absence of a needle. If this vaccine is unavailable, the HAS recalls that the four other influenza vaccines (FLUARIX TETRA, VAXIGRIP TETRA, INFLUVAC TETRA, FLUCELVAX TETRA) that hold a marketing authorisation (AMM) for children may be used.
To support the rollout of this vaccination, the HAS recommends that information materials be developed for the various health professionals involved in this vaccination (physicians, nurses, pharmacists, midwives, etc.) and for the general public, in particular in schools.
Below the age of 2 years, the HAS considers that including this vaccination in the vaccination schedule raises questions of acceptability and recalls that the available data in this age group on the effectiveness of influenza vaccination remain limited and do not allow conclusions to be drawn on the expected benefits in children under 2 years without comorbidities.
The HAS recalls that annual seasonal influenza vaccination is recommended from 6 months of age for people at risk of severe or complicated influenza and in particular for pregnant women (regardless of trimester of pregnancy) and the contacts of infants under 6 months of age with risk factors for severe influenza (people living under the same roof, childminders, health professionals and people in regular contact with the infant).
This vaccination recommendation will be reviewed in light of developments in available knowledge, in particular with regard to new long-term data on use, effectiveness and safety, and the availability of new vaccines.
Within the scope of reimbursement:
The Transparency Committee considers that FLUENZ TETRA must be used in accordance with its marketing authorisation (AMM) and the current vaccination recommendations (HCSP recommendations of 10 July 2014 and HAS recommendations of 2 February 2023) for the prevention of seasonal influenza in children with and without comorbidities aged 2 to 17 years inclusive.
The Committee welcomes the extension of the vaccination population to all children aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, in line with the recommendations made in its previous opinions, and supports the preferential use of FLUENZ TETRA, a quadrivalent, live attenuated influenza vaccine administered intranasally, given its better acceptability in children due to the absence of a needle and in line with the HAS vaccination recommendations of February 2023.
Please note that this vaccine cannot be used to vaccinate certain populations that are nevertheless targeted by the recommendations. Indeed, as stated in its SmPC (RCP), FLUENZ TETRA:
is contraindicated in immunocompromised children and adolescents, nor in their contacts, as it is a live vaccine;
must not be used in children and adolescents with severe asthma or wheezing episodes;
must not be used in infants and children under 24 months of age, for safety reasons linked to increased rates of hospitalisation and wheezing episodes in this population.
The Committee recalls that vaccination is the most effective tool for preventing influenza and associated complications, in particular in at-risk populations. Good vaccination coverage, including among health care workers, is essential.
Furthermore, hygiene barrier measures (wearing surgical masks and frequent hand washing) help limit the risk of transmission and contamination, in particular of very young children and people with particular risk factors.
These two measures are the cornerstone of influenza prevention.
Specific recommendations
Specific recommendations in view of the quality and safety requirements of care related to the medicinal product
The Committee welcomes the extension of the vaccination population to all children aged 2 to 17 years inclusive, in line with the recommendations made in its previous opinions, and supports the preferential use of FLUENZ TETRA, a quadrivalent, live attenuated influenza vaccine administered intranasally, given its better acceptability in children due to the absence of a needle and in line with the HAS vaccination recommendations of February 2023.
The Committee recalls that vaccination is the most effective tool for preventing influenza and associated complications, in particular in at-risk populations. Good vaccination coverage, including among health care workers, is essential.
Furthermore, hygiene barrier measures (wearing surgical masks and frequent hand washing) help limit the risk of transmission and contamination, in particular of very young children and people with particular risk factors.
These two measures are the cornerstone of influenza prevention.
Other requests
The Committee recalls that the vaccine FLUENZ TETRA, a quadrivalent, live attenuated influenza vaccine, is in particular contraindicated in children and adolescents with an immune deficiency due to a condition or to immunosuppressive treatment (see SmPC (RCP) for further details).
In accordance with its current SmPC (RCP), FLUENZ TETRA must not be administered to children and adolescents with severe asthma or current wheezing, as this type of patient has not been the subject of adequate clinical studies.
It is also stated that vaccinated persons should be informed that FLUENZ TETRA is a live attenuated virus vaccine that may be transmitted to immunocompromised individuals. Vaccinated persons are advised to avoid, as far as possible, close contact with severely immunocompromised individuals (e.g. recipients of a bone marrow transplant requiring isolation) for 1 to 2 weeks following vaccination. Clinical studies with FLUENZ indicate a peak in the presence of vaccine virus 2 to 3 days after vaccination. Where contact with severely immunocompromised patients cannot be avoided, the potential risk of transmission of the vaccine influenza virus must be weighed against the risk of contracting and transmitting a wild-type influenza virus.
FLUENZ TETRA must under no circumstances be injected.
There are no data on the tolerability of intranasal administration of FLUENZ TETRA in children with uncorrected craniofacial malformations.

acc597c3e582717c:
Re-evaluation at the request of the CT.
Key points
Favourable opinion for reimbursement only in "growth retardation (current height) less than or equal to - 3 SDS and adjusted parental height < -1 SDS, in the indication of children born small for gestational age with a birth height < -2 SDS and who have not caught up on their growth deficit (growth velocity < 0 SDS over the last year) at the age of 4 years or older."
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
The new data provided do not change the place of GENOTONORM (somatropin) in the therapeutic strategy. Somatropin may only be used within the strict indications of the marketing authorisation (AMM). The decision to treat must be based on the criteria in the therapeutic interest summary for each indication, and the posologies must always be respected. However, the somatropin dose may be adjusted according to growth, clinical tolerance and IGF-1 concentrations. The benefit of treatment must be reassessed regularly.
In growth retardation in children born small for gestational age, organic causes or treatments that may explain growth retardation must be sought and excluded before starting rhGH treatment. Growth stimulation in children can only be achieved before epiphyseal fusion. Experience with starting treatment just before puberty in children born small for gestational age is limited. Consequently, starting treatment just before puberty is not recommended. The criteria for initiating rhGH treatment, all of which must be met, are:
height justifying initiation of treatment ≤ – 3 SDS for chronological age;
birth height below – 2 SDS for gestational age;
children who have not caught up on their growth deficit (growth velocity < 0 SDS over the last year) at the age of 4 years or older;
height < – 1 SDS relative to the height expected from parental heights.
Children treated with rhGH will be followed up every 3 months in consultation, including once a year with the specialist, to assess the efficacy of this treatment and the benefit of continuing it. The criteria for stopping treatment are:
occurrence or progression of a tumour process;
after the first year of treatment, a growth velocity < + 1 SDS;
growth velocity < 2 cm/year;
epiphyseal fusion on radiographs. In children, treatment must be stopped after 1 year of treatment in non-responders (growth velocity < 2 cm/year). In all cases, somatropin treatment must be reassessed after 1 to 2 years of treatment, when sufficient follow-up of growth progression is available.
Specific recommendations
The Transparency Committee maintains its recommendation for exception drug status for growth hormone for children with growth retardation (current height) less than or equal to - 3 SDS and adjusted parental height < -1 SDS, in the indication of children born small for gestational age with a birth weight and/or birth height < -2SDS and who have not caught up on their growth deficit (growth velocity < 0 SDS over the last year) at the age of 4 years or older.

6d32170c74cef3e6:
**Listing:** First-time listing.
