6b044033fa3be32b:
Extension of indication.
Key points
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the indication “BRUKINSA, in combination with obinutuzumab, is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma who have received at least two prior systemic therapies”.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Given:
the low level of evidence from the pivotal study: a phase II study in which the primary endpoint (TRG) is considered of limited relevance;
the short follow-up relative to the indolent nature of the indication concerned;
the absence of a formal conclusion on progression-free survival and overall survival endpoints, and on quality of life;
the choice of comparator group, considered suboptimal, and the absence of comparison (direct and indirect) with the other treatments currently available, although such a comparison was feasible;
the lack of assured transposability to the population of patients to be treated in France, given the patient characteristics of the ROSEWOOD study;
the safety profile, marked by an increase in grade 3 adverse events (AEs) (62,9% versus 47,9%) and serious adverse events (SAEs) (40,6% versus 31,0%);
BRUKINSA (zanubrutinib) in combination with obinutuzumab is not a treatment option in the management of relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma (FL) that has received at least two prior systemic therapies.

