306f22131757db25:
Re-evaluation at the request of the CT.
Summary
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in “the treatment of Duchenne muscular dystrophy, resulting from a nonsense mutation in the dystrophin gene, in ambulatory patients aged 2 years or older”.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Given:
the data previously evaluated by the Committee, which had not convincingly demonstrated the efficacy of ataluren, namely: a phase 2b and a phase 3 study, both placebo-controlled, which showed no statistically significant difference on the primary endpoint of the 6-minute walk test assessed at 48 weeks; and a short, open-label phase 2 study conducted in children, of limited relevance, together with its comparison with results from a historical cohort that was not free of bias, of limited interest;
new data from a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 study, which also did not demonstrate superiority of ataluren over placebo on the primary endpoint of the 6-minute walk test assessed at 72 weeks;
indirect comparisons of data from patients treated with ataluren drawn from a registry with data from a historical cohort, methodologically and clinically open to criticism, which do not allow a clinical benefit of ataluren to be presumed;
the finding, given all the clinical data accumulated since marketing authorisation, that the clinical benefit of ataluren has not been demonstrated in Duchenne muscular dystrophy resulting from a nonsense mutation in the dystrophin gene in ambulatory patients aged 2 years or older;
the absence of demonstrated clinical interest in adding ataluren to corticosteroid treatment, which is the currently recommended treatment for this disease;
TRANSLARNA (ataluren) does not have a place in the therapeutic strategy.

fa20fd46097d47e5:
Initial listing
Summary
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the indication "TEVIMBRA en monothérapie, dans le traitement des patients adultes atteints d'un carcinome épidermoïde de l'œsophage localement avancé non résécable ou métastatique, après une chimiothérapie antérieure à base de sels de platine" ("TEVIMBRA as monotherapy in the treatment of adult patients with locally advanced unresectable or metastatic oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma, after prior platinum-salt-based chemotherapy").
What is its place in the therapeutic strategy?
Taking into account, on the one hand:
the superiority of tislélizumab demonstrated versus a chemotherapy regimen whose choice was left to the investigator (docétaxel or paclitaxel or irinotécan) on overall survival (primary endpoint) in a phase III, randomised, open-label study (RATIONALE-302), with a point estimate of the absolute difference in medians of 2,3 months in favour of tislélizumab;
But also taking into account:
the first-line therapeutic strategy, which has changed since the start of the pivotal trial and is no longer platinum-salt chemotherapy alone but the combination of chemotherapy and immunotherapy (except for patients who are not eligible, i.e. those with a TPS score < 1% or CPS < 10 or with a contraindication to immunotherapy);
There may be a group of patients who did not receive immunotherapy in combination with first-line chemotherapy because immunotherapy was not indicated (TPS score < 1% or CPS < 10 or contraindication to immunotherapy). However, we have no robust data in this setting. The pivotal study was not designed to assess the benefit of TEVIMBRA (tislélizumab) in patients with a TPS score < 1% or CPS < 10. Moreover, since TEVIMBRA (tislélizumab) is an anti-PD1 agent, there does not appear to be a rationale for using this molecule in situations of contraindication.
In PD-L1-expressing patients, there are no available data allowing assessment of a possible benefit of TEVIMBRA (tislélizumab) in second line in patients who have already received immunotherapy (corresponding to the current therapeutic strategy, and which, at the time of the study, was an exclusion criterion);
the limited representativeness of Caucasian patients in the study population (80% of patients included in the study were of Asian origin);
the absence of a formal conclusion on quality of life (exploratory endpoint and open-label study);
the Transparency Committee considers that TEVIMBRA (tislélizumab), as monotherapy, has, on the current state of the data, no place in the therapeutic strategy for oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) in adult patients with locally advanced unresectable or metastatic ESCC after prior platinum-salt-based chemotherapy.

