e5ff52fdd01df6f4:
Listing.
Key points
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the indication "in combination with dexamethasone, for the treatment of adult patients with multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior lines of therapy, whose disease is refractory to at least one proteasome inhibitor, one immunomodulatory agent and one anti-CD38 monoclonal antibody, and who have experienced disease progression during or after the last treatment. For patients who have already received an autologous stem cell transplant, the interval before progression must be at least 3 ans from the transplant (see section 4.4 of the RCP)."
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Taking into account:
an efficacy demonstration judged modest and not transposable to the population to be treated, since it was conducted in patients who do not correspond to those of the AMM (they represented only 33,1% of the population in the non-comparative HORIZON study and 4,4% of the population in the comparative OCEAN study);
several methodological biases leading to considerable uncertainty about the actual efficacy of the treatment for patients (non-comparative study for HORIZON, and exploratory post-hoc subgroup analyses for both studies);
the impossibility of assessing the benefit of the treatment in patients refractory to alkylating agents and/or melphalan;
the Transparency Committee considers that PEPAXTI (melphalan flufenamide), in combination with dexamethasone, has, on the current state of the data, no place in the therapeutic strategy for adult patients with multiple myeloma who have received at least three prior lines of therapy, whose disease is refractory to at least one proteasome inhibitor, one immunomodulatory agent and one anti-CD38 monoclonal antibody, and who have experienced disease progression during or after the last treatment (for patients who have already received an autologous stem cell transplant, the interval before progression must be at least 3 ans from the transplant).

529da6af8329082b:
Initial listing.
Key points
Favourable opinion for reimbursement in combination with other treatments for pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH), for the treatment of PAH in adult patients in WHO functional class (FC) II to III, with the aim of improving exercise capacity.
What progress?
A therapeutic advance in patient management.
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
According to French recommendations (2020):
In patients at low risk of death at one year, ERAs or PDE-5i are proposed as monotherapy or as up-front combination therapy;
In patients at intermediate risk, oral ERA + PDE-5i combination is proposed up front;
In patients at high risk, triple therapy including intravenous epoprostenol is recommended.
According to European recommendations (2022):
In patients at low or intermediate risk, oral ERA + PDE-5i combination is proposed up front;
In patients at high risk, triple therapy of ERA + PDE-5i + IV/SC prostacyclin analogue is recommended.
Monotherapy with an ERA or PDE-5i is recommended only by the French recommendations, and only in patients at low risk. Given this limited recommendation and the low proportion on monotherapy (4%) in the STELLAR study, the place of WINREVAIR (sotatercept) in low-risk patients treated with ERA or PDE-5i monotherapy is limited.
WINREVAIR (sotatercept) should be used in patients with PAH already treated with standard therapy.
In view of:
The current therapeutic management and its limitations in terms of efficacy and tolerability;
the mechanism of action of WINREVAIR (sotatercept) in a therapeutic context where it is important to target several pathophysiological pathways;
the demonstrated efficacy of sotatercept in the STELLAR study, notably on the 6-minute walk test;
its tolerability profile when added to other PAH medications;
WINREVAIR (sotatercept) is a first-line treatment, in adult patients with PAH in WHO functional class II or III, in combination with standard therapy.

42ca1974e13e4f37:
Listing

Key points

Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement in the marketing authorisation (AMM) indication: « Prevention of cisplatin-based chemotherapy-induced ototoxicity (CIS) in patients aged 1 month to 18 years with localised, non-metastatic solid tumours. »

What place in the therapeutic strategy?

Taking into account:
evidence of efficacy (statistically significant reduction) on hearing loss:
in the SIOPEL 6 study, hearing loss was defined as a Brock grade ≥ 1 at an age ≥ 3,5 years (20/57 children [35,1%]) in the CIS + TSS group vs. 35/52 children [67,3%] in the CIS-alone group); relative risk: 0,52, (IC95% [0,35 ; 0,78], p<0,001).
in the ACCL0431 study, hearing loss was defined by comparing ASHA criteria assessed at initiation and 4 weeks after the last cisplatin course (14/49 patients [28,6%] in the CIS + TSS group vs. 31/55 patients [56,4%] in the CIS group); odds ratio of 0,274 (IC 95% [0,11-0,66], p=0,0039);
several methodological biases in these two studies and data that do not formally exclude a lower efficacy of cisplatin's antitumour activity in the indication considered;
a safety profile marked by additional toxicity compared with the control group without sodium thiosulfate;
absence of quality-of-life data;
and absence of data on the benefit of this drug compared with a prolonged cisplatin administration schedule, notably over 24 hours, used in clinical practice in France and likely to be less ototoxic;
the Transparency Committee considers that PEDMARQSI (sodium thiosulfate), solution for infusion, has no place in the therapeutic strategy to prevent cisplatin-based chemotherapy-induced ototoxicity (CIS) in patients aged 1 month to 18 years with localised, non-metastatic solid tumours.

6b83969a7b71e60c:
Listing
Key points
Unfavourable opinion on reimbursement of TECENTRIQ as monotherapy in "the first-line treatment of adult patients with advanced NSCLC ineligible for platinum-based chemotherapy (see section 5.1 of the Summary of Product Characteristics (SmPC) for selection criteria)."
What place in the therapeutic strategy?
Taking into account:
the results observed in the IPSOS study, with a modest difference in overall survival (primary endpoint) of 1,1 months on medians compared with investigator's choice monochemotherapy (vinorelbine or gemcitabine): HR = 0, 78 IC95% [0,63 ; 0,97]; p=0,028. Median overall survival was 10,3 months in the atezolizumab group versus 9,2 months in the chemotherapy group (vinorelbine or gemcitabine);
the absence of demonstrated benefit in progression-free survival (exploratory endpoint);
the uncertainty about the transferability of the results observed in this study, given non-consensual ineligibility criteria for platinum-based treatment, a heterogeneous study population, and non-robust results in the marketing authorisation (AMM) population arising from a post hoc analysis (in 89% of the ITT population) cf. 4. Discussion.
a debatable comparator in the population considered;
the absence of any firm conclusion that can be drawn on quality of life at an advanced stage of the disease;
the excess toxicity in terms of serious adverse events (48,7% vs. 37,1%), and adverse events of special interest (34% vs. 18,4%);
the Transparency Committee considers that TECENTRIQ (atezolizumab) has no place in the therapeutic strategy.

